### Area 12 · Procedural and implicit memory: enacted knowledge `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by:** `ontology-rounds/round47-2026-09-15.md` §2 (**ImplicitMemBench**,
arXiv 2604.08064, ACL 2026). Read this pass.
**This area exists because a benchmark read in the tranche's nineteenth file
demonstrated that every memory design in this arena is measuring the wrong thing.**
It is the most consequential addition since Area 6, and it is a *negative* finding
about the arena rather than a design.

**Rank 1 — Proceduralization as the destination of consolidation: a store of
- **Brain part:** **non-declarative (implicit) memory** — the basal ganglia and
- **Design:** a **Learning / Priming-Interfere-Test** protocol as a first-class
- **Already exists — the benchmark, and the result is the finding:**
- **Backup documents (added):** `ontology-rounds/round84-2026-09-11.md` §§1, 5.
- **Corroboration (tranche 50, ORDER 1130 `Learning/Priming-and-Implicit-Memory.md`,
  298 lines) — the strongest *anatomical* evidence in the corpus for this area's
  rank, from a source independent of the benchmark.** Support **4 → 5**, design
  grade held `LOW`. The benchmark shows implicit memory is not being measured; this
  file shows what it is made of. Three results earn the increment: (1)
  **Warrington & Weiskrantz (1974)** — Korsakoff's patients, profoundly impaired on
  recall *and* recognition, **matched controls** on word-stem completion; (2)
  **Tulving, Schacter & Stark (1982)** — priming survived a **one-week** delay
  that destroyed recognition, so the two are **statistically independent systems**,
  not one system read two ways; (3) **Heindel et al. (1989) — a double
  dissociation**: Alzheimer's patients impaired on lexical/semantic priming but
  normal on pursuit-rotor learning, Huntington's patients the exact reverse. The
  third is the load-bearing one for a *three-slot* area: **"implicit memory" is at
  least two systems with different substrates** (cortical vs subcortical), which
  is why rank 1's single "proceduralization" destination cannot be the whole
  account.
  **Narrowing recorded against this area's rank 1, not a demotion:** the corpus's
  **H.M.** case has H.M. improving daily on a pursuit-rotor task with **no
  conscious memory of ever practising it** — which supports rank 1 — while the
  Heindel double dissociation says the implicit store is **plural**, and rank 1
  names one destination for all of it.
  **Load-bearing for the arena's own apparatus:** this file is the corpus's
  clearest statement of the replication crisis **splitting a field in two** —
  behavioural/social priming is *"effectively dead"* (Kahneman 2022; Bargh's
  elderly-walking effect failed in Doyen et al. 2012 and Pashler et al. 2012, with
  Doyen finding the effect **only when experimenters expected it**), while
  **cognitive priming is robust** (Weingarten et al. 2016 meta-analysis). A system
  that imports the social-priming result without that split inherits a dead
  finding.
- **Grade:** design `LOW` · Support: **8 independent sources** (ImplicitMemBench
  arXiv 2604.08064; Learning/Priming-and-Implicit-Memory ORDER 1130; Experience
  Compression arXiv 2604.15877 ORDER 1194; Procedural Graphs arXiv 2609.09153
  ORDER 1229; ReMe arXiv 2512.10696 ORDER 1726 — the count was stale at 5 through
  tranches 55–56 and is corrected here, +1 for ReMe)
- **Backup documents:** `round47-2026-09-15.md` §2; `round38-2026-09-07.md` §1
- **Grade:** design `LOW` for the benchmark and the failure it measures ·
