Compression Spectrum, arXiv 2604.15877, 354 lines) — a *quantified* version of
  this area's premise from a source independent of the benchmark.** Support
  **5 → 6**. The file's Level 2 result is the sharpest number the corpus offers
  on why implicit memory is hard to build: **curated skills add +16.2pp on
  SkillsBench while LLM self-generated skills add +0.0pp** — a 16-point gap
  between a skill written by something outside the model and one the model
  wrote itself. That is Area 12's thesis (explicit recall is not enactment)
  restated as a production gap. It also supplies the **missing diagonal**: no
  system supports *adaptive cross-level* compression, with cross-community
  citation rates below 1% (memory papers cite skill work at 0.7%, skill papers
  cite memory work at 1.2%) — the field is fragmented, which is *why* no
  procedural system exists. Grade held `LOW`: this is a benchmark of curriculum
  design, not a trial of a proceduralization mechanism. **Note the collision
  with Area 95 Rank 3**, which cites the same +16.2/+0.0 result as a neighbour —
  one file, one source, **not** two independent supports for two claims.
**Corroboration (tranche 56, ORDER 1229
`oracle/brain/.meta/archive/ontology-rounds/frontier-research-ontology-round84-2026-09-11.md`
§1 (**Procedural Graphs**, arXiv 2609.09153, Google Research) — a *third* independent
route to this area's claim, and the first that treats the procedural substrate as
an editable object rather than as an emergent one.** Support **6 → 7**, design
grade held `LOW`. The contribution is that "what-to-do" gets the same treatment
"what-is" has long had: a **Procedural Graph** whose nodes are procedures and
whose edges are transitions, queried by *"what do I do next?"* rather than *what
is X?* Three results earn the increment. (1) The **self-evolution loop is
gated**: diagnostic rollout → feedback-driven mutation → **validation gate**
(`val(G_cand) ≥ val(G_retained)`, adopt only on non-regression) → **rejection
memory**, where rejected candidates are *retained as negative constraints.* That
is the arena's own practice — `SKILL.md`'s "Never delete" and the recorded dead
ends in Areas 12 and 96 — arrived at as a published design, and it is the first
source in this arena's history that makes **keeping the rejected candidate**
part of the design rather than an audit convention. (2) The **static expert PG
matches or surpasses hand-designed graphs, and a self-evolved one matches or
exceeds the expert PG** — procedural knowledge is not privileged by being
curated, which is a real narrowing of this area's `curated > self-generated`
reading. (3) **Node localization** — *at each decision step, localize the active
node and read its surrounding subgraph* — is prospective memory as a retrieval
operation, arriving from the procedural side and landing on the same mechanism
this area's rank 2 names.
**Two design requirements this earns, and they cut against the current slots:**
the **validation gate is an external measurement**, and a candidate procedure
adopted because *the model that wrote it also judged it* is the
self-graded-loop shape **Area 95** shows compounds its own bias — so rank 1's
gate must read a held-out score, not a self-assessment. And the **soft
constraint** design — edge attributes *bias* the solver's next action *without
dictating it* — is the first source in this arena that distinguishes a
procedure which **guides** from one which **compels**, which is the distinction
Areas 4 and 12 both need and neither states.
**Corroboration (tranche 73, ORDER 1726
`oracle/brain/research/frontier-research-ontology-2026-09-07-round66.md` §1,
**ReMe**, arXiv 2512.10696, ACL 2026 Findings — 340 lines, read this pass, the
only one of this pass's twenty reads that resolved; see `VERIFICATION.md` V-73.1
for the corpus-root fault that blocked the other nineteen) — the first source in this
area's history that reports a **controlled outcome for a procedural layer**, where
every prior support was a benchmark exposing a gap, a compression result, or a
graph formalism. Support **7 → 8**, design grade held `LOW`.** The increment is
earned by the design of the comparison, not by the size of the effect: agents
carrying an explicit **procedural** layer (action schemas with preconditions,
effects and execution traces) reach **2.3× faster task completion and 37% fewer
errors** on ALFWorld and WebShop against an episodic+semantic-only baseline — i.e.
the third memory type is separated from the other two *by construction*, which is
the control this area's earlier sources lacked. The load-bearing mechanism claim
is that procedural memory stores **abstracted** execution patterns instantiated in
novel contexts, not traces replayed verbatim; that is the same
`enacted ≠ recalled` distinction the rank-1 design turns on, now with a number on
it.
**Two qualifications this earns, both cutting against the slot.** (1) The
comparison holds the *store* fixed and varies only the presence of L4, so it is
evidence that a procedural layer **adds to** episodic+semantic memory — not
evidence about **where** procedural content should live, which is the part of
rank 1 this area is actually about. (2) The file's own 47-author survey
(§6.1) classes L4 procedural as **"Emerging"** and L5 meta-cognitive as
**"Nascent"** — a field-wide consensus statement that this layer is *not*
settled. `LOW` is held rather than raised to `HIGH`: one paper, no independent
replication of the 2.3×/37% figures anywhere in this corpus, and the same file
that reports them reports no ablation isolating the abstracted-vs-verbatim
mechanism.
