#### ORDER 557 (`Cognitive-Development/Predictive-Processing-Development.md`) — the
#### arena's sharpest self-indictment, and it arrives from developmental neuroscience

**Support 2 → 3 on rank 1** (a genuinely independent source: this is predictive-processing
developmental neuroscience, not the personality/identity literature rank 1 came from).
**No rank or grade change.** What earns the read is a single sentence that describes
this arena's own construction exactly:

> **Ward et al. (2025), §7.2:** predictive processing is *"fundamentally incapable of
> accommodating development in its current form"* — it has **no defined starting
> point**, no mechanisms for categorization or generalization, and no account of
> qualitative changes and moments of insight. *"If PP cannot explain its own
> development — how the predictive machinery comes into existence — then it is
> incomplete as a theory of cognition."*

**This is Area 15's thesis stated against a neighbouring framework, and it applies to
this arena without modification.** The arena's opening move is to enumerate areas
retrospectively from files already read — an ordering derived from *what the corpus
happens to contain*, not from any developmental principle. That is a top-down
identity with a catalogue of lower layers appended, which is the exact inversion rank
1 exists to prevent, committed by the arena that wrote rank 1. Recorded rather than
smoothed over.

**The counter-proposal is in the same file, and it is the strongest external support
this area has received.** §1.1: the ontogenetic starting point is a set of
**low-precision, low-level-of-detail predictions** — broad and flexible, *"allowing
the infant to remain open to a wide range of experiences."* The mechanism is stated
precisely: initial observations generate prediction errors **with high precision
relative to the low-precision predictions**, and are therefore *informative*, which
drives rapid model updating. The first visual model *"may take the form of a uniform
expectation that light may hit the retina at any point and any intensity."*
**Design consequence for rank 1, and it is the operational form of bottom-up
construction: start imprecise, not empty-and-then-fill.** A system that begins with
confident priors has nothing to learn from; a system that begins with maximally
uncertain priors treats every input as maximally informative. The arena does the
latter in form (nothing is asserted without a source) and not the former in spirit
(each slot arrives already graded).

**Three further findings, each with a design consequence, recorded as reinforcement
rather than as new slots:**

- **§1.4 — synaptic density follows rise-and-fall, and the timeline is asymmetric.**
  Layer IIIc pyramidal spine density peaks at **ages 2.5–7**, layer V at **7–9**;
  prefrontal layer IIIc density falls significantly by **17** and reaches adult levels
  only around **30**. Read as design: overproduction then pruning, with the *highest*
  layer last and longest. A store built to hold everything has no pruning phase, and
  Area 6's forgetting pass is that phase.
- **§5.4 — precision weighting is U-shaped across the lifespan**: high in infancy,
  optimized in adulthood, declining in aging with *increased prior strength but reduced
  flexibility*. The declining half is **Area 41's reserve result arriving from the
  precision side**: older adults integrate auditory and visual information into more
  illusions (Brandl et al. 2021, MEG + dynamic causal modeling), and Auksztulewicz
  et al. (2026, N = 63, ages 18–75) find stronger top-down modulation of deviants but
  **weaker modulation by slow dynamics** — i.e. the system tracks surprise well and
  tracks gradual drift badly. **Design consequence: a slow-drift blind spot is a
  distinct failure from a surprise-detection failure, and no existing area separates
  them.**
- **§4.4 — developmental cascades.** An early precision-weighting abnormality builds a
  model from a noisier signal, which yields poorly calibrated, overly specific priors,
  which yield more errors, which further disrupt development. **This is the mechanism
  behind Area 25**: an error at write time does not stay local, it compounds through
  everything built on top. It is also the strongest available argument that a
  **supersedes chain without a retraction path** (Area 1, ORDER 553) is not a
  conservative choice.

**Two disputes preserved, not reconciled (R-J4).** §4.1 vs §4.2 vs §4.3: the
**hypo-prior** account of autism (Pellicano & Burr 2012 — weaker top-down priors,
perception *less* biased by expectation) versus the **aberrant precision** account
(Friston et al. 2013 — *overly precise* sensory errors) versus Van de Cruys et al.
(2014) — *"weak priors versus overfitting"*, where the deficit is **failure to flexibly
adjust precision** rather than either prior strength. The empirical section (§4.3)
reports the two disorders moving in **opposite directions** on the same ERP measure
(ASD: reduced frontal response to *unexpected* events; ADHD: reduced response to
*expected* events with strong PFC activation to unexpected) — which is good evidence
that precision is at least two-dimensional, and no account in the file says which
dimension. §7.4 adds a second unresolved tension: **critical vs sensitive periods** —
are models permanently fixed, or accelerable-and-partially-compensable? The file's
answer is hierarchical (*"lower levels of the hierarchy have earlier, more rigid
critical periods, while higher levels retain plasticity longer"*), which is a position,
not a resolution. **Both disputes are carried; neither is entered as settled, and
neither is averaged into a grade.**

