### Area 19 · Belief-to-action compliance: the enforcement gap `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by:** `enforcement-layer-supersede-prevention.md` §"The Core Problem" and
§"Why This Matters" (ORDER line 177, read whole this pass) — TRACE, arXiv
2606.13174, Zhou et al.
**Corroborated by:** `frontier-research-fca-semanticweb-evaluation.md` §4 (ORDER
191) — Cyc's recorded failure; `frontier-research-dual-memory-dynamic-ontology-
experiential-2026-sep-update.md` §1.1 (ORDER 190) — the progress/feasibility
ablation. **Re-read and corroborated tranche 18 by:** `graphiti-temporal-knowledge-graph.md`
(ORDER 309) — see the "seventh independent source" entry below.
**Why this is a brain part and not another facet of memory.** The arena's
eighteen areas are all about the **acquisition, storage, verification, retrieval
or revision** of a belief. This one is about the step after all five: **whether
the belief governs the action it is supposed to govern.** The human organ is the
declarative–procedural action-selection loop, and the finding that makes it a
brain part rather than an engineering nuisance is that the gap is **large,
measured, and not closed by making retrieval correct**. TRACE's number is the
occasion: **57.5% of applicable preference checks are violated even when the
preference is correctly stored and retrieved** — five-condition comparisons on
ClawArena and MemoryArena. The file's sentence is the thesis of this area:
*"retrieval correctness ≠ behavioral compliance."*
**The design requirement this earns is stated by the corpus in the plainest
words available:** *"detection without enforcement is theater."* A system that
notices a preference has been superseded and then does nothing about it has not
detected anything.

