### Area 20 · Image schemas: the sensorimotor grounding of abstract concept

**Earned by:** `frontier-research-neuroscience-memory-embodiment-topology-2026-09-03.md`
§2 (ORDER line 203, read whole this pass) — reporting **arXiv 2503.24110**
(image schemas as grounding primitives, answer-set-programming formalisation) and
**ODIN** (CEUR Vol-3352, mapping 116 Ontology Design Patterns to image-schema
profiles). Also §1.2 (SCM, arXiv 2604.20943) and ORDER 208 §6.1–6.2.
**Why this is a brain part and not a twentieth version of Area 8.** Through
nineteen areas, every one began from *symbolic* content — a document, a triple, a
trajectory, a schema, a transcript. Area 8 asks whether the structure extracted
from that content is faithful. **No area asked where the concept's form came from
before it was symbolised.** The human organ is the one Mark Johnson's work names:
**image-schematic processing** — the recruitment of recurring sensorimotor
patterns (CONTAINMENT, SUPPORT, SOURCE-PATH-GOAL, UP/DOWN, CONTACT) as the
scaffold from which abstract thought is built by metaphorical mapping. This is
distinct from Area 5's sensorimotor loop, which is a *channel*; this is the
*representational substrate* that makes any channel's content thinkable.
**The corpus's evidence is one paper plus one mapping study, and both are
formally grounded** — which is why this is not a weaker version of another area
but a different claim. The corpus also **disagrees with itself about whether this
is achievable at all**, and the arena holds all three positions.

**Qualification, tranche 49 (ORDER 1100, read whole) — the empirical
foundations of the concept-clearing task are weaker than the area assumed, and
the grade is held because the rank does not rest on them.** ORDER 1100 records
that the **action-sentence compatibility effect is contested in the corpus's own
right**: an 8-experiment failure (Papesh 2015), a 6-experiment mouse-tracking
failure (Greco et al. 2021), and a **12-lab pre-registered replication at
N ≈ 2,000 that found no reliable ACE** (Kaschak et al. 2020). The **literal vs.
figurative** distinction also collapses, since a corpus *is* a figure of speech
(*"we say a lot about cats and dogs"*) under an ACE-effect account. The design
is unaffected — it never claimed the effect holds universally — but **any slot
graded on "concepts are cleared by compatible actions" inherits this**, and the
arena has none. Recorded rather than acted on, per the rule that a
qualification is not a grade change when the graded claim is not the one
qualified.

**Rank 1 — Give every concept in the upper ontology at least one image-schema
