### Area 37 · The vigilance decrement: a monitor that has never been caught failing is not a monitor that works `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by `Attention/Vigilance-Decrement-Sustained-Attention.md` (ORDER 530)
§§"The signal-detection anatomy of a decrement", "Applied evidence", read whole
this pass. Corroborated within the same tranche by
`Attention/Attentional-Residue.md` (ORDER 519) §"Field observation of fragmented
knowledge work" and `Attention/Inattentional-Blindness-Change-Blindness.md`
(ORDER 523) §"Expert radiologists (2013)".
**Why this is a brain part and not a facet of Area 13.** Area 13 is the controller
that decides whether to continue. This area is about the **failure of detection
that the controller cannot observe**, and the corpus's finding is sharper than
"attention drifts": the 1948 Mackworth clock-test result and the 2025 review
disagree about *which* component decays, and **the two dominant answers imply
opposite fixes.** ORDER 530 §Overview states the consequence — *"a criterion shift
implies cheap fixes (feedback, incentives, prevalence management), while true
sensitivity loss implies resource depletion and mandatory rest."* A design that
does not know which it is facing is not a design.
**What makes this area load-bearing for the arena.** The arena's central device is
that a clean run is reported and a violation is caught. ORDER 530 §"Applied
evidence" says about the human counterpart that **at 1–2% target prevalence,
trained observers miss ~30% of targets, the effect survives full airport-screening
training, and neither incentives nor warning observers abolishes it**
(Wolfe et al. 2007; and the TSO replication, ORDER 530 §Sources 12). The corpus
then states the machine consequence in its own words at §Open Question 8: **"No
standard audit exists. The literature implies a concrete instrument — canary
targets injected at known prevalence, with hit/false-alarm tracking decomposed
into sensitivity and bias over system uptime — but this appears unbuilt in
practice."** A watcher with no canaries is not verified; it is unmeasured, and it
reports the same silence either way. That is Area 33's asymmetry arriving on the
arena's own instruments, from a literature it had not read.

**Rank 1 — Inject canary targets at known positions and known prevalence, and
  treat a monitor that has not missed one recently as unverified rather than as
  working.**
  - **Brain part:** Mackworth's countermeasure result, which is the founding
    finding of the field and the reason it was commissioned in 1943: performance
    feedback **eliminated** the decrement, while instructing subjects to try
    harder **did nothing** (ORDER 530 §"The founding experiments"). Knowledge of
    results is the one intervention that works where exhortation fails.
  - **Design:** every long-running checker — invariant script, drift check, read
    ratio — gets a synthetic event it is known to be able to detect, at a known
    rate, and reports its own hit and false-alarm counts over uptime. The
    distinction the design turns on is the corpus's: **hit-rate decline alone
    cannot distinguish a threshold problem from a discrimination problem**, so
    the instrument tracks the two separately (ORDER 530 §"Criterion drift maps to
    threshold rot").
  - **Already exists:** the modelling machinery, named in the corpus —
    biomathematical fatigue models (**SAFTE**), circadian alertness models,
    ACT-R-based microlapse theories, and EEG/fNIRS vigilance classifiers at
    typical binary accuracies **above 70%**, high nineties on two-state problems
    (ORDER 530 §"Existing predictive machinery"). The instrument is unbuilt; the
    estimators are not.
  - **Backup document:** `Attention/Vigilance-Decrement-Sustained-Attention.md`
    (ORDER 530) §§"Countermeasures translate with surprising fidelity",
    "Applied evidence", §Open Question 8.
  - **Grade:** the human finding **`HIGH`** (75 years, cross-lab, cross-species —
    ORDER 530 §"Cross-species generality" reports the decrement in rats and
    jumping spiders with pharmacological reversal); **the canary instrumentation
    design `UNTESTED`** · Support: **1 source**, and that source is a review, not a
    trial. The corpus is explicit that the intervention is unbuilt.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot that makes the *absence* of a failure
    signal into information rather than into a pass. Everything else in this area
    describes decay; only this one makes decay detectable before it has happened.
  - **Interaction:** **upstream of Area 33 and the direct answer to its Rank 1.**
    Area 33 says a detector is not a certifier; this says the detector needs its
    own detector. **Constrains Area 30** (per-episode cost): a canary that costs a
    re-verification pass is a per-episode cost paid deliberately, and at 14,589
    files (R-J5) the injection rate is a budget decision, not a detail.
    **Constrains Area 3**: effort spent monitoring a monitor is only worth
    allocating if the monitor is known to be live.

**Rank 2 — Decompose every reported aggregate into sensitivity and bias, and
  never report a rate without its base rate.**
  - **Brain part:** the **signal-detection anatomy** — a falling hit rate has
    three non-exclusive causes (conservative criterion shift, sensitivity loss,
    lapse-rate increase), and ORDER 530 §Core Mechanisms states that
    psychometric-curve fitting *"found evidence for all three components
    co-occurring."* The history is a live dispute: the 1995 meta-analysis
    (42 studies, 138 conditions, mean effect size **0.71**) concluded sensitivity
    losses are *"much more prevalent than previously believed"*; the 2016
    critique showed that **if false alarms sit at floor, a conservative bias shift
    mathematically manifests as falling d-prime even when discrimination is
    untouched**, demonstrated with a lure experiment; reanalyses have since split
    the difference toward *"all three components co-occur."*
  - **Design:** no checker in this workspace reports a bare rate. A miss rate
    without its base rate is unreadable — a 90% hit rate on a task with 1% true
    positives is a *worse* detector than 70% on a task with 50%. This applies
    directly to `arena_invariants.py` and `verify_reads.py`, whose verdicts are
    currently all-or-nothing pass/fail with no sensitivity/bias decomposition.
  - **Already exists:** **signal detection theory** (Green & Swets, 1963) and its
    floor-robust successors — ORDER 530 §Open Question 1 names the fix as
    *"widespread adoption of floor-robust metrics (log-linear Az, hierarchical
    Bayesian estimation) over A-prime is the cheapest path to resolution and has
    not yet happened at scale."* A-prime is the non-parametric default and it
    *"declines spuriously under any sufficiently extreme bias regardless of true
    d-prime."*
  - **Backup document:** `Attention/Vigilance-Decrement-Sustained-Attention.md`
    (ORDER 530) §"The sensitivity war", §"Measurement-practice failures".
  - **Grade:** the decomposition **`HIGH`** for the human phenomenon and the
    metric-artifact argument; **the reporting discipline `UNTESTED`** · Support:
    **1 source**. The corpus preserves the disagreement rather than settling it —
    §Open Question 2 puts the 1995-vs-2016 conflict as unresolved and names three
    candidate resolutions (genuine era effects, metric artifacts, publication bias
    in opposite directions). **Per R-J4 the disagreement is recorded, not
    reconciled, and no side is entered as settled.**
  - **Why rank 2 and not rank 1:** it improves the reading of a metric that is
    already collected; Rank 1 creates the evidence Rank 2 then needs. It is also
    the *only* slot that would have caught a specific defect already in this
    workspace: the last pass's own `verify_reads.py` NON-COMPLIANT verdict was a
    bare ratio whose cause (log rotation) it could not distinguish from fabricated
    marks, which is exactly the criterion/sensitivity confusion one level down.
  - **Interaction:** **directly hardens Area 33.** A pass/fail checker with no
    base rate is an A-prime. Pairs with Area 26 (discriminability is two-sided) —
    a rate without its base rate cannot distinguish the two classes it is
    supposed to.

**Rank 3 — Concede that indefinite continuous attention is not achievable, and
  build for distributed micro-restoration instead.**
  - **Brain part:** the **uni-hemispheric sleep result** — dolphins, of all
    species, are the only ones in the corpus that maintained detection across
    sessions up to **120 continuous hours**, by sleeping one hemisphere at a time
    (ORDER 530 §"Cross-species generality"). ORDER 530 states the design
    principle it implies: *"indefinite vigilance requires distributed
    micro-restoration rather than continuous full engagement."*
  - **Design:** a long-running monitor is decomposed into task segments with
    explicit checkpoints and re-verification boundaries, so that "rest" is a
    state the system can be in rather than a gap in its coverage. The corpus
    supplies the machine analogue directly: *"interleave monitoring with unrelated
    work, force periodic full state re-verification rather than trusting
    incrementally updated beliefs."*
  - **Already exists:** checkpoint/restore and checkpoint-and-resume
    architectures, named in the arena's own Area 32 and Area 4. **What does not
    exist in the corpus is a trial of them as a vigilance countermeasure** — the
    human evidence is that regular brief interruptions reduced the decrement
    (Jenkins), and the machine version is an inference.
  - **Backup document:** `Attention/Vigilance-Decrement-Sustained-Attention.md`
    (ORDER 530) §"Cross-species generality", §Open Question 5;
    `Attention/Attentional-Residue.md` (ORDER 519) §"The cost model has three
    separable components".
  - **Grade:** design **`UNTESTED`** · Support: **2 sources** for the mechanism
    (ORDER 530 and ORDER 519, which are independent literatures — vigilance
    decrement and task-switching residue — arriving at the same architecture from
    opposite directions). ORDER 519's own arbitration is the useful part: **the
    popular "23 minutes 15 seconds" resumption figure is not a measurement**, and
    that file says so against its own field, reporting the peer-reviewed anchors
    as *"mean ≈ 22.6 min (externally interrupted) and ≈ 29 min (internally
    interrupted), with large SDs (~54–56 min)"* and noting the canonical CHI 2008
    study *"did not report a single resumption-lag figure at all."*
  - **Why rank 3:** it concedes a limit rather than instrumenting one, and it
    ranks below Ranks 1 and 2 because both of those buy detection first. It is
    kept for the cross-literature convergence, which is the strongest signal in
    the tranche: two unrelated fields independently arriving at
    checkpoint-and-resume is worth recording even unadopted.
  - **Interaction:** **the scheduling layer under Ranks 1 and 2** — R-J5 states
    the scale assumption explicitly: at 14,589 files, the re-verification
    interval is a per-episode cost (Area 30) and the resume artifact is a
    prospective-memory obligation (Area 4). **It is the arena's second instance,
    after Area 35, of a claim that arrives from two unrelated fields and is
    recorded as convergence rather than as proof.**

