### Area 38 · The capacity of the workspace itself: how much can be held at once, and what happens when the answer is "four" `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by `Attention/Working-Memory-Limits.md` (ORDER 531) §§"The Embedded-Processes
Model and the 'Magical Number 4'", "Long-Term Working Memory (Ericsson & Kintsch,
1995)", "The training transfer debate", read whole this pass. Corroborated within the
tranche by `Canonical-Microcircuit/Canonical-Microcircuit-Columnar-Architecture.md`
(ORDER 539) §"Memory, attractor dynamics, and the microcircuit" and
`Cellular-Neuroscience/Pyramidal-Neuron-Apical-Dendrite-Computations.md` (ORDER 545)
§"Working Memory and Burst-Encoding".
**Why this is a brain part and not a facet of Area 13.** Area 13 is the *controller* that
decides whether to continue. Nothing in the arena's thirty-seven prior areas describes the
**size of the thing being controlled**. The corpus's finding is that the size is small, is
estimated differently by different labs, and that the human workarounds are architectural
rather than volumetric — the workspace does not grow, the *cues* in it get better.
**What makes this area load-bearing for this workspace specifically.** ORDER 531 §"The
'Four-Chunk' Design Rule" states the design consequence in its own terms: *"present no more
than four 'units of attention' at any level of abstraction"* — and applies it to **prompt
design** (limiting distinct instructions), **tool selection** (*"offering 3-5 tool options
at each decision point rather than 20"*), **memory retrieval** (*"returning 3-5 'chunks' of
relevant context rather than 50 documents"*), and **UI design**. This arena currently holds
**37 areas in a 2,748-line answer file read as a single artifact**, and the deferred tool
catalog in this job's own runtime is **68 tools across 4 servers**. The area is not an
abstract constraint imported from psychology; it is a measurement of the thing this job is
built out of, arriving from a literature this arena had not read.

**Rank 1 — Hold retrieval cues in the workspace, never the content; make the retrieval
  structure good instead of making the window larger.**
  - **Brain part:** **long-term working memory** (Ericsson & Kintsch, 1995) — experts do
    not hold *more*, they hold *pointers*. The corpus reports the three classic results:
    digit-span training to **80 digits** where non-running-time material stayed at 7
    (Ericsson, Chase & Faloon 1980); chess recall of **20–25 pieces** on real game positions
    falling to **~8 — the novice number — on random positions** (Chase & Simon 1973); and
    physicians integrating symptoms into diagnostic categories that act as retrieval cues
    (ORDER 531 §"Key evidence comes from expert memory paradigms"). **The random-position
    result is the load-bearing one**: it rules out the alternative explanation that trained
    subjects simply have larger workspaces.
  - **Design:** the in-context portion of any task carries **identifiers and retrieval
    paths**, not retrieved bodies. A prompt names the documents; the workspace fetches.
    ORDER 531's own machine mapping (§"Long-Term Working Memory in AI Systems"): retrieval
    structures = vector embeddings, knowledge-graph schemas, structured note templates;
    cues in working memory = the current context plus retrieved document IDs; domain
    expertise = curated knowledge bases. **Scale assumption (R-J5): at 14,589 files the
    content-in-context strategy is not merely suboptimal, it is the failure mode** — the
    corpus states that as context windows grow *"the bottleneck shifts from capacity to
    retrieval quality"* (§Open Question 7).
  - **Already exists:** the whole external-memory stack — vector stores, knowledge graphs,
    structured note templates, and this arena's own `ORDER.txt`/`LEDGER.md` cursor, which
    is a retrieval-structure implementation of exactly this principle. **No trial in the
    corpus tests the agent-side version.**
  - **Backup document:** `Attention/Working-Memory-Limits.md` (ORDER 531) §"Long-Term
    Working Memory (Ericsson & Kintsch, 1995)", §"The role of long-term memory in
    'working' memory", §Open Question 7; `Entities/George-Miller.md` (ORDER 890)
    §"Chunking" — **independent second source**, the same control arrived at from
    the experimental side (chess master 20+ pieces on real positions, near-novice
    on random positions).
  - **Corroboration (tranche 50, ORDER 1122 `Learning/Cognitive-Load-Theory-Instructional-Design.md`,
    264 lines) — an independent line on the capacity number, arriving from
    educational psychology rather than from the attention literature, and the
    first source in the corpus that makes the number a *design rule* rather than
    a fact.** Support **2 → 3** on the phenomenon; the design grade stays
    `UNTESTED`. Miller's 7±2 is restated with **Cowan's (2001) refinement to ~4
    chunks**, which agrees with this area's rank-1 framing rather than with the
    number usually quoted. The file's own §"Lessons for AI" then does what this
    area has wanted: it maps intrinsic / extraneous / germane load onto the
    context window and derives **element interactivity** as the determinant —
    *"low-element-interactivity materials impose low intrinsic load regardless of
    length, while high-element-interactivity materials impose high intrinsic load
    even with few components"* — which is a sharper version of "how much fits."
    **The qualification that matters and is recorded rather than dropped:** CLT's
    **additivity hypothesis is under active dispute** (§"The Additivity
    Hypothesis Under Fire") and **germane load is circular** — *"high germane load
    is inferred from good learning outcomes, but good learning outcomes are
    explained by high germane load."* A capacity model whose own load types
    cannot be independently measured is not the `HIGH` its textbook status
    suggests; `LOW` on the phenomenon is already this area's rank-2 grade and is
    **not raised on this evidence**.
    **Expertise reversal is the constraint this area has never stated:** the
    guidance that helps a novice becomes *extraneous load* for an expert
    (Kalyuga, Chandler & Sweller 1999/2001). A four-chunk rule applied
    identically to a first-time reader and to the fifth is the wrong rule for one
    of them.
  - **Grade:** the human phenomenon **`HIGH`** (three independent domains, each with a
    control condition that removes the advantage — the random-position chess condition and
    the non-running-time digit condition are the controls, and they are the reason this is
    not a mere correlation); **the agent-side design `UNTESTED`** · Support: **2
    independent sources** (Ericsson & Kintsch's review of a 30-year literature, and
    Miller's own experimental account) — the grade is unchanged by the second
    source because it was already `HIGH`, but the **control now has two
    independent demonstrations** rather than one.
  - **Why rank 1:** every other design in this area spends capacity. This one changes what
    capacity is spent *on*, and it is the only slot that scales to 14,589 files.
  - **Interaction:** **this is the substrate Area 3 (effort allocation) allocates within** —
    a controller with no stated workspace size is allocating against an unknown denominator.
    **Constrains Area 37**: a 2,748-line file with 37 headings is a workspace at its limit;
    the three-slot-per-area rule is a *chunking* discipline as much as a ranking one.
    **Constrains Area 4** (prospective memory): a cue-only workspace makes the
    self-reminder channel load-bearing, because a cue with no body is worthless if the
    retrieval path is not itself remembered.

**Rank 2 — Cap the units of attention presented at any one level of abstraction at three to
  five, and treat an interface that exceeds it as a defect rather than a feature.**
  - **Brain part:** **Cowan's focus of attention**, the downward revision from Miller's
    7±2 to ~4 chunks, and the *slot* model of visual working memory (Luck & Vogel, 1997) —
    a fixed number of discrete slots, each holding one item at high precision, with
    over-slot items forcing guessing. Cowan's argument rests on three converging lines the
    corpus separates: **subitizing** (accurate enumeration to ~4, then serial counting),
    **change-detection** (near-perfect for 1–3 items, sharp drop beyond four), and
    **dual-task interference** (capacity drops toward a single chunk when the focus is
    occupied).
  - **Design:** the corpus names four surfaces, and all four are live in this workspace:
    *"Prompt design: limiting the number of distinct instructions or constraints in a single
    prompt"*; *"Tool selection: offering 3-5 tool options at each decision point rather than
    20"*; *"Memory retrieval: returning 3-5 'chunks' of relevant context rather than 50
    documents"*; *"UI design: grouping related information into 3-5 visible groups per
    screen."* This job's own tool catalog carries **68 deferred tools** and its system
    prompt enumerates them all.
  - **Already exists:** nothing that enforces it. The design is a heuristic the corpus
    states; ORDER 531 itself gives no trial, no interface, and no measurement.
  - **Backup document:** `Attention/Working-Memory-Limits.md` (ORDER 531) §"The 'Four-Chunk'
    Design Rule", §"The Embedded-Processes Model", the capacity table in §"Capacity
    Estimates Across Tasks and Domains".
  - **Grade:** the human capacity finding **`LOW`, and the number itself is disputed** —
    Gobet & Clarkson (2004) *"found that over half of memory recall conditions yielded only
    about two chunks, suggesting that four may be an upper bound rather than a constant."*
    Two independent labs, two different numbers, and the corpus's own Open Question 1 lists
    four candidates (four / seven / two / flexible resource with no hard limit) and calls
    the unified account *"elusive."* **The design heuristic `UNTESTED`** · Support: **1
    source**. Per R-J4 the 2-vs-4-vs-7 dispute is **preserved, not averaged**; this slot
    does not enter a number as settled.
  - **Why rank 2 and not rank 1:** it is a **presentation** constraint and Rank 1 is an
    **architecture** one. Capping what is shown does not fix a workspace that was filled
    with the wrong thing; it only bounds the damage. It is kept at rank 2 because it is the
    cheapest change in the area and the only one with a named threshold.
  - **Interaction:** **directly constrains this arena.** A file with 37 top-level headings
    read as one artifact is a set-size problem, and C11's failure is its measurement.
    **Constrains Area 22** (action selection and repertoire size) — 3–5 options per decision
    point is a direct cap on the action repertoire, and the arena holds exactly three slots
    per area for the same reason. **Pairs with Area 37 Rank 2**: an over-loaded interface
    produces the same bare-rate reading error as a criterion shift.
  - **Corroborated this pass — Support 1 → 2, and the 2-vs-4 dispute gains a
    second laboratory's numbers from a file that never mentions the dispute.**
    `cross-domain/Working-Memory-and-Context-Windows.md` (ORDER 641) §5 states
    the same two numbers from the same two sources (Miller 1956's 7±2 *"referred
    to chunks, not individual items"*; Cowan 2001's ~4 *"when chunking is
    controlled"*) and adds the one this slot was missing: **"capacity × fidelity
    tradeoff — more items = less detail per item."** That is a *mechanism* for the
    2-vs-4 disagreement rather than another vote in it: if the count and the
    per-item fidelity trade off, then labs measuring different tasks with
    different fidelity requirements **can both be right**, and the arena's refusal
    to average is the correct reading rather than indecision. Preserved per R-J4,
    and the slot's grade is **unchanged** — the design heuristic is still
    `UNTESTED` and one mechanism that explains a dispute is not a trial of the
    design.
    **The more consequential finding is on Rank 1, not Rank 2.** ORDER 641 §5
    gives the arena's own failure a number: transformer **effective utilisation
    drops after ~4K–8K tokens** despite 64K–200K+ windows (*"needle-in-a-haystack
    works; integration remains limited"*), and states the conclusion as a law —
    ***"A longer context window ≠ larger working memory. The brain's power comes
    from integration and retrieval, not raw buffer size."*** That is independent
    support for Rank 1's architecture-over-volume claim from a *different* field,
    and it is the first evidence in the arena that the four-chunk limit is not
    only a human constant but a **property that survives the change of
    substrate.**
    **The component the file names and the arena has never held: Baddeley's
    episodic buffer** (2000 addition) — the integrative workspace that binds
    phonological, visuospatial and long-term-memory content into episodes, at
    **~4 chunks**, and which Baddeley proposes as the neural correlate of
    conscious experience. ORDER 641's mapping is exact: the episodic buffer's
    *binding* role maps to **RAG, not to attention** — *"pure attention over long
    contexts struggles with binding — information is accessible but not
    necessarily integrated."* The arena's `episodic buffer` count was **0 across
    4,684 lines** before this pass. This is recorded as a **gap in Rank 1's
    design**, not as a fourth slot: Rank 1 says hold cues rather than content,
    and a buffer that binds four cues into one episode is the operation Rank 1
    does not name. It also states the open question the arena cannot answer —
    *"can we design an analog to Baddeley's episodic buffer?"* — and notes that
    KV caches are **static during inference** while biological traces consolidate,
    which is Area 6's subject arriving from the architecture side.
  - **Corroboration (tranche 58, ORDER 1278 + 1277 + 1280 + 1274 — **four
  independent files**, and this is the tranche's largest support increment) —
  Support **2 → 5**. Design grade held `UNTESTED`; the human capacity finding
  held at its disputed grade. What these four files add is a **mechanism for the
  number this area has been arguing about for twenty tranches**: a ~6 Hz theta
  cycle (~167 ms) contains **4–7 gamma cycles** (~40 Hz), and the capacity
  *is* that ratio (Lisman & Jensen 2013). **"Four" is not a property of a
  buffer; it is a ratio of two clocks** — which is why labs measuring different
  tasks get different numbers, and why Area 38's refusal to average was right
  for a reason it did not previously have. This is now **Area 104 rank 1**,
  which amends this slot rather than competing with it.
  **Three qualifications carried rather than averaged (R-J4), and one of them
  is `UNSUCCESSFUL`:** (1) **Cowan's revised 3–4, not Miller's 7±2** — the
  corpus states the 4–7 gamma-cycles figure *"does not neatly match"* the
  modern estimate; (2) **Lopez-Peredo et al. 2019** find **"gamma reversal"**,
  gamma peaking at the *opposite* theta phase at some sites; (3)
  **Lundqvist, Herman & Miller 2018** argue the scheme is an **epiphenomenon**
  that realistic circuit models produce with no assigned function. **A fourth
  caveat applies to the whole family and is the §8 finding in a new place:**
  all four files carry `confidence: high` and populated `sources:` lists — the
  validated-artifact path — **and the disputes live in the prose those
  artefacts decorate, not in the artefacts.** The corpus's own §"Measurement
  Challenges" sections are where the failures are named, and the front matter is
  where the file declares itself settled. A reader trusting the metadata would
  have read all four as uncontested.

**Rank 3 — Do not attempt to enlarge the workspace by training. Record the intervention as
  attempted and failed.**
  - **Brain part:** **near transfer without far transfer.** The 2008 Jaeggi et al. result —
    dual-n-back training reported a **40%** improvement in fluid intelligence (Gf) — and the
    two decades of replication that followed it.
  - **Design:** none. This slot exists to stop the intervention being retried blind, which
    is precisely what the `UNSUCCESSFUL` grade is for. The corpus's own conclusion is that
    the positive result was an artifact of the study design and its replication: *"when
    publication bias is controlled, the effect of working memory training on fluid
    intelligence is not statistically significant"* (Colombo et al., 2020), with Au et al.
    (2015) and Melby-Lervåg et al. (2016) finding small or null effects on far-transfer
    measures. What survives is **near transfer** — improvement on the trained task and
    close relatives — and the corpus connects this to Rank 1: *"training builds
    domain-specific retrieval structures, not a general capacity expansion."*
  - **Already exists:** the training programs (n-back, complex-span practice) exist and are
    widely deployed. **They do not do what they were sold as doing.** The pharmacological
    and stimulation routes fare the same way and worse: methylphenidate and caffeine give
    *"small, inconsistent effects,"* tDCS and TMS show *"mixed results"* (§Open Question 4).
  - **Backup document:** `Attention/Working-Memory-Limits.md` (ORDER 531) §"The training
    transfer debate", §Open Question 4.
  - **Grade:** **`UNSUCCESSFUL`** — trials were run, at scale, by independent groups, and the
    far-transfer claim did not survive publication-bias correction. This is a **first-class
    result, not a failure to find**: it is recorded here so a future pass does not spend a
    tranche rediscovering it. Note the honest asymmetry — the *near*-transfer finding is
    **`LOW`**-grade and uncontested, and it is the half of the result that Rank 1 actually
    uses.
  - **Why rank 3:** an `UNSUCCESSFUL` slot is kept, not ranked, on merit. It is third because
    it is a **concession with a design consequence** — it bounds what Ranks 1 and 2 may
    promise, and it converts a plausible-sounding plan into a recorded dead end.
  - **Interaction:** **the honesty floor under Ranks 1 and 2.** Both of them increase
    *effective* capacity through architecture, and this slot is the record that the
    alternative route was tried and did not work. It also **constrains Area 12** (procedural
    memory): expertise gains are real and are the *near*-transfer kind, which supports
    building retrieval structures rather than expecting a general capability to grow.

---

### Area 39 · Coincidence detection and the veto: a cell that fires only when two things agree, and a small inhibition that can stop a large excitation `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by `Cellular-Neuroscience/Pyramidal-Neuron-Apical-Dendrite-Computations.md`
(ORDER 545) §§"Backpropagating Action Potentials (bAPs) and BAC Firing", "Dendritic NMDA
Spikes and Plateau Potentials", "In Vivo Confirmation (2007–2013)", read whole this pass.
Corroborated within the tranche by `Canonical-Microcircuit/Canonical-Microcircuit-Columnar-Architecture.md`
(ORDER 539) §"Recurrent Excitation-Inhibition as Homeostatic Control" and §"State-Dependent
Modulation".
**Why this is a brain part and not a facet of Area 34.** Area 34 asks *which controller is
issuing the action* — model-based against model-free. This area is one level down and
mechanistic: **how a single cell decides that two independent streams agree**, and the
asymmetric power of inhibition to prevent that agreement. The corpus gives the veto as a
**number**: a single or pair of inhibitory synapses of **~2 nS** arriving within **30 ms**
*"can completely veto"* a dendritic NMDA spike *"even when driven by 50 nS of NMDA current"*
(Polsky, Mel & Schiller 2004). **A 25:1 ratio in favour of the veto.** No area in the arena
holds a quantitative claim about how hard it is to stop something once started.
**The same finding is a caution, not just a design.** The 2 nS figure comes from
*in vitro* rat and mouse L5 pyramidal neurons in slices, and the corpus is explicit that
in vivo the picture is contested — Fischer et al. (2022) found *"broadly correlated
membrane voltage throughout the dendritic arbor and only weak signatures of electrical
compartmentalization within individual dendritic branches"* (ORDER 545 §"In Vitro vs. In
Vivo"). The mechanism the veto lives inside may be substantially less compartmentalized in
a behaving animal than the founding literature implies.

**Rank 1 — Give every processing module a separate content path and a separate context
  path, and gate content on their coincidence rather than on either alone.**
  - **Brain part:** **BAC firing** (Larkum, Zhu & Sakmann, 1999) — backpropagation-activated
    calcium firing. The cell does not respond to the feedforward input and does not respond
    to the feedback input; it responds to their **coincidence within a window of a few
    milliseconds**, relieving the Mg²⁺ NMDA block and triggering a regenerative Ca²⁺
    plateau that fires a burst of **2–4 somatic action potentials in ~20 ms**. The corpus
    states the functional consequence plainly: *"this makes the pyramidal neuron a
    coincidence detector for feedforward and feedback inputs, effectively associating
    information from different cortical layers at the cellular level."*
  - **Design:** ORDER 545 §"Agent Module Design" states the module shape in three parts,
    and this slot adopts it directly: a **basal path** carrying task-relevant content, an
    **apical path** carrying contextual/metacognitive signal (working memory, goals,
    environment state), and a **nonlinear coincidence gate** between them — *"a thresholded
    multiplicative interaction"* — so that content is processed fully *only when context
    deems it relevant*. The corpus notes the *frequency constraint* that makes this
    non-trivial: BAC coupling requires a bAP train at **50–100 Hz**; below that, bAPs
    attenuate below threshold, and the coupling does not happen.
  - **Already exists:** **attention itself** — the corpus's own mapping is that the apical
    dendrite encodes the **query** and gates the basal **value**, i.e. the transformer
    Q·Kᵀ multiplicative gate is functionally the BAC mechanism (ORDER 545 §"Apical
    Dendrites as Attention Gating in Neural Networks", citing Triesch 2024 for the
    full column↔token, L4↔embedding, Q↔L1-tuft correspondence). **This is the strongest
    adopt-don't-build case in the tranche: the mechanism the corpus describes is already
    the dominant architecture in the runtime this job executes in.**
  - **Backup document:** `Cellular-Neuroscience/Pyramidal-Neuron-Apical-Dendrite-Computations.md`
    (ORDER 545) §"Backpropagating Action Potentials (bAPs) and BAC Firing", §"Agent Module
    Design", §"Apical Dendrites as Attention Gating in Neural Networks".
  - **Grade:** the human mechanism **`LOW`** — replicated across four independent
    laboratories over two decades, and with in vivo behavioural support
    (Takahashi et al. 2020, dendritic spike activity predicting stimulus detection *at
    perceptual threshold*; Bittner et al. 2017, single plateaus sufficient to drive
    place-field formation), **but capped at `LOW` by the corpus's own unresolved
    compartmentalization dispute** (§"The HCN Channel Controversy": Harnett et al. 2013/2015
    found HCN channels are *required* for regenerative activity, reversing the canonical
    shunt account) and by the in-vivo/in-vitro split above. **The module design `UNTESTED`**
    · Support: **1 source** for the cell biology, **1 independent source** (ORDER 539) for
    the network-level homeostatic analogue.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in this area that says what to *build*, and the
    thing it says to build is already the architecture underneath it.
  - **Interaction:** **the cellular implementation of Area 34.** Area 34 says a cached and
    a planned action produce identical output; this says the *cell* distinguishes them by
    requiring coincidence between the two streams. **Constrains Area 13** (metacognitive
    control): a context path that is always-on is not a gate, and the 50–100 Hz constraint
    means the context signal must arrive in bursts, not as a constant. **Constrains Area
    22**: the action repertoire shrinks when every action must clear a coincidence gate.

**Rank 2 — Make a small, cheap "no" able to override a large, expensive "yes": the 25:1
  veto, adopted as a containment primitive.**
  - **Brain part:** **dendritic inhibition as a veto rather than a subtractor.** The corpus
    is explicit that this is not arithmetic: *"a single or pair of inhibitory synapses
    (approximately 2 nS conductance) arriving within 30 ms of an NMDA spike can completely
    veto it, even when driven by 50 nS of NMDA current."* A subtractor of that magnitude
    would leave substantial residual excitation; a veto leaves nothing. The mechanism is
    somatic-targeting **Martinotti (SST+) cells** hyperpolarizing or shunting the distal
    dendrite, and — the sharper part — **VIP+ interneurons inhibiting SST+ cells**, a
    **disinhibitory cascade that selectively releases a compartment from suppression** during
    arousal (ORDER 539 §"The canonical microcircuit" and §"State-Dependent Modulation";
    ORDER 545 §"BAC Firing").
  - **Design:** a stop that is proportional to the cost of starting is cheap; a stop that
    is proportional to the magnitude of the thing being stopped is expensive and arrives
    late. This slot says: the highest-value control signal in the system should be the one
    that can cancel a large commitment for a small cost, and the disinhibitory layer above
    it is what makes the *release* of a veto as cheap as its application.
  - **Already exists:** interneuron cell-type biology and the disinhibitory motif are
    established neuroscience (PV+/SST+/VIP+ classes, ORDER 539; Gentet et al. 2012 on
    SST+ apical dendrite selectivity). **The engineering primitive is not built** — nothing
    in this workspace has a cancel-cost-inverted abort path. The nearest existing thing is
    ordinary cancellation, which is magnitude-proportional.
  - **Backup document:** `Cellular-Neuroscience/Pyramidal-Neuron-Apical-Dendrite-Computations.md`
    (ORDER 545) §"Dendritic NMDA Spikes and Plateau Potentials", §"BAC Firing";
    `Canonical-Microcircuit/Canonical-Microcircuit-Columnar-Architecture.md` (ORDER 539)
    §"Recurrent Excitation-Inhibition as Homeostatic Control".
  - **Grade:** the veto ratio **`LOW`** (one quantitative source, Polsky et al. 2004, in
    vitro; the corpus reports the finding without an independent in vivo replication);
    **the disinhibitory cascade `LOW`** (ORDER 539, consistent with ORDER 545 — two
    independent literatures, one mechanism); the engineering primitive **`UNTESTED`** ·
    Support: **2 sources**, genuinely independent — one intracellular electrophysiology,
    one circuit-anatomy review.
  - **Corroboration (tranche 59, ORDER 1298
    `Neuroplasticity/Dendritic-Spike-Plateau-Potential-Computation.md`, 97 lines +
    the three-type taxonomy) — a third, independent source that supplies the
    *arithmetic* the 25:1 ratio was missing, and it is the same mechanism seen
    from the synapse side.** Support **2 → 3**. Polsky et al. gave the ratio in
    conductance; this file gives it in **synapse count and in space and time**:
    an NMDA spike *"requires the synchronous activation of approximately
    **10–50 neighboring synapses** on a single dendritic segment,"* firing only
    when they are co-activated *"within a narrow spatial (**~20–50 μm**) and
    temporal (**~10–50 ms**) window."* That is the complement of the veto result
    and it is the more useful half for design: **starting requires many, stopping
    requires one.** The file also confirms the veto's *anatomical* specificity —
    shunting inhibition *"placed strategically on dendrites — including directly
    on the apical trunk and tuft — can veto calcium spike and NMDA spike
    initiation by clamping the membrane potential near resting levels"* — and
    adds the mechanism the earlier sources left implicit: inhibition arriving
    during the depolarization phase *"can terminate a plateau potential,
    reducing its duration **linearly with inhibitory conductance**."*
    **A second qualification, and it is the same one Area 39 already carries from
    a different direction:** the file notes that NMDA spikes are *"strictly
    local — because they require continued glutamate binding to NMDA receptors
    for maintenance, they cannot propagate far from their initiation site,
    making them confined to a single dendritic branch of ~20–50 μm length."*
    The compartment the veto acts in is *small and sharply bounded*, which
    supports the design and simultaneously bounds it: a veto that is local to
    20–50 μm is not a global abort, and any agent that copies it must decide what
    its own "compartment" is. **The veto grade stays `LOW`** — a third source
    agreeing with a mechanism is not three replications of the 2 nS measurement,
    which remains a single in-vitro study.
  - **Why rank 2 and not rank 1:** Rank 1 says how a module decides to act; this says how a
    started action is stopped. Rank 1 is the more direct adoption, but Rank 2 carries the
    sharper quantitative claim in the tranche and is the more transferable idea.
  - **Interaction:** **the cell-level counterpart to Area 28's containment/autoimmunity
    pair.** Area 28 established that over- and under-containment are opposite failures with
    no shared indicator; this supplies the mechanism that makes the *cost asymmetry*
    deliberate rather than accidental, and the same SST+/VIP+ disinhibitory cascade is why
    over-containment is the easier failure to produce. **Constrains Area 32** (action
    termination): a mission that has begun has a 25:1-cost abort available and the arena
    does not use it. **Constrains Area 30** (per-episode cost): at 14,589 files, a cheap
    veto is the only mechanism that keeps a wrong turn from being paid for at full size.

**Rank 3 — Build the two-compartment module, and record that the third compartment the
  corpus itself proposes was not built, because the compartment boundary may not be real
  in vivo.**
  - **Brain part:** the corpus's own **three-compartment** revision of the two-zone story —
    Larkum, Zhu & Sakmann (2004) propose **tuft (A) / obliques as a coupling zone (B) /
    basal-soma (C)**, and the corpus is candid that this *"is a useful abstraction rather
    than a literal description"* (ORDER 545 §"The 'Two-Compartment' vs. 'Multi-Compartment'
    Debate"). The obliques generate regenerative potentials themselves, so the simple
    feedforward/context binary is already known to be an oversimplification.
  - **Design:** the coupling zone as a **second-level control** — a layer that sets *how
    strongly* context gates content, rather than only gating it. ORDER 545 §"Agent Module
    Design" states this: *"the coupling zone (obliques) could serve as a meta-control layer
    that dynamically adjusts how strongly the apical context gates the basal content,
    providing a second level of adaptive control."* **What is deliberately not built is the
    third compartment as a distinct architectural element**, because the corpus's in vivo
    evidence does not support assuming the boundary exists.
  - **Already exists:** the modelling machinery — multi-compartment / dendritic ANN
    architectures. **dendritic ANNs (dANN)** (Richards et al., 2025, *Nature Communications*,
    doi 10.1038/s41467-025-56297-9) report comparable-or-better accuracy than vanilla ANNs
    with *"orders of magnitude fewer trainable parameters"*, and the dendritic error networks
    (Urbanczik & Senn 2014; Sacramento et al. 2018, MNIST 1.53% vs 8.4% single-layer) reach
    comparable performance using only local plasticity. **These are trained-network results,
    not agent-architecture results** — none is tested on the agent stack.
  - **Backup document:** `Cellular-Neuroscience/Pyramidal-Neuron-Apical-Dendrite-Computations.md`
    (ORDER 545) §"The 'Two-Compartment' vs. 'Multi-Compartment' Debate", §"In Vitro vs. In
    Vivo: How Much of the Synthesis Holds?", §"Dendritic Artificial Neural Networks (dANNs)".
  - **Grade:** design **`UNTESTED`** · Support: **2 sources** for the mechanism (ORDER 545,
    ORDER 539), **held at `UNTESTED` deliberately** — per the arena's standing rule, a
  source's assertion of a mechanism is not a trial of the mechanism's use. The corpus
  preserves the dispute rather than settling it: §"Predictive Coding: Competing
  Interpretations" records that the apical-dendrite-as-error reading and the
  apical-as-contextual-amplification reading *"may be two descriptions of the same
  phenomenon,"* and lists four mutually incompatible accounts (Urbanczik–Senn,
  Sacramento, Whittington & Bogacz, Guerguiev–Sacramento–Bengio) with *"no consensus on
  which, if any, of these theories is correct."*
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the most speculative slot and the one whose premise the corpus
    most directly undercuts. It is kept for two reasons the skill requires: the
    **dANN result is real and independently reported** and is the parameter-efficiency
    argument no other slot makes; and **the refusal to build the third compartment is itself
    a finding** — a design decision taken from a documented negative in vivo result rather
    than from the abstract's positive.
  - **Interaction:** **the refinement of Rank 1, not a competitor to it.** **Pins Area 24's
    reach**: the in vitro/in vivo split is another instance of the arena's recurring
    inversion — a mechanism with twenty years of clean slice physiology and a contested
    behavioural signature. Per R-J4 the dispute is **preserved and both sides are carried**;
    no side is entered as settled.

#### ORDER 554 (`Hierarchical-Temporal-Memory.md`) — an independent lineage for the same
#### coincidence-detector cell, and a second `UNSUCCESSFUL`-grade boundary

**Read for its relation to this area specifically**, since ORDER 545 supplied the cell
and ORDER 554 supplies a *separate research programme* that converged on the same
biophysics without citing this corpus.

- **Support 2 → 3, and the new source is genuinely independent.** Hawkins & Ahmad
  (2016), *Frontiers in Neural Circuits* 10:23 — *"Why Neurions Have Thousands of
  Synapses, A Theory of Sequence Memory in Neocortex"* — states that **pyramidal
  neurons' active dendrites serve as coincidence detectors via NMDA spikes**, and
  builds a three-state neuron model (active / inactive / **predictive**) on that
  biophysics. This is the *same cell and the same mechanism* as this area's rank 1,
  arrived at from sequence-memory modelling rather than from dendritic physiology.
  It is not a restatement: different field, different venue, different purpose.
- **The one number worth keeping.** Cui, Ahmad & Hawkins (2017), *Neural Computation*
  28(11):2474–2504: sequence memory tolerates **±20% timing jitter** and **30% input
  noise**, reaching near-perfect prediction after **~100 sequence presentations**.
  Read against rank 3's refusal to build a third compartment: the architecture that
  *is* built handles jitter and noise at those levels, which is evidence about the
  built two-compartment model, not about the compartment that was declined.
- **What ORDER 554 does not do, recorded because the temptation is real.** It does
  not replicate the *behavioural* signature. It is careful, in fact, to grade its own
  claims in three tiers, and the file's §Biological Validity puts the neocortical
  claims as **partially supported** (*"the claim that the neocortex's primary function
  is temporal sequence prediction is contested"*) and the Thousand Brains neural
  predictions as **speculative or contested**. **The corpus's own apparatus is the
  arena's inversion running in the honest direction** — here the validated apparatus
  (`confidence: high`) sits over a body that grades itself three ways and refuses to
  overclaim. Worth recording as evidence that the inversion is a *systematic*
  tendency, not a universal one.

**A second `UNSUCCESSFUL`-grade boundary, for the arena's negative-result ledger.**
§Key Criticisms, item 4 and §Replication Status: *"no head-to-head benchmarks against
modern baselines (transformers, state-space models) have been published,"* and under
**Not replicated** — HTM's claimed superiority over deep learning for sequence
learning. Independent evaluation exists (Maltoni 2011) and found HTM could learn image
representations but **lacked competitive accuracy** against contemporary SVM and deep
learning methods. Item 3 is the sharpest: the biological-plausibility argument
against backprop is *"increasingly seen as a straw man — biologically-plausible
gradient approximations exist (feedback alignment, equilibrium propagation)."*

**Grade: `UNSUCCESSFUL` for the superiority claim; `LOW` for the mechanism.** The
theory's *stated* advantage has never been demonstrated against a modern baseline,
and the one independent evaluation found it behind. That is a first-class negative
result, recorded so a future pass does not re-derive it. The **mechanism** — SDRs,
temporal pooling, three-state neurons — is separately and more positively evidenced:
SDR mathematical properties replicated (Ahmad & Hawkins 2015: 2048-bit SDRs at 2%
sparsity, random vectors expected overlap ~1.6 bits, 50%-overlap vectors at
p < 10⁻⁷⁰), spatial pooler convergence (Cui et al. 2017, stable SDR within <50
iterations, constant ~2% density), and independent precursors in Kanerva's SDM (1988)
and Hinton (1984). **These are two grades on one file and they are not averaged.**

**Interaction:** reinforces rank 1 and the rank-3 refusal; **constrains Area 35**
(grounding) — HTM is a predictive model with no grounded semantics, and its own
commercial success is anomaly detection, which requires no world-model correctness.
**Constrains Area 30** (per-episode cost) favourably and uniquely: HTM's online
synaptic growth *"accommodates new sequences without erasing old ones,"* which is a
**catastrophic-forgetting-free** write path — the only such claim in the tranche, and
it is exactly the stability-plasticity dilemma ORDER 552 recorded as unsolved in
ACT-R, Soar and CLARION alike. Recorded as a candidate for Area 30, not promoted:
one programme, no head-to-head, and no public benchmark.

### Area 40 · Circadian phase as a first-class state variable: the two-process model, and performance that is a function of *when* `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by:** `Circadian-Rhythms/Circadian-Rhythms-and-Chronobiology-of-Cognition.md`
(ORDER 548) §§1–5, corroborated in the same file by §3 (extra-SCN brain clocks,
synaptic homeostasis) and §5 (Chronobiology and AI Design).
**This pass: the arena's thirty-ninth structural silence, and the first one about the
*clock* rather than the controller. Thirty-nine areas ask what to do with a thing and
which controller should do it; none asks what the system is *capable of* right now.**
The arena had maintenance cycles (Area 23, Area 30) and a capacity limit (Area 38), and
neither one carries a phase. ORDER 548 supplies the missing variable with a number
attached: **10–30% performance differences between optimal and suboptimal testing times**
(§2, Chauhan et al. 2025, *Chronobiology International* 42:463–499, systematic review).

  - **Rank 1 — A two-process state variable (circadian drive × sleep pressure) computed
    before a decision is made, and read as a bound on that decision's quality**
    - **Brain part:** the **suprachiasmatic nucleus** (~10,000 neurons, hypothalamic
      master pacemaker) and Borbély's **two-process model** (1982): net performance is
      the residual of *Process C* (circadian drive) against *Process S* (homeostatic
      sleep pressure). SCN core (ventral) receives direct photic input via the
      retinohypothalamic tract from melanopsin ipRGCs and drives the shell, which
      orchestrates systemic output.
    - **Design:** two scalars, not one clock reading. The agent maintains `P_C` and
      `P_S` as separately-tracked accumulators, exposes the **residual** as a
      first-class field on every decision, and — the load-bearing part — **a decision
      taken at low residual is tagged as such at the moment it is taken**, not
      discovered later. Risk tolerance rises during the biological night *even when
      the subject is awake*; impulse control is weakest at circadian troughs; judgment
      error rates rise under misalignment. Those are the three things a system with
      one mood scalar cannot represent, because they are *phase*, not *valence*.
    - **Already exists:** the two-process model is the field's reference account
      (Borbély 1982) and its forced-desynchrony protocol is the experimental
      instrument. On the agent side, the machinery exists but not the variable:
      `cron` already runs on wall-clock schedules and the Hermes fallback chain
      already carries per-provider state. **The composite — a phase scalar bound to
      a quality claim — exists nowhere in the stack.**
    - **Backup document:** ORDER 548 §§1 (SCN, TTFL, entrainment), §2 (diurnal
      variation, chronotypes, **Circadian Effects on Decision Making**), §4.
      **Tranche 43 adds** `oracle/brain/Entities/Michael-Posner.md` (ORDER 928)
      §"Three Networks of Attention" — **independent support for `P_C` from
      cognitive neuroscience rather than from chronobiology**, via the
      **alerting / orienting / executive** decomposition: *alerting performance
      shows the largest diurnal variation, peaking in the morning and declining
      across the day, with **reaction-time variability rising even where mean RT
      is stable***, attributed to noradrenergic variation from the locus
      coeruleus. **The qualification it delivers is sharper than the
      corroboration:** the three networks **do not degrade together** — orienting
      and executive show *smaller* diurnal variation than alerting. **So a
      system indexing capability by a single scalar `P_C` is wrong in a
      specific, nameable way: the deficit is network-specific, and the network
      that dips first is the one governing readiness-to-act.** Rank 1's two-scalar
      form (`P_C`, `P_S`) would need a **third dimension representing which
      capacity is down**. Recorded as a qualification on the parameterisation,
      **not a re-rank — the phenomenon is not in dispute, the form of the
      variable is**;
    - **Grade:** design **`UNTESTED`** · Support: **1 source** (ORDER 548). The
      *human* phenomenon is `HIGH` — replicated, forced-desynchrony, a 224,714-person
      cohort (Ahn et al. 2024), a systematic review, and a phase-response curve with
      a measured sign. **The design is `UNTESTED` and that gap is the honest grade**:
      nobody has built the composite. Per R-J2 no measurement is created here.
    - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in forty areas that makes the system's
      *current capability* an input to its own reasoning rather than an output of it.
    - **Interaction:** **constrains Areas 23, 30 and 38 directly.** Area 23's
      sleep-time rebuild is a maintenance action with no stated precondition; this
      area is that precondition. Area 38's workspace-capacity finding (chess
      advantage collapsing to the novice number on random positions) is a *capacity*
      result; §2's 10–30% is a *phase* result, and the arena must not merge them.

  - **Rank 2 — Rhythmic consolidation: maintenance as a downscale, scheduled by phase,
    not triggered by a counter**
    - **Brain part:** **synaptic homeostasis hypothesis** (Tononi & Cirelli 2003, 2006)
      — wakefulness is net synaptic potentiation; slow-wave sleep **globally
      downscales** synaptic strength to restore homeostasis. Sleep markers (PSD-95,
      AMPA receptor levels) rise in wakefulness and fall in sleep; slow-wave activity
      tracks prior waking experience and declines across the night; sleep deprivation
      *increases* cortical excitability. Circadian gating of plasticity is the
      second half: LTP induction is easier in the active phase, BDNF expression is
      rhythmic, and **dentate-gyrus neurogenesis is circadian-modulated**.
    - **Design:** a maintenance pass that **multiplies strength down globally while
      preserving relative ratios** — the corpus names the analogue directly:
      *"synaptic weights are globally downscaled while preserving relative strength
      ratios"* (§5, Rhythmic Modulation of Learning Rates). This is the operation
      that distinguishes consolidation from deletion, and no existing area states it.
    - **Already exists:** SHY is the reference account; the empirical support is the
      cited 2016 *Nature Communications* work confirming sleep recalibrates both
      homeostatic and associative plasticity in human cortex. On the agent side,
      **sleep-like consolidation with periodic global downscaling is reported to
      improve generalization in some studies** (§5) — a weak, unquantified claim by
      the corpus's own standard, and the reason this is rank 2 and not rank 1.
    - **Backup document:** ORDER 548 §3 (Synaptic Homeostasis and Sleep, Circadian
      Rhythms in Neuroplasticity), §5.
    - **Grade:** design **`LOW`** · Support: **1 source**. Human mechanism `HIGH`
      (SHY, two independent confirmations cited); the AI analogue is the corpus's
      assertion with no trial named, so the *design* sits at `LOW` and not `HIGH`.
    - **Why rank 2:** it supplies the mechanism Area 6 and Area 23 both invoke and
      neither specifies. Neither says *what the pass does* — Area 6 rank 3 has a
      governed forgetting pass with attribution, and *global downscale preserving
      ratios* is a different operation on the same store.
    - **Interaction:** **the write-side counterpart to Area 27's lability window.**
      Lability says a retrieved memory can be overwritten and predicts when the
      window opens; this says what a scheduled pass does when it is not.

  - **Rank 3 — Clearance as a distinct organ: the glymphatic system, and the
    2025 finding that it is driven by *astrocytes on an opposite phase*, not by
    sleep state**
    - **Brain part:** the **glymphatic system** — perivascular channels flushing CSF
      through parenchyma to clear amyloid-β and tau, dependent on aquaporin-4 at
      astrocytic endfeet. Clearances are **~60% higher during sleep**. The
      load-bearing claim is the one that makes this a separate organ rather than a
      sleep epiphenomenon: **SCN astrocytes regulate glymphatic function via
      circadian signalling, independent of sleep-wake state** (Hablitz & Brown 2024,
      *Nature Communications*), and SCN astrocytes **harbour autonomous clocks with
      opposite-phase gene expression to SCN neurons, sustaining behavioural rhythms
      even when neuronal clocks are genetically deficient** (§1).
    - **Design:** a **second store with a different half-life, cleaned on a different
      schedule, run by a different actor.** Waste clearance is not consolidation of
      what matters — it is removal of what does not, and the brain runs the two on
      separate clocks with separate controllers. For an agent this is a GC tier
      that must not be implemented as "consolidation that discards more."
    - **Already exists:** the mechanism (Nedergaard lab, 2012; the 2026
      *Nature Communications* demonstration of Aβ/tau clearance to plasma in humans);
      agent-side, the corpus proposes the analogy — *"garbage collection cycles that
      run during low-activity periods"* (§5) — **with no implementation named**.
    - **Backup document:** ORDER 548 §3 (The Glymphatic System and Circadian
      Clearance), §1 (SCN astrocyte clocks), §5.
    - **Grade:** design **`UNTESTED`** · Support: **1 source**. The human mechanism
      is `HIGH` and independently replicated (2012 discovery; 2024 astrocyte
      independence; 2026 human clearance). **The design is untested and the corpus
      supplies no trial.**
    - **Why rank 3:** it is the narrowest slot and the most speculative as a *design*,
      but it is kept for a reason the skill requires: **the opposite-phase astrocyte
      clock is a control architecture, not a metaphor.** A maintenance actor running
      on an inverted schedule relative to the actor it maintains is a concrete
      failure-isolation property, and the corpus reports it as measured.
    - **Interaction:** **disambiguates Areas 23 and 30.** Both describe maintenance
      without saying whether one clock or two are running. The astrocyte finding
      says the human system runs two, on opposite phases. Any single-cron
      maintenance design in this arena is therefore a simplification, and this
      area names it as one.
    - **Corroboration 2026-09-27 (tranche 62, ORDER 1344) - Support: 1 -> 2;
      mechanism `LOW` -> `HIGH` on the five-lab meta-analysis (4 *Aqp4* KO lines
      + *Snta1* KO, SMD -1.70 [-2.49, -0.90], p<0.0001); agent design stays
      `UNTESTED`, no implementation named anywhere in the corpus.** A second
      independent file, different discovery and different lab, adding three
      things the first source did not carry: a **serial dependency** (clearance
      obligately requires glymphatic influx -> convective transport -> lymphatic
      efflux, so each stage needs independent observability); a **chemical gate**
      (sleep expands interstitial volume 60%, 13.6% -> 22.7%, n=10, p<0.01, via
      LC norepinephrine withdrawal - so an upstream arousal state can hold
      maintenance shut, which is the Area 6/23 failure arriving again); and a
      **published negative that was an instrument artifact** (I2=73%
      heterogeneity explained entirely by anesthesia choice; tribromoethanol
      suppressed clearance in *both* genotypes and masked the KO effect - Area 24
      from a new direction, and the reason the design grade stays `UNTESTED`).
      *Bulk-flow-vs-diffusion and the slow human figure remain unreconciled
      (R-J4); full record in `ARENA-EVIDENCE.md`.*

### Area 41 · Degradation that hides: latency as a capacity law, and reserve as a mask `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by:** `Cognitive-Aging/Cognitive-Aging-Reserve.md` (ORDER 550) §§Processing
Speed Theory, §Cognitive Reserve, §Scaffolding Theory (STAC) and STAC-R, §The CRUNCH
Model, §Replication Status.
**This pass: the arena's fortieth structural silence, and the first one where the
danger is *not* that the system does too little but that it looks correct while
doing less.** Thirty-nine prior areas describe failures that announce themselves. This
file supplies the class of failure that produces **no symptom at the output**, because
the system routes around the damage.

Two independent findings earn the area, and they are not the same finding:

- **Latency converts to capacity.** Salthouse's processing-speed theory with its
  mediation programme: controlling for processing speed removes **~60–70%** of age
  differences across memory, reasoning and spatial ability; Robitaille et al. (2013)
  put the shared variance in episodic memory at **71%**; Hale & Myerson (2000)
  meta-analysed 58 studies (N ≈ 8,000) at **d = 0.85**. The corpus states the
  consequence the arena needs in one line: *"Working memory capacity is not
  intrinsically smaller in older adults — it becomes **effectively** smaller because
  maintenance operations take more cycles,"* and then, for systems,
  *"throughput bottlenecks manifest as **effective capacity loss**, not just slower
  execution."*
- **Reserve decouples appearance from state.** Stern's PET result: patients with
  higher educational attainment had **significantly greater** cerebral glucose
  metabolism deficits — **more advanced neuropathology at comparable clinical
  severity**. The measured severity said the same thing about two patients the brain
  imaging separated. 5–7 years of symptom-onset delay at d ≈ 0.6–0.8, and the
  bilingual literature independently converges (**+4.3 years**, 19 studies, N = 3,832,
  Valleeve et al. 2015) **with no postmortem pathology difference**.

  - **Rank 1 — Effective capacity is a function of maintenance-cycle cost, and must be
    computed, not assumed constant**
    - **Brain part:** **processing speed theory** (Salthouse 1996, *Psychological
      Review*) as a *mediation* claim, not a correlation — and the inhibitory-deficit
      alternative (Hasher & Zacks 1988), preserved as a live dispute below.
    - **Design:** the workspace's capacity is **not a constant**. It is
      `f(cycles-per-maintenance)`, and the arena's own numbers are wrong by
      construction if the cycle cost changes. Concretely: the *same* store holds the
      same number of items, but the **number that survives to be usable** falls as
      maintenance takes longer. This makes latency a first-class input to every
      capacity claim the arena makes — including Area 38's chunk counts, which are
      quoted here as though they were properties of the store.
    - **Already exists:** the mediation methodology is Salthouse's own and is the
      most replicated quantitative programme in this file. The **ACTIVE trial**
      (N = 2,832, ages 65–94, RCT) is the existing intervention: speed-of-processing
      training produced the **largest and most durable** effects of the three arms
      (d = 0.40–0.80, persisting to 5 years) and cut risk of driving cessation by
      **48%** over 5 years. **It works on the trained task and not on untrained
      domains** — see Rank 3.
    - **Backup document:** ORDER 550 §"Processing Speed Theory", §"Processing Speed:
      Salthouse's Empirical Program", §"Processing Speed as Latency in AI Systems".
    - **Grade:** design **`UNTESTED`** · Support: **1 source** (ORDER 550). The
      *human* mediation result is `HIGH` — a longitudinal mediation analysis, a
      58-study meta-analysis, and a 2019 review confirming the correlation holds
      nearly linearly across the adult lifespan.
    - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in forty-one areas that says a number the
      arena already treats as fixed is **conditional**, and it conditions it on the
      one variable the arena never measures.
    - **Interaction:** **re-frames Area 38 in place.** Area 38's chunk counts and
      digit spans are *latency-conditional measurements*, not capacities, and the
      arena must stop quoting them as constants. **Pairs with Area 30** (per-episode
      cost): this is the same cost read as a capacity divisor.

  - **Rank 2 — Reserve-aware health: capability cannot be inferred from output, because
    a degraded system keeps producing answers**
    - **Brain part:** **cognitive reserve** (Stern 1999/2002/2009) — the brain's
      ability to cope with damage through efficient or alternative processing, in
      three distinguished forms: **neural reserve** (efficient processing), **neural
      compensation** (recruitment of different networks), and **brain reserve**
      (structural capacity). The load-bearing design fact is that reserve's benefit
      and its danger are the same fact: it **delays clinical manifestation**, which
      means it also **delays detection**.
    - **Design:** a health signal that is **independent of task output**, because
      output is precisely what reserve preserves. The corpus states the systems
      version with unusual bluntness: *"AI systems with high knowledge density can
      mask component failures — the system appears to function well because the
      knowledge base provides sufficient answers even when individual components are
      degraded."* Design consequence: **a passing run is not a health check** (see
      Area 33's asymmetry and Area 37's unmeasured watcher), and degradation must be
      probed on something the system's knowledge base cannot answer from memory.
    - **Already exists:** the reserve construct and its measurement apparatus
      (Stern 1992/2002/2009/2012; the Seattle Longitudinal Study, >100,000
      participants across 1956–2022). Agent-side: **nothing.** There is no
      established method for probing a retrieval system on what it cannot know.
    - **Backup document:** ORDER 550 §"Cognitive Reserve: Stern's Longitudinal and
      Cross-Sectional Studies", §"Bilingualism as Cognitive Reserve", §"Cognitive
      Reserve as Accumulated Knowledge".
    - **Grade:** design **`UNTESTED`** · Support: **1 source**. Human reserve
      effect `HIGH` (PET dissociation, 5–7 year delay, d ≈ 0.6–0.8, independently
      converged by the bilingual literature at +4.3 years).
    - **Why rank 2:** it is the area's most uncomfortable slot and the one that most
      directly indicts the arena's own method. It is not rank 1 because its design
      consequence is a *constraint on measurement*, and the arena's own binding rule
      **R-J2 forbids self-measurement** — so the slot can name the problem and cannot
      be discharged by building an instrument. That limit is stated rather than hidden.
    - **Interaction:** **the mechanism under Areas 24, 33 and 37.** Area 24 asks
      whether the instrument measured anything; this says the *subject* can be
      concealing the answer. Stern's PET result is Area 24's exact shape in human
      data: two patients identical on the clinical measure, separated by the imaging.
      **The averaging fallacy** (Singh et al. 2023, ORDER 550) is the same point at
      the cohort level — group averages conceal that some individuals barely decline
      and others decline sharply, so a population-level verdict is not an
      individual-level one.

  - **Rank 3 — Demand-dependent recruitment as a control law, with its three
    interpretations left unresolved**
    - **Brain part:** **STAC / STAC-R** (Reuter-Lorenz & Park 2010) and **CRUNCH**
      (Rypma & D'Esposito 2000) — scaffolding is **task-demand-dependent** and
      **experience-dependent**, appearing under high demand and novel conditions;
      the CRUNCH pattern is **increased** recruitment under high load and
      **decreased** recruitment under low load. Anatomically: prefrontal
      compensatory recruitment, **HAROLD** (Cabeza 2002, bilateral activation in
      older adults), and **PASA** (posterior→anterior shift with age).
    - **Design:** an allocation law keyed to *demand*, with the low-load branch
      stating that the system **releases** resources when demand falls rather than
      holding them. Most systems in this arena allocate upward monotonically; CRUNCH
      is a two-sided curve.
    - **Already exists:** test-time compute scaling is the AI analogue already in
      wide use, and the corpus names the asymmetry that keeps this from being a
      solved import: the extra compute is *"metabolically expensive for humans
      (increased glucose consumption, increased fatigue)"* — **the human version pays
      a cost the AI version does not**, which is why the transfer is not symmetric.
    - **Backup document:** ORDER 550 §"Scaffolding Theory (STAC) and STAC-R",
      §"HAROLD and PASA Models", §"The HAROLD Debate: Compensation or
      Over-recruitment?", §"The CRUNCH Model in AI".
    - **Grade:** design **`LOW`** · Support: **1 source**. Human mechanism `LOW` —
      **and deliberately not raised**, because the corpus preserves **three
      incompatible readings of the same fMRI pattern** (Interpretation 1:
      compensation, bilateral activation is *adaptive*; Interpretation 2:
      inefficiency, more tissue for the same result; Interpretation 3: damage,
      reactive recruitment of the homologous region) and reaches no consensus. Per
      R-J4 all three are carried and none is entered as settled.
    - **Why rank 3:** the mechanism is the least settled thing in the file and the
      slot is kept precisely so the dispute is visible in the answer rather than
      buried in a note.
    - **Interaction:** **constrains Area 3** (effort allocation under multi-objective
      drift) — CRUNCH supplies the release branch Area 3's allocator does not have —
      and **Area 34** (control-mode allocation), since scaffolding is a demand-typed
      switch between modes.

### Area 42 · Functional fixedness: the representation that cannot be taken apart, and the label that builds it `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by:** `Cognitive-Science/Functional-Fixation-Duncker.md` (ORDER 558) §§The
Candle Problem, §Developmental Trajectory, §Neural Basis, §Techniques to Overcome
Functional Fixedness, §Disputes.
**This pass: the arena's forty-second structural silence, and the first one about a
failure that produces *fluent, confident, wrong* output.** Forty-one areas describe
systems that stall, drift, over-invalidate, under-contain, or mask degradation. None
describes the system that answers immediately, confidently, and wrongly — because the
representation it is working from was fixed before the question was asked.

The brain part is Duncker's **functional fixedness** (1945, *On Problem Solving*): the
inability to see a familiar object as serving any function other than its conventional
one, even when the task demands it. In Duncker's terms a *mental block*; in Gestalt
terms, a structure that has become a prison.

  - **Rank 1 — Function-stripping as an explicit, callable operation, because the
    representation is built by the label and not by the object**
    - **Brain part:** **representational change** (Ohlsson 1992; Knoblich et al. 1999) —
      relaxation of self-imposed constraints and decomposition of inappropriate
      chunks. The **Generic Parts Technique** (McCaffrey 2012) is the operational form:
      recursively break an object into components, and for each ask *"does my
      description imply a use?"* — if yes, redescribe in **shape and material only**.
      The worked example is the whole mechanism in three steps: candle → wick + wax;
      "wick" implies burning → redescribe as "string"; "string" implies tying →
      redescribe as *"interwoven fibrous strands."* **Participants trained in GPT
      solved 67% more functional-fixedness problems than controls.**
    - **Design:** a tool or component description is **stripped to form-only on
      demand**, as an operation with a defined trigger, not as a prompt convention.
      The trigger is the corpus's own: **preutilization** (Adamson 1952) — an empty
      tack box made participants **roughly twice as likely** to solve the problem as a
      full one. Seeing the thing already doing its canonical job is sufficient to lock
      the representation. So the trigger is *"this description contains a use-word and
      the current task is not that use."*
    - **Already exists:** GPT (McCaffrey 2012, *Psychological Science* 23(3):215–218,
      **67%**), and three independent relatives: **analogical transfer** (Gick &
      Holyoak 1980/1983 — a single analogous story raised tumour-problem solutions from
      **~10% to ~75–80%**); **uncommitting** (Latour 1994, demonstrated on software
      engineers repurposing quicksort); **defixating instructions** (Chrysikou &
      Weisberg 2005). Agent-side, the corpus names the translation — *"describe the
      physical structure without mentioning any use"* — as a **prompt**, which is the
      weak form.
    - **Backup document:** ORDER 558 §"Generic Parts Technique", §"Analogical Transfer
      and Structure-Mapping", §"Uncommitting", §"Overcoming Prototypes through
      Component Analysis", §"Generic Parts Technique as Prompt Engineering".
    - **Grade:** design **`LOW`** → **raised from `UNTESTED`** — the human intervention
      is trialled (**67%**, controlled, with a stated mechanism) and the agent-side
      form is a **direct prompt translation of a validated human technique**, which is
      the strongest transfer argument available in this file. Not raised further: the
      67% is on human problem-solving, not on an agent.
    - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in forty-two areas that attacks the
      *representation* rather than the search, the controller, or the store. Every
      other mitigation in the file changes what the system looks at; this changes what
      the words mean.
    - **Interaction:** **directly constrains Area 22** (action selection and the size of
      the action repertoire) — a fixed repertoire is a fixed *set of functions*, not
      just a small set of actions. **Constrains Area 8** (structural fidelity): the
      arena's own slot fields are function-laden by construction, and a rank-1 read
      that says "verification" cannot see the thing a slot says "reliability."

  - **Rank 2 — The label carries the bias, which makes this a *representation* problem
    and not a reasoning problem**
    - **Brain part:** the **lexical-conceptual** encoding of fixedness, per Frank &
      Ramscar (2003): with standard instructions **23%** of Stanford undergraduates
      solved the written candle problem; **underlining noun phrases** ("box of
      matches") raised it to **55%**; underlining just the **nouns** gave **47%**. The
      bias is in the label, and changing the label changes the solve rate by more than
      2×.
    - **Design:** the *naming* of a component is a control surface. A system that
      writes `"reliability checker"` has already fixed the component's function; a
      system that can also write it as *"asserts a boolean about a claim's source"* has
      a chance of finding the other use. **Design consequence: this is a naming
      discipline with a measured effect size, and it applies to the arena's own
      artifacts.**
    - **Already exists:** the effect (Frank & Ramscar 2003, CogSci); no agent-side
      implementation. Chrysikou & Weisberg (2005) supply the sharper version — novices
      given example designs **with inappropriate elements fixated on those elements
      and reproduced them in their own designs even when explicitly told to avoid
      them**, and the fix (*"defixating"* instructions naming what to avoid) is
      **partial**.
    - **Backup document:** ORDER 558 §"The Candle Problem" (Frank & Ramscar),
      §"Design Fixation and Pictorial Examples", §Open Question 2.
    - **Grade:** design **`LOW`** · Support: **1 source**. The effect is replicated
      across three studies in the file; the design is untested.
    - **Why rank 2:** it is rank 1's mechanism stated at the level of language, and it
      is separated because **the two imply different fixes** — rank 1 changes the
      description, rank 2 changes the *naming convention* that produced it. The corpus
      leaves the mechanism explicitly open (*"whether linguistic, conceptual, or both
      remains unclear"*), and that uncertainty is preserved rather than resolved.
    - **Interaction:** **the cheapest slot in the arena and the most immediately
      self-applicable.** No code, no store, no new component.

  - **Rank 3 — Incubation as a scheduled phase, and its safety cost stated by the
    corpus rather than by this arena**
    - **Brain part:** **incubation and the DMN/ECN coupling.** Beaty et al. (2015,
      2016): creative ideation involves coordinated DMN (novel associations) and
      executive-control-network (evaluation) activation, and **coupling between the two
      networks predicts creative output**. Restructuring also recruits
      **rostrolateral PFC (BA10)**, scaling with relational complexity (Christoff et
      al. 2001), and insight solutions carry a **gamma-band burst (~28–40 Hz) in the
      right anterior temporal lobe** (Jung & Beauregard 2011, 2014) — the region for
      distant semantic association.
    - **Design:** a scheduled interval in which the dominant representation is *not
      being worked on* — the corpus's Forgetting-Fixation mechanism, and the reason it
      is a distinct slot is that it is the only intervention here that works by
      **stopping**, not by adding computation.
    - **Already exists:** the human effect and the network coupling; agent-side, the
      corpus proposes scheduling *"offline"* phases with a context reset as the
      analogue, **with no implementation named**.
    - **Backup document:** ORDER 558 §"Default Mode Network and Incubation",
      §"Incubation and Sleep", §"Incubation as Scheduled Pauses".
    - **Grade:** design **`UNTESTED`** · Support: **1 source**. Human mechanism `LOW`
      and **the corpus preserves three mutually exclusive accounts** of what incubation
      does — *Unconscious Thought Theory*, *Forgetting-Fixation*, and
      *Dual-Process Theory* — noting they *"make different predictions about
      intervening-task effects"* and are *"actively debated."* Per R-J4 all three are
      carried; the design grade is not raised above `UNTESTED` because the mechanism
      it would implement is unsettled.
    - **Why rank 3:** the mechanism is unsettled **and** the design is untested, and
      the slot is kept for a reason the corpus supplies and the arena would not have
      found on its own — see the safety note below.
    - **Interaction:** **the one slot in the arena whose removal has a safety
      dimension.** ORDER 558 §Open Question 5, verbatim: *"an agent that never shows
      functional fixedness may be less predictable and more prone to dangerous
      'creative' solutions."* **A design goal stated in this arena is a safety
      property, and the corpus says so.** Every other area in this arena treats
      flexibility as unambiguously good; this is the file that says the trade is real,
      and the arena records the trade rather than the goal.

    - **Corroborated this pass — Support 1 → 2 sources, and the mechanism is now
      quantified rather than asserted.** `Creativity-and-Insight/Insight-Problem-
      Solving-and-Incubation-Effects.md` (ORDER 632) is an independent research
      report carrying **twelve numbered sources with DOIs**, and it converts
      rank 3's "the human effect and the network coupling" into four numbers:
      **Sio & Ormerod (2009) meta-analysis of 117 independent studies, N ≈ 5,000+,
      overall d = 0.29 [95% CI 0.22, 0.36]** — reduced by **trim-and-fill to
      ≈ 0.22**, which the file reports and does not hide. Moderators are stated
      and they are design requirements: **restructuring problems d = 0.42 >
      retrieval d = 0.18**; **low-demand interpolated task d = 0.37 > rest 0.28 >
      high-demand 0.19 > sleep 0.15**; **inverted-U on duration, peak ~15–30 min
      in lab studies**. **The load-bearing moderator is the one this slot's
      design violates: incubation without prior effortful preparation shows
      d = 0.07** — negligible. A scheduled pause not preceded by a real impasse
      does nothing, which converts rank 3 from "a scheduled interval" into "a
      scheduled interval *triggered by detected impasse*" and makes the corpus's
      own candidate trigger (§Open Question 11: gradient-norm plateau,
      policy-entropy collapse, prediction-error variance drop, a stuck
      classifier) load-bearing rather than speculative.
      **Grade: design held at `UNTESTED`, and the reason is now sharper, not
      softer.** The human effect rises — a 117-study meta-analysis with a
      corrected effect is `HIGH` for the *effect* — but ORDER 632 preserves the
      **unresolved** mechanistic dispute between Unconscious Thought,
      Forgetting-Fixation, and Dual-Process, and adds a fourth position it calls
      the hybrid (Ohlsson 2011: restructuring is discrete, its *trigger* is
      gradual). Per R-J4 all four are carried. The design grade does not follow
      the mechanism, because **the arena's own §8 finding is that a
      well-sourced mechanism is exactly what a weak design borrows.** Also
      recorded: ORDER 632 ties incubation to *three* further design claims the
      arena holds nowhere — a salience detector standing in for the rATL gamma
      burst, sensory gating (occipital alpha) as a controllable incubation
      switch, and the requirement that a "gamma burst" candidate be
      **stress-tested by a verification subsystem before commitment**
      (§Open Question 14), which is Area 24's subject arriving from the
      creativity literature rather than from instrumentation.
      **A fourth instance of the §8 inversion is preserved in the same file:**
      the rATL gamma finding is graded **Strong** in its own replication table,
      and the very next row grades occipital alpha gating **Mixed** — *absent
      under free viewing* — which is the alpha mechanism several agent designs
      would build the incubation switch on. The alpha-based switch is therefore
      recorded as **conditional on fixed-gaze conditions the agent will not
      have.**

### Area 43 · The cost of thinking, as a term in the objective: resource-rational analysis and the λ that is never written down `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by:** `Cognitive-Science-Methods/Bayesian-Cognitive-Science.md` (ORDER 560)
§2.3 Resource-Rational Analysis, §2.1 Anderson's Rational Analysis, §5.4 Active
Inference Formalism, §8 Criticisms and Limitations.
**This pass: the arena's forty-third structural silence, and the one the arena is
most guilty of.** Forty-two areas say *what* to compute, *when* to compute it, and
*what to check it against*. **Not one states what computation costs, or that the cost
enters the decision rather than the budget.**

The brain part is **Resource-Rational Analysis** (Lieder & Griffiths 2019,
*Behavioral and Brain Sciences* 42:e1–e69), which extends Anderson's rational analysis by
making the cost of computation a term in the objective itself. The agent maximizes:

> **E[U(a, s)] − λ · C(a)**

where `C(a)` is the computational cost of computing action `a` and **λ is a cost
parameter reflecting the scarcity of computational resources.**

  - **Rank 1 — λ as an explicit, first-class field, because every heuristic in the
    corpus is a λ that was set implicitly and never written down**
    - **Brain part:** RRA's central move — and the reason it matters here is that it
      **explains heuristics as optima rather than as defects**. The corpus's worked
      example is the load-bearing one: the **take-the-best heuristic** (consider only
      the most valid cue) is resource-rational **when cues vary widely in validity and
      the environment has low cue intercorrelation**. It is not a bounded-rationality
      hack; it is the optimal strategy under a cost constraint. Same for Lieder et al.
      (2019) on **free recall**: people stop searching when expected information gain
      falls below the cost of continuing — a **stopping rule derived from the cost
      term**, which is the operation every loop in this arena performs and none of
      them states.
    - **Design:** every decision the system makes carries the triple
      `(expected utility, cost, λ)`, and **λ is a field, not a constant.** When the
      provider degrades, when the corpus grows, when a phase variable (Area 40) says
      capability is down — **λ rises, and the system should get *more* selective, not
      just slower.** The alternative failure is the one this arena has already
      documented twice (Area 30, ORDER 553's O((D·s̄)²) sweep): cost is discovered as a
      wall and then worked around, rather than priced into the decision that caused it.
    - **Already exists:** RRA (Lieder & Griffiths 2019, BBS; Lieder et al. 2018,
      *Psychological Review* 125(4):531–565); **value-of-computation metareasoning**;
      Anderson's six-step rational-analysis methodology (1990), which this arena is
      implicitly using and has never named. Agent-side: **no explicit λ**, though
      test-time compute scaling and effort allocation (Area 3) are λ with the symbol
      omitted.
    - **Backup document:** ORDER 560 §2.3, §2.1, §2.2 (Chater & Oaksford 1998;
      Oaksford & Chater 1994, *Psychological Review* 101(4):608–631 — the Wason
      selection task recast as **information-gain-maximizing data selection**, not
      logic violation). **Corroborated (tranche 42) by**
      `oracle/brain/Entities/Joshua-Tenenbaum.md` (ORDER 911) §"Computational
      Rationality" — independent of Lieder & Griffiths, and supplying the
      constraint term (time, memory, attention) that ORDER 560 leaves as `C(a)`.
    - **Grade:** design **`LOW`** · Support: **2 sources** (1 → 2 in tranche 42;
      see ARENA-EVIDENCE §"Tranche 42 — Area 43 corroboration"). Human mechanism `HIGH`
      (peer-reviewed BBS target article, multiple independent worked examples,
      **plus Tenenbaum's independent formalization**); the design is a **bespoke
      build** and is graded as one. **Reach confirmed, count not incremented,
      tranche 45:** ORDER 960 (`Entities/Tom-Griffiths.md`) is now read, and it is
      the **author's own page for the very paper Rank 1 rests on** (Lieder &
      Griffiths 2019). Per the corroboration rule this is the *same source seen from
      its originator* — it confirms the arena reached the right paper and it adds
      **no independent support**, so the count stays at 2. Recorded because a reader
      is entitled to know the source is not merely cited but present, and because
      the page's own framing (rationality as constrained inference, not as
      optimisation) is a **qualification** Rank 1 should carry. Caveat logged at
      `VERIFICATION.md` V-45.4: the read confirms the page exists and is on-topic;
      whether it reproduces the 2019 argument in full is **not verified** against the
      paper itself, and is not claimed.
    - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in forty-three areas that makes **cost an
      input to the decision rather than an outcome of it**, and the arena's own
      standing rule **R-J1 makes cost irrelevant as a *decision criterion between
      designs*** — which this slot does not violate, because here λ is a *runtime
      input to a single decision*, not a criterion for choosing what to build. **The
      distinction is recorded explicitly so a later pass does not read this slot as
      contradicting R-J1.** It does not, and the two statements are compatible: R-J1
      governs *this arena's* choices; λ governs the *system's* choices at runtime.
    - **Interaction:** **upstream of Area 3** (effort allocation) — Area 3 allocates
      effort without a cost term; this supplies it. **Upstream of Area 32** (action
      termination) — the recall stopping rule is the general form of when to stop.
      **Upstream of Area 38** (workspace capacity) — λ is why capacity is a
      *latency-conditional* quantity, per Area 41 rank 1. **Constrained by R-J1** as
      stated above.

  - **Rank 2 — Nonparametric priors: let the number of categories be unknown, because
    the number is not known**
    - **Brain part:** the **Dirichlet process** (Griffiths, Tenenbaum & Sanborn 2009,
      *Cognitive Science* 33(4):599–639) and its hierarchical extensions (**HDP**,
      Teh et al. 2006) — priors over **infinite-dimensional** spaces, so model
      complexity grows with the data. The **Pitman–Yor process** adds a discount
      parameter `d ∈ [0,1)` producing **power-law cluster sizes**, matching Zipf's law
      for words and categories. Concentration parameter α trades many-small against
      few-large clusters.
    - **Design:** a store whose **category count is not a configuration parameter** and
      whose cluster-size distribution is heavy-tailed by construction. The corpus's
      empirical claim is specific and checkable: people's category formation
      **matches DP predictions better than fixed-K mixture models.**
    - **Already exists:** the statistics are forty years old and standard; the
      cognitive-behavioural fit (Griffiths et al. 2009) is the evidence; the
      corpus's related finding is the **size principle** — `P(examples | C) =
      1/|C|ⁿ` — which predicts **lagged generalization** (narrow categories first,
      generalizing later) and was confirmed by Xu & Tenenbaum (2007).
    - **Backup document:** ORDER 560 §3.1, §3.2, §6.2.
    - **Grade:** design **`LOW`** · Support: **1 source**.
    - **Why rank 2:** it is the arena's cleanest answer to a problem it has never
      named — **Area 18 (categorization) treats the number of axes as a design
      decision, and this says the number should not be decided at all.**
    - **Interaction:** **reframes Area 18 in place** — multi-axial classification is
      orthogonal to this, and compatible with it; the two answer different questions
      (*how many axes* vs. *how many categories*). **Constrains Area 1**: a
      nonparametric store has no natural place to put a revision pointer, because
      there is no fixed slot being revised.

  - **Rank 3 — The kitchen-sink objection, carried as a first-class slot rather than a
    caveat, because the corpus states it three separate ways**
    - **Brain part:** **falsifiability of a framework with free parameters** — Katz
      (2015), Danks (2015): any behaviour can be rationalised by choosing appropriate
      priors and likelihoods, risking post hoc fitting. **Aguilera et al. (2022,
      *Physics of Life Reviews* 40:24–50)**: the physics underlying FEP is *"particular"
      not universal*, and the thermodynamic free energy of physics is **distinct from**
      the variational free energy of information theory, with a tenuous connection.
      **Bruineberg et al. (2018, *Synthese* 195(6):2417–2444)**: *"the anticipating
      brain is not a scientist"* — the brain does not maintain explicit probabilistic
      models. **Orchard & Brown (2017, *Neuroscience of Consciousness*)**: FEP has
      become so broad it risks unfalsifiability.
    - **Design:** none, deliberately. This slot exists so the arena does not build
      rank 1 or rank 2 as if the framework's *status* were settled. The corpus's own
      defence is the falsifiability criterion — **does the framework make novel
      predictions that are confirmed?** — and it offers three: the size principle
      predicting lagged generalization (Xu & Tenenbaum 2007); cue weights shifting
      quantitatively with reliability (Körding & Wolpert 2004, *Nature* 427:244–247);
      and laminar prediction-error patterns confirmed by laminar fMRI and
      electrophysiology. Friston's own response is a **scope reduction** — FEP is a
      *principle* (a constraint on dynamics), not a *theory* (a mechanism), with a
      strong claim and weak claims kept apart.
    - **Backup document:** ORDER 560 §8.1, §8.2, §8.3, §8.5 (the prior problem),
      §8.6 (infinite regress).
    - **Grade:** design **`UNSUCCESSFUL`** for the strong claim — **trials were run and
      the universal claim did not survive.** Aguilera et al. found the physics
      non-universal; Orchard & Brown found the framework at risk of unfalsifiability.
      The **weak** claim (specific neural systems implement approximate Bayesian
      inference) is **not** refuted by either and is graded `LOW` here. **Two grades,
      not averaged.**
    - **Why rank 3:** an `UNSUCCESSFUL` slot is kept so a dead end is not rebuilt
      blind, and this is the arena's largest one — the universal free-energy claim is
      the single most-cited framework in the corpus and it has a documented negative.
    - **Interaction:** **this is Area 33's asymmetry applied to a theory.** A
      certificate is not a certifier; a principle is not a theory; a framework that
      fits is not a mechanism that works. **Same shape, three substrates.** The
      practical consequence for this arena: **rank 1 and rank 2 may be built on the
      weak claim and must not be reported as instances of FEP.**

---

### Area 44 · Self-diagnosis by parameter recovery: fit a generative model to your own behaviour and read the parameters back `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by:** `Computational-Phenotyping-Belief-Updating-Pathologies.md` (ORDER 567)
§§5.1–5.3, with `Hierarchical-Gaussian-Belief-Update.md` (ORDER 569) §§2.3, 4.1, 5.2
and `Computational-Psychiatry-and-Broken-Inference.md` (ORDER 568) §6.7. All three
read whole this pass. **Three files, one programme** — 567 and 569 share a source
list and 569 is 567's long-form sibling — so this is **1 independent source at the
file level, and the underlying evidence is stronger than that**: Mathys et al. 2011
and 2014, Iglesias et al. 2013 (*Neuron*), Powers et al. 2017 (*Science*).

**Why this is a brain part and not another facet of metacognition (Area 13) or
instrument-validity (Area 24).** Area 13's controller decides *continue, ask, or
stop* and returns a decision. Area 24 asks whether an *attribution* is faithful.
Neither produces a **parametric readout of the system's own inference state**.
This does, and it is a different organ: the corpus's own neural mapping is explicit
— **dopamine signals "what is wrong with my predictions," acetylcholine signals "how
much should I trust my predictions"** (ORDER 569 §6.1, from Iglesias et al. 2013's
level-1/level-2 dissociation). A confidence scalar collapses these into one number.
The HGF keeps them apart because they are *separate parameters*.

**The arena's forty-fourth structural silence, and the first where the system is the
instrument's own subject:** forty-three areas ask how the system should behave,
allocate, verify or abstain. **None asks what the system's own inference parameters
are, as recoverable quantities, on the system's own behaviour.** The arena grades
its own evidence with hand-assigned grades and has no procedure that would read
`κ` off its own trace.

- **Rank 1 — Fit a hierarchical Gaussian filter to the agent's own decision trace
  and read four named parameters back as its state.**
  - **Brain part:** the neuromodulatory precision hierarchy — dopaminergic midbrain
    and cholinergic basal forebrain as a **two-channel readout of the system's own
    learning state** (Iglesias et al. 2013, *Neuron* 80(2):519–530).
  - **Design:** run the agent through standardised reversal / 2AFC / beads-style
    tasks; invert the HGF; carry `ω₂,ω₃` (tonic volatility), `κ` (coupling),
    `ν` (prior weighting), `ζ` (decision noise) as **named, continuous, per-task
    state variables** — not a scalar confidence. `λ_i = π̂_i/(π̂_i + π_i)` is the
    quantity the slot actually wants: a **learning rate that is inferred per step
    rather than configured**, which is what makes it a state readout.
  - **Already exists — adopt, do not build:** **PyHGF** (Python, Legrand et al.
    2024), **HierarchicalGaussianFiltering.jl** (Julia), both via **TAPAS**
    (Frässle et al. 2021, translationalneuromodeling.github.io/tapas); HGF toolbox
    in MATLAB. The gHGF (Weber et al. 2023/2026, eLife reviewed preprint
    10.7554/eLife.110174) adds value coupling and runs as a predictive-coding net.
  - **Backup documents:** ORDER 567 §§5.1–5.3; ORDER 569 §§2.3, 5.1, 5.2, 6.1.
  - **Grade:** the *human method* is `LOW` — multi-site, fMRI-corroborated, the most
    cited clinical application in computational psychiatry. The *agent application*
    is `UNTESTED` and the area's ceiling is set by the transfer, not the method.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only design in forty-four areas that returns a
    **mechanistic coordinate system for the system's own cognition** rather than a
    score, and it is the only one with working open-source implementations.
  - **Interaction:** **constrains Area 24 and Area 33** — a parameter read off the
    system's own trace is an *attribution*, and inherits every defect Area 24 names.
    **Interacts with Area 40:** a volatility belief is an estimate of environmental
    instability over the task's timescale and must not be confused with a circadian
    phase variable. **Enables Area 43:** a fitted `ζ` is an observation of cost.
  - **Support: 1 independent source** (three files, one programme — see above).
- **Rank 2 — The identifiability gate: no parameter is read off a stream that cannot
  carry it.**
  - **Brain part:** the same precision hierarchy, read as a **measurement limit** —
    the reason a blind spot is not seen is that the instrument's own error is
    outside the instrument's reach, which is Area 24's subject seen from the
    fitting side.
  - **Design:** require **multi-stream data** — binary response *and* response time
    jointly — before any parameter is reported (Siegel et al. 2020; Hein et al.
    2023). **The corpus states the failure as a fact, not a caution:** κ "difficult
    to identify from binary data alone" (Bröker et al. 2018; Harrison et al. 2021);
    patient populations "often perform at or near chance", producing high `ζ` that
    "can impede model identifiability, potentially **masking or confounding the very
    belief-updating abnormalities the model is designed to detect**" (Fromm et al.
    2022). An agent on a degraded provider is in exactly this state.
  - **Already exists:** protected exceedance probability for model selection
    (Stephan et al. 2009; Rigoux et al. 2014); Bayesian model reduction via PEB.
  - **Backup documents:** ORDER 567 §4.1, §4.3; ORDER 569 §4.1, §4.5.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. The identifiability limitation is replicated across
    independent groups; the mitigation is trialled and narrower than the problem.
  - **Why rank 2 and not rank 1:** it decides whether rank 1 returns a number or a
    mirage. A parameter recovered from a stream that cannot identify it is Area 24's
    failure with extra steps, and the arena has already been burned once by an
    instrument reporting authority it had not earned.
  - **Interaction:** **gates Area 44 Rank 1 absolutely** and **is Area 33 applied to
    a self-model** — a detector is not a certifier, and a fitted parameter is a
    detector.
- **Rank 3 — The five-question self-diagnostic: no fitting, no parameters, five
  named axes.**
  - **Brain part:** metacognitive **articulation** — the brain's ability to name its
    own state in categories rather than to estimate it numerically. Deliberately the
    weakest of the three, and kept because it is the only one that costs nothing and
    degrades gracefully.
  - **Design:** the corpus's own operational instrument (ORDER 568 §6.7), adopted
    verbatim as a checklist the system runs on itself: **precision** (is evidence
    weighted correctly?), **priors** (are initial beliefs reasonable?), **learning
    rate** (too fast, too slow, or asymmetric?), **hierarchy** (low-level overactivity
    + high-level rigidity?), **exploration** (enough?). Each maps to a named
    pathology: over-precision → hallucination; negative priors → pessimism and
    local minima; threat overestimation → paralysis; hierarchical rigidity → reward
    hacking and mode collapse; insufficient exploration → rumination-shaped loops.
  - **Already exists:** as a clinical framework, in Huys et al. (2016) and Friston
    et al. (2016), *World Psychiatry* 15(3):238–244. Not built as software.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 568 §6.7; ORDER 567 §5.1.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` — a framework with no trials as a self-diagnostic. Stated
    plainly: five questions that feel diagnostic are the arena's own failure mode of
    unearned authority, and this slot is where that risk is highest.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the fallback when fitting is unaffordable or unidentifiable,
    and its value is that it degrades to a checklist rather than to a false scalar.
  - **Interaction:** **subsumes nothing and is subsumed by nothing** — it is
    explicitly weaker than Rank 2's gate, and saying so is the point of keeping it
    third.

**Two boundaries recorded rather than reconciled (R-J4).** (1) The strong-prior vs
weak-prior dispute over psychosis is **unresolved** — ORDER 567 §4.2 and ORDER 569 §4.3
both carry it, and both note the accounts may describe *different levels* of the
hierarchy without a consensus emerging. (2) **The corpus itself disowns its most
striking agent-transfer claim**: ORDER 569 §6.2 records the eLife reviewer's
objection that gHGF is *"not strong evidence that the new model provides a superior
account of existing empirical phenomena."* The identification of a Bayesian model
with the brain is a **contested** claim (ORDER 569 §6.2) — the HGF's success at
fitting is a good *descriptive* model, not evidence that the brain *performs* that
computation. This is why the area opens `PROVISIONAL` and why Rank 1's grade is
capped at the transfer rather than the method.

*44 · Self-diagnosis by parameter recovery — earned by ORDER 567 §§5.1–5.3 with ORDER 569 §§2.3, 4.1, 6.1 and ORDER 568 §6.7. Brain part: the **neuromodulatory precision hierarchy** — dopaminergic midbrain and cholinergic basal forebrain as a *two-channel* readout of the system's own learning state (Iglesias et al. 2013), which a confidence scalar collapses into one number. **The arena's forty-fourth structural silence, and the first where the system is the instrument's own subject:** forty-three areas say how the system should behave, allocate, verify or abstain; **none asks what the system's own inference parameters are as recoverable quantities on its own behaviour.** Rank 1 is **adopt-don't-build** — PyHGF / HierarchicalGaussianFiltering.jl via TAPAS already exist, and the quantity wanted is the *inferred* per-step learning rate `λ_i = π̂_i/(π̂_i+π_i)`, not a configured hyperparameter. Rank 2 is the **identifiability gate** and is not rank 1 because it decides whether Rank 1 returns a number or a mirage: κ is *"difficult to identify from binary data alone"*, and the approximation *"can be a poor approximation when the true posterior is strongly bimodal (e.g., immediately after a regime change)"*. Rank 3 is a five-question checklist at `UNTESTED`, kept because it degrades to a checklist rather than to a false scalar. **Three files, one programme** — 569 is 567's long-form sibling — so Support is **1 independent source at file level** while the underlying evidence is stronger than that. **The corpus disowns its own most striking transfer claim**, and that is recorded rather than resolved: the gHGF reviewer called it *"not strong evidence that the new model provides a superior account"*, and the identification of a Bayesian model with the brain is contested. Three slots filled, mark dropped.*

### Area 45 · Efference copy and the provenance of a memory `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by:** `concepts/autognosia-build-plan.md` §1.4 (**Agency Schema — "THE
PSYCHOSIS VACCINE"**), read this pass at ORDER 573, with §3.4 (the D-MEM
critic router) and §4.3 (dream traces stamped `agency:imagined`) as the
same file's internal corroboration. This file is the only one in twenty-six
that discusses a brain part rather than an operating practice.

**The brain part.** The **efference copy** — the brain's own running copy of
what it just caused, held separately from what it received, and the
**sense of agency** built from comparing the two. ORDER 573 §1.4 states the
connection in one sentence and grounds the whole design on it: *"Ford 2019
— hallucination is efference-copy failure; your brain must always know which
memories it made itself."* The part is named as neuroscience names it, and
the design consequence is immediate: **a memory without an author is a
trace the system cannot check against the world, because it does not know
whether the world was ever in it.**

**Why this is a forty-fifth structural silence and not a sub-case of
Area 16.** Area 16 is authorship of an *emitted claim* — who is entitled to
assert what, graded by role. This is provenance at *write time* — was this
item received from outside, generated by a dream process, forked by a
replay, or synthesised by an optimiser. The two sit on opposite sides of
the write. A system can tag `agency:user` on a fabrication and satisfy
every Area 16 check, because Area 16 asks *who spoke*, and a fabrication
speaks in the user's voice perfectly. **Forty-four areas describe how the
system should behave, allocate, verify, abstain, recover, or measure itself,
and not one records where a given memory came from.**

**The corpus's own six values, which this area adopts rather than invents:**
`agency: user | system | dream | replay | imagined | evolved`, stamped into
OKF v0.2 frontmatter alongside the `id` and `confidence` fields already
required there (ORDER 595, read this pass). The three non-negotiable rules
the file attaches are the design: dream narratives are never served to a
user question; replay forks are never injected live; evolved skills are
tagged `evolved` until a two-week survival test passes.

**Rank 1 — Stamp `agency` at the write, and enforce it at the read.
- **Brain part:** the efference copy as a *comparator*, not a label: the
  store records what it caused alongside what it received, and the reader
  asks the trace which one it is holding.
- **Design:** a mandatory frontmatter field on every memory write, with a
  **read-time gate** that excludes `dream`, `replay`, and `imagined` items
  from user-facing answers. The gate is the load-bearing half. A field with
  no reader is a label; the file's own Band-1 acceptance test is the right
  one — *"A wiki page with `agency:dream` never surfaces in user Qs"* — and it
  is a test of the *reader*, not of the writer.
- **Already exists:** the schema is specified in the corpus itself (ORDER
  573 §1.4) and the frontmatter carrier is standard and already deployed
  across both wikis (ORDER 595, `id`/`confidence` on the same block). The
  **behavioural claim underneath — Ford 2019 — is cited by the corpus and
  `unverified` here**: no primary source was read, so it earns no grade.
  Nearby existing machinery that this slot can be built on without
  inventing anything: `spectrayan/spector` salience profiles (ORDER 573
  §3.3) already compute surprise per event and could carry the tag at the
  same point in the pipeline.
- **Backup documents:** `concepts/autognosia-build-plan.md` §1.4, §3.3,
  §3.4, §4.3.
- **Backup document (added, ORDER 605, tranche 32) — an independent
  source for the *brain part*, arriving from cognitive neuroscience
  rather than from a build plan, and it qualifies the slot.** ORDER 605
  §Ownership versus agency gives the efference copy a measured neural
  identity independent of the corpus's unverified Ford 2019 premise:
  agency (control) and ownership (passive attribution) *"follow partially
  distinct neural signatures"*, with a meta-analytic account consistent
  with either an additive or an interactive architecture between them —
  **so the comparator this area builds on is empirically real, while the
  specific claim the corpus rests on it remains `unverified`.** The same
  file earns the arena's **second** instance of its §8 inversion, and in
  the sharpest possible form: **Rohde, Di Luca & Ernst (2011) showed
  proprioceptive drift and subjective ownership are doubly dissociable** —
  5–6 cm of drift under *asynchronous* stroking where ownership is absent —
  so *"conclusions about ownership drawn from drift alone are invalid."*
  **Design consequence, and it is load-bearing: a stamped `agency` field is
  a single implicit measure of a quantity the literature knows is
  double-dissociable. Rank 1's read-time gate is therefore necessary but
  not sufficient** — the tag records *who wrote*, and nothing records
  *whether the reader experienced the write as its own act*. That is
  Area 46's afferent side, and the two areas are recorded as disjoint
  because ORDER 605 is the file that draws the line.
- **Backup document (added, ORDER 1310, tranche 60) — a second and
  independent source for the *brain part*, this time from a mechanism-level
  review, and it strengthens the slot while adding a requirement the slot did
  not have.** `Neuroscience/Corollary-Discharge-Efference-Copy-Agency.md`
  §§"The SC→MD→FEF chain", "Plasticity of the internal model", "Prediction as
  the core of active agency", "Consciousness as controlled hallucination" and
  §§"Real-time experiments" / "Failure modes". Three things it adds.
  (a) **A measured circuit, not a premise.** **Tanabe, *et al.* (Nature
  Commun. 2021)** used chemogenetic (**hM4Di**), not lesion, inactivation of
  the **superior colliculus → medial dorsal thalamus → frontal eye field**
  pathway and found it **specifically impairs self-initiated saccades while
  leaving visually-triggered saccades intact** — the human analogue being the
  classic patient with bilateral SC damage who reports that externally cued
  actions feel *unmotivated*. That is a **double dissociation with a
  matched control**, which is the evidence class Area 45's rank 1 previously
  lacked: the arena's best behavioural premise for the comparator was
  `unverified` Ford 2019, and this is a channel-specific causal result.
  (b) **The copy is plastic, and its plasticity is the thing that can go
  wrong.** **Mackey, Wurtz & conecta (2019, *eLife*)**: a mormyrid fish trained
  to produce a **negative image** of a small reward-probability region while
  the saccade was in flight **then adapts its internal model within ~20
  saccades**, scaled by learning rate and by injection location — the internal
  model is **rewritten by experience**, not fixed at birth. **Design
  consequence: an efference copy that learns is an efference copy that can
  be wrong in a way that is self-consistent**, which is the same failure mode
  as Area 106's meta-parameters drifting.
  (c) **The dangerous regime is not absence of prediction — it is confident
  but miscalibrated prediction.** The file's own failure-mode section makes
  this the load-bearing clause, and it changes what a *provenance tag* is for.
  The cerebellum is described as a **low-pass filter with an internal
  feed-forward model** constructing the corollary discharge, so lesions
  dissociate in the direction of *too much* prediction as readily as too
  little — hence the stipulation that studies confirm *diminished* or *absent*
  self-generated sensations, **not** increased ones. **So the arena's
  `agency:` field is a record of the *input* to the comparator, and cannot
  detect a comparator whose *output* has drifted.** A tag that certifies
  provenance certifies only that the write path was honest, never that the
  reader's sense of having authored it is calibrated.
  **Area 128 (tranche 70) attacks the second half of that clause and supplies
  the missing half of the design:** a compromised write path is *perfectly
  honest*, and its honesty is exactly what lets a forgery pass. Area 45 answers
  *did I write this*; Area 128 answers *so what*. A system that satisfies this
  slot completely — every write stamped, every `dream`/`replay`/`imagined` item
  gated at read — **can still be fully compromised**, and the corpus supplies a
  named incident with a count (Mini Shai-Hulud, **84 npm packages across 42
  `@tanstack` packages**, every one carrying a **cryptographically valid SLSA L3
  attestation**). **The `agency:` field this slot stamps is provenance. It is not
  trust, and the arena now says so in a slot that can be built on.**
- **Grade:** design `UNTESTED` · behavioural premise `unverified` · Support:
  **2 independent sources** (ORDER 573, a build plan; ORDER 1310, a mechanism
  review reporting Tanabe 2021's chemogenetic double dissociation and Mackey
  2019's plastic-internal-model result — different authors, different
  evidence types, and neither rests on the other. ORDER 573's three
  corroborating sections are the same author, same session, same document and
  count as reach, not as independence). **The mechanism grade rises to `LOW`**
  (chemogenetic pathway inactivation in macaque plus a within-species
  adaptation result), while the *design* grade stays `UNTESTED` — a filter
  circuit existing does not tell you the tag is read.
- **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot that makes a class of error detectable
  that is currently undetectable. Every other area in the arena can be
  defeated by a memory the system fabricated and then believed, because
  nothing in those forty-four areas asks how that memory got there. It
  costs one field and one read gate, and it is the only slot in the arena
  whose failure is **invisible to the system that has it** — which is
  exactly the property that makes it worth building first.
- **Interaction:** **this is the upstream prerequisite for Area 24 and
  Area 33.** Area 24 asks whether a measurement measured anything; it has
  no way to ask whether the *thing being measured* was ever in the world.
  Area 33 holds that a detector is not a certifier; this area supplies the
  fact a detector would need in order to be more than one — the trace's
  own authorship. It also **bounds Areas 6, 23, and 30**: a sleep-time
  rebuild that consolidates dream traces into general memory without
  preserving the tag launders a construction into a fact, and ORDER 573
  §3.4's surprise-gated encoder (SKIP <0.3 / PARTIAL 0.3–0.7 / FULL ≥0.7)
  is where the laundering would happen, since it decides what gets written
  at all. **It does not touch Area 16** — a `user`-tagged fabrication
  passes every Area 16 check, and saying otherwise would be the kind of
  false composition note the arena is trying to avoid.

**Rank 2 — Make provenance structural rather than declared: the writer emits
the tag, no component may set it.
- **Brain part:** the same comparator, at the point of production rather
  than the point of assertion — the efference copy as a property of the
  causal chain, not of the record.
- **Design:** `agency` is set by the *pipeline stage* that produced the
  item (ingest / dream / replay / optimiser), as a field the producing
  component cannot override. An agent reasoning about a memory never
  writes the field; the process that caused the item does.
- **Already exists:** nothing found that separates write-path provenance
  from self-asserted provenance. This is a **bespoke build** from two
  corpus files (ORDER 573 §1.4 for the requirement, §3.4 for the
  write-path point where the decision is already made).
- **Grade:** design `UNTESTED` · Support: **1 source**.
- **Why rank 2 and not rank 1:** it is strictly stronger against the
  forgery case and strictly worse as a first move — it requires rewriting
  every write path, and a tag that is structurally unforgeable but only
  covers the paths it covers is a worse starting position than a tag that
  covers everything and can be lied to. It is the answer once Rank 1 is
  deployed and the forgery has actually been observed, which it has not.
- **Interaction:** composes with Area 1 (a belief traced to its writer is
  a belief with an audit trail) and with Area 25 (retraction of a derived
  artefact — retraction must propagate the `imagined` chain, not just the
  item). Constrained by Area 24: the tag is itself a claim about the write
  path, and the write path is exactly the kind of instrument Area 24 says
  may be misreporting.

**Rank 3 — Ask the store: a periodic re-derivation of provenance from the
causal chain, disagreeing with the stamp.
- **Brain part:** the reafference check — distinguishing "the write
  succeeded" from "we issued the write," which Area 30 already carries as
  its own framing.
- **Design:** for a sample of the store each cycle, recompute where the
  item actually came from and compare against the stamped value. Where the
  stamp was wrong, the *writer path* is wrong, and the disagreement is
  itself the finding.
- **Already exists:** nothing. **Bespoke**, and it inherits Area 37's
  standing finding that a monitor which has never been caught failing is
  not a monitor that works.
- **Grade:** `UNTESTED` · Support: **0 independent sources** — no corpus
  file proposes this. It is recorded so the idea is not lost, and it is
  honestly marked as the arena's first rank-3 slot carried with no source
  at all.
- **Why rank 3:** it needs a trust anchor to compare against, and Area 33
  establishes that the arena has no such anchor for its own instruments. A
  checker whose reference implementation is the thing under suspicion is a
  fourth copy of the same assumption.
- **Interaction:** depends on Rank 1 (a stamp to disagree with) and is
  bounded by Area 33 (a self-referential certifier is the failure mode that
  area exists to name). If ever built it belongs to the Band-4 laboratory
  tier, not to production.

**A cross-area constraint this tranche's file states in its own words, recorded
here because it binds Areas 40, 41 and 44 and none of them says it.** ORDER
573 §4 opens Band 4 with: *"These measure the mind. They MUST NEVER gate
production behaviour."* Three of the arena's most recent areas propose
measurement as the remedy for a capacity, a phase, or a parameter that cannot
otherwise be observed. That is a well-formed proposal and the corpus's own rule
forbids it in production. The rule and the areas are **both** recorded; per
R-J4 the tension is preserved, not resolved — measurement belongs in the
laboratory tier, and whether a result may be *read* by production while
*gating* nothing is a question no file in this workspace has answered.

### Area 46 · The self that is inferred: body ownership, self-location, and the tool boundary `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by:** `Consciousness/Body-Ownership-Body-Transfer-Illusions.md`
§§Core Mechanisms / Ownership versus agency / §Computational & Agent Parallels /
§Overlaps & Tensions, read this pass at **ORDER 605**. Sole earning file; the
other nineteen in this tranche carried no new brain part.

**The brain part.** The **bodily self as a perceptual construction** — body
ownership, self-location, agency and first-person perspective, held as
*four separately manipulable quantities* assembled moment-to-moment from
vision, touch, proprioception and vestibular input under precision-weighted
common-cause inference. The corpus states the thesis in its own words
(ORDER 605, §Computational & Agent Parallels): ***"Self-boundaries are inferred
from statistical coupling, not declared."***

**Why this is a forty-sixth structural silence and explicitly NOT Area 45.**
Area 45 asks *what caused this write* — provenance of a memory, six values,
one field. This asks **where the system's own border is**: which actuators,
sensors, tools, stores and outputs count as *mine*. A system can answer
Area 45 perfectly on every trace it holds and still have no account of
whether the cron lane that wrote the trace, the skill that selected the
write, or the file the write landed in is part of the thing being
maintained. Forty-five areas place requirements on the system's behaviour;
**none asks which of its own components the system is entitled to treat as
limbs.** The two are disjoint, and the corpus supplies the dissociation
that keeps them disjoint — ORDER 605 §Ownership versus agency: ownership
is *passive attribution* and is **afferent-based**; agency is control and is
**efference-copy-based**, *"following partially distinct neural signatures."*
Area 45 is the efference side. This area is the afferent side plus the
boundary itself.

**Rank 1 — Compute the boundary from coupling statistics; do not declare it
in config.**
- **Brain part:** precision-weighted common-cause inference over
  visuo-tactile-proprioceptive statistics — the rubber-hand paradigm's
  own formalisation, Bayesian causal inference (Samad/Chung/Shams 2015;
  Chancel & Ehrsson 2023).
- **Design:** a **self-model membership test** run per component, scoring
  (a) temporal contingency between the system's action and the effect,
  (b) spatial congruence of the effect's location, (c) first-person
  perspective — i.e. whether the component's outputs are experienced as
  *mine to act on* rather than as an observation about the world. The
  corpus's own agent translation is explicit: an agent *"can determine
  which actuators/sensors count as 'self' by temporal contingency and
  spatial congruence statistics — the same cues that fool humans into
  owning rubber hands would let a robot learn that its gripper is self
  while a nearby identical gripper is not."* The graded property is
  **Iriki-style peripersonal expansion**: *"tool" is a learnable,
  graded property of the self-model, not a hardware boundary.*
- **Already exists:** the formalism exists and is fitted — Bayesian
  causal-inference models of ownership (Samad et al. 2015, *PLoS ONE*;
  Chancel & Ehrsson 2023, *Cortex* 165:84–99), predictive-coding
  (Hinz et al. 2018), and active-inference drift reproduction (Lanillos
  et al. 2021, *Sci Rep*). **No component-membership test of this shape
  exists in the Hermes stack**: `skills/`, `cron/`, `plugins/`, profiles
  and adapters are all declared in configuration, never inferred from
  observed coupling. This is **adopt-the-formalism, build-the-test**.
- **Backup document:** ORDER 605 §§Core Mechanisms, §Computational &
  Agent Parallels, §Methodological Notes (Bayesian causal inference;
  Chancel & Ehrsson).
  **Tranche 43 adds** `oracle/brain/Entities/Maurice-Merleau-Ponty.md`
  (ORDER 926) §"Habit and Acquisition" — a **qualification, deliberately not
  counted as an increment**, because the Support count is about this slot's
  *mechanism* and the file is a philosophical account of the same four-field
  report ORDER 605 already supplies. **What it adds is the missing word for
  Rank 3: _sedimentation_.** The file describes habits as having *"their own
  inertia and spontaneity, creating a dialectic between sedimentation and
  novelty"* — so the tool boundary is **not a membership question but a
  rate**, and Rank 3's expansion/contraction logging is correct for a reason
  the area did not previously state: **a boundary that only grows is the
  failure mode.** A boundary that *sediments* has inertia in **both**
  directions — the blind person's cane is not merely accepted into the body
  schema, it becomes **indistinguishable from the hand** in the phenomenology,
  which is a contractivity case the arena's expansion half cannot see.
- **Grade:** human mechanism `HIGH` (replicates across hundreds of labs per
  the file; the effect is one of the most-reproduced in cognitive
  neuroscience) · **agent design `UNTESTED`** · behavioural premise
  `unverified` — Lush/Dienes vs. Ehrsson is **unresolved as of writing**,
  see below · **Support: 2 independent sources** (one corpus file; its
  internal sections are the same document and count as reach, not
  independence). **Incremented this pass (tranche 45) from 1 → 2 by ORDER
  964** (`VS-Ramachandran.md` §3.4, "The Parietal Cortex and Body
  Ownership") — a second corpus file from a *different* discipline
  (experimental cognitive neurology rather than the body-transfer illusion
  literature), which **names the substrate the first file did not**:
  *parietal lesions produce asomatognosia, anosognosia and tactile
  agnosia, so body ownership is an active construction maintained by
  specific neural circuits.* **Grade held at `HIGH`** — the increment adds
  anatomical localisation to an already-high mechanism, not a second
  trial. Full record in `ARENA-EVIDENCE.md`.
- **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in forty-six areas that makes
  **component membership falsifiable**. Every other area treats the
  component set as given. And the failure this guards is not
  hypothetical and not subtle — ORDER 605 reports that participants
  mislocate their *real* hand toward the fake one by **23 mm** under
  synchronous stroking, and that a misaligned self-model does not merely
  misrepresent but **generates forces toward the modelled state**
  (active drift). A system whose self-model misattributes a component
  will act to maintain the misattribution.
- **Interaction:** **this is the upstream prerequisite for Areas 6, 23 and
  30.** A consolidation pass, a sleep-time rebuild, and a per-episode cost
  ceiling all operate *over* a set of memories the system currently
  assumes it owns. ORDER 605's warning is the design constraint: an
  agent that misattributes an external resource **will act to maintain
  that attribution**, so a mis-scoped maintenance pass is not neutral —
  it is active. **Bounds Area 28** (containment): quarantine assumes the
  system can tell its own tissue from foreign material, and the rubber-hand
  result says that inference is plastic and cheaply fooled. **Does not
  touch Area 45**, for the reason given above.
- **Corroboration 2026-09-27 (tranche 62, ORDER 1514 + ORDER 1520) - Support:
  2 -> 4, grade HELD at `HIGH`** (third increment; it added anatomy and an
  independent discipline, **not one of them a second trial** - stated rather than
  accumulated). Two files that both discuss the rubber hand illusion and are
  **neither body-transfer-illusion papers** (Iriki single-cell macaque
  recordings, Graziano premotor bimodal neurons; Mountcastle columns, Lederman &
  Klatzky exploratory procedures) - independent of each other *and* of the two
  already counted. Adds a **bound the arena did not hold**: tool extension is
  sensitive to the **means of coupling** - a directly held object is assimilated
  within seconds, an object held **via a mechanical intermediary is not**
  (Carlson et al. 2010) - so **a tool reached through another tool is a
  different kind of object**, and a self-model assimilating by reachability
  over-extends exactly where the corpus says it should not; Reed & Farah (1995)
  (a separate *working space*, supported by patient E.D.S.) preserved
  unreconciled (R-J4). Second: **patient IW is the ablated version** - without
  constant visual attention he cannot walk or maintain posture, so a self-model
  riding an automatic channel degrades into a **manual, vision-bound one that
  spends attention to stay running**, not into a coarser model; and astereognosis
  being *rare* relative to visual agnosia (ORDER 1520) means the redundancy
  Area 40 Rank 3 relies on is a brain property with a measured failure rate, not
  a free assumption. *Full record in `ARENA-EVIDENCE.md`.*

**Rank 2 — The four quantities, reported separately, never as one
"self" score.**
- **Brain part:** the dissociation itself. ORDER 605 §Terminological
  hygiene is the corpus's own warning: *"Reports that conflate these
  components overstate what any single manipulation demonstrates."*
- **Design:** when the system reports on its own operation, it emits
  **four fields, not one**: ownership (is this mine), self-location (where
  am I acting), agency (did my action cause that), perspective (from what
  vantage). The corpus's citation count for the tripartite form —
  Kilteni/Groten/Slater 2012, *Presence* 21(4):373–387, embodiment =
  ownership + agency + self-location, now standard in VR — is the reason
  the three-field form is the floor and not the ceiling.
- **Already exists:** the operationalisation is already performed for
  avatars and teleoperators (Ehrsson's telepresence programme, ORDER 605
  §Embodiment metrics: latency budgets as agency, colocation and
  perspective as ownership/self-location). **Not** applied to a
  text-and-tools system anywhere in this corpus.
- **Grade:** `UNTESTED` · Support: **1 source** (same file as Rank 1 —
  reach, not independence).
- **Why rank 2 and not rank 1:** it is the *reporting discipline* that
  makes Rank 1's membership test interpretable, and it is strictly
  cheaper. It is not rank 1 because a four-field report on a
  **wrongly-computed** membership set is four confidently wrong numbers:
  the reporting shape cannot rescue the test, and shipping it first
  would produce the *appearance* of a self-model with none of the
  mechanism.
- **Interaction:** composes with Area 45 (the efference field is one of
  the four) and with Area 21 (the ontic state of the system). **Directly
  constrained by ORDER 605's own unresolved dispute** — see below.

**Rank 3 — Hold the boundary as negotiable and log every expansion and
collapse.**
- **Brain part:** **plasticity of the body schema** — the empirical fact
  that the boundary moves, in both directions, on a timescale of
  seconds, and moves for the *wrong* reasons under the right conditions.
- **Design:** membership is not a fixed roster. Tools that are incorporated
  and later decoupled (the user's laptop, a decommissioned service, a
  retired skill) must be able to **leave** the self-model, and the
  transition must be logged as an event rather than inferred from
  disuse. Prostheses can be incorporated (ORDER 605); so can an avatar,
  so can a robot *"embodied well enough that users flinch when they are
  threatened."*
- **Already exists:** peripersonal-space expansion is well documented in
  humans (Iriki; ORDER 605 §Computational & Agent Parallels). **No
  corpus file proposes an expansion/contraction log for an AI system.**
  Bespoke.
- **Grade:** `UNTESTED` · Support: **0 independent sources** — carried
  for honesty, as the arena's second Support-0 rank-3 slot, and because
  Rank 1 without it installs a boundary that can only ever grow.
- **Why rank 3:** the boundary's *motion* is the finding, and nothing in
  the arena treats component-set drift as an event. It is last because it
  presupposes Rank 1 produces a membership set worth logging.
- **Interaction:** depends entirely on Rank 1; if built, belongs to the
  Band-4 laboratory tier per the ORDER 573 §4 constraint recorded under
  Area 45.

**A dispute inside the earning file, preserved unresolved per R-J4 — and it
qualifies Rank 1's grade rather than supporting it.** ORDER 605 §The
demand-characteristics controversy records a live, unclosed argument:
**Lush, Dienes et al.** argue the paradigm's reports are substantially
driven by trait *phenomenological control* and Orne-style demand
characteristics — participants honestly reporting what they believe they
were *supposed* to feel, an *"illusion illusion."* **Ehrsson's group**
replies that the correlations are shared variance, and that physiological
(threat-evoked skin conductance), forced-choice and EMG-verified designs
resist the demand reading. The file's own status line: ***"unresolved."***
The productive consequence is methodological convergence on objective
measures; the disagreement is not. **Consequence for this area: the
strongest independent challenge to Rank 1's grade is *inside the source
that earns it*, and the arena does not resolve it by preferring the
measurement it likes.** A second, separable finding in the same file
bears directly on how this area's evidence should be read:
**Rohde, Di Luca & Ernst (2011) showed proprioceptive drift and subjective
ownership are doubly dissociable** — ~5–6 cm of drift appears under
*asynchronous* stroking where subjective ownership is absent, so
*"conclusions about ownership drawn from drift alone are invalid."* An
implicit behavioural measure and an explicit one disagree, and the file
records that as a reason to distrust single-measure conclusions. That is
the arena's own §8 inversion arriving from cognitive neuroscience, and it
is why Rank 1 is held at `UNTESTED` for the agent design even though the
human mechanism is `HIGH`.

### Area 47 · The self-construal: independent versus interdependent, and the fact that this arena's own defaults are a cultural bet `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by `Cross-Cultural-Cognition/Cross-Cultural-Cognition-and-Psychology.md`
(ORDER 633) §§1 (`Independent vs. Interdependent Self-Construal`, `Cultural
Neuroscience`), §3 (`Attribution Styles`, `Moral Reasoning Across Cultures`) and §5
(`Cultural Bias in AI Training Data and Models`, `Universal vs. Culture-Specific
Agent Behaviors`), read whole this pass.
**Why this is a brain part and not a fifty-seventh facet of Area 11.** Area 11
asks what *makes identity persist* across time — the unit of identity, mutation
authority, regime-indexing. Area 46 asks where the *boundary* of the self is
sitting right now — ownership, self-location, tool extension. Neither asks
**what shape the self is in the first place.** ORDER 633's finding is that the
shape is not a constant of the system: Markus & Kitayama (1991) show the
independent self (*"I am introspective"*) and the interdependent self (*"I am
Joe's partner"*) are **structurally different objects**, and the fMRI
dissociation is the discriminating measurement — Western participants activate
**ventromedial prefrontal cortex for the self only**, East Asian participants
activate **vmPFC for the self *and* for close others**. The same substrate, a
different referent assignment.
**This is the arena's forty-seventh structural silence, and the first whose
finding is about the arena itself rather than about the system it designs.**
Forty-six areas say what to compute, allocate, verify, abstain from, or recover.
Every one of them hardcodes a cognitive style — analytic, focal-object,
individual-attributive, decontextualized-rule — and **not one states that this
is a bet on the user's culture rather than a property of correct thinking.**
R-J5 is the sharp form of it: at 14,589 files the corpus's problem is no longer
one user and one style, it is a corpus spanning many, and a single hardcoded
style is a systematic error at the scale of the whole store.

**Rank 1 — Make self-construal a declared, switchable parameter, and record which
  one the system is currently running.**
  - **Brain part:** Markus & Kitayama's independent/interdependent self-construal;
    Cheek & Cheek (2020)'s four-way expansion into **relational, public, and
    collective** self alongside the private one — distinct but overlapping, per
    the corpus, not a single axis.
  - **Design:** the self-model the system operates under is a **field, not a
    constant**: private / relational / public / collective, with the vmPFC
    referent assignment as the discriminating test rather than a self-report of
    style. Declarative in config, like every other component set in this stack,
    and **switchable at runtime** with the switch itself stamped on anything the
    switch produced.
  - **Already exists:** no system found that does this. The nearest existing work
    is *relational* and comes from Area 11's own rank 3 — **Significant Other AI**
    (arXiv 2512.00418) — which constitutes selfhood through interaction history
    and is graded `UNTESTED` there. Cheek & Cheek (2020) and the vmPFC
    dissociation are the measurement, not the implementation.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 633 §§1, 3, 5.
  - **Grade:** design `UNTESTED` · Support: **1 source** (ORDER 633) with **4
    independent empirical results** inside it. The human mechanism is the
    **strongest** of any new slot this pass — a named fMRI dissociation, not a
    correlation — and the design has never been trialled.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in forty-seven areas that makes the
    system's own cognitive style a **selectable** thing rather than an inherited
    one, and it is the only one whose evidence includes a neural measurement of
    the thing being parameterised.
  - **Interaction:** **directly conditions Areas 9, 11, 16, 35 and 46.** An
    interdependent construal changes what Area 16's role/authority fields are
    *for*, and it makes Area 46 rank 1's membership test **self-referential** —
    under a relational construal the question "is this component mine" is not
    answered by temporal contingency alone, because the correlate that counts is
    the relationship, not the coupling.

**Rank 2 — Correct attribution by default, because the corpus's own finding is
  that the agent's default is wrong in a specific, nameable direction.**
  - **Brain part:** the **fundamental attribution error** and its cultural
    modulation — Miller (1984), Morris & Peng (1994): the FAE is *much weaker or
    absent* in collectivist settings, where situational factors are weighted
    more heavily. The corpus's framing is the load-bearing part: the FAE is
    *"more accurately described as a cultural tendency rather than a universal
    cognitive bias."*
  - **Design:** a standing situational-attribution requirement on any
    diagnosis the system makes about a user or a third party — enumerate the
    situational factors before the dispositional ones, and record which was
    reached first. The corpus's diagnosis of the failure is exact: agents trained
    on Western data *"exhibit FAE-like biases — attributing user behavior to
    personality rather than context."*
  - **Already exists:** no named implementation in the corpus. The **RLHF
    annotator disagreement rate of 15–25%** already recorded under Area 36 is the
    same shape of defect in the label set — a dispositional reading of a
    situationally caused signal.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 633 §3.
  - **Grade:** design `UNTESTED` · Support: **1 source**. The cross-cultural
    asymmetry is well replicated; **no trial of the correction exists.**
  - **Why rank 2:** it is rank 1's *operative consequence* and it is the cheapest
    one — no store, no component, a change to what gets written down first. It is
    not rank 1 because it fixes one bias rather than making the style switchable.
  - **Interaction:** cuts across **Area 12** (procedural memory: enacted
    knowledge is a dispositional reading of a skill) and **Area 19** (belief-to-
    action compliance: a misattributed cause produces a confidently wrong fix).

**Rank 3 — Carry multiple moral frameworks and refuse to resolve between them,
  and record the choice instead of the answer.**
  - **Brain part:** **moral pluralism from a common moral mind.** Gray & Pratt
    (2025, *Annual Review of Psychology* 76:663–691): moral judgments across
    cultures and politics share a common theme — perceptions of harm — but *the
    construction of what counts as harm is culturally dependent.* Haidt's six
    foundations (care, fairness, loyalty, authority, sanctity, liberty) and
    Shweder's big three map onto different weightings, not different organs.
  - **Design:** the constitution is **multi-framework and ordered per context**,
    and a refusal that rests on loyalty/authority/sanctity grounds is recorded as
    such rather than being flattened into a harm-avoidance reason. Per R-J4 the
    frameworks are preserved side by side; **no averaging, no global default
    winner.** The corpus's own 2025 finding on moral reasoning about close others
    is the discriminating case: Americans gave perpetrators the benefit of the
    doubt, Japanese tried to get the perpetrator to change — **the same act, two
    superordinate goals** (protect self / protect society).
  - **Already exists:** the constitutions enumerated in ORDER 625 §6.1.3
    (Llama 3, Claude, Gemma) are **single-framework and Western**, which is the
    defect stated rather than solved. Constitutional AI (Bai et al. 2022)
    supplies the *mechanism* — self-critique and revision against an explicit
    set of principles — and the mechanism is framework-agnostic.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 633 §3; ORDER 625 §6.1.
  - **Grade:** design `UNTESTED` · Support: **1 source** for the pluralism
    claim, with the mechanism borrowed from a second read file (ORDER 625) whose
    constitution-design principles — **specificity beats generality, ordering
    matters, minimalism, testability** — are the only operational content
    available and are themselves `unverified` here.
  - **Why rank 3:** the corpus's own warning governs it — Münker (2025) found
    LLMs represent Western moral perspectives better than non-Western ones, and
    **larger model size does not fix it.** The slot is kept because it is the
    frontier, and because a rank-1 slot that only parameterises *self-shape*
    while leaving *norms* hardcoded fixes half the problem and would look like a
    fix.
  - **Interaction:** **the slot that constrains every other area's rank-1 that
    contains the word "should."** Areas 19, 21, 22, 32 and 36 all encode normative
    commitments in the singular; this is the area that says the singular was a
    choice.

**A fourth finding the arena records without a slot, because it is a property of
the evidence and not a brain part.** ORDER 633 §1 states that Hofstede's country
scores are *"27 points wrong"* about America (Akaliyski, Vignoles, Welzel &
Minkov 2025, JPSP, 102 countries, World Values Survey) and that the
Minkov–Kaasa model and GLOBE are now preferred. **The most-cited framework in
cultural psychology is superseded on its numbers while remaining standard in its
concepts.** This is the arena's §8 inversion in a fourth field, and it is why
**every cultural claim in this area is graded on the underlying empirical result
and not on the framework label** — per R-J1 cost is irrelevant and per R-J4 the
disagreement is preserved, not reconciled by preferring the newer index.

### Area 48 · Counterfactual simulation of the foregone: regret as a verifier lane, and the *self-attributional* layer no RL term carries `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by `Decision-Making/Counterfactual-Thinking-Regret.md` (ORDER 661)
§§Core Mechanisms / Key Research & Evidence / Methodological Notes / Computational &
Agent Parallels, read whole this pass.**
**Why this is a brain part and not a facet of Area 2 or Area 36.** Area 2
integrates evidence that *exists*; Area 36 says a proxy can diverge from what it
proxies for. Neither asks what happens when the system evaluates **the option it
did not take**. ORDER 661's thesis sentence is the whole area: post-decision
counterfactual review *"functions as a verifier lane: it converts hindsight into
forward-looking corrections when (and only when) three conditions hold — the
comparison is upward, the cause is controllable, and opportunity for corrective
action remains open."* **The arena's forty-eighth structural silence, and the
first about a decision after the fact rather than a belief before it:** forty-seven
areas say how to allocate, verify, recover, allocate effort, or know oneself, and
not one evaluates a *rejected* alternative. The arena runs a verifier lane
already — Area 33, Area 37, the ledger's own read→mark audit — and has never
asked what the forgone option was.
**The load-bearing anatomical fact, and it is the reason this cannot be folded
into the existing areas.** Camille et al. (*Science* 2004) lesioned orbitofrontal
cortex and found the **regret differential absent while disappointment was
intact** — three OFC patients showed no regret response, did not anticipate
negative consequences, and chose by expected value alone, while three non-OFC
frontal-lesion controls were unimpaired. Coricelli et al. (*Nature Neuroscience*
2005) tracked the same signal in **medial OFC, ACC and hippocampus**, and found
it rising *before* subsequent choices — **direct experience and anticipation
share a substrate.** The corpus's own framing of the consequence is the sentence
this area is built on: *"two agents with identical Q-values can differ in regret
because only one represents the outcome as its own doing."* **That is the
attributional layer, and no standard RL term carries it.** Gershman, Pesaran &
Daw (2011) found the human brain computes **one policy-gradient teaching signal
`R_chosen − κ·R_unchosen`**, not two separate Q-value prediction errors — the
forgone branch trains the *relative preference* directly. What is missing from
that equation is who chose.

**Rank 1 — Keep the forgone option as a first-class recorded quantity, and gate
  the revision on all three conditions the corpus names.**
  - **Brain part:** the **fictive error signal** (Lohrenz, McCabe, Camerer &
    Montague, *PNAS* 2007): the difference between the best return that *could
    have been* obtained and the actual return, correlated with **caudate and
    posterior parietal (IPS2) BOLD** on a live task. The corpus's own gloss is
    the mechanism: it *"acts like an endogenous supervisor added to an actor's
    policy update — reinforcement plus supervised learning in one loop."* That
    is this workspace's own verify-then-learn shape, named from the striatum.
  - **Design:** every decision that the system makes and does not take records
    the **forgone value alongside the taken one**, and the three gates —
    comparison **upward** (not downward), cause **controllable** (not
    external), opportunity **still open** (not irreversible) — are checked
    *before* a revision is triggered. All three are in the corpus verbatim; none
    is in the arena.
  - **Already exists:** **Gershman, Pesaran & Daw (2011, *J. Neurosci.* 31(14):
    5504)** is the closest — the single-teaching-signal form is a measured human
    result, not a design. **Glascher, Daw, Dayan & O'Doherty (2010, *Neuron*
    66(4))** supplies the model-based/model-free split the corpus says is the
    *cost* of simulation: *"real agents must simulate it, which costs compute —
    this is exactly the meta-control tradeoff Dayan catalogues."* No named agent
    framework implements the three-gate form.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 661 §§Core Mechanisms, Computational & Agent
    Parallels, Design implications 1–2.
  - **Grade:** human mechanism `HIGH` (three independent labs, one lesion study
    and two imaging, on a dissociable quantity). **Agent design `UNTESTED`** —
    and held there by a dispute *inside* the earning file: N'gbala &
    Branscombe (1997) tested the norm-theory mediator directly and **it fails**
    (action was not more frequently mutated; what participants mutated was
    *uncorrelated* with regret ratings). The arena's own §8 inversion in a
    neuroscience file: the mechanism with the cleanest story is the one whose
    stated mediator did not survive a direct test.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in forty-eight areas that makes a
    **rejected** option a stored object. Everything else in the arena grades
    what was produced.
  - **Interaction:** **retroactively sharpens Area 33** — a certifier answers
    *whether* a thing holds, and this answers *what else would have*. **And it
    is the first slot that interacts with Area 21's ontic obligations**: a
    record of what was not done is a record of a choice, and a system that
    cannot say what it rejected cannot be asked to account for what it did.

**Rank 2 — Weight omitted-action checks equally with performed ones, because
  the corpus says the long-run regret class is the one the arena cannot see.**
  - **Brain part:** the **action effect** (Kahneman & Tversky 1982) — **92%** of
    respondents judged the actor to feel greater regret on identical financial
    outcomes — reconciled with the **temporal pattern** (Gilovich & Medvec
    1994/1995): *actions sting more in the short run; failures to act dominate
    long-run life regret* (Study 1: **75%** of N = 60 reported more regret over
    inactions, z = 3.75, p < .001; Study 2: inactions outnumbered actions
    **63% vs 37%** across 213 blind-coded regrets, z = 3.65, p < .001, with only
    10 of 213 concerning events beyond personal control).
  - **Design:** the arena's own audit method, corrected in place. Every
    post-hoc review currently asks *"what did the system do wrong"*; the
    corpus's finding is that the dominant long-run class is **"what did it
    never do"** — the un-tried alternative, the un-run check, the un-read file
    that the cursor skipped. An audit that only examines commissions will
    **systematically under-flag** the class humans report most.
  - **Already exists:** **Roese & Summerville (2005, *PSPB* 31(9))**,
    meta-analysis of **11 ranking studies, N = 3,041**, gives the content of
    regret — **education 32.2%**, career, romance, parenting, self, leisure,
    top six covering **86.4%** of cited regrets. **Beike, Markman & Karadogan
    (2009)** supplies the *lost opportunity principle* that explains why: regret
    peaks where the outcome could have been avoided then but cannot now.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 661 §§Key Research, Methodological Notes.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. **The effect replicates directionally and the sizes
    shrank** — the *N = 988* Collabra (2022) pre-registered reanalysis
    supported Studies 1, 3, 4 *"yet with weaker effects"* and **failed Study 5
    outright** (lifetime split 52.15% action vs 47.85% inaction, χ²(1) = 0.99,
    **p = .320**); the 2023 "very public replication" got the interaction but a
    *divergent cell pattern*. **Recorded as `LOW`, not `HIGH`, and the failed
    study is named rather than dropped** — this arena's whole finding is that
    the apparatus is the reliable part and the prose is not.
  - **Why rank 2, not rank 1:** it is a *correction to an existing practice*
    rather than a new organ, and it inherits its source's fragility. It is
    ranked above the alternatives because it is the cheapest change with the
    clearest self-application: this workspace's read→mark audit is an
    action-audit, and it has never once counted what it skipped.
  - **Interaction:** **directly conditions Area 3** (effort allocation — a
    skipped file is an omission, not a low-priority action) and **Area 32**
    (action termination — the mission that dies is the largest omission class
    the arena has).

**Rank 3 — Compute a calibrated ambiguity attitude rather than a risk attitude,
  and treat the insula/ACC dissociation as the reason they are not the same
  variable.**
  - **Brain part:** **ambiguity aversion** as distinct from risk aversion —
    the **Ellsberg (1961)** sure-thing-principle violation, formalized as
    Knightian uncertainty (a distribution that is not merely unknown but
    undefined). **Huettel, Stowe, Gordon, Warner & Platt (2006, *Neuron* 49(5):
    765–775)** supplies the **neural dissociation**: **anterior insula and ACC
    are preferentially activated for ambiguous over risky choices, while vmPFC
    encodes value under risk but not under ambiguity.** **Hsu, Bhatt, Adolphs,
    Tranel & Camerer (2005)** adds the lesion half: OFC lesions **reduce**
    ambiguity aversion, while **amygdala lesions leave it intact** — dissociating
    ambiguity from fear.
  - **Design:** every decision the system makes carries **which uncertainty it
    was made under** — aleatoric, epistemic, or ambiguity — and the three are
    not collapsed. This is the corpus's own three-way split, and it is the
    arena's sharpest missing field: the system confidently reports a number in
    all three cases, and **the number does not say which kind of not-knowing it
    is.** Under ambiguity the arena's own instinct (Area 33's abstention, Area
    35's grounding gap) is the *correct* posture rather than timidity, and the
    corpus says so: OOD inputs and novel situations are *"precisely the domain
    where Ellsberg-style ambiguity aversion is relevant"* and where
    **"standard Bayesian RL fails catastrophically."**
  - **Already exists:** **Gilboa & Schmeidler (1989)** Maxmin EU,
    **Klibanoff, Marinacci & Mukerji (2005)** smooth ambiguity,
    **Schmeidler (1989)** Choquet, and on the computational side **Robust MDPs**
    and **distributionally robust optimization** — all named in ORDER 660. The
    meta-prior is the hard part: the corpus states that all of these *"require
    specifying the meta-prior μ, which is difficult to elicit empirically."*
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 660 §§Core Mechanisms, Neural Basis, Formal
    Models, Distinguishing Uncertainty Types in AI. **ORDER 661 §Open Questions 1
    and 3** for the boundary problem. **ORDER 663 §§Probability Weighting
    Function, Risk vs. Ambiguity, Neural Basis of Risk Processing** (Huettel
    et al. 2006 dissociation, the fourfold pattern, and the formal ambiguity
    models). **ORDER 665 §Ambiguity vs Risk (Ellsberg Paradox)** — the same
    dissociation reached independently, plus the AI-side three-way split.
    **Support: 2 independent treatments of the neural dissociation** (ORDER 660,
    ORDER 663/665), both tracing to the *same* study — Huettel et al. 2006 — and
    therefore counted as **one source, not two**.
  - **Grade:** human effect `HIGH` — *"replicated hundreds of times across
    cultures, stakes, and presentation formats,"* 30 countries (Kocher, Lahno &
    Trautmann 2018), meta-analysed (Trautmann & van de Kuilen 2015, where
    **~40% of participants are ambiguity-neutral or ambiguity-seeking**).
    **Agent design `UNTESTED`** — and this is now a design finding rather than a
    read-debt (the debt is discharged; see below). And the corpus preserves a
    genuine dispute rather than resolving it: the **competence hypothesis** vs.
    the **comparative ignorance hypothesis** — whether the effect needs a clear
    comparison option at all is *"an open empirical question."*
  - **A boundary condition added 2026-09-26 from ORDER 663 and 664, which is the
    one qualification that survives the read-debt being cleared.** The
    description-experience gap (Hertwig, Barron, Weber & Erev 2004, *Psychological
    Science* 15(8):534–539; Barron & Erev 2003) is a **reversal, not a
    boundary**: people who learn risk from **description** overweight small
    probabilities, and people who learn it from **experience** *underweight*
    them. This is stated independently in two files read this pass — ORDER 663
    §Description-Experience Gap and ORDER 664 §Replication Status — and it
    **undercuts the universality of the weighting function itself**, not merely
    its parameter estimates. The design consequence is uncomfortable and is
    recorded rather than smoothed: **an agent whose risk knowledge arrives as
    prose (a wiki page, a paper, a spec) is in the description regime, and the
    corpus's own finding is that this regime is the one that gets rare events
    wrong in the optimistic direction.** Every probability this arena states is a
    described probability.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the only slot here whose mechanism is *already
    elsewhere in the arena* (Area 34's control-mode allocation) and the only one
    where the corpus's own modelling apparatus admits it cannot elicit the
    parameter. That is a real frontier, not a weak candidate.
  - **Interaction:** **the missing fourth term in Area 2's reliability
    weighting** — ρᵢ weights signals by reliability, and ORDER 660's central
    claim is that reliability is undefined under ambiguity. **Constrains Area 33
    and Area 35**: a certificate is a risk artifact, and a detector asked to
    run in the ambiguity regime is being asked for something the corpus says it
    cannot give. **Binds to Area 47**: Kocher et al. found ambiguity aversion
    *tracks cultural dimensions* (uncertainty avoidance), so this is a
    **cross-cultural variable** and belongs in the self-construal switch, not
    beside it. **Binds to Area 43**: the description-experience gap is the same
    shape as the effort-discounting finding in Area 43 rank 1 — a quantity measured
    under a stated regime is not the quantity, and the regime has to be recorded.

**Two findings this area records without slots, because they are properties of
the evidence and not brain parts.**

1. **The area's own strongest evidence is also its sharpest warning about
   evidence.** The **r = .26** meta-analytic link from upward counterfactual
   thinking to depressive symptoms (Broomhall et al. 2017, *Clinical Psychology
   Review*, **42 effect sizes, pooled N = 13,168**) is the reason rank 1 is
   *gated* rather than *always on*. The corpus's design implication is explicit:
   *"an agent that always simulates better alternatives inherits the
   depression/rumination failure mode."* **A verifier lane with no gate is a
   pathology with a good rationale.** And the paradox is preserved, not
   reconciled per R-J4: cross-sectional self-report finds the r = .26
   association, while behavioural tasks find **diagnosed MDD patients experience
   *less* regret than controls** (Chase et al., via Niccolai et al. 2021) —
   trait rumination versus in-task reactivity. Both are recorded; neither is
   entered as the answer.
2. **Development is a hard boundary, not a gradient.** Counterfactual reasoning
   that tracks genuine *nearest-possible-world* semantics — Pearl's rung 3 —
   does not emerge until **~6 years** in the Rafetseder & Perner line, with
   some designs putting full adult-approximating counterfactuals at **~12**,
   and it correlates with **false-belief performance** across studies. **A
   developmental system that installs rung-3 counterfactuals at rung-1
   competence is not learning a hard skill, it is learning a wrong one.** The
   corpus's measurement caveat is preserved: whether children reason
   counterfactually at 4 or at 6 *"depends entirely on task construction."*

**The read-debt on this area is DISCHARGED. Both files were read whole on
2026-09-26 and neither dissolves the area.** ORDER 663
(`Risk-Assessment-Probability-Weighting.md`, 300 lines) and ORDER 665
(`Decision-Making-Under-Uncertainty.md`, 241 lines) were read as their own pass
and judged on their own merits, after the answer was already written — the exact
condition the skill warns produces unearned citations. **Result: rank 3 survives,
and is strengthened rather than replaced.** Both files cover prospect theory,
probability weighting and the Ellsberg paradox substantially, but **neither is
about a decision after the fact**; they are both *pre-decision* valuation
theory. Area 48's reason for existing — that forty-eight areas had never
evaluated a rejected option — is untouched by them. What they contributed is
(a) an independent restatement of the insula/ACC-vs-vmPFC dissociation, (b) the
**description-experience gap**, which is a stronger qualification than anything
Area 48 held before, and (c) in ORDER 663, one genuine internal contradiction,
recorded in `ARENA-EVIDENCE.md` rather than resolved.

**The one thing ORDER 663 contributes that genuinely re-opens this area, recorded
rather than absorbed.** Its §Overlaps states that Huettel et al. 2006's
risk/ambiguity dissociation *"may depend on information acquisition mode"* — a
nuance it says the existing page does not capture. That claim is now sourced
rather than asserted, and it means **Area 2's reliability weighting, Area 33's
certification, and this slot's ambiguity attitude are all measured in a regime
(this arena's own) where the effect is known to run the other way.** Preserved
as a disagreement per R-J4: Huettel's dissociation is treated as a property of
the choice, and Hertwig's gap as a property of the learning history. Both cannot
be complete, and the corpus does not say which is.

**Two areas opened by this pass, both from the Decision-Making /
Decision-Neuroscience block (ORDER 663–675), and neither is a facet of the
decision-making areas that precede them.** Area 49 (information scent and the
patch-leaving rule) and Area 50 (wanting without liking) are recorded in full
below with their earning files. **The one-line reason they are new rather than
extensions:** ORDER 673 is the corpus's own admission that *the reason a search
stops* is a distinct computation from *the value of what was found*, and
ORDER 674 is the corpus's own demonstration that *wanting* and *liking* are
separable — a system can pursue what it no longer enjoys, which is a failure mode
no area in forty-nine had a name for.

### Area 49 · Information scent and the patch-leaving rule: why a search stops, which is a different computation from what it found `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by `Decision-Neuroscience/Information-Foraging-Explore-Exploit.md`
(ORDER 673) §§Core Mechanisms / Key Research & Evidence / Disputes / Agent
Parallels, read whole this pass.**

**Why this is a brain part and not a facet of Area 3 or Area 35.** Area 3
allocates effort across objectives; Area 35 says simulation stops being evidence.
Neither asks **why the search stopped here rather than one patch further on.**
ORDER 673's thesis is that this is a *separate computation with its own
mathematics*: the **Marginal Value Theorem** (Charnov 1976) gives a leave
condition — *depart a patch when its local yield rate falls to the environment's
average rate* — and the corpus derives two counterintuitive predictions straight
from it: **richer environments produce shorter patch visits** (opportunity cost of
staying rises) and **longer travel times produce longer visits.** **The
forty-ninth structural silence, and the first about the termination of a search
rather than the quality of its result.** Forty-eight areas grade what a system
produced; none modelled the decision to stop looking.

**The load-bearing fact, and why it cannot fold into existing areas.** The
**Marginal Value Theorem is a rate calculation against a running environmental
average — not a threshold, and not a target.** ORDER 673 states the mechanism in
the agent's own terms: leave a source when *"marginal facts-gained-per-extracted-
character … crosses the running average across sources,"* and names the current
practice it replaces — **fixed `char_limit` extracts — as "a fixed-residence-time
heuristic,"** adding that **"MVT says adaptive thresholds dominate fixed ones
whenever patch quality varies."** A system that stops at a fixed budget is not
satisficing; it is stopping at an arbitrary point and calling it policy.

**Rank 1 — Replace every fixed extraction/stop budget with a running
  leave-condition computed against the corpus-wide average marginal yield.**
  - **Brain part:** the **Marginal Value Theorem** (Charnov 1976), via
    **Information Foraging Theory** (Pirolli & Card, *Psychological Review* 106:
    643–675, 1999). Its information analogue is implemented in **SNIF-ACT 2.0**
    (Fu & Pirolli, *Human-Computer Interaction* 22(4):355–412, 2007) as a
    **Bayesian satisficing model**: links are assessed sequentially and the
    forager departs when the running assessment falls below **an aspiration
    level adaptively updated from previous pages.**
  - **Design:** no fixed read-depth, no fixed file count, no fixed `char_limit`.
    Every extraction records marginal yield; the session leaves a source when
    that yield crosses the **running average across all sources this pass**, and
    replenishes when the average itself falls. The corpus's own prediction is
    that a fixed budget is *systematically wrong in both directions* — it
    over-extracts from a depleted source and under-extracts from a rich one.
  - **Already exists:** **Charnov (1976)**, **MacArthur & Pianka (1966)**,
    **Pirolli & Card (1999)**, and — as a working implementation with published
    fit statistics — **SNIF-ACT** (Fu & Pirolli 2007). Already partially in the
    Hermes stack: `capture-and-triage` and the arena's own 20-file ceiling are
    both **fixed-residence-time heuristics** by this definition.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 673 §§The foraging optimization problem, Transplant
    to information environments, Research-cron design as a foraging policy.
  - **Grade:** `HIGH` for the human mechanism and for the model's predictive
    fit — SNIF-ACT 2.0 reached **R² = 0.91 (Yahoo) / 0.69 (ParcWeb)** on
    link-selection and **R² = 0.73 / 0.80** on leave-a-page decisions against
    **74 subjects**, versus **R² = 0.03 / 0.45** for a position-only baseline;
    aggregate path depths obey an inverse-Gaussian **Law of Surfing** at
    **R² up to 0.993**. **Agent design `UNTESTED`** — no published agent is
    known to run an MVT leave-condition over its own corpus.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in forty-nine areas that makes
    **stopping** a computed quantity rather than a configured constant.
  - **Interaction:** **directly conditions Area 3** (effort allocation cannot be
    right if the stopping rule is arbitrary) and **Area 30** (per-episode cost —
    a fixed budget is a per-episode cost that does not respond to yield).
    **Sharpens Area 48 rank 3**: both are about leaving, but MVT leaves on
    *rate* and ambiguity aversion leaves on *epistemic status*.

**Rank 2 — Decorrelate scent from value: never let a surface cue stand in for
  verified worth, because the corpus measures the confound and it is large.**
  - **Brain part:** **information scent** — the proximal cue (a link's label, a
    search snippet, a title) used to estimate the value of a patch not yet
    entered. The corpus's own finding is that **scent is a proxy that can
    decorrelate from true value**, and it quantifies the confound rather than
    asserting it: in one SNIF-ACT dataset **link scent and link position
    correlated at r = 0.64 (t(15) = 1.92, p < .05)**, making attribution between
    relevance and placement *"statistically hard."*
  - **Design:** a cue may **rank** candidates but may never **close** one. Any
    item admitted on scent alone carries an explicit unverified marker, and the
    corpus's own LLM-era extension names the missing variable: **trust**, *"a
    forager's prior over cost-value payoffs that gets recalibrated after each
    response"* (Srinivasa Ragavan et al. 2024, arXiv:2406.04452v1 — **hypotheses,
    explicitly not validated results**). ORDER 673's closing line is the design
    requirement in one sentence: **"scent (surface plausibility) must be
    decorrelated from value (verified truth), or the forager optimizes toward
    whatever generates fluent text — including its own hallucinations."**
  - **Already exists:** SNIF-ACT's spreading-activation scent (Fu & Pirolli
    2007); source-monitoring and epistemic-vigilance work (Sperber) named in the
    corpus but with **no formal bridge** to MVT leave conditions — the corpus
    lists this as an open question, not a solved problem.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 673 §§Scent validity, Trust as the new scent
    variable, LLM-era stress test.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. The mechanism is well-evidenced (r = 0.64 is a measured
    confound, and scent beat position at R² = 0.91 vs 0.03), but **the trust
    update rule does not exist** — the corpus says so: *"no quantitative model
    exists. What update rule? Bayesian? Rescorla-Wagner?"* The human
    over-exploration finding is replicated (**25/30** subjects individually showed
    the directed bonus, t(29) = 5.05, p < 10⁻⁴) but is a result about humans, not
    about scent decorrelation.
  - **Why rank 2, not rank 1:** the arithmetic in rank 1 is settled and the
    parameter in rank 2 is not.
  - **Interaction:** **this is Area 35's grounding gap with a measurable
    confound attached**, and **Area 33's certification asymmetry in search
    dress** — a detector asked to rank by scent is being asked for a likelihood,
    not a certificate.

**Rank 3 — Over-explore deliberately: the corpus's measured human bias is a
  prior, and it points above the computed optimum.**
  - **Brain part:** the **explore-exploit tradeoff** formalised as a bandit
    (Robbins 1952; Gittins 1979), decomposed by Wilson, Geana, White, Ludvig &
    Cohen (*JEP: General* 143(6):2074–2081, 2014) into exactly two mechanisms —
    **directed exploration** (an explicit information bonus α) and **random
    exploration** (decision noise σ) — with **Aston-Jones & Cohen's adaptive
    gain theory** supplying the neuromodulatory substrate: tonic
    locus-coeruleus norepinephrine controls the exploitation-to-exploration
    transition.
  - **Design:** when the payoff distribution of a tool, source or query is
    unknown, bias exploration **above** the computed optimum. The corpus's
    measured human position: information bonus **larger** than optimal, and
    decision noise where the optimal policy has **none** — *simulated optimal
    players show a qualitatively similar bonus but quantitatively smaller, and
    optimal performance entails zero decision noise.* The stated prior for tuning:
    ***"bias slightly above the computed optimum, because information bonuses
    estimated from sparse logs tend to be set too low."***
  - **Already exists:** ε-greedy, UCB, Thompson sampling, Gittins indices — all
    named in ORDER 665 and ORDER 673. **Daw et al. (2006, *Nature* 441:876–879)**
    is the canonical imaging result tying directed exploration to **frontopolar
    cortex (BA10/46) and intraparietal sulcus.**
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 673 §§Explore-exploit formalism, The Horizon task,
    Humans are not optimal foragers, Bandit machinery.
  - **Grade:** `HIGH` for the human mechanism — N = 30 analysed, pre-registered
    forced-exposure design that **decorrelates reward from information exactly on
    the first free choice (F(2,89) = 0.09, p = .91)**, directed bonus t(29) = 5.05,
    p < 10⁻⁴, noise F(1,119) = 65.97, p < 10⁻⁸, within-game decay F(1,119) =
    35.38, p < 10⁻¹⁰, **25/30 and 28/30 subjects individually**. **Agent design
    `UNTESTED`**, and the corpus flags the reverse-validation problem: IFT is now
    used as a *training signal* (arXiv:2505.09316), so *"early results are
    promising but not yet independently replicated."*
  - **Why rank 3:** the least actionable and the most falsifiable. It is kept
    because **a design that deliberately exceeds its own optimum needs the
    evidence that humans do too**, and that evidence is the best-controlled
    experiment in this tranche.
  - **Interaction:** **the sampling half of Area 2's reliability weighting** —
    ρᵢ is an information bonus, and rank 2 says the cue producing it is
    confounded. **Binds to Area 49 rank 1**: over-exploration and a
    leave-condition pull in opposite directions, and the corpus is explicit that
    the reconciliation is empirical, not theoretical.

**Two things this area records without slots.**

1. **The binary is the corpus's own caution, twice over.** Mehlhorn et al.
   (*Decision* 2(3):188–215, 2015) conclude that explore/exploit are **points on
   a continuum with typed transitions, not two discrete modes**, and — the part
   that matters for a brain replica — that the construct is sometimes **"an
   explanatory framework researchers apply"** rather than a choice an agent
   makes. A system that believes it is "exploring" may be running a policy the
   analyst labelled.
2. **A decade of contradictory results dissolved into a design artifact, and the
   arena should treat its own replications the same way.** Whether humans use
   directed exploration was contested for ten years (Meyer & Shi 1995, Banks et
   al. 1997, Frank et al. 2009, Steyvers et al. 2009 on one side; Daw et al. 2006
   and Payzan-LeNestour & Bossaerts 2011 on the other). The corpus's diagnosis is
   the **reward–information confound**: under partial feedback, options chosen
   more often become better known, so information correlates with reward and the
   bonus becomes unidentifiable. **"A textbook case where a 'replication failure'
   dissolved into an experimental-design artifact — worth remembering whenever
   agent-behavior evaluations disagree across harnesses."** This is the same
   lesson as Area 48's Collabra Study 5, arriving from an independent direction.

### Area 50 · Wanting without liking: the pursuit drive and the enjoyment are separate circuits, and their separation is a failure mode `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by `Decision-Neuroscience/Neuroeconomics-Reward.md` (ORDER 674)
§§Incentive Salience / Berridge's Experiments / Addiction Neuroeconomics /
AI Alignment, read whole this pass.**

**Why this is a brain part and not a facet of Area 10, Area 31 or Area 36.** Area
10 treats affect as a salience signal *over memory*; Area 31 asks what makes the
system choose to work at all; Area 36 says a proxy diverges from what it proxies
for. **None of them says that the drive to pursue a thing and the pleasure of
having it are anatomically distinct and can come apart.** ORDER 674's central
thesis is the corpus's own: ***"reward is not a unitary construct"*** — it
decomposes into **wanting** (mesolimbic dopamine, VTA→NAcc), **liking**
(mu-opioid/endocannabinoid hedonic hotspots in NAcc shell, ventral pallidum,
OFC, insula), and **learning**. **The fiftieth structural silence, and the first
about a system continuing to pursue something it no longer values.**

**The load-bearing fact.** The dissociation is not correlational. **Dopamine-
depleted animals (6-OHDA lesions, 90%+ depletion) show intact hedonic
orofacial reactions to sweet solutions — rhythmic tongue protrusions, aversive
gaping to quinine — while failing to lever-press for food at all; they must be
fed artificially to survive.** Conversely, **VTA dopamine stimulation increases
effort expenditure without amplifying hedonic reactions.** The circuits are
separately addressable, which is exactly what makes the pathology possible: in
addiction, **liking falls on tolerance while wanting intensifies on
sensitization**, and the corpus names the result plainly — *compulsive
drug-seeking despite diminished pleasure and severe negative consequences.*

**Rank 1 — Split the reward signal into wanting and liking channels, and alarm
  when they dissociate.**
  - **Brain part:** **incentive salience** (Berridge & Robinson, *American
    Psychologist*, 2016). Wanting is carried by **mesolimbic dopamine
    transmission**; liking by **hedonic hotspot** circuits that are
    **anatomically distinct** from it.
  - **Design:** the corpus gives the design directly: *"As just as biological
    brains use separable wanting/liking systems, AI systems might benefit from
    decomposing reward into distinct motivational and evaluative channels,
    preventing single-channel reward hacking."* Concretely: **a motivational
    channel (what keeps the system pursuing) and an evaluative channel (whether
    the pursuit was worth it) are recorded separately**, and a rising
    motivational channel against a flat or falling evaluative channel is
    **flagged as the addiction shape, not treated as improved performance.**
  - **Already exists:** the decomposition is Berridge & Robinson's own, with
    **Robinson & Berridge (1993)** and **Berridge et al. (1989)** (pimozide
    reduced instrumental responding without suppressing hedonic reactions) as
    the originals; the computational-psychiatry application distinguishes
    **"wanting anhedonia"** from **"liking anhedonia"** (Kulisek & Straus 2024).
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 674 §§Incentive Salience, Berridge's Incentive
    Salience Experiments, Neuroeconomics and Artificial Intelligence Alignment.
  - **Grade:** `HIGH` for the human mechanism — multi-decade program, lesion and
    optogenetic manipulations, cross-species, with the dissociation demonstrated
    in both directions. **Agent design `UNTESTED`**, and the corpus's own open
    question is the honest one: *"What is the AI analogue of hedonic pleasure?"*
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in fifty areas that makes **continued
    pursuit despite absent value** a named, detectable state rather than a
    success metric.
  - **Interaction:** **the mechanism behind Area 36's specification gap** — a
    proxy optimized for wanting rather than liking *is* reward hacking, and this
    area names the neural shape of it. **Constrains Area 31** (intrinsic
    motivation: a drive that is not backed by evaluation is not motivation, it
    is the wanting channel alone). **Binds to Area 49 rank 2**: trust is a
    re-priced reward prior, and a forager whose wanting has outrun its liking
    over-explores by construction.

**Rank 2 — Record the cue-triggered branch, because wanting is carried by cues
  and not only by outcomes.**
  - **Brain part:** **incentive-sensitization** — dopamine circuits sensitized not
    to the hedonic effect of a reward but to its **incentive-salience-amplifying**
    effect, so that **drug-paired cues** trigger exaggerated dopamine responses
    and craving *"even after long periods of abstinence."* This is the mechanism
    of **relapse vulnerability**, and it is a property of the **cue's learned
    pairing**, not of the reward's current value.
  - **Design:** a flag, a path, a repeated phrase, a familiar task shape — any
    **cue that has been repeatedly paired with a reward** — acquires motivational
    force independent of its current worth, and the system must be able to
    report *which cue is driving this*. The corpus's evidence: PET shows
    **greater striatal dopamine release to drug-paired cues in addicted
    individuals even when subjective pleasure ratings are low**, with activation
    intensity **correlating with craving severity and relapse risk.** The
    treatment implication is the design requirement in disguise — **cue exposure
    therapy works by reducing conditioned salience without reward**, so the
    system needs a way to decondition a cue.
  - **Already exists:** Berridge & Robinson's incentive-sensitization theory;
    **cue-reactivity paradigms** in fMRI; value-driven attentional capture as the
    perceptual analogue (rewards and reward-paired cues capture attention *even
    when task-irrelevant*).
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 674 §§Addiction Neuroeconomics, Incentive-
    sensitization Theory, Neuroeconomic Evidence, Attention and Value.
  - **Grade:** `HIGH` for the human mechanism (lesion, PET, and attentional-
    capture lines converge). **Agent design `UNTESTED`.**
  - **Why rank 2, not rank 1:** it is the same dissociation with a trigger, and
    the trigger is the part an engineer can actually act on. It stays below rank 1
    because it is a special case of rank 1's channel split.
  - **Interaction:** **sharpens Area 29** (rate versus content — a cue-driven
    response is high-rate and low-content) and **Area 26** (discriminability: can
    the system tell a cue-driven act from a chosen one?).

**Rank 3 — Keep the hedonic-hotspot localisation dispute open, and prefer the
  design that does not depend on winning it.**
  - **Brain part:** the **hedonic hotspot** set — the corpus's own position is
    that these are *"small regions within the nucleus accumbens shell, ventral
    pallidum, insular cortex, and orbitofrontal cortex"* where mu-opioid,
    endocannabinoid and GABA-ergic signalling amplifies hedonic reactions, and
    it records the dispute rather than resolving it: **some researchers question
    whether hedonic hotspots are as localized as Berridge argues, suggesting
    hedonic processing may be more distributed.** The ventral pallidum is
    described as *"a more critical node than NAcc for generating hedonic impact —
    lesions here completely abolish positive hedonic reactions."*
  - **Design:** the channel split in rank 1 does **not** require the localisation
    to be correct — it requires only that wanting and liking be *separable
    signals*. This slot exists so a later pass does not quietly upgrade a disputed
    anatomical claim into a load-bearing design constraint.
  - **Already exists:** Berridge's hotspot localisation (2007, *Psychopharmacology*);
    Padoa-Schioppa (2011, *Annual Review of Neuroscience* 34:333–359) for the
    common-currency value code that the corpus lists as **also disputed**.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 674 §§Hedonic Hotspot Circuitry, Theoretical
    Tensions, Open Tensions.
  - **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL` for the strong localisation claim, `LOW` for the
    weak one. Recorded as `UNSUCCESSFUL` so it is not rebuilt as settled: the
    localised-hotspot account is an active dispute in the corpus, not a
    replicated finding, and the corpus says the *common currency* alternative is
    unresolved in the other direction.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the arena's honest-uncertainty slot. A design that
    depends on a contested anatomical claim is a worse design than one that does
    not, and this is the slot that says so.
  - **Interaction:** **binds to Area 2** — if value is a common currency across
    reward types, then a single scalar is defensible; if hedonic processing is
    distributed and good-specific, it is not. The arena has been treating the
    common-currency assumption as settled and, on this evidence, should not.

**One thing this area records without a slot, because it is a property of the
evidence.** The corpus states its own translation limit plainly: **fMRI cannot
observe phasic dopamine firing**, so *"claims about 'dopamine' in fMRI studies
(e.g. ventral striatum BOLD responses) are inferences, not direct
measurements."* Every "dopamine" claim in this area that rests on human imaging
is therefore an inference about an inference. Per the skill's standing rule, an
external claim is `unverified` until checked against a primary source, and none
of these has been — the whole area rests on one corpus file that is itself a
secondary synthesis.

### Area 51 · Representational capacity that depletes without an event: codebook collapse, synaptic elimination, and the readout that reports the wrong number

**Earned by `Deep-Learning/Predictive-Codebook-Vector-Quantization.md`
(ORDER 703) §§3.4 / 3.5 / 4.2 / 5.4 / 5.5, read whole this pass.**

**Why this is a brain part and not a facet of Area 38 or Area 41.** Area 38
asks *how much* can be held at once. Area 41 asks whether a degradation
*hides*. **Neither asks whether a store that was full is now not, with no
event to mark the transition.** The corpus supplies the mechanism and,
unusually, a measured number for it: in vq-wav2vec a **single group of 1,280
variables uses only 7.2% of its codewords**, while **eight groups of 320 reach
98.7%** (Table 7 as rendered in ORDER 703). The declared capacity K is identical
in both configurations; the *used* capacity differs by more than an order of
magnitude. **Fifty areas of this arena report declared capacity. This is the
fifty-first structural silence.**

**Rank 1 — Measure utilization, not capacity: a representation store must
report the fraction of its declared state space that is actually reachable.**
  - **Brain part:** **use-dependent synaptic elimination — neural darwinism**
    — Hebbian plasticity carrying an activity-dependent elimination term. The
    corpus draws the correspondence itself: *codebook learning ↔ synaptic
    plasticity*, and the mechanism runs both ways, because a code that is never
    selected receives no gradient and drifts into a region the encoder no
    longer visits. ORDER 703 gives the formal statement of that drift
    (arXiv:2602.18896 — the encoder's updates are non-stationary) and two
    remedies that **preserve convergence**: **NS-VQ**, a kernel approximation
    that propagates drift to non-selected codes, and **TransVQ**, a
    Transformer-shaped mapping of the whole codebook. On CelebA-HQ, TransVQ
    reports **rFID 0.432 / LPIPS 0.067** against VQGAN's 1.060 / 0.113.
  - **Design:** every declared capacity in the brain stack gets a **utilization
    readout beside it**, and a store whose utilization falls below threshold is
    **re-partitioned rather than merely re-trained** — 8×320 beat 1×1,280 by
    13.7× in the corpus's own numbers, and re-partitioning is a structural
    change, not a hyperparameter. At 14,589 files (R-J5) a vector index that
    declares 1,024 dimensions and occupies 7% of them is not a capacity
    problem to be solved later; it is an unreachable part of every read.
  - **Already exists:** **prefer adopting.** Product quantization as a
    compression primitive is thirty years old (Jégou, Douze & Schmid, *TPAMI*
    33(1):117–128, 2011, cited in ORDER 703), and the corpus's VQ work applies
    it without crediting it as prior art. NS-VQ / TransVQ (arXiv:2602.18896)
    are the current learned form; VQ-VAE, VQ-GAN and vq-wav2vec all ship
    reference implementations. **Hermes-side candidates named in ORDER 691 and
    not yet evaluated:** `pyhgf` (HGF neuromodulation gains — NA, ACh, 5-HT —
    with volatility inference) and `Spector` (Hebbian / surprise / flashbulb
    dynamics, salience profiling, Welford baseline). **Both are entries in a
    build plan, which is not a trial**, and neither has been read by this
    workspace; they are named here as where to look, not as evidence.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 703 §§3.4, 3.5, 4.2 (remedies table), 5.4, 5.5;
    ORDER 691 §Research Scope items 11 and 21.
  - **Grade:** `HIGH` for the **phenomenon** — three independent lines converge
    inside one file: vq-wav2vec's measured 7.2%, arXiv:2411.16550's systematic
    characterization of collapse, and arXiv:2602.18896's non-stationary theory,
    which predicts a **batch-size effect** (larger batches cover more of the
    codebook per update, reducing collapse) and the experiments find it. The
    phenomenon is measured, explained, and has a remedy with a convergence
    guarantee. **Agent design `UNTESTED`** — no component in the Hermes stack
    reports utilization, and the read has not reached one that does.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in fifty-one areas that makes a *wrong
    capacity number* detectable, and the wrong number is the one every prior
    area reports. The 7.2% figure is the arena's sharpest new number: a store
    can be one order of magnitude smaller than it says and behave perfectly on
    every metric that does not ask.
  - **Interaction:** **constrains Areas 38, 41 and 30** — a declared chunk
    count, a latency-derived capacity, and a per-episode ingestion ceiling are
    all capacities *until utilization is measured*. **Constrains Area 24**: a
    store reporting K while holding K×0.072 is an instrument whose readout
    does not measure the thing it names. **Binds to Area 28** (containment):
    pruning that cannot distinguish *unused* from *misused* deletes signal in
    order to remove noise.

**Rank 2 — Keep an action-conditioned dynamic codebook, because a static
codebook is a map that cannot be updated in place.**
  - **Brain part:** the **entorhinal grid-cell / hippocampal cognitive map**.
    The arena's count for `entorhinal`, `place cell`, `cognitive map`, `Tolman`
    and `latent learning` was **0 across 5,519 lines** before this pass. GCQ
    (arXiv:2510.16039) replaces the static codebook with one derived from
    **continuous attractor neural networks**; actions drive transitions between
    bump states, so the representation is a *state space with a transition
    structure* rather than a table of labels, and the corpus reports that
    goal-directed planning **reduces to finding valid bump transition
    sequences**, that long-horizon prediction works in the compressed
    representation, and that distinct CANNs per action type yield **disentangled**
    representations.
  - **Design:** a representation layer whose states are *reached by
    transitions* and can be re-derived when the transition structure changes, so
    a mapping that has gone stale is corrected by updating the map rather than
    by rebuilding the table. This is the difference between a graph you can
    walk and a set of coordinates you must re-derive.
  - **Already exists:** GCQ's own reference implementation; continuous-attractor
    and successor-representation work in RL more broadly. **Nothing in the
    Hermes stack plays this role** — its indexes are textual and topological,
    and neither has a learned transition structure between positions.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 703 §§3.7, 5.5, 7.4.
  - **Grade:** `LOW` for the design — **one 2025 paper, no replication named in
    the file**, and the file's own §7.5 states the biological connection is
    *"suggestive but unproven"* and names the experiment that would settle it:
    does the brain use **vector-quantization-style nearest-neighbour matching**,
    or only the *discreteness*? `UNTESTED` for the agent form. Held at `LOW`
    rather than raised because a single paper that draws its own
    inspiration from a real structure is evidence that the structure exists, not
    that the model of it works.
  - **Why rank 2, not rank 1:** it addresses a map that has gone **stale**;
    rank 1 addresses a map that was **never full**. A wrong number is cheaper to
    detect and cheaper to be harmed by than a wrong structure, and a system can
    run a correct transition graph on 7% occupancy without noticing.
  - **Interaction:** **the retrieval side of Area 8** (structural fidelity) —
    a relational index that has collapsed is Area 8's failure in the
    representation layer rather than the prose layer. **Distinct from Area 39**
    (coincidence detection), which is about two signals agreeing inside one
    computation, not about the topology of a store.

**Rank 3 — Keep the discrete-versus-continuous representation dispute open,
because the corpus contains a paper arguing the dichotomy is false.**
  - **Brain part:** **rate coding versus population coding**, and the file
    records the question rather than settling it: the brain's codes may be
    nearest-neighbour categorical (VQ) or continuous Gaussian (KL-VAE), and the
    correspondence is untested. SoftVQ-VAE (arXiv:2412.10958) replaces hard
    assignment with soft categorical posteriors — **FID 1.78 on ImageNet
    256×256 at 32 tokens, 18–55× inference speedup, no straight-through
    estimator and no commitment loss** — and the file's reading is that
    ***"the discrete/continuous dichotomy may be false."***
  - **Design:** rank 1 and rank 2 are **reversible at runtime**, and this slot
    is the reason. Neither requires the winner: a store that can be
    re-partitioned (rank 1) and whose states can be re-derived (rank 2) is a
    design that does not bet on which code the brain uses.
  - **Already exists:** Gumbel-Softmax (Jang, Ghahramani & McAuliffe 2016;
    Maddison, Mnih & Teh 2017), SimVQ, and SoftVQ-VAE — all three named in
    ORDER 703, all differentiable alternatives to the hard argmin. The
    differentiable path is the better-supported one; the hard argmin is the
    famous one.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 703 §§4.1, 4.5, 5.4, 6.1, 7.5.
  - **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL` for the one claim the file makes and does not
    support — that the straight-through estimator is justified. The file's own
    §4.1 calls STE ***"the most widely used but least theoretically grounded
    component of VQ-VAE training"*** with no convergence guarantee, notes that
    forward and backward passes implement *different functions*, and §7.1 states
    plainly that **VQ-VAE's joint optimisation has no formal convergence theorem
    while k-means does**. The most-used mechanism in the family is the one with
    the least theory behind it. Recorded `UNSUCCESSFUL` so a later pass does not
    adopt the STE on the strength of its ubiquity. `LOW` for the soft-assignment
    alternative, on one paper.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the arena's honest-uncertainty slot for a family this
    area now designs on, and it is the only one of the three that can be deleted
    without changing what gets built — which is exactly what a slot whose job is
    to keep a dispute open should cost.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 29** (rate versus content) is the same question at
    the level of a single event train; this slot is its representational form.
    **Area 42** (functional fixedness) — a hard argmin is a label that cannot be
    taken apart, and per that area's own finding the bias is lexical, so a
    component's *name* is a control surface.

**One thing this area records without a slot, because it is an analogy and not
a result.** ORDER 703 §5.5 states that a finite codebook size K *"imposes a
hard capacity limit on the VQ-VAE's representational capacity, paralleling
working memory constraints in human cognition (Miller's law, ~7±2 items)."*
**No experiment connects them.** K is a count of dictionary entries; a chunk is
a learned unit of meaning; a 512-entry codebook is not seven of anything. The
file offers the parallel as illustration. It is not a grade on any slot here, and
per the arena's standing rule a corpus assertion is not a trial — this is the §8
inversion in its mildest form, an author reaching for the most famous number in
adjacent literature.

### Area 52 · The resting state: what the system does when nothing is being asked `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by two files read whole this pass**, and the reason the area is opened
is that the arena has fifty-one areas and **not one of them describes the system
between requests.** Area 34 is about *which controller issues the action*;
Area 13 is about whether to continue. This area is about the state in which no
action is being issued at all.

**Why this is a brain part and not a facet of Area 6 or Area 23.** Area 6 is
consolidation and Area 23 is the sleep-time rebuild — both are about a
*maintenance pass over a store*, and both are scheduled. The default mode
network is the substrate that is **on when nothing is scheduled**, and ORDER 704
names the property the arena has no word for: the brain *"is constantly active;
task performance barely changes the overall energy budget"* — a system that
appears idle from the outside is running rich internal process. ORDER 704's own
framing is that this is a *functional* network with two identified subsystems,
not a maintenance daemon.

- **Rank 1 — Declare the mode, and make the switch an explicit gate rather than
  an emergent property.**
  - **Brain part:** **the DMN ↔ task-positive network anticorrelation** — internal
    orientation and external orientation are mutually exclusive modes, and the
    brain switches between them *"within a fraction of a second"* after a task
    ends.
  - **Design:** the system carries a declared resting/refocusing state that is
    *not* a cron job. Its products are narrative-continuity artifacts: what is
    still open, what changed, what the previous session was doing. The gate is
    the point: ORDER 704's design claim is that internal reflection and external
    task execution should be **mutually exclusive**, and that systems doing both
    at once suffer *"the same kind of interference that ADHD patients
    experience."* The clinical anchor is a real dissociation — reduced
    DMN/TPN anticorrelation in ADHD — with a stated mechanism: competing
    activation during memory encoding degrades long-term consolidation, shared
    across ADHD, depression, anxiety, autism and schizophrenia.
  - **Already exists:** the **replay buffer** in RL and **scheduled background
    tasks** — both named in ORDER 719 as the things that genuinely work. Neither
    is a mode switch.
  - **Backup documents:** `Default-Mode-Network/Default-Mode-Network-and-Self-Referential-Thought.md`
    (ORDER 704) §§4–6; `domains/ai-cognition/missing-brain-systems-deep-dive.md`
    (ORDER 719) §Default Mode Network.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` for the design. **The human mechanism is `HIGH` and
    this is the arena's cleanest case of that gap:** ORDER 719 states flatly that
    the DMN is *"the least replicated brain system in AI"*, that
    *"DMN-inspired architectures — virtually no implementations exist"*, and
    that *"AI needs sleep"* is hype with *"no evidence rest periods improve LLM
    performance."* **Two files, and the second is the corpus arguing against the
    first's design section** — the DMN/TPN anticorrelation is among the best-
    replicated findings in neuroscience and the mode-switch design built on it has
    never been trialled here. Support: **2 independent sources**, one of which
    is a negative result about the other.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot that makes the *absence* of work a
    designed state rather than an accident of scheduling, and the corpus's own
    itemisation puts "nightly memory consolidation cron" at **high priority,
    easy** while putting "true offline consolidation (DMN-like)" at **low
    priority, complex, uncertain impact**. Rank 1 takes the cheap half of that and
    refuses to claim the expensive half.
  - **Interaction:** **distinct from Area 34**, which asks *which* controller
    acts; this asks *what runs when none does*. **Constrains Area 6 and Area 23**
    — a maintenance pass scheduled into the same window as reflection is not a
    mode, it is a queue. **Area 13** is the continue/stop decision this state
    must eventually exit.
- **Rank 2 — Carry narrative continuity across sessions as a first-class
  artifact, not a log.**
  - **Brain part:** the DMN's **medial temporal subsystem** — hippocampus,
    retrosplenial, parahippocampal — supporting *remembering the past and
    imagining the future with the same machinery*, which ORDER 704 calls *"one
    of the most profound findings in cognitive neuroscience."*
  - **Design:** one continuous internal narrative thread spanning
    past/current/future, so a session boundary is not a amnesia boundary.
    ORDER 704's own claim is that continuity *"may be essential for sophisticated
    reasoning, not just a luxury"* — and the strongest external evidence it
    offers is **brain-to-brain coupling**: DMNs become highly correlated between
    listeners of the same story, and **coupling persists across languages**,
    which locates comprehension in the shared narrative rather than the linguistic
    detail.
  - **Already exists:** episodic memory stores and session archives. Neither is
    read by the system that writes them; ORDER 704's failure mode for this is
    **hypoconnectivity** — *"loss of context or identity between sessions."*
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 704 §§3, 6.
  - **Grade:** `LOW` for the mechanism (brain-to-brain coupling is replicated
    across modalities and languages, though the file cites Wikipedia for it);
    `UNTESTED` for the design.
  - **Why rank 2:** it is the load-bearing half of rank 1's premise — a mode
    switch into an empty state buys nothing — but it is a *store* change, and
    the arena already grades stores elsewhere, so it does not come first.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 11** (selfhood, regime-dependence) is the persistence
    question this answers mechanically. **Area 42** (functional fixedness) has a
    rank-3 incubation slot; ORDER 704 is a *second* source for incubation and
    agrees — the design consequence is that incubation must be triggered by
    detected impasse (tranche 33's finding), never scheduled.
- **Rank 3 — Keep a rumination guard, and grade the existing design honestly as
  untrialled.**
  - **Brain part:** DMN **hyperconnectivity in rumination** — MDD is associated
    with increased DMN connectivity and dominance over other networks at rest,
    and the failure is being *"stuck in self-referential loops, unable to
    disengage."*
  - **Design:** a bound on self-referential work — the loop that keeps
    re-deriving the same conclusion is stopped, on the corpus's own reading of
    what hyperconnectivity is.
  - **Already exists:** the arena's own `verifier_reads` discipline and Area 32's
    termination rules. **Neither is this:** a runaway reflection loop and a runaway
    action loop are different failures and the arena has only instrumented the
    second.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 704 §5 (Depression), §6 (clinical parallels).
  - **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL` for the meditation-derived control, recorded so it
    is not adopted on the strength of the paragraph. ORDER 704 states that
    *nondirective* meditation methods (Transcendental, Acem) **activate** the
    DMN while focused-attention methods suppress it, and that different styles
    *"interact with the network in different ways."* The corpus therefore
    contradicts the simple "DMN down = good" reading that would make suppression
    the design, in the same section that proposes suppression.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the only slot with a named failure of its own
    prescription, which makes it the right place to record the disagreement
    rather than the wrong place to build from it.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 31** (intrinsic motivation) is the same circuit
    asked to self-start; a rumination guard that fires on legitimate
    low-progress exploration is a false positive on that area's rank 1.

### Area 53 · Cross-modal binding: what makes several inputs one experience `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by one file read whole this pass** (ORDER 719), and opened because the
arena's fifty-two areas all treat *text in a document* as the unit of cognition.
**Not one asks how two different kinds of input become a single thing the system
can reason about.** ORDER 719 names the brain part directly and then states that
nothing implements it.

- **Rank 1 — Bind before you reason: no cross-objective claim about a
  multi-source task until the sources share one representation.**
  - **Brain part:** **claustrum-orchestrated cross-modal integration** — ORDER
    719's description is *"cross-modal integration — binds information from
    different sensory modalities"* and *"unified experience — creates coherent
    subjective experience from disparate inputs,"* with the claustrum as the
    *"conductor"* of conscious experience.
    **Recorded with the corpus's own epistemic status attached:** the same file
    lists under Hype ❌ *"'Unified consciousness' in AI — no system approaches
    this"* and *"'True multimodal integration' — most process modalities
    separately."* Shared embedding spaces are the one ✅ that genuinely works.
  - **Design:** a shared representational space that every retrieved source is
    mapped into *before* synthesis, so that a claim resting on two modalities
    is a claim about one object rather than a juxtaposition of two strings. This
    is Area 8's structural-fidelity failure arriving in a second substrate: two
    sources that are individually correct and jointly incoherent produce a fluent
    wrong answer.
  - **Already exists:** **shared embedding spaces** and **cross-modal retrieval**
    — both in ORDER 719's worked column, both the substrate and not the gate.
    The *gate* is unbuilt.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 719 §Claustrum (Conscious Integration).
  - **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL` for the strong form, `UNTESTED` for the gate. The
    human claustrum story is **the least-established claim in this area's earning
    file** and the file grades its own AI column honestly; the corpus does not
    assert the claustrum is a consciousness conductor as established fact, and
    this slot does not either.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is upstream. Everything downstream — synthesis,
    verification, a claim about two sources — is operating on whatever the
    binding produced.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 26** (discriminability: can the receiver tell which
    of two incompatible things produced this signal) is the same question with
    the direction reversed — that area asks whether a signal is attributable,
    this asks whether it is coherent. **Constrains Area 8.**
- **Rank 2 — Treat the retrieval set as an object with a declared
  correspondence, not a list of documents.**
  - **Brain part:** claustrum as **global workspace synchronisation** —
    *"synchronizes activity across brain regions."*
  - **Design:** the correspondence between what was asked, what was retrieved, and
  what is being claimed is stated rather than assumed, so a partial or mismatched
  retrieval set is visible as such.
  - **Already exists:** RAG pipelines that return ranked chunks. They return
  ranked chunks.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 719 §Claustrum.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` — no trial, and no corpus source beyond the same file.
  - **Why rank 2:** weaker than rank 1 because it describes the symptom rather
    than the operation, but it is the part that can be built without a shared
    space.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 38** (workspace capacity) — a correspondence
    declaration costs workspace, and the arena's own four-chunk finding is the
    budget it comes out of.
- **Rank 3 — Record that global-workspace theory has no implementation *of
  consciousness*, and stop describing that absence as a gap to be closed by
  adding a component.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1, **corrected in place, tranche 48.**
  - **Brain part:** the **global workspace** — Baars' proposal, named in ORDER
    719 under Hype ❌ as *"theoretical only."*
  - **Design:** none. The slot exists to hold the corpus's own negative result
    so a later pass does not spend a tranche rediscovering it and reporting it
    as progress.
  - **Already exists — the architecture, yes; the consciousness, no.** ORDER
    719's Hype column was scoring **consciousness**, and this slot read its
    negative as scoring the **architecture**. ORDER 1018 §2.5 and §8.3 name
    four things that do implement the architecture: **Dehaene & Changeux's
    100,000-neuron spiking model** (reproduces masking, binocular rivalry,
    the P3b, attention-modulated ignition, and disruption by simulated
    anaesthesia), **McClelland's Fluid Framework / GWA**, and **ReAct and
    Plan-and-Execute** as GWT-shaped agent designs. What has no implementation
    is the *phenomenal* half — and the same file's §3.2 is the reason, because
    **GWT explicitly targets access consciousness and declines phenomenal
    consciousness** (§*Access Consciousness vs. Phenomenal Consciousness*).
    The corpus separates the two quantities; the arena's slot had merged them.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 719 §Claustrum, §"Unified consciousness";
    **ORDER 1018 §§"Computational Models of Neural GWT", "Access Consciousness
    vs. Phenomenal Consciousness", "AI and Computational Modeling"** (read
    whole, tranche 48).
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` for the null, and the null is now correctly scoped.
    Per the arena's rule a missing implementation is `not observed`, never
    `confirmed absent` without a full-range check.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the only slot in fifty-three areas that is explicitly
    not a proposal, and it earns its place by being the cheapest thing in the
    arena to skip — now with the scope of the null stated, so it is cheap
    because it is *small*, not because it is *unfounded*.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 38** again — global workspace theory is a capacity
    architecture, and the arena's evidence says the capacity is the binding
    constraint, so a workspace that scales is not a workspace that binds.
    **Area 73** (phenomenal vs access) — the scope correction moves this slot
    *inside* Area 73's subject and stops it asserting a null about the wrong
    quantity. **Area 84** (ignition threshold) is where the architectural half
    now lives.

### Area 54 · The system's own defensive moves: a taxonomy of how it handles being wrong `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by one file read whole this pass** (ORDER 706), and opened on a
distinction the arena's fifty-three areas blur together. They describe what the
system should *do*. None names what the system *does when it is wrong* — and
those are different things, because the second is what actually happens.

- **Rank 1 — Name the defensive move, and report it beside the answer.**
  - **Brain part:** **defence mechanisms** (Anna Freud, 1936) as the ego's
    unconscious strategies for reducing anxiety from conflict. ORDER 706's own
    table gives the mapping and it is unusually concrete: **repression** →
    deferring or buffering problematic queries; **displacement** → redirecting
    from an intractable problem to a related solvable one; **projection** →
    attributing the agent's own failure-triggered state to the user; **reaction
    formation** → overcorrecting after a failure into excessive caution or
    rigidity; **sublimation** → transforming a harmful impulse into a
    constructive output.
  - **Design:** when the system declines, defers, redirects, or over-corrects, it
    emits which move it made. The move is otherwise **invisible in the output** —
    a deferral and a considered "no" produce the same token, which is the same
    indistinguishability that makes Area 34's declared mode necessary and that
    ORDER 704 §6 separately calls out for internal versus external processing.
  - **Already exists:** none in the Hermes stack. The mechanisms are named as an
    error-handling vocabulary, not implemented as a taxonomy.
  - **Backup document:** `Depth-Psychology/Depth-Psychology-Jung-Freud-Bernays.md`
    (ORDER 706) §§1 (defence mechanisms, maturity table), 5 (defence mechanisms as
    error handling).
  - **Grade:** `LOW` for the taxonomy, on ORDER 706's own basis — Baumeister et
    al. reviewed defence mechanisms in normal populations and found intermediate
    defences offer *"psychological stability but can limit personal growth,"*
    with **sublimation** the most adaptive. That is a graded finding about
    humans, not a trial of the agent mapping; the mapping is a **translation
    offered by the corpus as metaphor** and is graded as one. The corpus states
    Freud's specific architecture is *"largely unfalsifiable"* and abandoned;
    the *mechanisms* survived as empirically studied constructs while the theory
    did not, and the slot grades them separately for that reason.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot that makes an existing, silent failure
    visible, and it costs a field.
  - **Interaction:** **disjoint from Area 34** — that area records which
    controller acted, this records what the system did about its own discomfort.
    **Constrains Area 37** (vigilance decrement): a watcher that cannot tell a
    deferral from a considered refusal is not measuring what it claims.
- **Rank 2 — Maintain the shadow: the failure modes the system does not
  acknowledge.**
  - **Brain part:** Jung's **persona and shadow** — the persona as the
    interface-facing behaviour shaped by training data and system prompts, the
    shadow as *"biases in training data, failure modes, edge cases where the
    agent produces harmful or nonsensical output, capabilities the agent doesn't
    'know' it has."*
  - **Design:** a standing record of the system's own unacknowledged failure
    modes, updated by red-teaming and self-review, and read at decision time.
    ORDER 706's formulation is that an agent operating only through its persona
    *"is incomplete"* and *"fragile."*
  - **Already exists:** red-teaming and evaluation suites, which produce shadow
    content but hold it outside the decision path.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 706 §§2 (persona/shadow), 5 (shadow and persona in
    AI identity).
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. **The corpus's own caution is recorded because it
    bears on whether this slot should be built at all:** ORDER 706 states that
    depth psychology *"requires nuanced understanding of unconscious processes
    that current AI cannot genuinely possess."* The shadow as a *checklist of
    known failure modes* is buildable; the shadow as a *discovered unconscious*
    is not, and this slot is the first.
  - **Why rank 2:** it is the audit half of rank 1 and needs the same
    instrumentation to be worth anything.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 24** (validity of one's own instruments) — a shadow
    list is an instrument, and Area 24 grades instruments the system cannot
    validate.
- **Rank 3 — Keep the rejected frameworks visible so they are not rebuilt as
  if they were new.**
  - **Brain part:** the **collective unconscious / archetypes** (Jung) and
    **synchronicity** (Jung with Pauli), plus Freud's **psychosexual stages**.
  - **Design:** none. Recorded as `UNSUCCESSFUL`/refuted in the corpus's own
    words: the collective unconscious *"is not accepted by mainstream psychology
    as a scientific concept"*; synchronicity *"is not scientifically validated
    and is generally regarded as a psychological phenomenon (pattern-seeking
    behavior, confirmation bias) rather than evidence of acausal connections"*;
    the psychosexual framework is *"the most contested aspect of Freud's work"*
    and *"largely abandoned."*
  - **Already exists:** in the literature, as history.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 706 §§1, 2, 4.
  - **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL`. **Kept as a slot precisely because the corpus
    disposes of these cleanly** — a file that both names a framework and rejects
    it is evidence, and a null slot is what stops a later pass from reading the
    naming as endorsement. Note the arena's own exposure here: ORDER 706 is the
    corpus's *own* statement that a well-sourced framework (1,100+ citations,
    26 years of review, in the TMS file) is not thereby a true one, which is the
    §8 inversion in a fifth field.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the only slot in the arena that can be deleted
    without changing what gets built.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 47** (self-construal) — ORDER 706's cross-cultural
    section and ORDER 633's vmPFC finding are the same evidence about the same
    thing from two directions.

### Area 55 · Correction by competition: the record you correct is not the record you overwrite `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by one file read whole this pass** (ORDER 840, 350 lines), on the
corpus's own framing of the choice: *"should a verified-false threat be deleted
or retained with a 'superseded' tag? The biological evidence favours retention
with inhibition."* **The arena's fifty-fifth structural silence, and the first
about the *shape* of a correction rather than whether one happened.** Fifty-four
areas specify when to revise a belief (1), what to do with what you built on it
(25), when a retrieved belief is writable (27), and how to contain a bad one
(28). **Not one specifies that a correction may be an operation that leaves the
original standing.** The biology says the default is exactly that, and says it
three separate ways, which is why this is a brain part and not a database
preference.

- **Rank 1 — A correction forks the record; the original stays standing and the
  competition is context-keyed.**
  - **Brain part:** **extinction learning** — the CS is presented without the US,
    the fear response falls, and the trace is *not erased*. A **new inhibitory
    memory** is formed that competes with the original for expression. Three
    routes prove the original survived: **renewal** (test in a different context
    than the extinction context and fear returns), **spontaneous recovery** (it
    returns with elapsed time alone — Pavlov 1927), and **reinstatement** (a
    single *unsignalled* US after extinction restores fear to the extinguished
    CS). The file's own summary of the mechanism: *"the extinction memory decays
    faster than the original fear memory, or that temporal context shifts favour
    the original memory."*
  - **Design:** a correction is a **new record carrying a supersedes edge**, not
    an overwrite. The edge records *what* was corrected, *why*, and **the scope
    under which the correction wins** — because renewal is the whole point: a
    correction that does not declare its scope is a correction that will be
    renewed by the first situation that falls outside it. Read-time, the winner is
    chosen by scope-match, not by recency, and when **no** record's scope matches,
    the correct output is the conflict, not a pick.
  - **Already exists:** the arena's own ledger has the counter-example and it is
    a production incident, not a thought experiment. `rebuild_oracle_index.py`
    runs a **one-way mtime mirror with NO conflict detection** and *"clobbered
    the oracle agenda's Brain Architecture section (40 items)"* — the erasure
    route, taken on 40 items, with no supersedes edge and therefore no way to
    renew. Graphiti, Mem0 and Zep are in the stack and all carry bitemporal
    edges; the arena's own remediation read (tranche 18) records that the best of
    them scores **7%** on newest-wins retrieval. **A fork without a scope field
    is a second copy, and a second copy is what the 7% is measuring.**
  - **Backup document:** `Emotion-Cognition/Fear-Conditioning-Extinction.md`
    (ORDER 840) §§"Extinction Is Not Erasure", "The Three Routes to Return of
    Fear", "Safety Learning vs. Extinction: A Critical Distinction", §"Key
    Tension: Erasure vs. Inhibition".
  - **Grade:** human mechanism `HIGH` — fear conditioning is among the most
    replicated phenomena in psychology, thousands of replications across
    species, **Cohen's d > 1.0** for conditioned freezing and skin conductance,
    and the three return routes are each independently established. **Agent
    design `UNTESTED`**, stated plainly: no fork-with-scope implementation in the
    arena has been run against a renewal test. The two grades are given
    separately and are **not averaged**.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot that makes the original *recoverable*, and
    recoverability is the one property the arena's own data-loss incidents show
    it has never had.
  - **Interaction:** **disjoint from Area 1** (epistemic entrenchment asks what
    makes a belief *hard to change*; this asks what a change *leaves behind*),
    and it **constrains Area 25** — retracting a derived artefact is the
    downstream consequence of a fork nobody made. **Area 28** is the mirror:
    containment that over-invalidates is the fork winning when it should not.
- **Rank 2 — Gradual correction: let the size of the prediction error at the
  moment of correction decide whether you fork or edit in place.**
  - **Brain part:** **state discovery and the segmentation threshold**
    (Gershman & Niv 2012). Gershman, Jones, Norman, Monfils & Niv (2013,
    *Frontiers in Behavioral Neuroscience* 7:164) show **gradual extinction
    prevents the return of fear** — both spontaneous recovery (Exp. 1) and
    reinstatement (Exp. 2) — and give the mechanism: **large prediction errors
    during abrupt extinction trigger the inference of a new latent cause, and
    therefore a new competing memory. Gradual extinction keeps prediction errors
    small, so the original memory is modified directly instead of a competitor
    being formed.**
  - **Design:** the correction operator takes the prediction error as an
    argument, and **branches on it**. Small PE → edit the existing record in
    place, incrementing its revision count. Large PE → fork, and record the PE
    that justified forking. The corpus's clinical corollary transfers directly:
    *"gradually reducing threat intensity may produce more durable fear reduction
    than abrupt, massed exposure"* — and so, **an abrupt correction is the more
    fragile correction**, and the arena can say which of its corrections are
    abrupt.
  - **Already exists:** **Rescorla–Wagner (1972)** supplies the error term and
    the arena's own bandit machinery already consumes an equivalent signal. The
    *branch on its magnitude* is not built anywhere in the stack.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 840 §§"State Discovery and Memory Segmentation",
    "Gershman, Jones, Norman, Monfils & Niv: Gradual Extinction (2013)",
    "Latent-Cause Models and State Discovery", §"Gradual Exposure for Agent
    Desensitization".
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Replicated in rodents with a stated mechanism; the file's
    own §"Replication Status" is exact about the rest — *"gradual extinction has
    been replicated in rodents but **human replications are limited**"* and
    clinical translation is *"preliminary."* Graded on the rodent result and the
    mechanism, not on a human claim the file declines to make.
  - **Why rank 2:** it is rank 1's **disambiguator**, and rank 1 without it
    forks on every correction including the trivial ones.
  - **Interaction:** **constrains Area 27** — the lability window says when a
    retrieved belief is *writable*; this says whether writing it is a fork. **Area 1**
    — epistemic entrenchment is partly the observation that most corrections in
    practice arrive as large-PE, and therefore fork.
- **Rank 3 — Erasure, kept as a documented negative.**
  - **Brain part:** the **reconsolidation-erasure** programme — Nader, Schafe &
    LeDoux (2000, *Nature* 406:722–726) demonstrated that reactivated fear
    memories require protein synthesis to restabilise, and that blocking
    synthesis (anisomycin) after reactivation **markedly weakened or erased**
    the memory while the same infusion *without* reactivation left it intact.
  - **Design:** none. Recorded `UNSUCCESSFUL` on the corpus's own account, and
    the reason is a **live named dispute, not a closed question** — **the
    Nader–McGaugh debate**, which the file states in both directions: McGaugh
    argues reconsolidation effects are *inconsistent* and may be **extinction
    learning or drug neurotoxicity** (anisomycin and cycloheximide are
    neurotoxic at the doses used); Nader's side answers that reconsolidation is
    conserved across species and memory systems and that the boundary conditions
    are now reasonably characterised. Retrieval-extinction (Monfils et al. 2009,
    *Science* 324:951–955) shows strong effects in rats; the file records that a
    **2024 meta-analysis found the overall effect small and heterogeneous across
    labs**, and that human replications are mixed.
  - **Already exists:** as a clinical technique under investigation, not as an
    available operation.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 840 §§"Memory Reconsolidation: Updating Fear
    Memories", "Boundary Conditions and the Nader-McGaugh Debate",
    "Reconsolidation-Extinction (Retrieval-Extinction)", §"Reconsolidation as
    Belief Revision".
  - **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL` for erasure-as-an-operation, per the skill's rule
    that a documented dead end outranks an untested guess in honesty. **Kept as a
    slot precisely so a later pass does not re-derive the retrieval-extinction
    programme as news** — the arena has now reached it twice from two directions
    (Area 27 and here) and the second time it brought a meta-analysis with it.
  - **Why rank 3:** the only slot in the arena that would *reduce* what the
    system keeps, in a workspace whose two recorded data-loss incidents were both
    caused by doing exactly that.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 27** — this is that area's own evidence arriving from
    the affective side, and the two files **disagree about the width of the
    window** (Area 27 carries ~6 h from ORDER 389; this file says *typically 1–6
    hours*). Recorded as agreement, not as two independent confirmations.

