### Area 57 · The phase-dependent sign of arousal: a gain that is a trade, and a trade that reverses `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by two files read whole this pass** (ORDER 838, 149 lines; ORDER 844,
362 lines), which state the same law from two directions and disagree about
where it comes from. **The arena's fifty-seventh structural silence, and the
first about a signal whose *sign* is a function of what the system is currently
doing.** Fifty-six areas ask how much effort, confidence, urgency or drive to
apply. **Not one asks whether the same value would have the opposite effect one
step later in the same operation** — and the corpus says it would, in both
directions, with numbers.

- **Rank 1 — Never apply one arousal scalar uniformly; carry the phase, because
  the sign flips with it.**
  - **Brain part:** the **arousal–memory asymmetry across phases**, stated
    independently by both earning files. ORDER 838: acute stress **impairs
    retrieval** (Roozendaal, glucocorticoid effects) while enhancing
    consolidation — *"emotional arousal benefits the storage phase (via
    norepinephrine and amygdala activation) but can impair the retrieval phase
    (via cortisol and glucocorticoid receptor activation in the prefrontal cortex
    and hippocampus)."* ORDER 844 §"Timing Effects: When Cortisol Arrives
    Matters": elevated cortisol **impairs encoding**, **enhances consolidation**,
    and **impairs retrieval** — three phases, three signs, and the file's
    statement of the consequence: *"a subject tested immediately after a
    stressful event (encoding/retrieval impaired) may show a different
    performance curve than a subject tested after a consolidation delay."*
    The mechanism is **dual-affinity receptor competition**: high-affinity
    mineralocorticoid receptors are constitutively active at baseline and
    stabilise existing synapses; lower-affinity glucocorticoid receptors engage
    only when cortisol rises, enhance consolidation of recent traces, and at high
    levels drive dendritic retraction. **The inverted-U is what two receptors of
    different affinity produce**, and it is why ORDER 844's own §"Tensions"
    section records that the behavioural Yerkes–Dodson curve and Joëls'
    two-phase mechanistic model *"are not necessarily contradictory"* while
    warning that the curve *"conflates these mechanisms."*
  - **Design:** arousal is a **(magnitude, phase) pair**, never a bare scalar,
    and the consumer is selected by phase. Concretely and at 14,589-file scale:
    the same intensity that should **sharpen** retrieval (a hard, novel, central
    question) should **not** be applied to a bulk ingest, and the same intensity
    that should **gate** a write is the intensity most likely to have produced
    it. The arena currently has no way to express this because it has never
    recorded that its own "importance" signal is phase-ambiguous.
  - **Already exists:** **eligibility traces** in RL, and the arena's own
    Area 6 consolidation machinery, both already carry a time-indexed quantity —
    which is the same shape and is currently used for a different purpose
    (credit assignment, not gating). The reversal is not implemented anywhere.
  - **Backup document:** `Emotion-Cognition/Emotional-Memory-Enhancement.md`
    (ORDER 838) §§"Consolidation Over Time", "Stress and Retrieval Impairment:
    The Yerkes–Dodson Complement"; `Emotion-Cognition/Stress-Arousal-Cognition.md`
    (ORDER 844) §§"MR/GR Receptor Dynamics and the Inverted-U", "Timing Effects:
    When Cortisol Arrives Matters", "The Two-Phase Model of Glucocorticoid Action".
  - **Grade:** `LOW`, on the mechanism, and the two source-strengths are kept
    separate. Emotional-memory enhancement is described by ORDER 838 as *"among
    the most replicated in cognitive neuroscience"*, and it reports Murphy &
    Isaacowitz (2008) aggregating **171 neuroimaging studies** at
    **d ≈ 0.5–0.8** recognition / **d ≈ 0.4–0.7** recall. **The preregistered
    multi-lab replication (n > 1,000) confirmed the main effect at
    d ≈ 0.3–0.5**, and ORDER 838 draws the correct conclusion itself —
    *"smaller effect sizes than typical single-lab studies, suggesting some
    inflation in the historical literature due to publication bias."* **Design
    `UNTESTED`.** No grade is raised above `LOW` because the largest
    meta-analytic number in the file is the one its own text discounts.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in the arena that makes an existing
    signal *unsafe to apply as currently applied*, which is the precondition for
    every other fix here.
  - **Interaction:** **constrains Area 6** (consolidation) and **Area 23**
    (sleep-time rebuild) — both are phase-specific operations and this is the
    first statement of what may be applied in each. **Area 43** (the cost of
    thinking, λ) — the λ is exactly the arousal magnitude whose phase-sensitivity
    the arena has been ignoring. **Area 27** (the lability window) is the
    empirical case: high PE opens the window *and* is the condition under which
    the correction is most likely to be wrong.
- **Rank 2 — Central/peripheral is an explicit budget, not a hidden trade.**
  - **Brain part:** the **weapon-focus / central–peripheral trade-off**. ORDER 838:
    emotional arousal enhances memory for **central** details — those bearing the
    emotional meaning of the scene — **at the expense of peripheral** ones. The
    canonical demonstration is Loftus et al. (1987): witnesses to a crime
    remember **the weapon** in detail and are impaired on the **perpetrator's
    face, clothing, or vehicle**. Easterbrook (1959) supplied the mechanism
    sixty years earlier as **cue-utilization** — *high arousal narrows the range
    of cues attended*, focusing processing on the arousing stimulus itself.
    Kensinger (2007) formalised it as paired specific/general trade-offs. Mather
    & Sutherland's **arousal-biased competition** (2011) states the consequence
    most sharply for an agent: arousal **enhances high-priority stimuli and
    impairs low-priority ones** — it is not a global gain, it **sharpens the
    competitive advantage of representations that were already prioritised**.
  - **Design:** the salience field of Area 10 becomes **two** fields, and the
    system's answer to a salient item is accompanied by a declaration of what it
    is *therefore not looking at*. The corpus's own framing of the open question
    is the right one to preserve: **when should the system decline to narrow?**
    — because peripheral detail is exactly what becomes relevant later, and a
    system that has spent its budget on the central detail has not saved
    anything.
  - **Already exists:** nothing. Nothing in the Hermes stack records a coverage
    cost alongside a salience gain.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 838 §§"Central vs. Peripheral Trade-off",
    "Arousal-Biased Competition Theory (Mather & Sutherland, 2011)",
    "Arousal and Neuromodulation", §Open Question 2.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Loftus's weapon focus is one of the most-cited findings in
    eyewitness literature and ORDER 838 grades the underlying arousal–memory link
    at the effect sizes quoted in rank 1; **the trade-off specifically** is
    graded on the same basis and the design is `UNTESTED`. The file also names
    the replication-side caution — Neisser's position that flashbulb memories
    are *"the upper tail of a continuous distribution"* rather than a separate
    mechanism — preserved, not resolved. **Corroborated this pass, second
    independent source** by ORDER 879 (`Entities/Elizabeth-Loftus.md`,
    §"Weapon Focus and the Central-Peripheral Tradeoff") — the same trade-off
    from Loftus's own primary account, giving **2 independent sources**. It does
    not raise the grade: the second source is the *same research programme* seen
    from its originator, which is a cross-reference confirming reach, not
    independent replication.
  - **Why rank 2:** it is rank 1's consequence expressed on the coverage axis,
    and a phase-aware gain with no coverage accounting still loses information
    silently.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 38** (workspace capacity) — a narrowing budget is a
    capacity claim, and this is the same trade in the attention dimension.
    **Area 51** (representational capacity) — codebook collapse is a
    peripheral-starvation failure. **Area 10** — this is the correction to it.
- **Rank 3 — Confidence and accuracy are separate instruments and the corpus
  proves they come apart; hold them apart.**
  - **Brain part:** the **flashbulb confidence–accuracy dissociation**. Talarico
    & Rubin (2003) tested 54 Duke students the day after 9/11 and retested at 7,
    42 or 224 days: flashbulb and everyday memories **did not differ in the rate
    at which consistent or inconsistent details were recalled — both declined**.
    The difference was **subjective**: vividness, recollection and *belief in
    accuracy* declined for everyday memories and **not** for flashbulb memories.
    Sharot et al. (2007) add a real neural signature — Manhattan participants
    closer to the World Trade Center (mean ~2 miles vs ~4.5) reported more
    sensory detail and stronger amygdala activation during retrieval.
  - **Design:** the arena reports one number where the biology produces two, and
    they move in opposite directions. Any confidence field in the arena must be
    stored **beside** a separately-derived accuracy field, and a rise in the
    first may never be reported as evidence about the second. This is the arena's
    **§8 inversion arriving from a memory paper rather than from the corpus's own
    audit reports**, and it is the third independent field in which it has
    appeared.
  - **Already exists:** nothing. The arena's `LOW`/`benchmark-suspect` marking
    rule is the closest existing mechanism and it is a *rule*, not an instrument.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 838 §§"Flashbulb Memories: Brown, Kulik, and the
    Special-Mechanism Hypothesis", "Duration of flashbulb confidence-accuracy
    dissociation", §Methodological Pitfalls; ORDER 840 §"Summary of the Debate".
  - **Grade:** `HIGH` for the **dissociation** — it is preregistered, retested at
    three delays, and the two measures are reported separately by design. The
    **design** is `UNTESTED`, and the two are not averaged. Note the honest
    limit the file itself sets: whether the gap *"is stable across all
    populations or varies with age, personality, and event type remains under
    investigation,"* and the preregistered replication of the *enhancement*
    effect is `LOW` per rank 1.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the only slot that can be deleted without changing what
    gets built, and it is rank 3 for that reason rather than because it is
    weakly evidenced.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 24** (validity of one's own instruments) — this is
    that area with a specific, preregistered, human demonstration attached.
    **Area 37** (vigilance decrement) — a watcher whose confidence rises while
    its hit rate falls is exhibiting exactly this.

