### Area 59 · Aesthetic valuation as a three-way dissociation, and the label effect that moves it without moving the object `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by one file read whole this pass** (ORDER 842, 494 lines, 35 numbered
sources). Brain part: the **aesthetic triad** (Chatterjee & Vartanian 2014) and
the **beauty / liking / wanting dissociation**. **The arena's fifty-ninth
structural silence, and the first about a signal whose *value is not a property
of the thing valued*:** fifty-eight areas treat any score as a function of the
object scored, and this one is a controlled demonstration that the same object
receives a different value under a different label, with nothing about the object
changing. **Aesthetic judgment is the corpus's cleanest available instance of a
top-down expectation changing a valuation measurement, and it is the sharpest
available argument that the arena's own scores are not read-outs.**

- **Rank 1 — Keep beauty, liking, and wanting as three separate signals, and
  never let one stand in for another.**
  - **Brain part:** the **aesthetic triad** — **sensory-motor** (perceptual
    analysis plus embodied simulation; FFA, PPA, EBA, mirror-system engagement),
    **emotion-valuation** (mOFC, ventral striatum, ACC, insula), and
    **meaning-knowledge** (default mode network, LPFC, temporal poles) —
    combined with Berridge & Robinson's **liking vs wanting** separation carried
    over from the corpus's own decision-neuroscience lineage. The triad's claim is
    that aesthetic experience is an **emergent property of interaction among the
    three**, not the output of any one. Cross-modally, Brown et al. (2011) ALE
    meta-analysis of **93 studies** across visual, auditory, gustatory and
    olfactory aesthetics converges on mOFC, ventral striatum, anterior insula and
    ACC, with modality-specific additions.
  - **Design:** three value heads with separate readouts, not one preference
    score. The corpus's own statement of why: in humans these **dissociate**
    (an artwork judged beautiful but not liked; kitsch liked but not judged
    beautiful; addictive drugs high wanting, low liking), while **in standard RL
    they collapse into a single reward signal** — and the corpus's inference is
    that *"multi-objective RL with separable value heads may produce more
    human-aligned agents."* The failure this prevents is named in the same file:
    **reward hacking produces "adversarial beauty"** — images that maximise the
    reward model while lacking semantic coherence, **which is the meaning-knowledge
    component being absent and nothing else being wrong.**
  - **Already exists:** RLHF reward heads and DPO exist and are single-scalar;
    PickScore / ImageReward / HPSv2 are the aesthetic-reward artifacts and
    exhibit exactly the failure described. Nothing in the Hermes stack has three
    value heads. **The nearest existing thing is Area 50's wanting/liking
    dissociation, which is two of these three** — this area adds the third and is
    therefore a genuine extension rather than a duplicate.
  - **Backup document:** `Emotion-Cognition/Neuroaesthetics-Aesthetic-Judgment.md`
    (ORDER 842) §§"Core Mechanisms: The Aesthetic Triad",
    "Computational & Agent Parallels" items 1–2 and 7,
    "Overlaps & Tensions" items 1 and 10.
  - **Grade:** the human neuroanatomy is **`LOW`, not `HIGH`, and the cap is the
    file's own** — it reports **median statistical power below 0.3** for typical
    effect sizes (Chen & Yang 2021, 45 fMRI studies, median n = 16), **over 80%
    of studies using Western stimuli**, WEIRD sampling throughout, and states
    *"no study to date has cleanly dissociated all three in a single paradigm."*
    A framework with a replicated core region and a **0.3 median power** is not a
    high grade, and the arena's `benchmark-suspect` rule applies. The design is
    `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Why rank 1:** it changes the *type* of the signal, and every other slot
    here is a correction to a score this one replaces.
  - **Interaction:** **extends Area 50** (wanting without liking) with a third
    term — Area 50 separated two, this separates three, and the third
    (perceptual merit) is the one with **no motivational component at all**.
    **Area 36** (specification gap) is this slot's failure mode by another name:
    "adversarial beauty" is reward hacking with the meaning component deleted.
    **Area 53** (cross-modal binding) — the triad is a three-system interaction
    and cross-modal generality is its most contested boundary.
- **Rank 2 — Condition every valuation on its context, and treat the label as a
  control surface.**
  - **Brain part:** the **meaning-knowledge** component's top-down modulation of
    valuation. Two experiments, both with the perceptual input held fixed.
    **Kirk et al. (2009)**, N = 14: identical abstract images labelled *"museum"*
    versus *"computer-generated"* produced different **mOFC** responses (peak
    [6, 50, −8], p < .05 FWE) plus entorhinal activation, and museum-labelled
    images were rated **15% more attractive** (p < .01). **Huang et al. (2011)**,
    N = 14: the same Rembrandt portrait labelled *"authentic"* versus *"copy"*
    recruited mOFC under the authentic label and **frontopolar cortex (BA10) plus
    right precuneus** under the copy label — a **0.8% BOLD difference**. The
    object did not change; the belief about it did.
  - **Design:** a valuation is only interpretable against the **provenance
    metadata attached at read time** — which is why this slot is worth more here
    than the neuroscience it came from. This arena grades every slot with a
    `Backup document:` line naming a corpus file it was read from, and the
    citation rule is the mechanism that makes the grade checkable. **The Kirk
    result is the experimental demonstration that the same artefact carries a
    different value under a different label, and the arena's entire method is a
    labelling discipline that is currently applied to citations and not to
    scores.**
  - **Already exists:** the corpus's own citation apparatus *is* the mechanism —
    `verified: []` blocks, `confidence:` values, `stale_after` dates, 446
    colliding basenames documented at `citation_remediation.py`. The arena's
    **§8 inversion finding** is that these are *generated and checked as
    artifacts* while the prose they decorate is not. Kirk et al. predict exactly
    that: the artifact's label raises the apparent value of the thing it labels,
    without changing the thing.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 842 §§"Semantic Context & Top-Down Modulation",
    "Replicated findings" table (context/label modulation of mOFC, robust across
    4–5 studies), "Computational & Agent Parallels" item 3.
  - **Grade:** the context effect is the **most robust row in the file's own
    replication table** (`Robust, 4–5 studies, d = 0.5–0.9`, alongside the mOFC
    beauty row at `d = 0.8–1.5`), so the human effect is `LOW` at the same cap
    as rank 1 and for the same reason — same studies, same power problem, not
    two independent bodies of evidence. Design `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**
    (same file).
  - **Why rank 2:** it is the highest-yield *consequence* in the file and it
    applies to the arena itself, which is a reason to take it seriously and not
    a reason to make it rank 1 — it qualifies a valuation, it does not change
    what is being valued.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 24** (validity of one's own instruments) — this is
    that area's thesis with a controlled demonstration. **Area 16** (role and
    authorship) — a source's label changes what its content is worth, which is
    the same point about trust. **Area 1** (belief revision) — the copy label
    raises frontopolar activity, i.e. suspicion, which is a *belief* about the
    same pixels.
- **Rank 3 — Hold the aesthetic/valence conflict open, and keep the two
  processing-fluency accounts from being merged.**
  - **Brain part:** **epistemic emotion** (Silvia 2005; Menninghaus et al. 2019)
    — aesthetic emotions arise from **cognitive appraisal of form, meaning and
    value**, not from survival-relevant triggers — and the resulting
    **valence-based failure**, since *"being moved"* (Menninghaus et al. 2015)
    involves **simultaneous positive and negative affect** (tears of joy), which
    a single valence scalar cannot represent. Underneath it sits an unreconciled
    dispute: **processing-fluency theory** (Reber et al. 2004) predicts
    *familiar, simple* stimuli are preferred; **predictive-coding and
    optimal-arousal** accounts predict *moderately novel, complex* stimuli; the
    data support the **inverted-U** (Marin & Leder 2013, R² = 0.62). The file
    states the field suffers **theoretical fragmentation** — at least four
    frameworks coexisting, *"not mutually exclusive but rarely integrated"* — and
    that **a unified theory remains elusive**.
  - **Design:** do not collapse a mixed-valence signal into a sign, and do not
    pick a fluency account over a novelty account. Carried as a live dispute.
  - **Already exists:** nothing; no component in the stack produces affect at all.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 842 §§"Aesthetic Emotions vs. Basic Emotions",
    "Theoretical Fragmentation: Competing Frameworks",
    "Overlaps & Tensions" items 3 and 6.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` for the design; the human claims are `LOW` and the
    file supplies the reason twice over — mirror-neuron claims in humans
    **remain controversial** (Hickok 2009's "eight problems"; Kilner & Lemon
    2013), and the file's own §3 warns against exactly the inference this arena
    makes everywhere: observing mOFC activation during beauty judgments and
    concluding *"'mOFC computes beauty' is a reverse inference fallacy"* (after
    Poldrack 2006). Support: **1 source** (same file).
  - **Why rank 3:** the dispute is real and the arena is forbidden to resolve it,
    but a slot whose content is *"two theories are both partly right"* changes
    nothing that gets built until one of them is tested.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 56** — a mixed-valence profile is that area's vector
    doing the job its scalar could not, which is the strongest available
    in-corpus argument for rank 1 of Area 56. **Area 7** (dual-process) —
    fluent processing is the fast route and meaning-knowledge the slow one, and
    they carry **opposing** hedonic sign.

