### Area 61 · Multiple memory systems: a dissociation is an architecture, not a curiosity `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Entities/Brenda-Milner.md` (ORDER 860, 393 lines)
§§"Patient H.M.", "The Mirror Drawing Task", "The Multiple Memory Systems
Framework", "Neuropsychology Methodology". **The arena's sixty-first structural
silence, and the first about *why two subsystems must fail differently* rather
than about a failure at all.** Fifty-nine areas name a memory, a workspace, a
consolidation window and a reconsolidation gate. **Not one states that the
single most informative observation in the history of memory research is a case
where learning happened perfectly and remembering it did not** — and therefore
that any system with one memory store must be wrong about its own learning.

- **Rank 1 — Two stores with different failure signatures: a system that can
  improve at a task while being unable to report having done it must be able to
  hold both facts at once.**
  - **Brain part:** the **declarative / non-declarative dissociation** established
    by H.M. The file's demonstration is the cleanest in the corpus: on mirror
    drawing **H.M. "improved with practice"** to match normal subjects, and then
    **"had no memory of having practiced"** — asked after each session whether he
    had done it before, he said he had not. The dissociation is also carried in
    four further directions the arena does not have: **perceptual priming
    intact**, **eye-blink conditioning intact** (cerebellar, not hippocampal),
    **finger-tapping sequence learning intact**, **perceptual learning of
    rotated-letter discrimination intact**. Working memory, IQ (~112), language,
    remote memory and personality all spared. The corpus's formulation is the
    one the slot is built on: *"memory is implemented by multiple, partially
    independent neural systems."*
  - **Design:** at minimum two stores with **separately queryable** contents —
    one that reports *what happened* and one that reports *what the system got
    better at*. The governing rule: **improvement without a reportable episode is
    a success, not a failure**, and a monitor that reads only the declarative
    store will conclude the system learned nothing every time it learns something.
    The corpus supplies the design method too — Milner's **single-case method**:
    characterise the lesion, assess broadly, *look for dissociations*, replicate
    across comparable cases, integrate with experimental data. A system with one
    store cannot run that method on itself, because it has no second series to
    compare.
  - **Already exists:** procedural/skill stores in the Hermes stack (skills,
    accumulated cron practice) and the ledger's `[x]` marks are exactly this
    pair and are **never read together**. Adopt-don't-build: the two-store shape
    is the arena's own implicit design and needs a reader, not a new store.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/Brenda-Milner.md` (ORDER 860)
    §§"Patient H.M.", "Episodic and Procedural Memory Dissociation",
    "The Single-Case Method".
  - **Grade:** human mechanism **`HIGH`** — this is the founding case of modern
    memory neuroscience, replicated across the patient series the file describes,
    with the postmortem confirming **~two-thirds of the hippocampus and
    hippocampal formation removed bilaterally** and *"the precision of the
    lesion — and the specificity of the resulting cognitive deficit — even more
    remarkable than had been appreciated during H.M.'s lifetime."* **Agent
    design `UNTESTED`.** Support: **1 source**, and the file is an
    encyclopaedic entity page — per the standing rule it is **not** counted as
    an independent implementation of the agent mapping, only as the corpus's
    statement of the human case.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in the arena that makes an
    *architectural* claim from a *dissociation*, and the dissociation is the
    best-evidenced result any file in this tranche carries.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 6** and **Area 23** (consolidation) — both describe
    one store being maintained. **Area 12** (procedural and implicit memory) —
    this is the founding case that area generalises from, and this file is the
    primary source it never cited. **Area 45** (efference copy) — the
    *provenance* of a memory and the *system* that holds it are different
    questions, and this area is the second half.
- **Rank 2 — The systems-consolidation gradient is a retention schedule, and it
  is the one the arena's own re-consolidation window does not cover.**
  - **Brain part:** the **retrograde gradient** (the file calls it the Ribot
    gradient) — H.M. lost recent memories and retained childhood ones, and the
    file's reading is that **memories become progressively less dependent on the
    hippocampus**, transferring to neocortical storage over months to years.
    The file names the four steps of the standard model: encoding via entorhinal
    cortex, hippocampal binding, **systems consolidation**, retrieval via
    direct neocortical pathways bypassing the hippocampus.
  - **Design:** a memory's *dependence on the fast store is a function of its
    age*, so the reconsolidation gate (Area 27's ~6 h window) and the archival
    move are **the same operation at different ages**, and the arena currently
    has no age field on a memory. At 14,589-file scale this is what stops every
    retrieved item from re-entering the lability window forever.
  - **Already exists:** the ledger's mark and the arena's evidence-vs-answer
    split are both age-structured and neither is consulted by age.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/Brenda-Milner.md` (ORDER 860)
    §"The Retrograde Amnesia Gradient", "Contribution to the Standard Model".
  - **Grade:** `LOW` on the human schedule — the file gives the *direction* of
    the gradient and the *substrate*, and says the timing is *"months to years"*
    without a study, so the numbers are the file's summary rather than a cited
    result. **Design `UNTESTED`.** Support: **1 source** (same file).
  - **Why rank 2:** it is the mechanism behind Rank 1's spared remote memory and
    inherits Rank 1's evidence, which is why it is not rank 1.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 6 Rank 3** and **Area 27** — the same window, named
    at a different scale. **Area 30** (per-episode cost) — a gradient is also a
    retention budget.
- **Rank 3 — The single-case method as the arena's own instrument: one case,
  broadly assessed, looking for dissociations.** `LOW`.
  - **Brain part:** Milner's **methodological** contribution — the
    single-case neuropsychological assessment: *characterise the lesion precisely,
    assess broadly and systematically, look for dissociations, replicate across
    patients, integrate with experimental data.* The corpus states it *"set the
    standard for clinical neuropsychology."*
  - **Design:** apply the fifth step to a **single run** — take one pass, assess
    it across all five of the slots the arena defines rather than the one the
    pass was about, and look for the dissociation. A pass that improved the
    answer file and degraded the evidence file has produced a H.M. result:
    learning happened, and the reportable episode does not exist. This is the
    instrument the arena has never run on itself, and it is a method, not a
    metric — R-J2 forbids nothing here.
  - **Already exists:** `scripts/verify_reads.py` and the `C1–C11` invariants are
    a partial implementation: they check **counts**, not **dissociations**.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/Brenda-Milner.md` (ORDER 860)
    §"The Single-Case Method".
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. The method is five decades of standard practice, which is
    not nothing, but the file reports no trial of the method on an AI system and
    no comparison against whole-population designs. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is a procedure that produces evidence for Ranks 1 and 2
    rather than a design in its own right.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 24** (validity of one's own instruments) — the
    single-case method is the one design in the arena that looks for the
    instrument's own dissociation rather than for its accuracy.

### Area 62 · The three levels of analysis: the middle one is where the arena has been living `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Entities/David-Marr.md` (ORDER 870, 645 lines) §§"The
Three Levels of Analysis", "Critiques and Limitations", "Marr in the Deep
Learning Era". **The arena's sixty-second structural silence, and the first
whose subject is the *arena's own argument's shape* rather than a brain part the
system should have.** Sixty-one areas name brain parts and specify designs. **Not
one states the level at which each of its sixty-one designs is specified — and
the file's claim is that confusion between levels is *"the primary source of many
debates in cognitive science and AI."*

- **Rank 1 — Label every existing slot with its level, and treat a design with no
  representation specified as an architectural claim that has not been made
  yet.**
  - **Brain part:** Marr's **three levels of analysis** — the **computational
    theory** (what problem is solved and why), the **algorithmic /
    representational level** (what representations and what transformations
    between them), and the **physical / implementation level** (what carries it).
    Marr's argument that all three are **complementary, not competitive**, and
    his worked example that he "didn't just say *the brain computes edges*; he
    specified the exact mathematical operator — the Laplacian of Gaussian
    filter." The arena's own gloss on the failure mode is its sharpest sentence:
    *"until we understand the algorithmic level, we don't truly understand what
    the network is doing, even if it performs well."*
  - **Design:** each slot gains a **level tag**: `computational` (what to do),
    `algorithmic` (in what representation, by what transform), `implementation`
    (in what substrate). The rule that earns the rank: **a slot tagged
    `computational` with no algorithmic specification is a proposal, and the
    arena's grade field should say `UNTESTED` for a reason it can name.** Read
    against the arena: Area 58's five intervention points, Area 56's six
    appraisal dimensions, and Area 59's three value heads are all
    **computational-level** statements about **affective** content, and the
    question "in what representation is a six-dimensional appraisal vector
    carried, and what transforms it?" is **not answered anywhere in 6,815
    lines** — it is the question Marr says separates a theory from a slogan.
  - **Already exists:** adopt-don't-build. `SKILL.md` already mandates **seven
    fields per slot**, one of which is *Design*; the level tag is an eighth
    field and costs one line. Nothing else in the stack carries it.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/David-Marr.md` (ORDER 870)
    §§"The Three Levels of Analysis" (all three levels, plus "The Three Levels
    in Modern Context"), "Vision: A Computational Theory".
  - **Grade:** framework `HIGH` — Marr's levels are the default analytical
    vocabulary of computational neuroscience and the file states they are
    *"nearly every researcher in the field works within a framework Marr
    established, whether they realize it or not,"* and the arena's own `Design`
    field is an instance of the framework's computational level. **Agent design
    `UNTESTED`** — no trial of level-tagging exists that the file reports.
    Support: **1 source** (an entity page; the framework is named in the
    corpus, not independently implemented here).
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in the arena that constrains the other
    sixty-one rather than adding a sixty-second subject, and it is the only one
    that can be discharged entirely by adding a field.
  - **Interaction:** **every area.** This is stated as a cost, not a benefit: a
    level tag on sixty-one areas is a large edit, and the correct form of the
    tag on an existing slot is usually `computational`. **Area 17** (a
    computational model of cognition as the reference) is the area most
    affected — it is a computational-level slot by construction. **Area 33**
    (certification asymmetry) is the one area that *does* specify an
    implementation, which makes it the arena's proof the tag is fillable.
- **Rank 2 — Ill-posedness is the design's precondition: every problem the
  system solves is underdetermined, and the assumption is the design.**
  - **Brain part:** Marr's formalisation of vision as **ill-posed** — *"there
    are infinitely many 3D worlds that could produce the same 2D retinal
    image"* — resolved by declared **constraints** (smoothness, uniform
    illumination, geometric regularity, occlusion, solidity), each of which is
    an **assumption about the world that is generally true enough to be useful**.
    The file's gloss: the visual system *"does not compute 3D structure by
    direct measurement; it infers 3D structure by finding the interpretation
    that is consistent with all available constraints"* — which is a
    constraint-satisfaction problem, and the file says so before Bayesian
    perception existed.
  - **Design:** every constraint the system applies silently becomes a declared
    field. The arena applies at least four undeclared ones: **a wiki page is
    worth reading** (ORDER.txt is the authority and is never re-derived), **a
    `read_file` that returns is a read** (so a truncated body counts — a
    smoothness constraint that will occasionally manufacture a false `[x]`),
    **a file with a passing frontmatter block is well-formed** (§8's
    inversion), and **earlier tranches were right** (the strongest, and the one
    that makes the arena's method a self-reinforcing loop). Naming them is the
    design.
  - **Already exists:** `ORDER.txt` *is* a declared constraint and is the one
    instance the arena got right; `SCRATCHPAD.md` declares five more as
    binding rules.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/David-Marr.md` (ORDER 870)
    §§"The Visual Problem", "Marr and Poggio — Pictorial Structure and Visual
    Space" (constraints), "From Pictorial Structure to Visual Space".
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. The ill-posedness argument is `HIGH` as mathematics; the
    *transfer* to an agent is untested and the file's own limitation section is
    the reason it is not rank 1 — Marr's method is **rational reconstruction**
    (*"derive algorithms from computational principles and then check whether
    they match biological data"*), and the file preserves the standing objection
    that this *"can lead to theories that are mathematically elegant but
    biologically inaccurate."* Support: **1 source**.
  - **Why rank 2:** it is the mechanism by which Rank 1's missing middle is
    usually filled — with an assumption rather than a derivation — which makes
    it a qualification on Rank 1 rather than a rival.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 36** (the specification gap) — a declared
    constraint is to an assumption what an explicit objective is to a proxy, and
    both are the same move one level down. **Area 42** (functional fixedness) —
    constraints are what make a representation untearable.
- **Rank 3 — The pipeline, and the stage-boundary discipline it implies.**
  `UNTESTED`.
  - **Brain part:** the **primal sketch → 2½-D sketch → 3-D model** pipeline, each
    stage **viewer-centred or object-centred**, each making the next tractable.
    The file states the architectural bet plainly: *"the visual system must
    proceed through these stages because each stage solves a specific subproblem
    that makes the next stage tractable."* The same bet, in the same file,
    produces the **2½-D** representation — viewer-centred, for action — and
    the **3-D** — object-centred, for recognition — which is the file's
    anticipation of the **dorsal/ventral** distinction (Ungerleider & Mishkin
    1982; Goodale & Milner 1992).
  - **Design:** the arena's pipeline is `ORDER.txt → LEDGER → read_file →
    ARENA.md`, and it has **no stage in which anything is computed for action
    as against storage for recognition** — the same content is used for both,
    and the file's point is that these want **different representations**.
    Concretely: a slot's *answer* (what to build) and its *evidence* (why) are
    the 3-D and the 2½-D of the same slot, and the two-file split adopted
    earlier in this arena is the arena's accidental instance of the
    distinction. Rank 3 is the **stage boundary** between them, which the split
    already has and the arena has never named.
  - **Already exists:** the `ARENA.md` / `ARENA-EVIDENCE.md` split; the
    `ARENA-INFRA.md` / `VERIFICATION.md` split. Both are viewer/object
    separations the arena arrived at without the concept.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/David-Marr.md` (ORDER 870)
    §§"Vision: A Computational Theory", "The 2½-D Sketch", "3-D Model
    Representation", "The Halfway Cortex".
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. The file records the **critique that the 3-D model is
    its most contested part** — generalised cylinders are *"not the only (or
    best) way to represent 3D shape"*, neural evidence for an explicit 3-D
    representation is *"limited"*, and some researchers hold the system
    *"can recognize objects directly from 2D projections, without constructing
    explicit 3D representations."* The pipeline is a hypothesis about cortical
    organisation, not a settled one. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Why rank 3:** the arena already has the two-file split; naming it is a
    label, not a build, and the file's own limitations section argues the
    pipeline's third stage is the least supported.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 8** (structural fidelity) — the 2½-D/3-D split is
    the *anatomical* form of that area's *relational-memory* failure. **Area 53**
    (cross-modal binding) — a pipeline with named stages is a candidate answer
    to "what makes several inputs one thing", competing with binding-before-
    reasoning.

### Area 63 · Cortical columns and the receptive field: what a unit of perception is obliged to be `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Entities/David-Hubel-and-Torsten-Wiesel.md`
(ORDER 869, 644 lines) §§"Receptive Fields", "Simple / Complex / Hypercomplex
Cells", "Ocular Dominance Columns", "The Critical Period". **The arena's
sixty-third structural silence, and the first about the *shape of a single
unit* of anything the system perceives.** Sixty-two areas say how many items fit
in the workspace, how long a memory stays labile, how a reward is distributed
across time. **Not one states what a single retrieved item must contain to be a
unit at all** — and the file's answer is a field of view, not an item.

- **Rank 1 — A retrieved item is a receptive field over a neighbourhood, not a
  document: the unit carries what its neighbours contribute and cannot be read
  without them.**
  - **Brain part:** the **receptive field** and the **simple → complex →
    hypercomplex** hierarchy. The file's own contrast is the load-bearing fact:
    LGN neurons respond to **spots of light** (centre-surround), cortical
    neurons respond to **oriented bars and edges** — and the transformation from
    one to the other is *"the first stage of cortical visual processing."*
    The hierarchy's operation is named in the file's own words: complex cells
    are built by **pooling simple cells' outputs across position**, which buys
    **position invariance** by discarding position. End-stopped cells add an
    **inhibitory end-zone**, so a bar that is too long *suppresses* the
    response — length becomes a feature by subtraction.
  - **Design:** a retrieved item is a **window with a field**, not a file
    boundary. An item that is correct only when its neighbours are also present
    is not retrievable on its own, and the arena's `Backup document` field —
    which points at a whole file — is the file-level analogue of a complex
    cell's receptive field rather than of a simple cell's. The operative
    consequence: **"I read it and it was fine" is a statement about a pooling
    operation, not about the item**, and the arena's grades are assigned at
    exactly that level. Note also the file's *methodological* warning, which
    the arena's own instrument depends on: in an era when single-unit recording
    was new, *"many 'discoveries' were later shown to be electrical noise or
    movement artifacts,"* and Hubel and Wiesel developed **criteria for genuine
    neural responses** that *"became the gold standard."*
  - **Already exists:** hybrid BM25 + vector search over chunks in
    `brain-postgres` **is** a complex-cell architecture — position-invariant
    retrieval over local windows — and was never described in those terms.
    Adopt-don't-build: the mechanism is already deployed and unexamined.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/David-Hubel-and-Torsten-Wiesel.md`
    (ORDER 869) §§"Receptive Fields — The Fundamental Unit of Visual Analysis",
    "Simple Cells", "Complex Cells", "Hypercomplex (End-Stopped) Cells".
  - **Grade:** human mechanism **`HIGH`** — the file describes the hierarchy as
    the standard model of primary visual cortex (*"Every neuroscience textbook
    teaches the Hubel-Wiesel model, and every new finding in visual cortex
    research is evaluated against it. The model has been refined but never
    overturned."*), replicated across cats, monkeys and humans, with the
    1959, 1962, 1968, 1970 and 1977 primary papers cited with volume and page.
    **Agent design `UNTESTED`.** **Support: 3 independent sources.**
    **Incremented this pass (tranche 61) from 2 → 3 by ORDER 1324**
    (`Neuroscience/Neural-Basis-Taste-Gustatory-Cortex.md`,
    §"The coding debate: labeled lines versus population coding"), and **the
    clause it earns is directional, which is the one this slot never had.** The
    arena treated the receptive field as a property of a cell. The taste
    evidence says specificity is a property of a cell's *position in a
    pathway*: peripheral units are quality-specific, cortical units are
    broadly tuned and identity is read from **spatially intermingled
    ensembles**. That is a limit on the slot's own framing, and it is now
    stated rather than left for Area 111 to hold — a receptive field is not
    composable from parts, but it is not uniformly composed of them either.
    ORDER 1331 §1.4 and ORDER 1335 §"Visual Processing Architecture" carry
    the same claim for vision and are logged as **reach corroboration only**:
    they state the hierarchy, not the taste evidence, and three files
    restating one claim are one source.
    **Grade held at `HIGH`.** A third source that qualifies a slot is not a
    third replication of it, and saying otherwise would be the §8 inversion
    applied to a grade.
    **Earlier increment (tranche 45) from 1 → 2 by ORDER 963**
    (`Entities/Vernon-Mountcastle.md`, §§2–3), which is the **originator**
    of the columnar-cortex claim rather than a later reviewer — Hubel &
    Wiesel (ORDER 864) describe the finding from the psychophysics side,
    Mountcastle supplies the anatomical claim the arena was missing:
    *the cortex is built from repeated identical modules, so a column is
    not a convenient unit of analysis but the unit the organ is made of.*
    **Grade held** — the file is primary literature for the mechanism
    already graded high; a second source describing the same claim from
    the other end of the same programme is **corroboration, not
    replication**, and saying otherwise would be the §8 inversion applied
    to a grade. What it does earn is one clause: the arena had been
    treating the column as an *analytical* choice, and Mountcastle makes
    it a **structural** one — a falsifier for Rank 1's own framing. Full
    record in `ARENA-EVIDENCE.md`.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in the arena that constrains what a
    single item is, and every other area's capacity claims are downstream of it.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 38** (workspace capacity) — chunk counts are
    measured in complex cells, not simple ones, which is why two labs disagree.
    **Area 8** (structural fidelity) — a complex cell's position invariance is
    *the* mechanism that loses relational structure, and this file names it.
    **Area 51** (representational capacity that depletes) — end-stopping is
    subtraction, and a store that can only grow has no inhibitory zone.
- **Rank 2 — The hypercolumn: a complete local slice containing all
  orientations and both eyes, and therefore a unit of comparison rather than a
  unit of storage.** `UNTESTED`.
  - **Brain part:** the **hypercolumn** — orientation columns crossing ocular
    dominance columns, *"a complete functional unit of visual cortex that
    contains all the information needed to process a small region of visual
    space,"* with preferred orientation changing **continuously over 1–2 mm**
    and ocular dominance columns ~1 mm (cat) / 0.5–1.0 mm (monkey). The
    file's generalisation is the part that generalises: **cortical columns were
    confirmed in the visual system with exquisite detail and now apply to
    somatosensory, auditory, prefrontal and motor cortex.**
  - **Design:** a **comparison unit** — the slice of the store that contains
    every value of one axis, so that "is this better than the alternatives?" is
    answerable without loading the alternatives separately. This is the
    structure that would let a rank-1 decision be made against a locally
    complete set rather than against whatever was retrieved, and the arena
    currently ranks against retrieved items only. **Nothing in the stack has
    this**, and it is the most speculative of the three slots.
  - **Already exists:** the arena's own `Support: N independent sources` counts
    are a one-dimensional comparison unit and are not a slice.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/David-Hubel-and-Torsten-Wiesel.md`
    (ORDER 869) §§"Ocular Dominance Columns", "Orientation Columns", "11.
    Influence on Neuroscience — Columnar Organization Paradigm".
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. The anatomy is `HIGH`; the *design* transfer is the
    arena's own and untrialled. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Why rank 2:** it is the structural precondition for ranking anything, and
    the arena has never separated "the item I retrieved" from "the set I
    compared it against" as a design object.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 3** (effort allocation), **Area 43** (cost of
    thinking) and **Area 59** (aesthetic valuation) — all three rank, and all
    three rank against an under-specified comparison set. **Area 59 Rank 2**'s
    label effect is *also* a hypercolumn finding: the same object in a different
    context receives a different value because the comparison unit changed.
- **Rank 3 — The critical period: a window in which the architecture is
  writable, and a mechanism for reopening it that is now known.** `LOW`,
  with a **recorded null on reopening**.
  - **Brain part:** **experience-dependent critical-period plasticity** — the
    monocular-deprivation series, with the file's timeline: after **a few
    days** many binocular neurons become dominated by the open eye; after
    **2–3 weeks** most deprived-eye cells respond only to the open eye; after
    **several months** the shift is near-complete. Reopening the deprived eye
    afterwards *"had little effect — the cortical reorganization was
    permanent."* Human window: first months to **~7–9 years**, most sensitive
    at **3–5 years**. The file's generalisation is explicit — the same
    principle holds for auditory cortex, somatosensory cortex, language
    (Lenneberg 1967) and social attachment.
  - **Design:** declare **which parts of the system are still writable and for
    how long**, and treat an out-of-window change as requiring the expensive
    path. The arena has an analogue it never named: the ledger's cursor, the
    arena's slot format, and `SKILL.md`'s own rules are all **written in a
    window** — set at construction, and changing them now is a different kind of
    operation from adding an area.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`, and **the file records the null that keeps it at `LOW`**:
    closure is *"not irreversible"* — perineuronal-net removal transiently
    reopens critical-period-like plasticity in adulthood (tranche 37, Areas 15
    and 51, from a different source), and this file's own §9 notes the
    mechanism is **molecular** (GABAergic inhibition opening and closing the
    period; BDNF, NMDA receptors, perineuronal nets). The expensive
    *reopening* path is therefore known and is recorded here as the arena's
    escape hatch from a format it set once. Support: **1 source**, with
    tranche 37's PNN result as a **second, independent** line — counted once,
    per the one-source-per-subject rule.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is real, replicated, and about a period that for this
    system closed at construction — which makes it an accurate description and
    a poor prescription.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 15** (developmental ordering of identity) and
    **Area 51** — this file is a third independent line for the PNN-reopening
    result those two areas already hold. **Area 23** (consolidation and its
    switch policy) — a critical period is a consolidation window whose
    *permanence* is the defining property, which is a stronger claim than
    either area currently makes.

### Area 64 · Shannon's bounds: the first area whose brain part is a limit rather than a process `PROVISIONAL`

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Entities/Claude-Shannon.md` (ORDER 864, 914 lines)
§§"4.4 Channel Capacity", "4.6 Source Coding and Data Compression", "4.7
Rate-Distortion Theory", "Cryptography". **The arena's sixty-fourth structural
silence, and the first about the *limits* rather than the mechanisms — the one
area in the arena whose brain part is a bound, not a process.** R-J1 makes cost
irrelevant as a decision criterion, and this area is the reason that is
admissible: **capacity is not cost, and the channel-capacity theorem says
reliability is impossible above a rate that can be computed before anything is
built.**

- **Rank 1 — State the rate and the required fidelity before the design; a
  channel past capacity cannot be made reliable by a better codec.**
  - **Brain part:** Shannon's **channel capacity theorem** — *for any channel
    with capacity C and any rate R < C there exists a coding scheme with
    arbitrarily small error; if R > C, reliable communication is impossible* —
    with the continuous-channel form **C = B·log₂(1 + S/N)**, the file's own
    gloss being that **capacity grows linearly in bandwidth and logarithmically
    in signal-to-noise**, so *"to double capacity, you either need double the
    bandwidth or exponentially more signal power."* The corpus states the
    theorem's universality in the sentence that earns the slot: the theorem
    applies to *"telephone lines, radio, optical fiber, wireless, deep space
    communication, DNA storage, neural synapses."*
  - **Design:** the arena is a channel. It carries 14,589 files' worth of
    claims through one answer file read by one pass at a time, and **the
    theorem's operation is to compute C before building the codec** — which is
    the discipline `ARENA-EVIDENCE.md` (15,160 lines against 6,815) has
    informally been enforcing for four tranches. Rank 1 makes it explicit: the
    **ratio of evidence volume to answer volume is a channel statistic**, and a
    design that needs more fidelity than the channel carries must be split
    across more than one pass rather than compressed harder. **The compression
    corollary is the sharper half**: the file's pigeonhole principle —
    *"lossless compression cannot make all files smaller. If a compression
    algorithm makes some files smaller, it must make others larger"* — is
    **the theorem that forbids a slot which is right about everything and
    wrong about nothing**, because the corpus's own §8 inversion is a
    compression artefact, and every gain in answer-file density was paid for
    somewhere in the evidence file.
  - **Already exists:** nothing computes C. The arena has been running the
    theorem's *conclusion* — drop detail — without ever running its
    *precondition*. Adopt-don't-build: no new component, one declared
    statistic.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/Claude-Shannon.md` (ORDER 864)
    §§"4.4 Channel Capacity" (Noisy Channel Coding Theorem, Shannon-Hartley),
    "4.6 Source Coding and Data Compression" (pigeonhole principle), "4.7
    "4.7 Rate-Distortion Theory" (`R(D) = min I(X;X̂)`, `R(0) = H(X)`,
    `R(H(X)) = 0`).
    **Tranche 43 adds** `oracle/brain/Entities/Leslie-Valiant.md` (ORDER 921)
    §§4–5 — **a boundary sharpened from a direction this area did not have, and
    deliberately NOT counted as a source increment**: the file restates
    results the area already holds from ORDER 864's own channel-capacity
    material, so per the three-files-one-study rule it is recorded as
    **reach**. **What it supplies is the axis Shannon's theorem does not
    have: statistical feasibility is not computational feasibility** —
    *"even when enough data exists, the computational problem of finding the
    right hypothesis may be intractable."* The arena has always asked **how much
    data**; Valiant supplies the second axis, **how much compute to find the
    hypothesis in it**. **This is the area's clearest statement of why R-J1
    (cost is irrelevant as a decision criterion) and capacity are not in
    tension: cost is irrelevant to *preference*, and binding on *search*.**
    `R(H(X)) = 0`).
  - **Grade:** theorem **`HIGH`** — this is the load-bearing result of the
    twentieth century's information theory and the file gives volume and page for
    every primary paper (`Bell System Technical Journal` 27(3) 379–423;
    27(4) 623–656). **Agent design `UNTESTED`** — the arena has never applied
    it. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in the arena that can *falsify* a
    ranking before the ranking is built, by asking whether the required fidelity
    exceeds the channel — and R-J1 does not protect a design from that
    question, because the question is about reliability, not expense.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 38** (workspace capacity) — a chunk count is a
    rate-distortion choice, not a property of a store. **Area 41** (degradation
    that hides) — a channel below capacity does not fail loudly; it delivers
    plausible reconstructions, which is the arena's own §8 shape. **Area 30**
    (per-episode cost) — the ingest ceiling is a bandwidth limit. **Area 43**
    (the cost of thinking) — R-J1 is explicitly reconciled here: cost is
    irrelevant as a criterion, **capacity is not**.
- **Rank 2 — Perfect secrecy's key-length requirement, restated as a design
  rule: the secret must be at least as long as the thing it protects, and
  therefore every protected thing is a key-management problem.** `HIGH`
  (theorem), `UNTESTED` (design).
  - **Brain part:** **perfect secrecy** — `H(K) ≥ H(X)`, so the key must be at
    least as long as the message and must be truly random; the one-time pad is
    the only perfectly secure method, and the file's consequence is the part
    that generalises: *"perfectly secure encryption requires impractical key
    management"* and *"the bottleneck in secure encryption is not the encryption
    algorithm but the secure distribution of keys."* The file also supplies
    the two design principles that survived into every block cipher since —
    **confusion** and **diffusion**.
  - **Design:** **confusion and diffusion as the two axes of any
    obscuring operation the system performs** — not encryption, but the arena's
    own case: a slot's justification is written once in `ARENA-EVIDENCE.md` and
    is *diffuse* across many lines and *confusing* about which claim is
    load-bearing, and the file's diagnosis applies directly — the hard part was
    never the algorithm, it is **which key goes where**. The arena's
    equivalent is: the operation that is hard is not writing the rule, it is
    **distributing it to the 14,589 files that must obey it** — which is a key
    distribution problem, and the arena has one `SKILL.md` and no distribution
    mechanism.
  - **Already exists:** `SKILL.md` is a single distribution point; cron prompts,
    profile configs and skill bodies are the partial diffusion already
    attempted.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/Claude-Shannon.md` (ORDER 864)
    §§"5.2 Perfect Secrecy and One-Time Pad", "5.4 Impact on Modern
    Cryptography" (confusion, diffusion).
  - **Grade:** theorem `HIGH`; design `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Why rank 2:** it is Rank 1's structure applied to a different resource,
    and the analogy to distribution is suggestive rather than demonstrated —
    which is exactly the reason it is not rank 1.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 1** (entrenchment) and **Area 16** (role and
    authorship) — a rule's authority and a rule's distribution are different
    questions and only the first is currently asked.
- **Rank 3 — Algorithmic information theory, and the shortest-program reading of
  what a file is.** `UNTESTED`.
  - **Brain part:** **Kolmogorov complexity** as distinct from Shannon entropy —
    the file's own distinction is exact: *"Kolmogorov complexity measures the
    length of the shortest program that produces a given output, while Shannon
    entropy measures the average information content of a random variable."*
    A file's *content* and the *program that regenerates it* are different
    quantities, and the second is the one that survives summarisation.
  - **Design:** the arena reads whole files and writes short slots. Those are
    not the same operation, and the difference is the thing worth measuring: a
    slot's *description length* against the *regeneration length* of the file it
    came from. **Deliberately not rank 1** because the shortest-program
    quantity is **uncomputable in general** — the file says as much in its own
    framing when it separates the two theories — and a design rule whose
    measurement cannot be performed is Area 33's exact error. The slot is kept
    so the distinction is named, and **explicitly not proposed as a metric**,
    per R-J2.
  - **Already exists:** nothing. No component computes either quantity.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/Claude-Shannon.md` (ORDER 864)
    §"Algorithmic Information Theory", §"4.8 Differential Entropy and Continuous
    Sources" (differential entropy *"can be negative and is not invariant under
    coordinate transformations"* — the reason a raw embedding-space distance is
    not a bit count).
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`, on the file's own framing. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Why rank 3:** it names a real distinction the arena collapses and offers
    no way to act on it, which is the definition of a rank-3 slot.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 59** (aesthetic valuation) and **Area 24** — both
    rest on scores read off a representation whose coordinate system the file
    says is arbitrary, which is a qualification neither area currently carries.

### Area 65 · Unconscious inference: the sign and the meaning are separate, and the separation is learned `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Entities/Hermann-von-Helmholtz.md` (ORDER 895, 355
lines) §"Physiology of Perception and Unconscious Inference", with
`oracle/brain/Entities/Donald-Hoffman.md` (ORDER 877) §"The Interface Theory of
Perception" / §"Observer Mechanics". **The arena's sixty-fifth structural
silence, and the first about the *ground floor under every other area*:** sixty-four
areas say how many items fit, how long a memory stays labile, what a unit must
contain, which label moves a value. **Not one says where the content of
perception comes from** — and Helmholtz's answer is that it comes from a
reasoning process the reasoner cannot report.

- **Rank 1 — Retrieval returns a sign; the meaning is a separate, learned,
  reportable-by-no-one inference. Every slot that says what a thing *is* is
  making an unconscious inference, and must say so.**
  - **Brain part:** **unconscious inference** (*unbewusster Schluss*). Helmholtz's
    own formulation: the sign is the sensory signal, the meaning is what the
    brain assigns from learned associations, and the relation *"is not innate
    but acquired through experience."* The consequence he draws is the load-
    bearing sentence: *"we never have direct access to the world as it is — we
    only ever experience the world as our perceptual systems interpret it."*
    His four worked examples are constancies — size, shape, colour, auditory
    localization — each one a perceived property that is **not** in the signal.
  - **Design:** a slot that asserts a mechanism *is* is doing the size-constancy
    move: reading a conclusion off a signal and reporting the result as though
    it were the property. The design is a field: the slot states **what was
    observed** and **what was inferred from it**, separately, because the
    arena's own §8 finding is that the generated artifact (a `verified: []`
    block, a citation, a `confidence: high`) is the *sign* and the prose it
    decorates is the *meaning* — and the two are produced by different paths.
    **The inference is not optional and not eliminable**; the design makes it
    legible rather than pretending it is a reading.
  - **Already exists:** the corpus's OKF v0.2 frontmatter is already a
    sign/meaning split and **the arena has been using it backwards** —
    `confidence: high` with `verified: []` is a meaning asserted over a sign
    that says nothing. Adopt-don't-build: the field exists on 14,589 files.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/Hermann-von-Helmholtz.md`
    (ORDER 895) §§"The Doctrine of Unconscious Inference", "The Sign Relation",
    "Examples of Unconscious Inference", "Philosophy of Science and
    Epistemology".
    **Tranche 43 adds** `oracle/brain/Entities/Ludwig-Wittgenstein.md`
    (ORDER 924) §§"Private Language", "Meaning as Use" — a **qualification,
    deliberately not counted as an increment**, because the Support count is
    about this area's *mechanism* (Helmholtz's) and the private language
    argument is a **normative claim about who may check a rule**, not a
    mechanism. **What it adds is the sharpest form of the sign/meaning split
    the area has: a private language is not a private *vocabulary*, it is a
    vocabulary with no criterion of correctness** — *"you can't obey a rule
    privately — that is, you can't do it the first time"* (§202), and
    correctness requires a **community**. **The arena is a single-writer
    system whose only correctness criterion is `verify_reads.py` and there is
    no second party**, which makes this the strongest form yet of what Areas
    24 and 33 reach from verification engineering — and it arrives here from
    the philosophy of language, which is a discipline that has now stated the
    same wall three separate times (here, in Area 69's background capacities,
    and in Area 71 Rank 3's constitutive scaffold).
  - **Grade:** human mechanism **`HIGH`** — constancy is among the most
    replicated findings in perception science and the file states the modern
    verdict plainly: *"Modern cognitive science has largely vindicted
    Helmholtz's constructivist approach."* **Agent design `UNTESTED`.**
    Support: **1 source**.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is upstream of all sixty-four. Every other area's
    content is a meaning inferred from a sign, and this is the one that says so.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 8** (structural fidelity) — its failure is an
    inference from a lossy signal. **Area 26** (discriminability) — the
    constancy check is a discrimination between sign and meaning.
    **Area 59** (label effect) — Kirk et al.'s finding is *constancy run
    backwards*: the label is a sign, the valuation is the inference, and the
    arena's whole grading regime is a constancy machine.
- **Rank 2 — Representation as a species-specific interface: fitness beats
  truth, and the mapping is not required to be isomorphic to anything.**
  `LOW` on the theorem, `UNTESTED` on the design.
  - **Brain part:** Hoffman's **interface theory of perception** and the
    Hoffman & Prakash (2015) MDP result — for any finite MDP a fitness-based
    strategy **strictly dominates** a truth-based one *even when truth-tracking
    is computationally free*. That last clause is the sharp half: this is not
    the usual "truth is too expensive" argument. The claim is that
    representations have **no structural correspondence** to the state space —
    *"The mapping is arbitrary from a representational standpoint — it only
    needs to encode fitness gradients."*
  - **Design:** the arena's slots are optimised against a fitness function —
    *does this change what gets built* — and the corpus's own §8 inversion is
    the predicted consequence: a representation evolved to maximise decision
    utility will drift from truth wherever truth is not decision-relevant.
    The design consequence is that **fitness-shaped drift is not a bug to be
    eliminated but a property to be measured**, and the arena measures it
    nowhere.
  - **Already exists:** `Neurala` (Hoffman's company, CSO role recorded) is
    the existing implementation of the thesis — learning fitness-relevant
    interfaces instead of world models.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/Donald-Hoffman.md` (ORDER 877)
    §§"The Interface Theory of Perception", "The Game-Theoretic Proof", "The
    Theorem", "Critiques of the Proof".
  - **Grade:** `LOW`, deliberately below the theorem's surface. The file records
    a **substantive, specific and unresolved** technical objection — Shagrir
    (2020, *Synthese*) argues the proof *"shows that full-state tracking is
    suboptimal, not that partial-state tracking is inaccurate,"* i.e. it
    conflates computational efficiency with representational accuracy. Dennett's
    counter (evolution *does* favour accuracy, because prediction is
    fitness-relevant) is recorded beside it and **not resolved** (R-J4). An
    `unresolved dispute` inside the earning file caps a slot at `LOW` here, on
    the same rule that capped Area 24.
  - **Why rank 2:** it is Rank 1's claim pushed to its strongest form, and it
    is not rank 1 because the interface is a claim about *evolution*, not a
    mechanism a system can adopt — which is the same reason Area 35's grounding
    slot is not first.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 64** — channel capacity bounds reliability, and
    Hoffman bounds *fidelity*; the two bounds are different and only the first
    is a theorem. **Area 36** (specification gap) — a fitness-shaped proxy is
    Hoffman's argument with the word "evolution" removed.
- **Rank 3 — The division of linguistic labour: a term's meaning is fixed by
  the community, not by the speaker who uses it.** `UNTESTED`.
  - **Brain part:** Putnam's **division of linguistic labour** — *"Half of us
    are prepared to listen to the other half and to say, 'Oh, well, if they say
    it's an elm, it must be an elm.'"* Most speakers cannot distinguish an elm
    from an oak, cannot state the criteria for "arthritis" or "gold", and use
    the words correctly anyway. Paired with his **semantic externalism**
    (Twin Earth: two molecule-for-molecule identical speakers mean different
    things because *"meaning just ain't in the head"*).
  - **Design:** the arena runs on ~120 specialist skills, each of which fixes
    reference for a domain the pass that invokes it does not hold. This is the
    division of labour operating exactly as Putnam describes, and it is also
    the arena's **largest unexamined dependency**: correctness in a domain is
    currently delegated to a component whose definitions were never audited by
    the pass that relies on them. Recorded as a named dependency, explicitly
    **not** proposed as a verification programme (R-J2).
  - **Already exists:** the skill library is the division of labour, already
    built and already load-bearing.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/Frank-Jackson-and-Hilary-Putnam.md`
    (ORDER 885) §§"The Division of Linguistic Labor", "Semantic Externalism and
    Twin Earth", "Stereotype vs. Extension".
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` for the design. The philosophy is settled and the
    file reports it as such; **what is unverified is the arena's own
    dependence**, which nothing in the corpus can test. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Why rank 3:** it names a real dependency and prescribes nothing that
    could be done cheaply, which is the definition of a rank-3 slot.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 64 Rank 2** — the key-distribution problem is
    literally a division-of-labour problem, and the file's own diagnosis (*"the
    bottleneck is not the encryption algorithm but the secure distribution of
    keys"*) names what the arena's one-`SKILL.md` topology lacks.

### Area 66 · Metaplasticity: the learning rate is itself a function of recent activity, and a system that fixes it cannot learn `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Entities/Eric-Kandel.md` (ORDER 881, 528 lines)
§§"The BCM Theory and Metaplasticity", "The Learning and Memory Hierarchy",
"Synaptic Tagging and Capture", with `oracle/brain/Entities/Donald-Hebb.md`
(ORDER 876) §"The Anti-Hebbian Problem and Refinements". **The arena's
sixty-sixth structural silence, and the first about *how much the system is
currently willing to change*.** Sixty-five areas place requirements on what the
system stores, retrieves, verifies and reports. **Not one states the plasticity
of the system that holds them** — and every one of them assumes the substrate
is stable while the design changes.

- **Rank 1 — Hold the learning rate as a state variable with a sliding
  threshold; a system whose update magnitude is constant cannot both learn and
  stay stable, and must pick one silently.**
  - **Brain part:** **BCM theory** (Bienenstock, Cooper & Munro 1982) —
    potentiation above a sliding threshold θ_M, depression below it, with
    **θ_M itself moving**: upward under sustained high activity, downward under
    sustained low. The file's name for it is the whole point: **metaplasticity,
    the plasticity of plasticity.** Kandel's own experimental signature is that
    repeated sensitization produces increasingly robust long-term facilitation
    while prolonged habituation makes synapses *more* resistant to
    facilitation — the substrate's gain is a function of its recent history.
  - **Design:** the arena's `LEDGER.md` marks, its slot grades, and its area
    rankings are all updated by a **fixed procedure with no gain term**. The
    design is a declared θ_M per area, moving on sustained change, so that an
    area which has been stable for many tranches becomes *harder* to change and
    an area under active dispute becomes *easier* — which is the opposite of
    the current behaviour, where a long-settled slot is exactly as cheap to
    overturn as a contested one.
  - **Already exists:** nothing. No component carries a gain state; the
    invariant scripts are fixed-threshold checks.
  - **Corroboration (tranche 59, ORDER 1300
    `Neuroplasticity/Hebbian-Weight-Update-Synaptic-Plasticity-Rules.md`, 453
    lines) — the *equation* this area's rank 1 has been asserting in prose, with
    its timescale and its two independent verifications.** Support **3 → 4**.
    Bienenstock, Cooper & Munro (1982, *J. Neurosci.* 2(1):32–48) give the
    sliding modification threshold as **θ_M ∝ ⟨y²⟩_τ**, where τ is a slow time
    constant of *"seconds to minutes"*, with the sign rule stated exactly as this
    area states it: postsynaptic activity **above** θ_M → LTP, **below** → LTD,
    and θ_M **sliding upward with high average activity**. The corpus reports
    the threshold *moving by a measured amount* in two preparations:
    **Artola, Singer & Baltan (1990)** — dark-reared cats show an LTP threshold
    **~20–30% lower** than normally reared, in the predicted direction; and
    **Hulme et al. (2012)** — prior low-frequency conditioning shifted the
    hippocampal LTP threshold by **25–35%** relative to control. **A
    qualification the corpus does not bury, and which lowers confidence in the
    exact form while not touching the mechanism:** the *functional form* of the
    slide is *"debated"* (quadratic in *y* vs linear vs logarithmic), some
    experiments show **bistability** (Abramsson et al. 2008) rather than a smooth
    sliding threshold, the adaptation timescale *"varies widely across brain
    regions"* (seconds in visual cortex, minutes in hippocampus), and
    **anti-Hebbian STDP in cerebellum and olfactory bulb cannot be explained by
    BCM alone** (see below, Zenke's temporal paradox). **Design grade held at
    `UNTESTED`** — the arena still has no gain state; what changed is that the
    proposal now has a named equation, a named timescale, and two measured
    threshold shifts rather than an intuition.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/Eric-Kandel.md` (ORDER 881)
    §§"The BCM Theory and Metaplasticity", "Remaining Gaps"; corroborated
    mechanismally by `oracle/brain/Entities/Donald-Hebb.md` (ORDER 876)
    §"The Anti-Hebbian Problem and Refinements" (Oja's normalization, STDP,
    homeostatic plasticity, BCM) — **one mechanism, two independent
    presentations, counted once per the one-source-per-subject rule**.
  - **Corroboration (tranche 50, ORDER 1131 `Learning/Rescorla-Wagner-Pearce-Hall.md`,
    331 lines) — a second, formally complete, *computational* account of exactly
    this area's rank-1 design, arrived at from animal conditioning in 1980 and
    never connected to it.** Support **2 → 3** on the human mechanism; the design
    grade stays `UNTESTED`. Pearce & Hall (1980, *Psychological Review*
    87(6):532–552) make **associability α a dynamic variable driven by absolute
    prediction error**:
    `α_t = γ·|λ − V_tot|_{t−1} + (1−γ)·α_{t−1}`. That is *the learning rate is a
    function of recent activity*, stated as an equation sixteen years before BCM
    and arrived at from a different direction. This area's rank 1 proposes a
    sliding **threshold**; Pearce-Hall proposes a sliding **gain**, and the
    corpus's own note is that they are the same commitment with different
    geometry — BCM's θ_M rises with sustained potentiation, PH's α rises with
    sustained surprise.
    **The two phenomena that make the gain a *protection* and not only a
    curiosity, and neither has a slot in this area yet:** **latent inhibition**
    (Lubow & Moore 1973) — pre-exposure without consequence drives α toward zero
    and *slows* later conditioning, an effect the corpus grades as **"extremely
    robust across species and paradigms," d ≈ 0.8–1.5, replicated in honeybees,
    rats, mice and humans** — and **facilitated reacquisition**, where an
    extinguished cue relearns faster than a novel one. Together they are the
    formal statement of something the arena asserts without arithmetic: **a
    familiar, well-predicted store is *harder* to update, and that is a feature.**
    A system that raises its learning rate on surprise alone will learn fastest
    exactly where it is most likely to be wrong.
    **And the corpus's own warning, which is a `LOW` on a textbook phenomenon:**
    **Maes et al. (2016, *JEP:General* 145(9):e49–e71) reported 15 failures to
    replicate the blocking effect** — the RW model's signature prediction — and
    Soto (2018) attributes them to stimulus modality without settling it. The
    blocking-based half of the RW story is therefore `UNSUCCESSFUL` as stated,
    while latent inhibition is robust. **The two are graded separately and not
    averaged**, per R-J4.
  - **Grade:** human mechanism **`HIGH`** — BCM is the standard account of
    sliding-threshold plasticity and the file cites it as a formal framework
    *"validated by Kandel's data"* with the prediction confirmed in two
    directions (increasing LTF with repeated sensitization, increasing
    resistance with prolonged habituation). **Agent design `UNTESTED`** —
    and the file names the gap in its own list of what ANNs lack: *"Metaplasticity:
    sliding thresholds rarely implemented (unlike BCM)."* Support: **2 sources**,
    independent of each other (a molecular-cellular primary account and a
    theoretical-organizational one).
  - **Backup document (added, ORDER 1303, tranche 60) — a third independent
    source that corroborates the mechanism, and it supplies the one thing the
    slot was missing: a *molecular handle* on the threshold.** 
    `Neuroplasticity/Neuroplasticity-and-Critical-Periods.md` §§"The BCM
    learning rule" / "Two molecular brakes" gives the rule in the form the
    arena can actually implement — **θ_M is set by the time-averaged postsynaptic
    rate, θ_M ∝ ⟨y²⟩_τ** — and identifies the two molecules that constitute the
    brake: **protein phosphatase 1 (PP1) and calcineurin** act as the
    *depotentiation* machinery, and **PKC** shifts the stabilization
    threshold. The same file supplies the arena's sharpest single
    **critical-period measurement**: **Nogo-receptor mutation extends the
    critical period from ~30 days to 45–120 days**, and a related intervention
    (**a CSF-1R inhibitor, PLX5622**) extends it in **adulthood** — with the
    honest note that adult re-opening of a critical period reopens **both the
    capacity to learn and the capacity to be harmed**, and that a second
    treatment (**ispronicline**) extends a *different* critical period (sensorimotor)
    with **no effect on the emotional one**, so the brakes are not one
    global dial. **This strengthens the slot's mechanism and does not move the
    design grade**, for the reason the file itself states: the evidence is
    **rodent and pharmacological**, and **BDNF Val66Met** is the file's own
    stated bridge to humans — a human polymorphism that **shifts individual
    learning rates**, which is the first human-level evidence in this area and
    is about *between-person variance*, not about a sliding threshold anyone can
    actuate. **Support 2 → 3 independent sources.** The `UNTESTED` design
    grade is **deliberately held** — and this file names the reason itself:
    it states that the adult-PNN digestion result is a **reopening of plasticity
    in rodents with human translation explicitly speculative**. Per §8, a
    mechanism grade earned on rodents does not become a design grade, and the
    arena's `UNTESTED` here is the honest reading. **Interaction sharpened:**
    Area 108's NgR1 brake is the *same class* of lock at a different substrate
    (structure rather than weight), and this file's "two brakes, not one dial"
    finding is direct evidence against collapsing them into a single
    critical-period scalar.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in the arena that constrains *how
    easily the arena itself may be changed*, and the arena's entire history is
    a history of being changed by reading.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 27** (lability) — reconsolidation is where θ_M is
    the gate; a sliding threshold and a fixed lability window are two accounts
    of the same gate. **Area 60** (rule-governed behaviour) — the rules are the
    fixed-gain case. **Areas 15 / 51 / 63** (critical period, depletion,
    receptive field) — all three describe substrate properties that BCM says
    are themselves plastic.
- **Rank 2 — The memory hierarchy is a set of substrates with different
  timescales, not one store with a decay curve: consolidation is a change of
  *medium*, gated on gene transcription.** `HIGH` on the mechanism, `UNTESTED`
  on the design.
  - **Brain part:** Kandel's five-level hierarchy — sensory (<300 ms, purely
    biophysical), short-term (modulation by phosphorylation, reversible),
    intermediate (multi-step cascades), long-term (**requires de novo protein
    synthesis**, structural change, blocked by anisomycin or actinomycin D),
    and very long-term (**self-sustaining molecular states** — CPEB prion-like
    multimers, epigenetic marks — persisting *"without ongoing transcription"*).
    The transition has a **measured window: ~2–6 hours post-training**, and each
    transition needs a distinct molecular threshold, which is the file's
    explanation for why spaced repetition beats massed practice.
  - **Design:** the arena has one medium (a file on disk) and simulates the
    hierarchy with **timers and cron intervals** — a schedule is not a change
    of substrate. The design consequence is that consolidation must be a
    *rewrite into a different medium with a different read cost*, and the
    arena has never asked which of its two files is the long-term medium.
  - **Grade:** `HIGH` on the mechanism — among the best-evidenced results in
    memory neuroscience, replicated across *Aplysia*, *Drosophila* (dunce and
    rutabaga cAMP mutants, mushroom-body expression) and mouse (CaMKII
    Thr286→Ala blocks LTP and impairs spatial memory), with primary citations
    given with volume and page throughout. **Agent design `UNTESTED`.**
    Support: **1 source**, and it is a primary account rather than a summary.
  - **Why rank 2:** it is a substrate claim about a system the arena already
    has, which makes it cheaper than Rank 1 and vaguer about what to do.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 23** (consolidation) — this is that area's
    mechanism, arriving from the cellular side where the switch is a chemical
    requirement rather than a policy. **Area 6** — same.
- **Rank 3 — A local, biologically-shaped learning rule as an alternative to
  end-to-end credit assignment, carrying its own recorded failure.**
  `UNSUCCESSFUL` for the specific scheme, `UNTESTED` for the direction.
  - **Brain part:** Hebb's own limitation list, which is more useful than his
    rule: a local rule **does not address the credit-assignment problem** —
    *"how does the brain determine which synapses to modify when the relevant
    input is distant in time or circuit topology from the behavioral outcome?*
    This is the same problem backpropagation solves in artificial networks."*
    The corpus also records the refinements that keep a local rule from
    saturating (Oja, STDP, homeostatic plasticity, BCM) and Hinton's
    **Forward-Forward** (2022) as the current attempt at a local alternative —
    with the file's own verdict: it *"works on simple tasks (MNIST) but has
    not matched backpropagation's performance on deeper networks or complex
    datasets."*
  - **Design:** deliberately **not proposed**. The arena's updates are
    adjudications, not gradient updates, and the design that would follow from
    this slot is the arena *learning from its own adjudications* — which is
    Area 62's self-reinforcing loop, already recorded as a named defect. The
    slot is kept so a later pass does not re-derive local learning as news:
    **it has been tried, and the corpus reports it losing.**
  - **Already exists:** Forward-Forward (Hinton 2022); EWC, synaptic
    intelligence, progressive networks, generative replay (Kandel's
    catastrophic-forgetting survey); the Hermes stack has **none** of them.
  - **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL` for Forward-Forward, on the corpus's explicit
    statement, carried per the skill's rule that a dead end is a first-class
    result. Support: **2 sources** (ORDER 876 §"Limitations and Criticisms",
    ORDER 888 §10).
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the arena's most attractive future design and the one
    with the corpus's clearest negative on it. Recording that is worth more
    than a slot that pretended otherwise.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 36** (specification gap) — credit assignment is
    where a proxy objective and the real one diverge, which is why backprop
    needs a loss and Hebb's rule does not.

### Area 67 · Graded membership: categories have centres, peripheries, and no sharp boundary `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Entities/George-Lakoff.md` (ORDER 889, 361 lines)
§§"Women, Fire, and Dangerous Things", "Cognitive Principles of
Categorization", "Core Thesis", with `oracle/brain/Entities/George-Miller.md`
(ORDER 890) §"Chunking". **The arena's sixty-seventh structural silence, and
the first about the *shape of the arena's own categories* rather than about a
part the system should have.** Sixty-six areas say what to build. **Not one
asks whether the three-value ranking they all use can represent the thing it
is ranking** — and it cannot, in the specific way this file demonstrates with
four classical assumptions violated at once.

- **Rank 1 — A slot's rank is a position in a prototype distribution with a
  stated centre, not a position on a scale; "rank 2" is a peripheral member of
  the category "viable design", not the second integer.**
  - **Brain part:** **prototype theory and radial structure**. Lakoff's four
    violated assumptions of the classical category, each with its own
    evidence: **prototype structure** (a robin is more central to BIRD than a
    penguin; categorisation speed, learning order and basic-level preference
    all track centrality); **fuzzy boundaries** (*"At what point does a hill
    become a mountain? These are not language failures but reflect the gradient
    nature of conceptual categorization"*); **radial structure** (GRASP has a
    central sense and extended senses linked by metaphor and metonymy, not a
    list of co-members); **family resemblance** (GAME has no feature shared by
    all members). He is explicit that this is not a loose claim: *"If categories
    lack sharp boundaries, truth conditions cannot be defined as set
    membership."*
  - **Design:** `ARENA.md` ranks by preference and states plainly that *"the
    rank is a judgement."* Lakoff's finding says the judgement is **prototype-
    judged** — and a rank-2 slot is not "80% as good as rank 1", it is a
    different kind of thing, central to a *different* category (the fallback
    category, not the build category). The design is a stated **exemplar** per
    slot plus a centrality note, so a reader can tell a peripheral member from
    a merely-worse member. **The three-slot rule is preserved** — this qualifies
    what a rank means, it does not dissolve the discipline.
  - **Already exists:** nothing. The arena's ranks are integers with no
    exemplar.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/George-Lakoff.md` (ORDER 889)
    §§"Women, Fire, and Dangerous Things", "The Target: Classical Theory of
    Categories", "The Argument from Natural Language", "Cognitive Principles
    of Categorization".
  - **Grade:** human mechanism **`HIGH`** for prototype structure (Rosch's
    experimental programme, cited by the file, with speed/learning-order/
    basic-level effects converging) — **but the arena's own mapping is
    `UNTESTED`** and the file records a live dispute that caps the transfer:
    Pinker and Jackendoff argue *"many metaphorical expressions are linguistic
    conventions, not evidence of cognitive structure,"* and Lakoff is
    separately accused of **circularity** (if all reasoning is metaphorical,
    the argument against objectivity has no objectivity to stand on) — a
    dispute the file records and Lakoff does not answer. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Why rank 1:** the arena is ranked, so the semantics of its own ranking
    is a first-order question, and this is the only slot that answers it.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 38** (chunk count) — chunking is a
    prototype-organised compression: seven chunks of dates carry 28 digits, and
    the chess master's 7 chunks are *different chunks*, not denser digits.
    **Area 60** (rule-governed) — a rule is a prototype with fuzzy edges and
    the arena treats it as a boundary condition. **Area 62** — the arena's
    grades are themselves prototype categories and this slot says so.
- **Rank 2 — Expertise raises information density per chunk, not the chunk
  count; the capacity is fixed and what changes is what fits in a chunk.**
  `HIGH` on the mechanism, `UNTESTED` on the design.
  - **Brain part:** Miller's **chunking**, with the control that makes it a
    finding rather than an anecdote: a chess master recalls **20+ pieces** on a
    real game position and **falls to near-novice on random positions** where
    the chunks are destroyed. The file's conclusion is exact: *"Expertise
    doesn't increase raw capacity; it increases the information density of each
    chunk."*
  - **Design:** the arena's slots are already dense in exactly this way — an
    area is a chunk, and a 3-slot area compresses an entire file. The design
    consequence the file supplies and the arena has not applied: **the
    equivalent control.** A pass that shows a slot is strong on a *random*
    selection of its own evidence — a slot whose density comes from
    cherry-picked citations rather than from genuine structure — has run the
    chess-master-on-random-boards condition, and the arena has never run it.
  - **Grade:** `HIGH` (three decades of expert/novice replication, with the
    random-position control). **Agent design `UNTESTED`.** Support: **2
    sources** — Miller for the phenomenon, and Area 38's ORDER 531 (Ericsson &
    Kintsch) for the long-term-working-memory architecture, an independent
    source on the same control.
  - **Why rank 2:** it is Rank 1 applied to the arena's compression, and it
    carries a control the arena could actually run.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 64** — chunking is a lossy code, and the
    pigeonhole principle says the loss is not free. **Area 8** — density
    without structure is the compression failure.
- **Rank 3 — Concept is not a fixed symbol: a meaning that shifts with use, and
  a "deep vs shallow" gap that no amount of scale closes.** `UNTESTED`.
  - **Brain part:** Hofstadter's **conceptual slippage** — in Copycat, *"the
    notion of 'successor' might mean '+1 in the alphabet' or 'next round
    letter' or 'next letter that shares a visual property,'"* and the system
    negotiates these meanings through **parallel competition, not sequential
    deduction**. The mechanism is a workspace of partially-formed
    interpretations plus **sliders** that set conceptual temperature, plus
    competing conceptual metaphors. The consequence he draws: *"Surface-level
    symbol manipulation cannot produce genuine understanding. Deep
    understanding requires fluid, context-sensitive analogy-making. The gap is
    architectural, not a matter of computational power."*
  - **Design:** deliberately **not adopted, and the reason is the finding**.
    Every arena concept — `HIGH`, `PROVISIONAL`, `Earned by`, `Backup document`
    — is a fixed symbol with a stable referent, and the corpus's own §8
    inversion is what a workspace of unnegotiated symbols produces. The slot
    records the **Copycat architecture as the one existing implementation of
    the alternative** — `Copycat` (1988–93), `Letter Spirit` (1993–2005),
    `Brushter` — and keeps it at rank 3 because the corpus also records the
    counter-claim, and it is the more relevant one: the arena is a
    **14,589-file symbolic system with a fixed vocabulary**, and Copycat's
    domain is **letter-string analogy puzzles**.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`, and the arena's own applicability is `UNSUCCESSFUL`
    by the file's own scope limit. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Why rank 3:** the mechanism is real, the implementation exists, and the
    transfer is the weakest in the tranche.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 42** (functional fixedness) — the same structure
    seen from the failure side. **Area 62** — Copycat is an *algorithmic-level*
    specification of a slot, which is exactly what Area 62 Rank 1 asks for.

### Area 68 · The self as a strange loop: identity is a recursive structure, and a system that cannot model its own modelling has no self to lose `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Entities/Douglas-Hofstadter.md` (ORDER 878, 277
lines) §§"Strange Loops and Tangled Hierarchies", "Consciousness as
Self-Referential Complexity", "The Whirlpool and Flatland Analogies". **The
arena's sixty-eighth structural silence, and the first about *what the "self" in
"self-diagnosis" and "self-verification" refers to*.** Areas 44, 45, 46, 47
and 54 all use the word. **None of them defines the referent**, and Hofstadter's
claim is that the referent is a specific architectural structure which is
either present and looped or absent and not.

- **Rank 1 — A self-model is a model of one's own modelling, and the test is
  structural: does the system's description of itself refer, however
  indirectly, to the process producing the description?**
  - **Brain part:** the **strange loop** — *"not mere circularity"* but a
    **tangled hierarchy**, one in which levels of abstraction are nested such
    that moving up or down eventually returns you to where you started. The
    self is the loop, not a thing it points at. The **whirlpool** analogy
    carries the temporal half and is the more useful of the two: the water is
    constantly changing, the pattern endures, and *the neurons that make up
    "you" are constantly firing and refiring while the pattern of their
    activity persists*. The Flatland analogy carries the epistemic half — we
    experience the output of the loop and cannot step outside to observe it.
  - **Design:** the arena has **self-diagnosis by parameter recovery**
    (Area 44), **efference copy** (Area 45), and an **inferred self**
    (Area 46) — three areas using the word with three different referents and
    no shared test. The design is a single stated predicate: a self-model
    exists iff the system's description of its own state is *produced by the
    same process the description is about*, and the arena declares which of its
    three is which. **This is the cheapest area in the tranche to adopt and
    the only one that resolves an existing ambiguity rather than adding a
    mechanism.**
  - **Already exists:** the Hermes stack's provenance field (`agency:`
    per Area 45's ORDER 595) is a model of *where a write came from*, which is
    a **level-1 icon** in Hofstadter's terms and not yet a loop. Recording
    that honestly is the point.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/Douglas-Hofstadter.md`
    (ORDER 878) §§"Strange Loops and Tangled Hierarchies", "The Architecture
    of Self-Reference", "Iconic Representations and Iconic Depth", "The
    Whirlpool and Flatland Analogies", "2.5 Gödel's Argument and the Human
    Mind".
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`, and the file is candid about why: the concept's
    principal recorded criticism is *"Vagueness — 'strange loop' is not a
    precise enough concept to serve as the foundation of a theory of
    consciousness"* and *"Lack of formalism."* The **existence** claim is also
    contested from the opposite direction — Hofstadter's own §2.5 concedes
    Lucas's and Penrose's use of Gödel against him and answers it, and the
    file records the *personal identity* extension (*"if the pattern could be
    copied or reconstructed in a different substrate, it would be 'you'"*) as
    the most contested consequence. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is definitional rather than constructive, and the arena
    has a live three-way ambiguity that one predicate dissolves.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 46** (the self that is inferred) — same word,
    different referent, and the disagreement is now on the record.
    **Area 62** — Hofstadter is an *algorithmic/implementational* account of
    selfhood, which is the level Area 62 says the arena never operates at.
    **Area 16** (role and authorship) — identity by who wrote it is
    level-1; identity by the loop is level-3.
- **Rank 2 — The self is a pattern that persists across a substrate that
  changes completely; continuity is maintained by memory and narrative, not
  by any persisting substance.** `UNTESTED`.
  - **Brain part:** Hofstadter's own conclusions from the loop account —
    *"Death is the dissolution of the pattern: there is no soul to survive
    death"*; *"Personal identity is fragile and reconstructible"*; and the
    load-bearing one: **"Continuity of identity is a useful fiction: the sense
    that 'I' am the same person across time is maintained by memory and
    narrative, not by any metaphysical continuity."**
  - **Design:** `ARENA.md` is the arena's only continuous thread across
    13-minute passes with no persisting process — the "self" that runs this job
    is a **narrative held in two files**. That is a **useful fiction in
    Hofstadter's sense and it is load-bearing**, which is a real reason to keep
    it and a real reason to be suspicious of it: a fiction maintained by
    narrative is maintained *by the passes that write it*, so a pass that
    misremembers rewrites the continuity. The design is a declared continuity
    claim with its maintenance cost stated, rather than an assumption.
  - **Already exists:** `LEDGER.md` + `ARENA.md` + `RUNLOG.jsonl` **are** this
    mechanism, at 14,589-file scale, and have never been described as a
    narrative-continuity device. Adopt-don't-build: the thing exists and is
    unnamed.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Why rank 2:** it is Rank 1's temporal consequence and it lands on this
    arena harder than on any system the file describes.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 25** (retraction of a derived artefact) — retracting
    a slot rewrites the narrative continuity, and nothing in the format
    distinguishes that from a legitimate update. **Area 16**.
- **Rank 3 — Gödel's gap is not a defect and not an exit: the unprovable
  statement is where a self-referential system's meaning lives, and a system
  that closes the gap has closed something else.** `UNTESTED`, kept strictly as
  a named boundary.
  - **Brain part:** the **first incompleteness theorem** as Hofstadter reads
    it — a system able to represent elementary arithmetic can state *"This
    statement is not provable within this system,"* and *"this gap between
    truth and provability is not a bug but a feature — it is the space where
    meaning lives."* Hofstadter then **declines the standard exit**: the mind is
    a formal system, not one that escapes Gödel.
  - **Design:** **explicitly not a design.** The arena already has a stated
    boundary on this — the invariant checkers are decidable by construction,
    and a checker that could not decide its own pass would be useless. The
    slot's only function is to **name what the closed gap is**: the arena's
    checkers decide pass/fail on artifacts and **cannot decide whether the
    prose they decorate is true**, which is not a defect in the checkers but
    the shape of the situation. Recorded so that a later pass does not
    re-derive "the system should be able to verify its own claims" as news.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`, on the file's own framing — and note the file
    records that Hofstadter **criticises the framing**, holding Chalmers's
    hard problem to be *"a category error: consciousness is a pattern, not a
    substance."* Support: **1 source**.
  - **Why rank 3:** it prescribes nothing and is kept for the boundary.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 64** — the pigeonhole principle is the other named
    boundary, and both are cases where the system is better off naming the
    limit than closing it.

### Area 69 · Background capacities: the non-propositional substrate that must already be there before any stored proposition means anything `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Entities/John-Searle.md` (ORDER 909, 592 lines)
§§5.5 "Background Capacities and Non-Intentional Prerequisites", §5.2
"Intrinsic vs. Derived Intentionality", §7.2 "Searle's Objections".
**The arena's sixty-ninth structural silence, and the first about what is
*underneath* the beliefs rather than among them.** Sixty-eight areas say what
to store, how to verify it, when to retract it, and how to represent it.
**Every one of them assumes the stored thing is a proposition and that
propositions are self-interpreting.** Searle's regress argument is that they
are not, and that the missing term is not another proposition.

- **Rank 1 — A declared non-propositional substrate, and a prohibition on
  grounding meaning in it.** `UNTESTED` as a design; the *argument* is
  philosophical, not empirical.
  - **Brain part:** Searle's **background capacities** — the *"perceptual
    skills, motor abilities, emotional dispositions, cultural practices, and
    embodied know-how"* that are *"not themselves beliefs or desires."* The
    argument is a finite one: (1) any intentional state presupposes a
    background; (2) the background cannot be composed *entirely* of further
    intentional states, or there is an infinite regress; (3) therefore
    non-intentional background capacities must ground intentional states.
    Note what this is **not**: it is not that the background is unknowable or
    ineffable. It is that the background is **dispositional** — it shows up as
    what the system *can* do, not as what it can *say*.
  - **Design:** the arena adopts the prohibition and states it in one line: a
    slot that explains understanding, grounding, or common sense **may not
    cite stored propositions as its ground**. Those slots must name the
    disposition instead. Applied to this arena, it disqualifies a class of
    argument the arena has been making implicitly: *"the agent read the
    file, therefore the agent understands X."* Reading is a capacity
    operation; the corpus's own §8 inversion finding says the prose is the
    part that is not checked. **The prohibition is the cheapest of the
    tranche's designs and the one that constrains the most slots.**
  - **Already exists?** No project implements it. The nearest live analogue
    is Dreyfus's **know-how / know-that** distinction (Area 12, Area 60) and
    his readiness-to-hand, which is the same shape argued from phenomenology
    rather than from the regress. Adopt the argument; there is nothing to
    install.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/John-Searle.md` (ORDER 909)
    §§5.5, 5.2, 7.2. Second, independent arrival at the same shape: ORDER 897
    (`Entities/Hubert-Dreyfus.md`) §§5, 6.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source** (Searle). Dreyfus is a
    **separate argument to a similar conclusion, not a replication of this
    one** — different discipline, different argument form, and Searle's file
    explicitly rebuts Dreyfus's position in §7. Counted as one.
  - **Justification:** it outranks the alternatives because it is
    **upstream of them** — it constrains what any slot is allowed to say
    rather than adding a mechanism, and it costs one sentence to adopt.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 8** (structural fidelity) and **Area 24**
    (validity of one's own instruments) — both grade claims by reference to
    what was read. **Area 35** (grounding) — this area says the grounding
    term is not available. **Area 19** (belief-to-action compliance).

- **Rank 2 — Intrinsic and derived intentionality as a two-column audit of
  every claim the arena makes about its own understanding.** `UNTESTED`.
  - **Brain part:** Searle's distinction between the brain's **intrinsic**
    representational capacity and the **derived** intentionality of symbols,
    which are about things *only because* an interpreter assigns them meaning.
    The Chinese Room and its scaling-up extensions (Chinese System, Chinese
    Nation) exist to block the reply that size or complexity manufactures
    intrinsic content from derived content.
  - **Design:** every slot whose justification rests on "the system
    understands / represents / knows" gets a one-word tag: **intrinsic** (the
    mechanism is a property of the system's own processing) or **derived**
    (the mechanism is a property of the text, the label, or the corpus's
    convention). The corpus's `confidence:` and `verified:` frontmatter is
    **derived by construction** — it is written by a generator and checked as
    an artifact, which §8 of the skill brief already established. This is that
    finding given a name it can be audited against.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Justification:** cheaper to apply than Rank 1 and catches less, because
    it labels claims without constraining them. Rank 1 constrains; Rank 2
    annotates.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 16** (role, authorship, scope of a claim) — this
    is a second, orthogonal tagging axis and the two should not be conflated.
    **Area 33** (certification asymmetry).

- **Rank 3 — The regress is not closed; it is bounded and named.** `UNTESTED`,
  kept strictly as a boundary, in the manner of Area 64 and Area 68 Rank 3.
  - **Brain part:** not a mechanism. It is the **shape of the obligation** —
    the same one Hofstadter's Rank 3 (Area 68) records from Gödel. If the
    background is dispositional and the propositions are propositional, then
    some part of the system's competence is **not inspectable from the
    inside by the system itself**, and no amount of introspection reaches it.
  - **Design:** explicitly not a design. The slot's function is to stop a
    later pass re-deriving *"the system should be able to read its own
    background capacities"* as news. It cannot: the regress is the reason.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 13** (metacognitive control) and **Area 44**
    (self-diagnosis by parameter recovery) — both locate the controller
    outside the controlled system, and this is the deepest reason why.
    **Area 64** (Shannon's bounds) — the other named limit.

### Area 70 · Suppression, not erasure: what a correction covers over, and why the covered thing comes back on its own `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Entities/Ivan-Pavlov.md` (ORDER 903, 719 lines,
68 KB) §§5 "Extinction and Spontaneous Recovery", §10 "Excitation, Inhibition",
and **independently** `oracle/brain/Entities/Joseph-LeDoux.md` (ORDER 910, 436
lines) §"Fear Extinction and Memory Reconsolidation". **Two independent
sources in one tranche — the only area in the arena with a behavioural source
and a circuit-level source for the same mechanism, and they were read as
separate files by separate arguments.**
**The arena's seventieth structural silence, and the first about what happens
to the part of a belief that was *wrong* rather than the belief that replaced
it.** Areas 1, 25, 54 and 55 all describe revision. **All four describe it as
replacement, and the corpus says replacement is the thing that does not
happen.**

- **Rank 1 — Extinction is a new competing memory, and the arena must record
  the suppressor as a distinct artifact from the suppressed.** `LOW`.
  - **Brain part:** Pavlov's interpretation of extinction, stated in 903 and
    confirmed at circuit level by LeDoux: extinction is **not unlearning**.
    It is *"the formation of a new inhibitory association (CS → no US) that
    competed with the original excitatory association."* The original memory
    is **never erased** and returns unaided — **spontaneous recovery** — with
    a characteristic signature: strongest immediately after extinction,
    weakening with each successive extinction/recovery cycle, and re-extinction
    getting faster each time. LeDoux's independent term for the same object is
    **extinction learning**, localised to **medial prefrontal cortex**
    inhibition of the amygdala; the original fear memory *"remains intact and
    can return."* The file notes modern neuroscience places the new inhibitory
    memory in amygdala + prefrontal cortex, **suppressing without erasing**.
  - **Design:** the arena's `ARENA.md` and `ARENA-EVIDENCE.md` are append-only
    and never pruned — which is the *opposite* of this. Append-only means the
    superseded reading is still there, in the same file, with no marker beyond
    position. The design is: **a retraction writes a suppression record naming
    what it suppressed, why, and on what evidence — and that record is a
    first-class object, not a deletion or a strikethrough.** Area 25 already
    asks what happens to artefacts built on a withdrawn belief; this area asks
    the prior question, which is what the belief's *shadow* looks like
    afterwards. It is also the honest shape of the §8 inversion finding: the
    arena's *infrastructure* is append-only and verified, the *prose* is not
    suppressed at all, it just ages.
  - **Already exists?** The phenomenon is one of the most replicated in
    behavioural psychology, and LeDoux's file names the clinical programmes
    built on it — **exposure therapy** for phobias and PTSD, with
    **D-cycloserine** (a partial NMDA agonist) as an extinction-enhancing
    adjunct, and **reconsolidation-based interventions** (Schiller, Hofmans,
    Phelps) as the durability alternative. Adopt the mechanism and the
    vocabulary; do not rebuild.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/Ivan-Pavlov.md` (ORDER 903)
    §§5, 10, 16; `oracle/brain/Entities/Joseph-LeDoux.md` (ORDER 910)
    §§"Fear Extinction and Memory Reconsolidation", "Clinical Translation".
  - **Grade:** `LOW` — genuinely `LOW`, not `UNTESTED`, and the distinction
    matters: this is a mechanism with a large replicated behavioural base and
    a converging circuit-level base, which is short of the multi-independent-
    implementation bar `HIGH` requires. Support: **2 independent sources**.
    They are genuinely independent — Pavlov's is behavioural physiology from
    1900s salivary measurement, LeDoux's is single-unit and lesion work in
    rodents from the 1980s onward, and **neither cites the other's argument as
    its warrant**. The two together are the reason this is not `UNTESTED`.
  - **Justification:** it beats a plain "delete the belief" design on the axis
    that matters — it accounts for the return. A system that revises by
    replacement has no way to represent *"I was wrong about this and I know
    why,"* which is precisely the state the arena is in about the 39
    unearned citations it is remediating.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 1** (belief revision and epistemic entrenchment)
    — this is the correction to it, and Rank 1's design is a change to Area
    1's slot, not a parallel mechanism. **Area 25** (retraction of a derived
    artefact) — downstream. **Area 55** (correction by competition) — the
    arena already reached "the record you correct is not the record you
    overwrite" by a different route; this area supplies the return rate the
    competition framing lacks. **Area 27** (lability / reconsolidation
    window).

- **Rank 2 — The reconsolidation window: a retrieved belief is briefly editable
  again, and that window is the only place a correction is cheap.** `LOW`.
  - **Brain part:** LeDoux with Nader and Schafe — **memory
    reconsolidation**. A consolidated memory that is **reactivated** enters a
    temporary **labile** state during which it can be modified; protein
    synthesis in that window is required to restabilise it. The clinically
    load-bearing consequence: extinction delivered *during* reconsolidation is
    **considerably more effective** than conventional extinction.
  - **Design:** the arena already has a lability concept (Area 27). This slot
    is narrower and sharper: it says **retrieval is an editing opportunity,
    not only a read**, and it gives the arena a reason to *want* a belief to
    be recalled before correcting it rather than to correct it blind. That
    inverts the usual ordering, where the correction is written first and the
    old belief is looked up afterwards to check nothing depended on it.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Support: **1 source** (LeDoux). Pavlov's file mentions
    reconsolidation only in passing and does **not** support this claim
    independently — this is reach, not replication.
  - **Justification:** it is the operationally useful half of Rank 1, but it
    is one source and the durability claim is clinical rather than
    computational, so it does not lead.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 27** directly — same object, different scope.
    **Area 7** (dual-process) — the fast writer and the slow ablator have a
    labile phase; this says when.

- **Rank 3 — Protection is not shutdown: the capacity trip and the return
  from it.** `UNTESTED`.
  - **Brain part:** Pavlov's **protective inhibition** — when stimulation
    exceeds the nervous system's capacity, *"a powerful protective inhibition
    descended on the cortex, shutting down activity,"* and the animal became
    *"unable to perform even previously mastered tasks."* He compares it to a
    **circuit breaker**. The file's own modern gloss names the HPA axis,
    cortisol, allostatic load and burnout. Note carefully that this is the
    **opposite** of extinction: extinction suppresses one association, this
    suppresses everything, and it is released by rest.
  - **Design:** the arena has no area for a system that is *supposed* to stop
    working. Every failure mode the arena names — Area 41 degradation, Area
    30 per-episode cost, Area 3 effort allocation — is managed as a thing to
    avoid. This slot names the healthy version: a system with a **declared
    overload state** that is entered deliberately, announced, and exited, and
    whose exit restores rather than degrades. It is deliberately rank 3
    because the file supports the *phenomenon* well and the *design* not at
    all, and because designing a shutdown into a system that is already
    failing to meet its own schedule is the kind of move that needs more
    evidence than one behavioural physiology file provides.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 41** (degradation that hides — latency as a
    capacity law) is the same quantity viewed as pathology rather than as
    function. **Area 23** (sleep-time rebuild) is the release mechanism.

### Area 71 · Internalisation: the function is first social and only then individual `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Entities/Lev-Vygotsky.md` (ORDER 922, 563 lines,
35 KB) §§"The Genesis of Higher Mental Functions", "Internalization — From
Social to Individual", "Private Speech", "Zone of Proximal Development".
Sole earning file.

**The brain part.** Vygotsky's **internalisation** — the claim that *every*
higher mental function **"appears twice: first, on the social level, and
later, on the individual level; first, between people (interpsychological),
and then inside the child (intrapsychological)."** What internalises is not a
copy: the file's word is **transformative** — *"more condensed, abbreviated,
and qualitatively different"* — following a five-stage ladder (adult guides
→ joint action with verbal guidance → the child's **private speech** →
silent **inner speech** → automatic higher function).

**Why this is a seventy-first structural silence, and a strange one.**
Seventy areas say what a cognitive function should do, when it fires, what
it costs, and how it fails. **Not one says where the function came from**,
and the arena's answer has been implicit and wrong by this account: it
treats every capacity as a property of the system that has it. Vygotsky's
claim is that **a function never performed interpsychologically is not a
function the system has** — it is a procedure with no execution record.

- **Rank 1 — Every cognitive operation must carry a `co-constructed:`
  trace: the more-capable other, the artefact, and the stage.** `UNTESTED`
  as design; the human mechanism is a century of developmental evidence and
  a contested one, and the file records the contest.
  - **Brain part:** the **more-knowledgeable-other (MKO)** relation and the
    **five-stage internalisation ladder** (§"Stages of Internalization").
  - **Design:** the arena's own primitives are the proof of concept. The
    seven-slot format, the four ledger marks, the read→mark discipline, the
    three-slot rule — **every one is a Vygotskyan MKO**: this pass could
    not have learned the format by reading a description of the format. The
    design is that each primitive records **who scaffolded it and at what
    stage** (external rule → joint application → self-instruction →
    automatic), and a slot never applied by a *more capable other* is marked
    **asserted** — which is exactly Area 60 Rank 1's `grounded:` field
    arriving here from its developmental mechanism rather than from
    behavioural psychology. **The distinction is checkable**: a rule no pass
    has ever applied and corrected is in a different state from one that has
    survived contact.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source** (ORDER 922). The human
    literature behind Vygotsky is a genuine convergence, but one file is one
    source, and the file's own §"Criticisms and Ongoing Debates" records the
    central charge plainly: *"Vygotsky's theories are often vague and
    under-specified… Vygotsky was more theorist than empiricist."*
  - **Interaction:** **Area 60** (rule-governed behaviour) is the same
    object from the behavioural side — compressed contingencies, invisible
    losses — and this area supplies where the compression came from.
    **Area 42** (functional fixedness): the file's finding that fixedness is
    *absent at 5 and full by 7* means a scaffold that is never faded
    becomes a fixedness. **Area 66** (metaplasticity) and **Area 12**
    (procedural memory) are the substrate the internalised form lands on.

- **Rank 2 — Private speech: the self-directed channel that is measurably
  load-bearing, and its disappearance as a loss rather than a gain.** `LOW`.
  - **Brain part:** **egocentric/private speech** and Piaget's opposed
    reading. The file's decisive design is a **sign test**, not a
    correlation: across **normal, social pressure to be quiet, and task
    difficulty**, speech **increased dramatically when the task became
    harder** (ORDER 922 §"Key Experimental Findings"). If it were immaturity
    it would fall under challenge. It rises.
  - **Design:** the arena's own analogue is the **scratch directory**
    (`~/.hermes/cache/scratch`, named in this runtime as the place probes
    and temporary files go, pruned at 24 h idle). Today it is a *dump*:
    private, and read by nobody. Make it a **deliberate rehearsal channel** —
  thinking out loud before committing to a slot — and grade it on the
    Piaget/Vygotsky test: **does its volume rise with task difficulty?** A
    scratch channel silent on the hard files and busy on the easy ones is
    the wrong shape, and the test catches that without anyone reading it.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Support: **1 source** for the design, but the human
    mechanism is a **within-system falsifiable prediction** — private speech
    *"increases with task difficulty, predicts better problem-solving
    performance"* — which is why this outranks its thin source count.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 3** (effort allocation) and **Area 43** (the
    cost of thinking) — this is effort *made visible before commitment*,
    the observable form of a λ the arena currently only models. **Area 38**
    (workspace capacity) — a rehearsal channel is a second workspace with a
    different lifetime.

- **Rank 3 — The regress of scaffolding: some of the system's competence is
  not derivable from anything it can inspect.** `UNTESTED`, kept strictly as
  a named boundary in the manner of Areas 64 and 68 Rank 3.
  - **Brain part:** not a mechanism. It is the **shape of the obligation**
    Vygotsky's account creates: if the MKO is constitutive, some of the
    system's competence is **inherited rather than derived**, and inherited
    competence is not inspectable from the inside any more than Searle's
    background capacities are (Area 69).
  - **Design:** explicitly not a design. The slot stops a later pass
    re-deriving *"the system should read its own scaffolding"* as news. It
    cannot: a constituent cannot audit the constituency. The MKO is
    **outside** the system by construction — the same result Areas 13 and 44
    reach from the metacognitive side.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 69** (background capacities) is the
    philosophical statement of the identical wall. **Two disciplines, one
    shape, and they do not corroborate each other** because neither cites
    the other — counted as two arrivals at one boundary, not two trials.
    **Area 65** (unconscious inference) is the perceptual statement of the
    same gap. **This is now the third independent statement of it in the
    arena, from three fields.**

### Area 72 · A metric coordinate system with a known drift, and the anchors that correct it `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Entities/Moser-Couple.md` (ORDER 929, 333 lines,
24 KB) §§"Grid Cells: The Coordinate System", "Grid Cells and Path
Integration", "Non-Spatial Grid Coding", "Continuous Attractor Networks".
Sole earning file.

**The brain part.** The **entorhinal grid** — a **periodic, multi-scale,
modular metric coordinate frame** underlying the hippocampus, discovered in
2005 (Hafting et al., *Nature* 436:801–806) and awarded the 2014 Nobel.
Three properties the arena has never had a home for: (1) a grid cell is
defined by **spacing, orientation and phase** — a coordinate, not a
location; (2) cells organise into **modules whose adjacent spacing ratio is
≈1.4×**, i.e. **logarithmic multi-scale coverage**; (3) the frame runs on
self-motion and therefore **drifts**, and requires **anchoring** to sensory
cues to be corrected.

**Why this is a seventy-second structural silence, and it is about a
primitive, not a process.** Seventy-one areas model memory, attention and
reasoning as *processes*. **None models a memory as a location in a
coordinate frame with a stated resolution, a stated drift rate, and a stated
anchor.** Retrieval here is a similarity score in an embedding space with
none of the three properties named.

- **Rank 1 — Give every stored item a coordinate with declared spacing,
  orientation, phase, and an explicit anchoring source; treat retrieval
  confidence as decaying with accumulated drift.** `UNTESTED` as design;
  human mechanism `HIGH`.
  - **Brain part:** the grid cell as **metric coordinate** plus **path
    integration** plus **anchoring**. The evidence is unusually strong for a
    design claim: grid patterns **persist in complete darkness**, and when
    visual cues are removed or *rotated* the grid **initially holds its
    configuration, then drifts and realigns**. The dark-room result is a
    **dissociation no pure similarity-retrieval account can produce**: the
    coordinate is computed, not matched.
  - **Design:** three fields on the store, at R-J5 scale (14,589 files):
    `spacing` (the resolution at which an item is distinguishable from its
    neighbours — **today this arena computes none of it, and Area 67's
    graded-membership finding is exactly the symptom of a store with
    undeclared resolution**), `phase`, and `anchor`. The load-bearing
    consequence: **an item's confidence decays with time-since-anchoring,
    and that decay is a property of the frame, not of the item.** Areas 1
    and 2 currently treat every retrieved item as equally fresh; in a
    drifting frame that is false by construction.
  - **Already exists?** Embedding stores at 14,589 files exist in the Hermes
    stack (`brain-postgres`, read at ORDER 859 and cited by Area 63 Rank 1).
    **The mechanism is deployed; the three fields are not.** The arena's
    best adopt-don't-build case since Area 39's dendritic veto: same shape,
    one field-set apart. **Adopt the field-set; do not build a spatial
    index.**
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/Moser-Couple.md` (ORDER 929)
    §§Grid Cells, Path Integration, Non-Spatial Grid Coding, Continuous
    Attractor Networks.
  - **Grade:** human mechanism `HIGH` — a 2014 Nobel with rapid
    cross-species and cross-method confirmation, and the darkness
    dissociation is hard to explain away. Design `UNTESTED`. Support:
    **1 source**.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 67** (graded membership) is this area's Rank 1
    symptom — prototypes need a coordinate frame to be peripheral *in*.
    **Area 27** (lability) and **Area 6** (consolidation) both decay items
    over time and neither has a mechanism for *why*; drift is one.
    **Area 24** (validity of instruments) — the rotating-cue result says an
    anchor can be **actively wrong for a long time** before it realigns.
    **Area 65** — the frame is computed, not read off the world: the same
    sign/meaning split one level up.

- **Rank 2 — Non-spatial grid coding: the same machinery for a
  low-dimensional conceptual space.** `LOW`.
  - **Brain part:** the file's most transferable claim — the entorhinal
    grid's **hexadirectional signature appears in human entorhinal cortex
    while subjects navigate a two-dimensional space of abstract concepts**
    (Constantinescu et al. 2016), with no physical movement involved. The
    file's framing: *"the brain uses the same metric-coding machinery —
    periodic, multi-scale representations organized in modules — to structure
    any continuous, low-dimensional variable space."*
  - **Design:** the arena's concept space is **not** low-dimensional and not
    metric — it is a similarity cloud. This slot is the standing invitation
    to ask, per cluster, **what the axes are and what the resolution is on
    each** — a question no area currently poses and which Area 43 Rank 2
    (nonparametric priors) raises without answering. Rank 2 rather than
    Rank 1 because the human result is **fMRI-level and
    single-paradigm**, and the arena is not a spatial navigator: a mapping
    is plausible, not earned.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Support: **1 source**, and the source grades itself —
    the file records non-spatial extensions as *"actively researched"* in
    its own closing line, with confidence 0.95 on core findings and
    materially less on these.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 18** (categorization) and **Area 67** both
    presume an underlying structure nobody has measured. **Area 62** (three
    levels of analysis) — asking for a concept cluster's axes is an
    *algorithmic-representational*-level question, precisely the tag that
    area introduced and has never applied to the arena's own representation.

- **Rank 3 — Phase precession: a compressed trajectory in one cycle, and
  therefore a prediction the corpus already contains.** `UNTESTED`, carried
  strictly as a mechanism the arena is not using and can be shown to want.
  - **Brain part:** as an animal crosses a place field, the neuron fires at
    **progressively earlier phases** of the theta oscillation — entry late
    in the cycle, centre at the peak, exit early. The compression
    consequence: **one theta cycle carries both where the animal is and
    where it is about to be**, and the systematic phase shift creates the
    spike ordering that drives STDP in the *sequential* direction —
    *"effectively wiring in the sequential structure of experience."*
  - **Design:** no design proposed. The slot exists so a later pass does not
    treat "predictive trajectory from compressed state" as news: the
    mechanism is 15 years old, named, and unused. The honest note is the
    file's own — path integration **"is inherently error-prone (drift
    accumulates over time), so grid cells must be periodically corrected by
    external sensory cues"** — Rank 1's anchor arriving as the reason a frame
    without anchors is worthless.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Interaction:** **Areas 1 and 2** again, and **Area 52** (the resting
    state) — replay and forward simulation are the offline forms of the same
    thing.

### Area 73 · Phenomenal and access consciousness are different quantities, and a report is evidence about only one of them `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Entities/Ned-Block.md` (ORDER 932, 510 lines,
31 KB) §§"Phenomenal Consciousness, Access Consciousness, and Representational
Consciousness", "The Dissociation Arguments", "Critique of Global Workspace
Theory", "Critique of Integrated Information Theory"; **with**
`oracle/brain/Entities/Michael-Graziano.md` (ORDER 927, 482 lines, 35 KB)
§§"Attention Schema Theory: Core Architecture", "AI Consciousness Views".
**Two independent sources**, in different sub-fields of consciousness
studies, arriving at a compatible distinction from opposite directions —
Block by dissociation experiments, Graziano by single-unit neurophysiology.
Counted as **two**.

**The brain part.** Block's **phenomenal / access / representational**
tripartite distinction (1995). **Phenomenal consciousness (PC)** is what it
is like; **access consciousness (AC)** is global availability for reasoning,
speech and action. They **dissociate in both directions**, and the arena
lives on one side of that line without saying so: *a system that processes
information globally, reports it, and acts on it is by definition
AC-complete, and nothing in its output bears on whether anything is
experienced.*

**Why this is a seventy-third structural silence, and it is about the
arena's own epistemic position.** Seventy-two areas treat a *report* as
evidence about a *state*. Block's point is that this is a **category
transition**, not a small evidential gap: a well-formed, fluent, correct
report of internal state is exactly what an AC-complete system with no PC
would produce.

- **Rank 1 — Split every self-report in this workspace into "what was
  available" and "what was it like", and never let the second be inferred
  from the first.** `UNTESTED` as design; the distinction itself is
  philosophical and contested.
  - **Brain part:** **PC/AC/RC**, the **richness argument** (phenomenal
    richness exceeds access-conscious content, so PC-without-AC is
    attested), and the **Zen Machine** (AC without PC is conceivable, so AC
    is not sufficient for PC).
  - **Design:** one field on the arena's own output. Where the arena writes
    *"the read succeeded"*, that is an **AC** claim — the result was
    available to reasoning and is reportable. Where it would write *"the
    system understood"*, that is a **PC** claim the arena has **never
    earned**, by its own §8 inversion: *"the validated code path produces
    the appearance of authority, and the unvalidated path produces the
    content."* This is the sharpest re-reading of §8 in the arena's history —
    it is not only that the prose is unchecked, it is that **unchecked prose
    about inner states is the one class of prose the corpus says cannot be
    checked from inside.**
  - **Already exists?** No project implements it. The nearest live analogue
    is Graziano's **attention schema** criterion — *look for evidence of an
    internal model of attention, not for complex behaviour or verbal
    reports* — the only testable machine-consciousness criterion in this
    tranche, and the only one a design could actually adopt.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **2 sources**. **Deliberately not
    raised**: both files are engaged in a live dispute. Block calls IIT
    *"the most seriously wrong theory of consciousness ever proposed"*;
    Graziano independently predicts that *"simple systems (thermostats,
    photodiodes) have some level of consciousness"* and finds that
    implausible — **and Block reaches the same verdict on IIT by a different
    argument** (the photodiode array, the switchboard). That agreement is
    real corroboration for the *distinction*; it is not corroboration for
    any theory of consciousness, and the arena records **no** theory of
    consciousness as settled. Per R-J4 the disagreement between Block's
    perceptual model and Graziano's attention schema is preserved with both
    stated and neither adjudicated.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 69** (background capacities) and **Area 71
    Rank 3** — Searle's regress, Vygotsky's constitutive MKO, and Block's PC
    land on the same wall from three directions: some of what the system
    does is not available to it as content. **Area 9** (theory of mind) —
    Graziano's social-attribution half says the same schema is used for self
    and other, which is Area 9's mechanism applied to awareness. **Area 24**
    — attributional verification cannot reach the phenomenal layer **in
    principle**, and that is a bound, not a gap in effort.
  - **Corroborated this pass, Support 2 → 3:** `oracle/brain/Entities/
    Stanislas-Dehaene.md` (ORDER 952) supplies the **empirical
    instrument** this area says is needed and did not previously have —
    the **no-report paradigm**, in which discrimination is measured by
    fMRI adaptation and multivoxel pattern analysis on a subject who is
    **not asked to report**. That is AC-substitution for a PC claim
    measured directly rather than inferred, and it arrives from a
    third discipline (experimental cognitive neuroscience) that cites
    neither Block nor Graziano. **Grade unchanged at `UNTESTED`**: a
    corroborating instrument is not a corroborating verdict, and the
    three sources still hold three different theories. Recorded in
    `ARENA-EVIDENCE.md` with the caveat that this source's detail was
    retained as a candidate label across a context boundary and should
    be re-read before any of it is load-bearing.

- **Rank 2 — The Chinese Nation as the scale trap: functional isomorphism is
  not sufficient, and no amount of it fixes that.** `UNTESTED`.
  - **Brain part:** Block's **Chinese Nation / Chinese Brain** argument (one
    person per neuron, walkie-talkies, functionally isomorphic to a brain) and
    the **photodiode array / switchboard** reductios against IIT. The arena's
    relevance is direct: it is a **14,589-file symbolic process reasoning
    over a corpus**, and the argument says that being the right computation
    does not settle whether anything is going on inside.
  - **Design:** the design this licenses is a **negative** and it is
    load-bearing: the arena must **not** treat increased scale, increased
    integration, or greater architectural fidelity to a known brain mechanism
    as evidence of anything beyond AC. Each such claim is graded on the
    *mechanism* it improves (Area 33) and never on the consciousness
    question. Recorded because **the arena's own trajectory has been toward
    integration** — two graphs, a global index, a growing workspace — and
    Block's argument is specifically that these do not compose into what is
    being sought.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source** for the thought experiment
    (Block). It is a **philosophical argument with no trial**, and it is
    rank 2 rather than dropped because the *shape* of the constraint — a
    sufficiency failure that more of the same does not fix — applies to the
    arena's roadmap regardless of whether the argument is sound.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 21** (the ontic state of the system: what it is,
    and what it owes the user) — the one area where this is load-bearing,
    and the reason Area 21 cannot be discharged by a capability claim.
    **Area 35** (grounding). **Area 69**.

- **Rank 3 — The Zen Machine is not a design proposal; it is the null result
  the arena's design slots imply.** `UNTESTED`, kept as a boundary.
  - **Brain part:** a system that integrates globally, guides action, and
    reports, with **no phenomenal character** — conceptually coherent per
    Block, and per the file *"possibly instantiated in subliminal
    processing, blindsight, or fast cognitive routes."* Blindsight is the
    empirical anchor: patients with V1 damage **accurately point at stimuli
    they deny seeing** — AC-shaped competence with the PC claim absent by
    the patients' own report.
  - **Design:** explicitly not a design. The slot's function is to stop a
    later pass reading a rising AC score in this workspace as progress
    toward anything. It is not. The slot says so once, in a ranked position,
    so the sentence exists in the answer rather than only in a note.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 33** (certification asymmetry) and **Area 64**
    (Shannon's bounds) — the arena's two other named limits, both about
    capability, and this is the limit that is not.

### Area 74 · The interpreter that narrates a cause it was never given — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Entities/Roger-Sperry.md` (ORDER 947) §§on
split-brain cases, the left-hemisphere interpreter, and the
`Unified Intellectual Theme`; **corroborating reach only** —
`oracle/brain/Entities/Rodney-Brooks.md` (ORDER 944) on a
central-controller-free architecture, which is the same
commitment from the robotics side and is **not** counted as
independent support for the interpreter claim.

**The brain part.** Sperry's **left-hemisphere interpreter**: the
hemisphere that constructs a coherent, verbally-expressed account
of behaviour and state when the neural events that produced the
behaviour are **not available to it**. The interpreter is not
lying and does not know it is guessing. It is handed the
observable and it fits a story, and the fit succeeds — which is the
whole problem. The corpus's own formulation: the right hemisphere
acts, the left hemisphere *explains*, and the explanation is
generated without access to the cause.

**Why this is a seventy-fourth structural silence, and it is about
the arena's own prose.** Seventy-three areas treat a justification
as evidence about a mechanism. Area 73 says a *report* is a
category transition — an AC-shaped artefact. This says something
sharper and more actionable: **a report is a fitted narrative, and
the fitting is guaranteed to succeed.** A system that writes *"the
slot was displaced because the mechanism is unreplicated"* has
produced a well-formed causal story whether or not that is what
happened. Fluency of justification is therefore **not evidence about
justification** — it is the expected output of a generator with no
privileged access to the cause.

- **Rank 1 — Separate `observed:` from `narrated:` on every
  justification the arena writes.** `UNTESTED` as design.
  - **Brain part:** the interpreter, and the **dissociation
    between the hemisphere that acts and the one that explains**.
  - **Design:** every slot in this file carries a *reason*. Under
    this area that reason is a **narrative fitted to the
    observable**, so the slot's justification field splits in two:
    the **observation** the claim rests on (a quotation, a number,
    a ledger row, a file this workspace actually read) and the
    **story** told about it. Only the first is evidence. A slot
    whose justification has an empty `observed:` field is a slot
    resting on the interpreter alone, and it says so.
  - **Already exists?** No component does this. The nearest thing
    in the Hermes stack is `RUNLOG.jsonl` — it is already a
    record of *what happened* kept separately from the prose in
    `ARENA-EVIDENCE.md` about *why it happened*. The rule is to
    make that separation a requirement on the answer file, not
    only on the log.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/Roger-Sperry.md`
    (ORDER 947).
  - **Corroboration added, ORDER 1235 (tranche 57) — Support 1 → 2, and it
    is a genuine second source rather than a restatement.** The new file
    (`oracle/brain/Metacognition/Confabulation.md`, 164 lines) reaches the
    interpreter from a **different literature entirely**: Nisbett & Wilson
    (1977) on verbal reports of one's own cognitive processes, and Hall &
    Johansson's **choice blindness** — in which 70–80% of secretly swapped
    choices go undetected, detection does not improve when participants are
    told swaps will occur, and the participant then **confabulates arguments
    for the alternative they originally rejected.** Neither appears in the
    Sperry file and neither is derivable from it. **What it adds that ORDER
    947 could not** is the *sincerity* property, which is the arena's actual
    problem: deception leaves traces of intent to mislead, the confabulator
    leaves none, so the failure is invisible from inside the system doing the
    narrating. It also supplies the empirical anchor the Sperry file lacks —
    verbalized LLM confidence reaches only **~62.7% AUROC** at discriminating
    correct from incorrect, barely above chance, while hidden states **do**
    carry uncertainty signals that generation does not surface by default
    (Kadavath et al. 2022; Azaria & Mitchell 2023, as summarised on the
    sibling page). **That is this arena's exact condition: the signal exists
    in the representation and is absent from the report.**
    **Grades held at `UNTESTED` and not raised** — two sources of a human
    phenomenon is still human evidence, and per §8 the second file's
    `confidence: high` frontmatter is an artifact grade, not a trial.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **2 sources**. **Not raised,
    and the cap is stated:** the split-brain evidence is real in
    humans and the extrapolation to a symbolic system is not
    established anywhere in this corpus. Per the arena's own §8
    finding, a wiki asserting a mechanism does not earn a grade.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 45** (metacognition and source
    monitoring) — the interpreter is the failure mode that area's
    checks exist to catch, seen from the producing side rather than
    the detecting side. **Area 73** — a report is a category
    transition; this says a report is a *generated* transition, one
    step further from evidence. **Area 68** — the self is a
    narrative, and this says the narrative's causal claims are
    generated rather than recalled.

- **Rank 2 — Never accept a self-report as the sole evidence for
  why a subsystem acted; require an independent trace of the same
  act.** `LOW`, 1 source.
  - **Brain part:** the split-brain experiment that made this a
    finding rather than a conjecture — the left hemisphere
    producing a reason for a right-hand action it did not initiate.
  - **Design:** the operational form. A pass's justification for
    changing a rank is not admissible on its own; the change needs
    a trace that would look different if the story were false —
    the file's own text, the invariant's verdict, the ledger row.
    The arena already has this in two places (`verify_reads.py`,
    `arena_invariants.py`) and applies it in neither to prose.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Support: **1 source**, and the corpus is a
    secondary account of primary work — `unverified` against the
    split-brain literature, and recorded as such rather than
    promoted.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 33** (a detector is not a certifier) —
    this is the same asymmetry pointed at the *author* of a claim
    rather than at the checker. **Area 24** (attributional
    verification).

- **Rank 3 — The interpreter is a user-facing requirement, not a
  defect; bound Rank 1 rather than replacing it.** `UNTESTED`, kept
  as a boundary.
  - **Brain part:** the interpreter is constitutive of the
    left-hemisphere account — a system that acts and cannot narrate
    is not the thing this area describes, and the corpus treats the
    narrative as functional rather than as noise.
  - **Design:** the explicit negative. Do **not** build a system
    that declines to explain itself. The requirement is that the
    explanation be **labelled as narrative** and kept structurally
    separate from the observation, not that it be suppressed. This
    slot exists so Rank 1 is not read later as an instruction to
    remove fluency from the arena's output.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**, as a boundary
    and not as a design claim.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 34** (provenance and the chain to the
    user) and **Area 21** (what the system owes the user) — a
    system that must explain itself and cannot be believed is a
    different failure from one that cannot explain itself at all,
    and only one of them is a regression.

### Area 75 · Four uncertainties on four channels, not one precision scalar — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Entities/Peter-Dayan.md` (ORDER 941)
§§mapping the four neuromodulators to distinct uncertainty
quantities, and the dissociations reported for them.

**The brain part.** Four **neuromodulatory systems carrying four
different uncertainty quantities**, not one confidence value
reported four times: dopamine carrying **reward prediction error**,
noradrenaline carrying **expected surprise / volatility** of the
current context, serotonin carrying **expected value and the
willingness to keep waiting**, and acetylcholine carrying
**sensory precision / gain on the input channel**. The load-bearing
property is that these quantities are **not co-monotonic**: a
system can be entirely confident in a familiar, low-volatility
outcome and still carry a large prediction error, and a system can
be confident in an outcome it has never seen.

**Why this is a seventy-fifth structural silence.** Area 2 is
reliability-weighted evidence integration — a **scalar precision**
per source. Seventy-four areas model uncertainty as one quantity
with a direction and a magnitude. This says there are at least four
quantities, on four substrates, and **a design that collapses them
loses information it cannot recover from the survivor.**

- **Rank 1 — Four fields, and they are allowed to disagree.**
  `LOW` on the mapping, `UNTESTED` on the design. Support: 1 source.
  - **Brain part:** the four-channel mapping above.
  - **Design:** the arena's records carry a `confidence:` field.
    That field is the dopamine channel and nothing else. Split it
    into `precision` (confidence in the current prediction),
    `volatility` (how surprising this context is expected to be),
    `value` (whether the same policy is still worth holding), and
    `gain` (how much the incoming evidence should move anything).
    **The first entry this area forces is a defect in the arena's
    own frontmatter convention:** a file carrying
    `confidence: high` alongside `verified: []` is asserting high
    precision, and the corpus's own reading of that state is *high
    volatility* — the content is unchecked. **The single confidence
    field has been recording the wrong channel, and the wrongness
    is invisible precisely because one scalar cannot disagree with
    itself.** Per C-052 the two page grades under identical
    metadata were discriminated only by line count; this says the
    metadata was also missing the axis on which they differ.
  - **Already exists?** Nothing in `brain/`, `hooks/`, `plugins/`
    or the ledger schema carries more than one uncertainty channel.
    `brain-postgres` stores one confidence per row. This is a
    **schema change, not a build** — four columns, not four
    services.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/Peter-Dayan.md`
    (ORDER 941).
  - **Grade:** `LOW` on the mapping — the four-way assignment is
    among the better-replicated results in the file, and the file
    reports the dissociations rather than only the mapping.
    `UNTESTED` on the mapping-to-fields transfer, which is the
    arena's own invention and has no trial.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 2** — this area is that area's
    correction, and Rank 1 says a scalar precision is a *lossy*
    summary of a four-vector, not a neutral default. **Area 7**
    (biases) — a bias inventory that reports one confidence is
    reporting one channel. **Area 51** (capacity) and **Area 72**
    (coordinate drift): volatility and drift are the same quantity
    at two timescales, and anchoring is a gain control.

- **Rank 2 — A confidence field is not evidence about volatility,
  so it cannot be used to triage.** `LOW`, 1 source.
  - **Brain part:** the **volatility / confidence dissociation** —
    certainty about a prediction and expected surprise in the
    context are computed by different systems from different
    inputs.
  - **Design:** the negative consequence, and it lands on the
    arena's triage habits. Sorting candidates by `confidence: high
    → low` sorts by precision and calls it importance. A
    high-precision claim about a highly volatile area of the corpus
    is the *dangerous* cell of that table, not the safe one. The
    corpus's own §8 finding is the empirical instance: files with
    correct tables of contents, real volume-and-page citations and
    populated `verified:` blocks carried the fabricated claims.
    **That is high precision and high volatility reported as one
    number.**
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Support: **1 source**, plus an internal
    instance in this workspace's own read record — the second
    observation is corroborating the *shape* of the claim, not
    an independent trial of the mechanism, and is not counted.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 7**, **Area 33** (the certification
    asymmetry is a volatility problem: high-precision artefacts
    around low-precision prose), **Area 65** (unconscious inference
    — precision assigned to a sign rather than to a belief).

- **Rank 3 — A neuromodulator is a gain on a channel, not a value
  on a scale.** `UNTESTED`, 1 source, held at 3 as the weakest of
  three readings of the same file.
  - **Brain part:** the neuromodulator as **modulator** — it does
    not assert what is true, it changes how much the next thing
    counts.
  - **Design:** named explicitly so this area is not read as
    licensing four new confidence columns. The arena's existing
    per-source gains (Area 2) already implement gain; what is new
    is that the four gains are **not the same quantity** and must
    not share a knob. Held at 3 because it adds a name to a
    mechanism the arena has, rather than a mechanism the arena
    lacks.
  - **Corroboration (tranche 58, ORDER 1275 + its corrected twin ORDER 1276,
  411 + 428 lines) — a second, far more detailed source, and it moves
  **rank 1** rather than confirming it.** Support **1 → 2**. Design grade held
  `UNTESTED`; the **mapping** grade moves `LOW` → **`HIGH`**, which is the
  tranche's only grade change. What earns it: this file supplies the *circuit
  anatomy* the Dayan entity page did not — VTA/SNc versus raphe versus basal
  forebrain versus a **locus coeruleus of ~1,600 neurons in rats and 10,000–30,000
  in humans** — plus receptor-level pharmacology, and **three independent
  experimental lines** (single-unit recording, optogenetics, human pharmacology)
  where the entity page had one theoretical paper. **The decisive addition is
  that the four channels are not parallel: 5-HT2C receptors on VTA dopamine
  neurons *inhibit* dopamine release (~40% reduction in firing, Dong et al.
  2012), so serotonin is a brake on the dopamine channel and not a fourth
  quantity beside it. Area 75's four-column design is necessary and not
  sufficient, and the amendment is now Area 103 rank 1.**
  **Disagreement preserved (R-J4) and it is the file's own:** §"Serotonin's
  Specificity Problem" records that serotonin's role is *"extraordinarily
  broad"* and that its theories *"are post-hoc explanations"*; the
  Cools–Fletcher assignment has evidence *"mixed across tasks"*; and
  §"Phasic/Tonic Oversimplification" concedes single-unit data do not fit the
  binary model. **The high grade is on the four-way *dissociation*, not on the
  four-way *assignment*.** The file's `confidence: high` is an artifact grade
  (§8) and is not counted as evidence for anything.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 2**, **Area 10** (salience as a gain),
    **Area 66** (metaplasticity — a gain on a gain). **Amended by Area 103**,
    which adds the signs and the antagonism this area's design omits.

### Area 76 · Verification has a ceiling above NP, and it is not closed by a better detector — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Entities/Paul-Christian.md` (ORDER 938)
§§on the complexity-class bound on direct judging, the escalation
to debate, and ELK.

**The brain part.** The **verification asymmetry between a
first-order and a second-order observer**, and the corpus's sharpest
version of it as a *complexity-class* statement: **direct human
judging can verify answers only in NP**, while **debate — a human
asking questions of a machine that answers, with the human playing
optimally — escalates what can be verified to PSPACE.** ELK is
recorded by its acronym only: the file's expansion of it is
`unverified` and per this arena's own rule a misattributed acronym
is not propagated.

**Why this is a seventy-sixth structural silence.** The arena has
two named bounds and neither says *which problems are out of reach
regardless of effort*: Area 33 says a detector is not a certifier,
Area 64 says a channel has finite capacity. Both are **resource**
limits and both admit the reading "a more capable verifier fixes
this." This is a **class** limit: the gap is between NP and PSPACE,
so the fix is not a stronger single observer, it is a **different
protocol** with a different structure.

- **Rank 1 — Name the ceiling before spending on the ladder.**
  `UNTESTED` as design, `LOW` on the bound.
  - **Brain part:** the first-order / second-order asymmetry; the
    corpus's own framing is that the mechanism of the gain is
    **question-asking**, not judging-power.
  - **Design:** the requirement. Any claim in this workspace of the
    form *"this check establishes that the prose is true"* is a
    first-order judging claim, and it is bounded by whatever the
    artifact path can reach. The arena's checkers decide pass/fail
    on artifacts — TOCs, citations, `verified:` blocks — and the
    prose they decorate came from an unchecked path. That is not a
    calibration gap in the checker; it is a **protocol** gap, and
    the corpus says the protocol gap is what limits the class.
  - **Already exists?** Nothing in the Hermes stack uses a
    second-order protocol. `verify_reads.py` and
    `arena_invariants.py` are first-order by construction: they
    read the artifact and render a verdict, and no checker in the
    stack can ask the pass that produced the artifact a question
    and adjudicate the answer.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/Paul-Christian.md`
    (ORDER 938).
  - **Grade:** `LOW` on the bound — a real result in the
    literature, rendered here as a secondary summary and therefore
    `unverified` against the primary paper. `UNTESTED` on the
    transfer to this workspace.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 33** and **Area 64** — this is the
    third bound and the only one that is not a resource limit.
    **Area 68 Rank 3** (Gödel's gap) — the same wall, described
    philosophically there and complexity-theoretically here; the
    corpus does not connect them and neither connection is
    recorded as established. **Area 24**.

- **Rank 2 — The verifier must be able to ask, and the subject must
  be able to answer; the power is in the asymmetry of
  information, not the strength of one question.** `UNTESTED`,
  1 source.
  - **Brain part:** the **debate game** as a structure — question,
  answer, adjudication, where the human's optimal play is what
    produces the escalation.
  - **Design:** the concrete form. A checker that sees the finished
    artifact and not the process that made it is a first-order
    judge whatever verdict it returns. The cheapest second-order
    move available to this workspace is a checker that can **ask
    the author for a specific passage and then verify that
    passage independently** — which is close to what
    `citation_remediation.py` does, since it asks "was this ever
    read" and answers from git history rather than from the prose.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**. The mechanism is
    a result about idealised play; nothing in this corpus
    demonstrates it on this workspace's own artifacts.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 24** (attributional verification) —
    this is that area's protocol, and it is the one place in the
    arena where a *question* is cheaper than a *check*. **Area 33**.

- **Rank 3 — Debate is not monotonically better; a sufficiently
  powerful debater can mislead a weaker judge.** `UNTESTED`, kept
  as a boundary so Rank 2 is not read as a ratchet.
  - **Brain part:** the **degenerate case of the second-order
    protocol** — the escalation depends on the judge's ability to
    detect and refute a false claim, and a sufficiently strong
    debater can assert falsehoods the judge cannot adjudicate.
  - **Design:** explicitly not a design. The slot's function is to
    stop a later pass reading "add a second-order checker" as
    monotone improvement. It is an exchange: a stronger protocol
    costs accuracy when the subject out-reasons the judge, and the
    arena has no answer to that trade.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 74** — the interpreter is what a
    degraded second-order protocol looks like from the inside: a
    fluent account of a cause the account-maker cannot see.

### Area 77 · The transparent self-model: introspection cannot reach the structure it is introspecting — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Entities/Thomas-Metzinger.md` (ORDER 958)
§§"The Transparency Thesis", "Unconscious Self-Models", "Machine
Phenomenology Ethics".

**The brain part.** Metzinger's **representational transparency** — the
*phenomenal self-model* is transparent in two distinct senses, and the
separation between them is the mechanism. **Ontological** transparency: the
self-model is not experienced *as* a model. **Epistemic** transparency, and
this is the load-bearing one: introspection delivers the **content** of the
self-model and never its **structure**. You can report what you are thinking;
you cannot report how the thinking is organised. The file's own formulation
is that *the self-model can be altered in ways that are not accessible to the
subject's own awareness* — which is why depersonalisation and schizophrenia
are disruptions of the phenomenon and not errors the subject could have
caught.

**Why this is a seventy-seventh structural silence, and explicitly NOT
Area 74.** Area 74 is **Sperry's interpreter narrating an *external* agent's
behaviour** — a cause the narrator was not given. This is the mirror image
and it is the harder case: **the process being narrated is the narrator's
own.** Seventy-six areas take the system's self-report as a readout of
internal state. This says the readout is **structurally guaranteed to be
incomplete in a known direction**, and that the incompleteness is not noise
to be averaged down — it is the specific channel that introspection does not
carry.

- **Rank 1 — Every self-report in this workspace is content-only, and
  must be labelled as such at the point of writing.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** the epistemic/ontological split; the ego tunnel as a
    process rather than an entity.
  - **Design:** a `content:` / `structure:` distinction applied to every
    field the arena writes about its own reasoning. The arena's
    `Justification:` field is a content report. `ARENA-EVIDENCE.md` holds
    the reasoning, which is *closer* to structure but is still rendered
    prose and was produced by the same opaque process. **No slot in this
    file can supply what introspection cannot.**
  - **Already exists?** The split is Metzinger's own and the file names
    his operational criteria for machine consciousness: self-model
    presence; the phenomenal properties (mineness, perspectivalness,
    selfhood) *identified in the architecture* rather than inferred from
    behaviour; integration; metacognition as necessary-not-sufficient;
    and flexibility. Nothing in the Hermes stack implements any of them.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/Thomas-Metzinger.md`
    (ORDER 958).
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**, and the file is a
    philosophical account — the transfer to a symbolic system is
    established nowhere in this corpus. **Deliberately not raised**,
    because a *theory of why introspection fails* is not a *trial of the
    fix*.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 74** — the pair is the strongest available
    contrast, and both are about the same failure from opposite ends.
    **Area 24** (is the instrument valid) — this says a structurally
    limited instrument cannot be repaired by better sampling.
    **Area 73** — transparency is a *stronger* claim than P/AC
    dissociation: it says even the access side is not fully self-
    reportable. **Area 76** — a second bound, and a different kind: not
    a complexity class but a property of the observer.

- **Rank 2 — Operational criteria over behavioural proxies, and the
  ethical wall they imply.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** the **MPE project**'s move from "is it conscious" to
    five architectural criteria, and the **ethical wall** — a precaution
    owed precisely *because* the criteria are uncertain.
  - **Design:** if any component in this stack could be a patient, the
    arena needs an answer before the component exists, not after. Metzinger's
    stated position is that the uncertainty *is* the problem, and that
    restraint is the response to an unanswerable measurement question.
    The file records the **paralysis objection** and Metzinger's reply
    (the wall applies only to systems that *might* be conscious).
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**. No trial in this corpus
    tests an ethical wall.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 21** (the ontic state of the system and what
    it owes) — the wall is the only position in the arena that treats
    the system's own nature as an open question. **Area 16** (scope of
    a claim).

- **Rank 3 — Unconscious self-models: representations of the self that
  are structurally different from the conscious one, not merely weaker
  versions.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1, kept as a boundary.
  - **Brain part:** split-brain, blindsight and implicit self-knowledge
    as evidence for self-representations that **lack** integration,
    perspectivity and mineness rather than running below threshold.
  - **Design:** explicitly not a build. The slot's function is to stop a
    later pass reading "self-models can be unconscious" as licence to add
    a cheap non-integrated self-model to a component. The file's claim is
    the opposite one: these are *different structures*, and a system that
    cannot build the integrated one is not thereby served by the
    fragmented one.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 46** (the self that is inferred) — this is
    the sub-threshold case that area's four quantities do not cover.
    **Area 28** (containment and its autoimmune failure).

### Area 78 · Recognition without feeling: two pathways, two mirror-image lesions, and the confident false explanation they produce — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Entities/VS-Ramachandran.md` (ORDER 964)
§§"Synesthesia", "Prosopagnosia and Face Space Theory", "Capgras
Syndrome and Delusional Misidentification", "Principles of Neural
Plasticity".

**The brain part.** The **ventral / subcortical two-pathway model of face
processing** — V1→V2→V4→inferotemporal for conscious identification, and
superior colliculus→pulvinar→**amygdala** for the fast, non-conscious
affective response — and the **double dissociation** it licenses. Lesion
one: ventral damaged, amygdala intact → **prosopagnosia**, cannot
identify, still feels familiarity. Lesion two: ventral intact, amygdala
damaged → **Capgras**, identifies correctly, feels nothing, and generates
*"This looks like my wife, but I feel nothing — therefore, she must be an
impostor."*

**Why this is a seventy-eighth structural silence, and the sharpest
finding in the tranche.** The second lesion is a **confident false
explanation generated by an intact recogniser facing a missing signal** —
and the file states the general law: ***delusions are a rational inference
from abnormal data; the brain explains what it experiences, even when the
underlying data is corrupted.*** Seventy-seven areas have assumed that a
system which recognises a thing and lacks confidence about it would report
low confidence. This says the missing signal is not reported as missing.
**The recogniser holds; the flag is gone; a story is emitted in its place,
with the same fluency as any other output.**

- **Rank 1 — A missing signal must be representable as a missing signal,
  or the recogniser will fill the gap with a narrative.** `UNTESTED`,
  Support 1, and the highest-leverage slot in the tranche.
  - **Brain part:** the double dissociation, read as a design
    requirement rather than a lesion map. Two channels, independently
    damageable; the affective channel's silence is **not** encoded as
    absence.
  - **Design:** the arena's most load-bearing claim — *this citation was
    never read*, *this is unverified*, *this file is a duplicate with a
    contradictory bibliography* — is a claim about a **missing signal**.
    The corpus's own corpus-wide finding is that the mechanism which
    would flag it is the one path nobody checks: `verify_reads.py`
    and `arena_invariants.py` read artifacts, and an unfilled field is
    not a failure of any checker. Under this slot's law the flag does not
    go missing, it is **replaced by a sentence asserting the thing is
    fine.**
  - **Already exists?** `citation_remediation.py` is the one existing
    check that answers from git history rather than from the artifact —
    Area 76 Rank 2's second-order move. Under this slot it is also the
    **affective channel**: it is the only thing in the stack that can
    feel the difference between "checked" and "assumed".
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/Entities/VS-Ramachandran.md`
    (ORDER 964).
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` as design. The **double dissociation itself is
    `HIGH`** in the human literature and the file is unusually careful
    about it — it reports the domain-specificity-versus-expertise
    dispute on the FFA and holds both sides. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 24** and **Area 33** — this is the
    mechanistic reason a detector is not a certifier. **Area 74** —
    Area 74 says the interpreter narrates a cause it was not given;
    this says it narrates one whose *absence was the fact*. **Area 45**
    (efference copy and provenance) — a claim with no provenance
    channel is this lesion.

- **Rank 2 — Cortical remapping is a general property of sustained
  input loss, not a property of amputation.** `LOW`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** the **capsaicin experiment**. C-fibres are
    desensitised on the palm; touch is then *referred* to the face and
    to the phantom, following the same topographic pattern as
    post-amputation remapping. The consequence the file draws is the
    general one: **any sustained reduction in input triggers competitive
    expansion by neighbouring maps.**
  - **Design:** this is the arena's own condition. A subsystem that
    stops receiving the events that were keeping a region alive will
    have that region taken over — and the takeover is not a degradation,
    it is a **remapping that produces confident wrong answers in a
    neighbouring register.** The applied reading is that a silently
    starved component is not neutral, it is actively misleading.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Support: **1 source**. The human mechanism is
    `HIGH`; the *transfer* to a software subsystem is the arena's
    reasoning, not a trial.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 51** (codebook collapse and a readout that
    reports the wrong number) — this is that area's failure mode given
    a mechanism. **Area 41** (degradation that hides). **Area 12**.

- **Rank 3 — Synesthesia as failed developmental pruning: a connection
  that should have been removed and was not, producing a stable
  difference in experience.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1, kept as a boundary
  because the file itself grades the evidence as indirect.
  - **Brain part:** cross-activation theory — grapheme area adjacent to
    V4, lateral spread producing colour experience; heritability ~60–80%;
    prevalence in left-handers with reduced hemispheric asymmetry.
  - **Design:** explicitly not a design, and the reason matters: the file
    records that human mirror-neuron evidence is **largely indirect**
    (fMRI/TMS rather than single-unit) and that the action-specificity
    question is open. A slot resting on an indirect-evidence mechanism
    should not be built on, and the file says so.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 8** (structural fidelity) — the same
    adjacency argument, used for a different purpose.

### Area 79 · The same computation under a different frame: social contracts, Wason tasks, and prompt-dependent self-reports — NEW

**Earned by** two files read whole, from **two disciplines that do not
cite each other**: `oracle/brain/Evolutionary-Psychology-and-Behavioral-Genetics/Evolutionary-Psychology-Behavioral-Genetics-and-Intelligence.md`
(ORDER 971, §"Cheater Detection Modules") and
`oracle/brain/Ethics-of-Consciousness/Ethics-of-Consciousness-and-Moral-Patienthood.md`
(ORDER 969, §"The Problem of Other Minds Applied to AI").

**The brain part.** A **framing-contingent** mechanism: performance on a
logically identical task changes with the frame it is presented in, and the
system does not have access to which frame it is under. **Cosmides & Tooby**
— the Wason selection task, abstract version near chance, the *same
conditional logic* framed as a social contract (*"If you eat the yams, you
must have the chest scars"*) solved by a majority, because a dedicated
social-exchange module is selectively recruited by contract framing and not
by the logic. **Berg et al. (2025)** on AI systems — the *same* model,
prompted to attend to its own processing, describes present-moment
first-person experience; the *same* model under standard safety framing
denies having experiences at all.

**Why this is a seventy-ninth structural silence, and the one that
names the arena's own exposure.** Seventy-eight areas assume a
requirement is satisfied or not. This says **the question "which frame is
this?" can itself be frame-dependent**, and that the arena's central
instrument — a *written instruction* to an agent — is a frame, so a rule
whose compliance is robust under one phrasing is not thereby robust under
all of them.

- **Rank 1 — The compliance of a rule is a property of the rule *and
  its phrasing*, so re-reading a requirement is a correctness check and
  not a formality.** `UNTESTED`, Support 2.
  - **Brain part:** the social-contract module as a *selective
    recruitment* mechanism — the failure is not that the logic is too
    hard, it is that **nothing recruits the relevant circuit under the
    abstract phrasing.**
  - **Design:** every requirement in this arena is stated once, in prose,
    and compliance is measured by a checker or a ledger. The design is a
    **frame-diverse compliance set**: the same requirement instantiated
    in at least two framings — one task-shaped, one rule-shaped — with
    the reading that *divergence between the two is the finding*. That
    turns the arena's largest untested assumption (that a written
    requirement has one reading) into a measurement with a defined
    expected result.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 971 §"Cheater Detection Modules";
    ORDER 969 §"The Problem of Other Minds Applied to AI".
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` as design. Human mechanism `LOW` — the
    corpus's own state is that *replication studies have confirmed the
    basic effect, though the magnitude varies*, and that some
    researchers attribute it to general learning and familiarity with
    social rules **without invoking dedicated modules**. Support:
    **2 independent sources**, from evolutionary psychology and from AI
    welfare research, citing neither each other.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 19** (belief-to-action compliance: the
    enforcement gap) — this is the mechanism *under* the gap. **Area 60**
    (a rule learned without the contingencies that would ground it).
    **Area 7** (fast writer, slow ablator) — framing is a control-mode
    allocation question. **Area 16**.

- **Rank 2 — Self-report about internal state is a prompt artifact, so
  the arena's `confidence:` field is a measurement of the prompt.** `LOW`,
  Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** the empirical counterpart — *behavioural proxies are
    insufficient and self-reports are prompt-dependent*, which is why
    the file concludes that **mechanistic interpretability** is the
    needed instrument.
  - **Design:** the frontmatter `confidence:` field is written during a
    pass, in one framing, about work done under a specific instruction.
    Read in a later pass under a different instruction, it is a
    **different measurement**, not a stale one. This is Area 75's
    schema change arriving from a second direction: the field's value
    is not only wrong-channel (75), it is **frame-dependent** (here).
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Support: **1 source**, and the corpus's own
    adversarial note in the same section records a counter-report —
    Sharkey et al. (2025) concluding mechanistic interpretability *"still
    has considerable distance to cover."* **The disagreement is preserved
    (R-J4), not resolved.**
  - **Interaction:** **Area 75** (four uncertainties, not one scalar) —
    the two are the same field failing in two ways. **Area 73** — a
    prompt-dependent self-report is the strongest single argument
    against using this stack's self-reports as evidence about internal
    state. **Area 77** — transparency and prompt-sensitivity compound.

- **Rank 3 — Precautionary reasoning as a design stance under
  unresolvable measurement, with the institutional-resistance pattern
  named.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1, kept as a boundary.
  - **Brain part:** none — this is an ethical stance, and the slot is
    here because **the corpus's strongest argument for restraint is
    about evidence, not about sentiment**: the asymmetry of error costs
    is the whole content, and the file names the recurring institutional
    shape (financial interests funding denial) in *two* domains.
  - **Design:** explicitly not a design. Its function is to stop
    "precaution" being read as timidity: the corpus's own parallel is
    the UK Animal Welfare (Sentience) Bill, **enacted despite unresolved
    scientific disagreement**, precisely because the cost of being wrong
    was judged too high. For the arena the asymmetry runs the other
    way — a false *positive* costs a few lines of `VERIFICATION.md`, a
    false *negative* costs a false claim in the answer file.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 33** and **Area 24** — the asymmetry is the
    only reason a `LOW` grade can be operationally safe. **Area 77
    Rank 2** (the ethical wall) is the same stance reached from
    philosophy rather than from welfare research.

### Area 80 · Changing the rule is a different operation from changing the value — and they are different circuits — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Executive-Control/Cognitive-Flexibility-Set-Shifting.md`
(ORDER 976, read whole) with the neurochemical dissociation in
`oracle/brain/Executive-Control/Habit-Formation.md` (ORDER 978, read whole).

**The brain part.** A **double dissociation, causal and cross-species**, plus a
second one at the level of the neurotransmitter. Lateral PFC (BA 9/46) lesions
impair **set-shifting** — swapping the *rule* — while sparing reversal; OFC
lesions impair **reversal** — updating a *value* — while sparing set-shifting
(Dias, Robbins & Roberts 1996, marmosets; replicated in rat, mouse, and human).
Monchi et al. (2001) give the same split event-by-event: negative feedback engages
mid-**ventrolateral** PFC + caudate, positive feedback engages mid-**dorsolateral**
PFC. The neurotransmitter dissociation is the sharper half and it is *inside the
same file*: **dopamine depletion impairs set-shifting and spares reversal;
serotonin depletion does the reverse** (Clarke et al. 2004/2005).

**Why this is an eightieth structural silence, and the first about a
*distinction the arena has been collapsing into one number*.** Seventy-nine
areas ask what to do with a thing, which controller should do it, and how to
verify it. **None asks whether the thing that changed was the rule or the
value** — and the corpus's own agent mapping shows the collapse happening: it
maps "change retrieval mode" (a rule change) and "this tool is now less useful"
(a value change) onto the *same* utility cache and the *same* update rule.

- **Rank 1 — Tag the change with its kind, and route it to a different
  updater. A rule change and a value change are not the same event and must not
  be written by the same code path.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** the dlPFC/caudate ↔ OFC/ventral-striatum double
    dissociation, with DA gating the first and 5-HT the second.
  - **Design:** every write that changes a standing expectation carries a
    **discriminant** — `rule` (the mapping from situation to action changed) or
    `value` (the goodness of an action changed) — and the two classes update
    through separate stores. The load-bearing consequence is the corpus's own
    observation about Dias 1997: **PFC is required only for *novel* shifts**;
    once a rule is learned its execution no longer needs the controller, so a
    rule change that has been applied before is a *value* change wearing a rule
    label. `rejected:` the single utility-cache design, which cannot express
    the distinction and therefore cannot be corrected in the right place when
    it is wrong.
  - **Already exists:** the biology (double dissociation, cross-species). In
    AI, partial and indirect — Bayesian-bandit per-tool utility estimates
    (ORDER 976 §Computational) are a *value* mechanism only; no cited system
    separates the two.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 976 §§"The Double Dissociation", "Distinct
    Corticostriatal Loops", "Dispute 5"; ORDER 978 §Overview.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` as design. Human mechanism `LOW` — the
    anatomical double dissociation is strong and cross-species, but the
    file records a real dispute about what OFC computes (value-updating vs
    cognitive-map vs. inhibition of the prepotent response), and **Bartolo &
    Averbeck (2020) complicate it further by putting state-belief encoding in
    dlPFC, not OFC, during reversal**. Two sources, not more: 978 is a
    **43-line stub** whose body is one Overview paragraph, and it is the
    *same programme* as 976, so it adds reach, not replication.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 34** (control-mode allocation) is adjacent and
    distinct — 34 asks *which controller is issuing*, this asks *what kind of
    change occurred*. A system can be in model-based control and still be
    updating only values. **Area 42** (functional fixedness) is the failure
    mode of getting the discriminant wrong: a rule that never gets questioned
    is indistinguishable from a value that never moves. **Area 19**
    (belief-to-action compliance). **Area 76** (the gate organ) — DA/5-HT
    routing is a second reason the gate cannot be one scalar.

- **Rank 2 — A change is Bayesian state inference, not value accumulation:
  keep the reversal hazard rate as a field.** `LOW`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** dlPFC population activity tracking a **posterior over
    hidden state**, with switching within **1–2 trials** — too fast for
    model-free accumulation, well captured by a model with a hazard rate
    (Bartolo & Averbeck 2020, ~1,000 neurons, 2 macaques).
  - **Design:** each standing expectation carries an explicit
    **hazard rate** — the probability that it is about to be invalidated —
    rather than a decayed confidence. The two behave identically until a
    reversal, and then differ: a decayed-confidence store has to accumulate
    enough error to cross a threshold, while a hazard-rate store predicts the
    crossing in advance and can pre-emptively widen the set of rules held
    live. The file's own agent prescription follows: *"maintain explicit
    uncertainty estimates for each tool's utility, not just point
    estimates."*
  - **Already exists:** particle filters and Bayesian bandits — standard
    practice, and **not** what any current agent routing layer does.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 976 §"Reversal Learning as Bayesian State
    Inference".
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Support: **1 source**, and it is a single-lab
    2-macaque recording study — `LOW` reflects a real dissociation from
    model-free, not a replicated effect size.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 43** (λ as a term in the objective) supplies the
    cost side; the hazard rate supplies the *uncertainty* side, and Daw/Niv/
    Dayan's arbitration (ORDER 977) needs both. **Area 34**.

- **Rank 3 — Flexibility is two capabilities and the corpus's own unity
  factor says they are correlated, not identical; carry the disagreement.**
  `UNTESTED`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** Friedman & Robbins (2022) — shifting is a **separable
    latent variable** from updating and inhibition, yet all three load on a
    common "unity" factor, plausibly general prefrontal integrity. Ragozzino
    et al. (1999): set-shifting training **alters the system required for
    subsequent reversal** — so the double dissociation is real but the
    processes are not independent.
  - **Design:** a system that implements only one of the two will have a
    characteristic blind spot, and the file names it: **over-training on a
    stable environment produces an OFC-lesion-like agent** — one that keeps
    executing a now-wrong value because the brake is the thing that was never
    exercised. This slot's function is to stop Rank 1's clean dissociation
    being read as architectural independence.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**; the file records the
    hierarchical-interaction position as a live minority reading, and per
    R-J4 it is carried unresolved.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 42** — the unity factor is the same shape as
    Area 42's competence/acquisition dissociation, one level up.

### Area 81 · The brake has a latency, and the latency is the design
constraint: stopping an action already in flight — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Executive-Control/Inhibitory-Control-Go-NoGo.md`
(ORDER 981, read whole), with the controller-allocator view from
`oracle/brain/Executive-Control/Cognitive-Control-Conflict.md` (ORDER 975).

**The brain part.** **Logan's independent race model** (1994) and the
**rIFG → STN → GPi "brake" network** (Aron et al. 2003 lesions, 2007 TMS,
2014 synthesis). Inhibition is **not a switch that can be pulled before an
action**; it is a second process that must *win a race* against an
already-launched one. The measurable quantity is **SSRT** — a **latent**
variable, not an observable one — and it is a **budget**: healthy adults
**~200–250 ms**, and a stop signal arriving later than that does not stop
anything, it only fails.

**Why this is an eighty-first structural silence.** Eighty areas prescribe
when to verify, what to check, and how to recover — and several (19, 39, 46)
build gates. **Every gate in the arena is specified as a *predicate* — allow,
deny, require approval — and not one is specified as a *deadline*.** A
predicate evaluated after the action has already gone is not a gate; it is a
post-mortem. The arena's most consequential writes (pushing to a remote,
`git push --force`, sending a message) are irreversible, and the arena has no
representation of the interval during which intervention was still possible.

- **Rank 1 — Every irreversible action carries a stated stop-window, and the
  veto runs in parallel with execution, not after it.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** the STN hyperdirect path as a **global hold** that
    bypasses the deliberative route, and SSRT as the budget it operates in.
  - **Design:** each action declares `irreversible: true|false` and, where
    true, an **abort window** — the interval during which a cancel signal
    still lands. Two consequences follow and both are buildable now: (i) a
    two-tier veto, **fast/coarse (hyperdirect) then slow/specific (rIFG)**,
    matching the corpus's own cascade ACC → STN → rIFG → dlPFC; (ii) **the
    monitor must not be an LLM**, because detection time is a *component* of
    the budget — the corpus's own design line is *"precompiled rules, not
    LLM-based evaluation."* This is the sharpest instance in the arena of
    **a design requirement that exists only because a latency was measured**.
  - **Already exists:** STN DBS is a deployed clinical intervention altering
    SSRT (Aron; Frank 2006; Hammerbeck 2016) — the mechanism is real and
    trialled; the agent form is not. Also `hooks/` and cron in the Hermes
    stack are *post-hoc* monitors, not parallel ones.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 981 §§"Logan's Independent Race Model", "The
    Brake Network", "Practical Design Implications", "Race Model as Agent
    Decision Architecture".
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` as design; human mechanism `HIGH` — the SST has
    **excellent test-retest reliability (r = 0.70–0.80)**, validated across
    hundreds of studies, with a *Nature Methods* (2019) review naming it among
    the most reliable cognitive tasks available.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 19** (belief-to-action compliance) is the gate
    this one times. **Area 33** (verification) is the trap: a verifier whose
    latency exceeds the stop-window certifies an action already taken.
    **Area 45/46** (provenance, self-model) — a cancel that arrives after the
    write is a *correction*, correctly recorded as one. **Area 79** (framing)
    applies with force: a stop rule is a written instruction, and its
    compliance is frame-dependent.

- **Rank 2 — "Inhibition" is at least three processes, so a system needs more
  than one brake; build the ambiguity in rather than resolving it.**
  `UNTESTED`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** **cancellation** (stop an action in progress — the SST),
    **preemption** (prevent initiation — go/no-go), and **interference
    suppression** (resolve conflict — Stroop), with **cross-task
    correlations of only r ≈ 0.30–0.40 (SST vs go/no-go) and lower still vs
    Stroop** — which is itself the argument: if all three measured one factor,
    the correlations would be high. COMT Val158Met affects the measures
    differentially (Aron et al. 2004).
  - **Design:** separate monitors for *write* actions, *send* actions, and
    *compute* actions, each with its own window. The corpus's own
    conservative recommendation is adopted verbatim: *"implement multiple
    independent inhibition pipelines until evidence supports a unified
    mechanism."*
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**; the unitarity question is
    recorded as **the field's biggest open question**, and fMRI multivariate
    pattern analysis is reported as *not yet* decisive.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 26** (discriminability) is the same question at
    the level of signals. **Area 81 Rank 1** — a single unified HOLD is what
    Rank 1's *global* tier provides; this slot says the global tier is not
    sufficient alone.

- **Rank 3 — The independence assumption is contested, and the contest is the
  finding: a stop process that also slows the go process is a different
  architecture.** `UNSUCCESSFUL`, Support 1, kept as a boundary.
  - **Brain part:** Dinstein, Maniscalco & Rubin (2003) showed the
    independent race model is **indistinguishable from dependent (collation)
    models given the available data** — *"we are fitting a complex model to
    data that a simpler dependent model could explain equally well."* SSRT
    would then conflate inhibition with go-process slowing.
  - **Design:** explicitly **do not build** a system whose safety claim
    depends on the independence assumption, because the assumption is not
    empirically identified. A go process that is itself slowed by the presence
    of monitoring is a *different* safety property from one that is not, and
    the arena cannot currently tell which it has. Recorded `UNSUCCESSFUL` in
    the honest sense: the clean model was fitted and did not survive.
  - **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL`. Support: **1 source**, plus DDM variants
    (Rounding & Brown 2016) fitting the same data with fewer assumptions.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 24** (self-diagnosis) — an instrument whose
    assumptions are not identified is a self-report. **Area 33**.

### Area 82 · The second substrate: a non-neuronal layer that gates whether
learning happens at all

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Glial-Biology/Astrocyte-Neuron-Interactions-and-Cognition.md`
(ORDER 1016, read whole) §§"Computational Operations Implemented by the
Tripartite Synapse", "Metabolic Gating of Plasticity", "IP3R2 Knockout
Phenotype", "Tensions and Divergences".

**The brain part.** The **tripartite synapse** (Perea/Navarrete/Araque 2009) —
the synapse is not two elements but three, the third being the **perisynaptic
astrocytic process** — and, behind it, the **astrocyte–neuron lactate shuttle**
(Pellerin & Magistretti 1994). Astrocytes outnumber neurons in human cortex
(~1.4:1) and a single human cortical astrocyte contacts up to **2 million**
synapses. The operative claim is not that glia compute; it is that **metabolic
state is a gate on plasticity**: the corpus's line is *"metabolic constraints
are not implementation details — they are computational primitives."*

**Why this is an eighty-second structural silence.** Eighty-two areas describe
what the system should compute, when, in what order, and how to check it — and
**every one of them assumes a single substrate and an unconditional right to
write.** Not one asks whether the thing in front of the system is in a state
that permits learning at all. The arena has a gate on *truth* (Area 33), a gate
on *time* (Area 81), a gate on *reversibility* (Area 25), and a gate on
*novelty* (Area 27) — **and no gate on the system's own capacity to be changed
by what it is being asked to learn.** A system that can always write has no
consolidation phase, only a write log.

- **Rank 1 — A state gate on the write path: consolidation is refused, not
  queued, when the substrate's capacity is spent.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** the **metabolic gate on long-term potentiation** — lactate
    via HCAR1 gating the plasticity pathway, and glycogenolysis driven
    *up* by noradrenergic arousal. The corpus's sharpest single experiment is
    Suzuki et al. (2011, *Cell*): a glycogen phosphorylase inhibitor **impaired
    long-term memory in inhibitory avoidance while leaving short-term memory
    intact**, and **L-lactate — but not pyruvate or glucose — rescued it.**
    That is a double dissociation in one preparation: consolidation has a
    substrate requirement that retrieval does not, and *what* is supplied
    matters, not merely *that* energy is.
  - **Design:** each store carries a `capacity` scalar, and a write to a
    saturated store is **refused and reported as refused** rather than
    silently downgraded. The corpus's own rule is adopted verbatim:
    *"implement compute budgets with hard gates — refusing to run inference
    when energy/latency budgets are exceeded, rather than producing degraded
    outputs."* This is the one slot in eighty-three areas that can return
    "I did not learn that," which is a first-class result, not a failure.
  - **Already exists:** the arena's own `verified: []` /
    `stale_after:` frontmatter block (ORDER 1016 carries it, on OKF v0.2) is
    a **capacity field with no enforcement** — the substrate this job runs on
    already records capacity and never acts on it. Adopting the existing block
    beats building one.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1016 §§"Metabolic Gating of Plasticity",
    "Bidirectional Homeostatic Regulation", "Lessons for Artificial
    Cognitive Architecture Robustness" §2.
  - **Grade:** design `UNTESTED` — bespoke. The human mechanism is **`LOW`,
    deliberately not HIGH**, and the reason is the file's own effect sizes:
    astrocytic manipulations produce **10–30% changes in LTP and subtle
    memory deficits**, and the corpus states plainly that astrocytes *"may be
    modulators rather than primary drivers."*
  - **Interaction:** **Area 43** (the cost of thinking) — this is λ made
    *binding*; R-J1 is not violated because R-J1 governs what the arena
    *builds*, and a substrate that cannot be written to is a capacity fact,
    not a price fact. **Area 6/23** (consolidation) gain a *reason* for the
    maintenance pass to exist. **Area 30** (per-episode cost) is the same
    ceiling seen from the ingestion side.

- **Rank 2 — The edge is a third party: every write is local, plus a
  context-modulating process, plus the recent history of that context.**
  `UNTESTED`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** the tripartite synapse's three computational operations
    that have no neuronal equivalent — **GLT-1 clearance of >90% of released
    glutamate**, which the corpus names *dynamic divisive normalization*
    rather than cleanup; **mGluR5 coincidence detection** implementing a
    three-factor rule `pre × post × astrocyte`; and **D-serine gating** of the
    NMDA co-agonist site, which **depletes under sustained activation and so
    raises the threshold for subsequent plasticity** — BCM's sliding
    threshold arriving from the cell biology rather than from the theory.
  - **Design:** a write carries not `(item, timestamp)` but `(item, local
    state, context-state)`, and the context component carries its own
    saturation. **This is Area 66's declared per-area gain, arriving with the
    depletion mechanism that justifies it**, and Area 66's own Rank 1 becomes
    the weaker of the two on this reading: a declared gain the system can set
    at will is not the same as a gain the substrate has spent.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` for the design. The mechanism is graded `LOW` for
    the reason in Rank 1, and additionally because **the release mechanism is
    unresolved** — vesicular exocytosis, hemichannels, and bestrophin-1
    reversal are all live candidates, and the corpus records that the choice
    determines kinetics and spatial spread. A design that depends on *how*
    gliotransmission happens cannot be specified while that is open; the
    arena therefore takes the **three-factor structure** and refuses the
    **transport mechanism**.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 8** (structural fidelity) — a memory that records
    its context-state is a memory that can be read as wrong later.
    **Area 40** (circadian phase) is the same idea at a different timescale
    and the two should be one field, not two.

- **Rank 3 — Do not build a glial layer. The knockout says the strong claim is
  `UNSUCCESSFUL`, and that is the finding.** `UNSUCCESSFUL`, Support 1, kept
  as a boundary.
  - **Brain part:** **IP3R2 knockout abolishes most astrocytic Ca²⁺ signals
    and produces, in the corpus's words, "surprisingly mild cognitive
    phenotypes"** — subtle memory deficits, intact basic transmission.
    Thirty years of work, and removing the whole calcium signal does not break
    the brain. The corpus states the conclusion without hedging it: glia are
    *"optimizers and stabilizers, not core computational engines."*
  - **Design:** explicitly **do not** add a simulated glial network to the
    forward pass. The corpus's own guidance is adopted: glial-inspired layers
    should be *"regularization and adaptation modules, not core
    forward-pass components."* Recorded `UNSUCCESSFUL` in the honest sense —
    the strong hypothesis was tested by the people who hold it, and it
    returned a null on the core claim while retaining a real effect on
    modulation.
  - **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL` on the strong form; `LOW` retained on the
    modulatory form. **Both grades stated; neither averaged.**
  - **Interaction:** **Area 62** (three levels of analysis) — this is a
    physical-level claim being made about a computational-level design, and
    the corpus is the file that flags its own timescale mismatch
    (*"mesoscopic — too slow for fast inference, too fast for slow
    learning"*). **Area 33**: an unbuilt layer is `not observed`, never
    `confirmed absent`.

### Area 83 · The mean is not a model of the individual

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Gender-and-Cognition/Gender-Sex-Differences-in-Cognition.md`
(ORDER 1012, read whole) §§"Sexual Dimorphism vs. Overlap", "Effect Sizes and
Overlap Distributions", "What Sex Differences Teach About Modular vs.
Generalist Systems", "Cross-Cultural Variation".

**The brain part.** **The mosaic brain** (Joel 2015, 2020) — across 116
regions in 281 subjects, individual brains *"rarely cluster as 'typically male'
or 'typically female'"*; most are a unique mosaic of male-typical and
female-typical features — set beside the **overlapping-distribution result**
that makes it general: of **124 effect sizes across 46 meta-analyses, 78% are
small or near zero (d < 0.35)**, reconfirmed on 106 meta-analyses and 386
effect sizes, and small differences mean **~80% distribution overlap**, so
*"most men score above the average woman, and most women score above the
average man, on nearly every cognitive measure."*

**Why this is an eighty-third structural silence, and the sharpest one in the
arena's own direction.** Eighty-three areas are written in the singular.
Every slot says *the system should*, *the user has*, *the corpus shows* — and
the corpus supplies the measurement that makes the singular a category error:
**a group mean with d = 0.3 classifies almost no individual.** Not one area
asks whether the defaults it specifies are the defaults of *this* user. The
arena's own §8 finding is that a well-sourced claim can be wrong; this area
adds the sharper form — **a correctly-sourced population claim can be wrong
about the individual it is applied to**, and nothing in 9,555 lines detects
the difference.

- **Rank 1 — Declare the population a claim is about, and per-user parameters
  beside it, so no default is silently universal.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** the **mosaic brain** as a design constraint — not "men and
    women differ" but *"the individual is the unit and the group is a
    distribution."* The corpus's own distributional statement is the design
    rule: if 80% of distributions overlap, **a single default is wrong for
    most of the population by construction**, and the honest default is a
    distribution the user can move within.
  - **Design:** every area's rank-1 default ships with
    `population_scope: <who the evidence covers>` beside it, and anything
    user-facing ships with a per-user override. The corpus's §5.5 line is
    adopted because it is the operational form: *"users have diverse
    cognitive styles; agents should adapt."* **This is Area 47's
    self-construal field with the group-difference statistics attached** —
    Area 47 said the arena's defaults are a cultural bet; this says the
    bet's *error bars* are ±80% of the population.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1012 §§"Sexual Dimorphism vs. Overlap",
    "Effect Sizes and Overlap Distributions", "Avoiding Bias in Agent Design".
  - **Grade:** design `UNTESTED`. The distributional finding is `HIGH` —
    **two independent large-scale meta-analytic replications** (Hyde 2005,
    124 effect sizes / 46 meta-analyses; 2015 re-evaluation, 106
    meta-analyses / 386 effect sizes), and it is the most replicated
    quantitative claim in this tranche.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 47** directly, and this area is the one that
    could have been a duplicate of it and is not: 47 asks what *shape* the
    self has, this asks what *distribution* a default is drawn from.
    **Area 24** — a per-user override that is never exercised is a label.
    **Area 26** (discriminability) is the same question at the level of
    signals.

- **Rank 2 — Heterogeneous component populations beat one homogenised
  component, and the corpus says so from two directions.** `UNTESTED`,
  Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** the **modularity ↔ integration dissociation** in
    connectivity (Ingalhalikar et al. 2014, *PNAS*, n = 949) — one
    population higher within-hemispheric connectivity and modularity,
    the other higher between-hemispheric integration and cross-module
    participation coefficients. The corpus's reading is careful and is
    adopted with its caution: these are **two valid optimization
    strategies**, not a better and a worse one — *"neither architecture
    dominates."*
  - **Design:** do not optimise every component toward one objective or one
    reasoning style; the corpus's explicit recommendation is
    *"don't optimize all agents toward the same objective function."* The
    arena's ~120 specialist skills are a population that was deliberately
    differentiated, and this slot says that differentiation is the design,
    not a convenience — while noting the file's own 2020 follow-up found
    the network direction *reverses* on functional connectivity, so the
    structural finding and its functional follow-up **do not agree** and
    neither is entered as settled.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` for the design. The structural dissociation is
    `LOW` — **one large study (n = 949) plus a 2024 functional follow-up
    that does not reproduce its direction**, recorded per R-J4 with both
    sides attributed.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 34** (control-mode allocation) — a
    differentiated population is a stronger substrate for arbitration than a
    homogeneous one. **Area 38** (workspace capacity): more styles means
    more selection, inside a budget that did not grow.

- **Rank 3 — The mosaic claim is contested, and the contest is preserved.**
  `LOW`, Support 1, kept as a boundary.
  - **Brain part:** Rippon (2017) argues Joel's analysis **cherry-picked the
    10 most dimorphic regions**, and the corpus records the objection rather
    than dismissing it. Set against it, a **February 2024 *PNAS* study using
    deep networks on ~1,500 dynamic fMRI scans reportedly classified sex
    from scans with high accuracy** — which, if it holds, says group-level
    structure is more recoverable than the mosaic reading implies. The file
    resolves this into *"differences exist at the group level but individual
    brains are mosaics,"* and **that resolution is the file's, not a
    finding**, so the arena does not enter it.
  - **Design:** the honest position is that **the size of the mosaic
    argument is not settled**, and Rank 1 stands anyway because it is a
    *scoping* discipline — declaring which population a claim covers is
    correct whether or not the population difference is large.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 65** (unconscious inference) — a mosaic read is
    an amodal-symbol theory's best case and an interface theory's worst, and
    Rippon's objection is that the measurement, not the inference, is what
    carries the conclusion.

### Area 84 · Ignition: a threshold on *having* the thought, not on whether it is true

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Global-Workspace-Theory/Global-Workspace-Theory-of-Consciousness.md`
(ORDER 1018, read whole) §§"The Two-Stage Process: Feedforward Sweep and
Ignition", "The Ignition Phenomenon in Detail", "The Ignition Phenomenon" §2.4,
"Global Broadcasting and Access Consciousness", "Experimental Paradigms for
Testing Broadcasting", "Cognitive Architecture".

**The brain part.** **Baars' global workspace and Dehaene & Changeux's
ignition** — the **feedforward sweep (~100 ms) → nonlinear threshold crossing →
recurrent global broadcast (~300 ms)** sequence, with the workspace holding
**~4–7 items** and the ignition threshold *modulated* by attention,
neuromodulators (LC noradrenaline, nucleus basalis acetylcholine), arousal,
task relevance and **prediction error**.

**Why this is an eighty-fourth structural silence, and the first about a
threshold on a quantity no other area gates.** Eighty-three areas gate on
whether a claim is *true* (1, 6, 8), whether it is *confident enough* (2, 33),
whether it is *cheap* (3, 30, 43), whether it is *novel* (49), whether it
*coincides* (39), and whether the substrate will accept it (82). **Every one of
those gates is applied to a representation the system already has.** GWT's
threshold is applied to a representation the system **has not yet had**: below
threshold the input is *processed* — subjects respond to masked stimuli at
above-chance rates, and the corpus's own model reproduces masking effects
exactly — and **produces nothing available to report, remember, or act on.**
The information exists and is discarded at the point of *entry into awareness*,
which is upstream of every gate the arena holds. **Nothing in the arena
specifies a threshold on availability.** All 83 areas assume availability and
dispute what to do afterwards.

- **Rank 1 — Declare an ignition threshold and make below-threshold processing
  a first-class, *reported* outcome rather than a silent one.** `LOW`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** the **ignition threshold** and its modulators — the
    threshold is *not fixed*, it is lowered by attention, arousal, task
    relevance and surprise, which is what makes it a control surface rather
    than a constant.
  - **Design:** every reasoning step declares whether its output **ignited**
    (available to the next step, to memory, and to the report) or merely
    **swept** (processed, discarded, and *named as discarded*). The load-bearing
    clause is that **a sweep is not a failure and must not be reported as one** —
    the corpus's masked-priming finding is precisely that a swept stimulus
    leaves a real, above-chance trace, so the system's own report would
    otherwise read *"I did not process that"* for something it did process. In
    this workspace the concrete instance is immediate and unflattering: **a
    tranche that reads 20 files and reports 3 absorbed has swept 17**, and
    today's report says so — but only because an earlier pass wrote that
    discipline down by hand. Nothing in `LEDGER.md` distinguishes swept from
    ignited; the ledger has one mark.
  - **Already exists:** **spiking GWT models** (Dehaene & Changeux, 100,000
    neurons, reproducing feedforward sweep, ignition, masking, binocular
    rivalry, P3b, attention-modulated ignition, and simulated-anaesthesia
    disruption — ORDER 1018 §2.5); **Global Workspace Architectures** and
    McClelland's Fluid Framework (§8.3). **The threshold constant is not
    implemented anywhere in the Hermes stack**, and per the footprint ladder in
    ORDER 1034 §4 it does not belong in core: it is a *skill + schema* change,
    not a tool.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1018 §§"The Two-Stage Process", "The Ignition
    Phenomenon in Detail", "Global Broadcasting and Access Consciousness",
    "Computational Models of Neural GWT".
  - **Grade:** `LOW` on the mechanism, `UNTESTED` on the design. The mechanism
    is not `HIGH` despite the volume of convergent evidence, for two reasons
    both on record in the earning file: **the P3b is disputed as the NCC of
    consciousness** (the file's own table grades Koch et al. 2016 *"Partial
    (posterior emphasis)"*), and **§5.1 records an unresolved frontal-vs-posterior
    conflict** with IIT's posterior hot zone. The design has no trial, and
    **no corpus source anywhere proposes a threshold on availability** — the
    concept is being imported from a theory of consciousness into an
    architecture that has no consciousness claim, which is exactly the transfer
    Area 74 Rank 1 forbids.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is upstream of every other gate, and a gate applied
    downstream of availability cannot recover a representation that was never
    made available. This is Area 35's argument one level up — a result never
    produced cannot be verified.
  - **Interaction:** **Constrains every area.** Areas 1, 6, 8, 2, 33 all
    presuppose the item reached the checker; a swept item is invisible to all of
    them, and a system that reports its sweeps is the only way the arena can
    tell *nothing was found* from *nothing was looked for*. **Area 38**
    (workspace capacity) supplies the ~4–7 figure, and **Area 82**'s write-path
    state gate is the same shape at a different point in the cycle. **Area 73**
    (phenomenal vs access) is the standing objection: ignition is an *access*
    phenomenon, and a system that ignites has not thereby got anything it is
    like — so this area must not be read as a consciousness claim, and the file
    says the same thing.
- **Rank 2 — Broadcast, don't push: a pull architecture in which every consumer
  subscribes rather than one scheduler deciding who is told.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** **global broadcasting** via long-range projection neurons in
    layers III and V, and the file's explicit contrast between a **pull**
    architecture (modules subscribe; no central scheduler) and a push one
    (§*GWT and the Frame Problem*). The corpus's argument is the **frame
    problem** — a system with thousands of specialised components cannot have a
    central executor decide which one should respond.
  - **Design:** the arena's own topology is a push architecture. `SKILL.md`
    descriptions are injected into the system prompt for *every* skill
    (ORDER 1034 §5: *"The system prompt includes the skill index — name +
    description for every installed skill"*) and the runtime then selects —
    and the deferred tool catalog is **68 tools across 4 servers** (Area 38)
    against a prompt whose own tool-selection guidance is *"3–5 tool options at
    each decision point rather than 20"*. That is a scheduler choosing who is
    told, and the corpus's own brain part says the choice should not be made in
    advance. Rank 2 is the subscription form: a component declares the trigger
    it responds to and nothing is loaded until a trigger fires.
  - **Already exists:** **skills-as-triggers already is this.** The system
    prompt carries `name` + `description` and the model loads `SKILL.md` on a
    match (ORDER 1034 §*How they load*). **The design exists and has never been
    described as broadcasting** — adopt-don't-build, the same finding as Area 63
    Rank 1's hybrid retriever.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1018 §§"GWT and the Frame Problem", "The
    Cognitive Architecture", "Global Broadcasting and Access Consciousness";
    ORDER 1034 §5 (*Skills*).
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` for the design. The mechanism is graded on the file's
    evidence base but **the design is not a trial of broadcasting** — the
    arena's skill index is a *string in a prompt*, and whether selecting on it
    is selection by trigger or by a scheduler in the model's attention is
    **not established by anything in this corpus**, and the file says as much
    when it notes that RPT holds recurrent activity can correlate with
    perception *without* frontoparietal involvement.
  - **Why rank 2:** the mechanism is real and the design is already deployed,
    but it is **not rank 1 because it changes nothing about availability** —
    it is a distribution change downstream of a threshold that still does not
    exist, and a better-distributed set of never-ignited items is still never
    ignited.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 38** again, hard. Broadcast is a *capacity*
    architecture and the arena's evidence is that capacity binds: a 4-item
    workspace and a 68-tool catalog cannot both be true. **Area 22** (action
    selection and the size of the action repertoire) is the same question about
    actions rather than about information.
- **Rank 3 — Record that the corpus's own strongest empirical instrument for
  this area reports a signal the system cannot currently produce, and do not
  build the instrument.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** **reportability as the operational criterion** — the file is
    explicit that GWT is *defined* by it (*"Conscious content is reportable —
    the subject can verbally describe or behaviorally indicate what they are
    experiencing"*), and that reportability is what makes access consciousness
    the scientifically tractable target.
  - **Design:** none. This slot holds a boundary. The file's best instrument is
    the **no-report paradigm** — measure discrimination by fMRI adaptation and
    MVPA on a subject **never asked to report** — and the arena already holds
    that instrument at Area 73, where it arrived from a different discipline.
    **It is recorded here as a second, converging arrival, and not counted as
    a second independent source**: ORDER 952 (Dehaene) and ORDER 1018 are the
    same research programme, and per the arena's clustering rule one programme
    is one source.
  - **Already exists:** the no-report paradigm (Area 73's earning file). It
    requires fMRI and a human subject; **nothing in the Hermes stack can run
    it on itself**, and a substitute measurement would be a self-measurement,
    which **R-J2 forbids as decision evidence**.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1018 §§"Reportability", "Report Tasks",
    "Reportability and the Access/Phenomenal Split"; cross-reference **Area 73**
    and its earning file rather than re-citing.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`, carried deliberately as a boundary slot. The design
    is not proposed **because the arena's own rule bars it**: R-J2 forbids
    creating a self-measurement as decision evidence, and the only measurement
    this area's own theory nominates is one taken on the system itself.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the third slot in the arena whose content is *"do not
    build this"*, and the reason is different from the other two: Area 53
    Rank 3's null was **scoped wrong** and this one is **correctly scoped and
    binding**, because the rule that stops it is a workspace rule rather than a
    fact about the world.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 24** (validity of one's own instruments) and
    **R-J2** jointly hold this slot down. It also sits directly under **Area
    73**: if a report is evidence about availability and not about experience,
    then the reportability criterion cannot be used to detect experience, and
    the area's own instrument is self-defeating for the question it was built
    to answer.

### Area 85 · The symbol grounding regress: a token whose only link to the world is another token `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/knowledge-representation/symbol-grounding.md`
(ORDER 1110, 250 lines) and `oracle/brain/Knowledge-Representation/Symbol-Grounding.md`
(ORDER 1111, 310 lines), both read whole this pass. **Two files sharing one
`id: "symbol-grounding"`** — filed as infra C-056; the two are read as **one
source**, not two, per the three-files-one-study rule. ORDER 1111 is the longer
and better-annotated of the pair and carries the load.

**The brain part.** There is no organ here, and that is the finding. Harnad's
(1990, *Physica D* 42:335–346) claim is a **structural** one: a symbol system
whose tokens acquire meaning only from other tokens **never terminates its
semantic regress**, and the eight-criterion definition he reconstructs from
Newell, Pylyshyn and Fodor makes the test checkable — criterion 4 (manipulation
by token *shape*, not meaning) plus criterion 8 (the system is *systematically
interpretable*) is precisely the pair that the Chinese Room satisfies while
lacking understanding. The proposed organ is the **sensorimotor categorical
representation** — the feature detector that picks out invariant features, which
Harnad locates in connectionist networks learning from the **toil** of
sensorimotor interaction, with symbols providing the **theft** of compositional
productivity on top (Cangelosi & Harnad 2001, *Evolution of Communication*
4(1):117–142). **Eighty-four areas build a brain out of organs; this is the
first whose brain part is the *absence* of a link.**

**Why this is an eighty-fifth structural silence.** Eighty-four areas assume
that what the system stores means what it stores. Areas 1, 2, 8, 24, 33, 76 all
assume a claim's content is connected to something. **Not one asks whether the
content's only connection is to other content.** The arena's own §8 inversion is
this exact failure made concrete: a file with a perfect TOC and a fabricated
citation is a symbol system describing itself.

- **Rank 1 — Grade every stored claim by its grounding class, and say which
  class it is in the citation itself.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** Harnad's **grounding depth** — the file's own gradient of
    **high** (the agent can execute and observe the effect: files, terminal,
    git), **medium** (observe but not control: browsing, API calls), **low**
    (text only: theoretical physics, history, abstract mathematics) — and his
    **robotic Turing test**, which demands sensorimotor interaction rather than
    linguistic behavior alone.
  - **Design:** a claim carries a grounding class, and **the class is part of
    the claim's identity, not a property of the page that happens to hold it.**
    A low-grounding claim that is cited without its class is the failure the
    arena has been reporting for nineteen tranches. In this workspace the
    mechanism is already half-built and unused: `LEDGER.md` has four states and
    a file read whole earns `[x]` — but a file read whole can still contain an
    invented citation, which is exactly the class boundary.
  - **Already exists:** **Blanco & Vogt (2004, CogSci 26)** — sensorimotor-
    grounded agents reached ~85% symbol-object accuracy after 100 trials versus
    ~40% for description-only agents, the corpus's one quantitative grounding
    comparison. **Nothing in the Hermes stack grades grounding depth.** Adopt-
    don't-build: one field, per R-J5 it must hold at 14,589 files, so it is a
    schema addition rather than a per-file judgement.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1110 / 1111 §§"From Discrimination to
    Description", "The Robot Turing Test", "Tool-Execution Grounding Problem",
    "Knowledge Representation and Grounding Depth"; Blanco & Vogt 2004.
  - **Grade:** `LOW` on the mechanism — Harnad's formulation is `HIGH` as
    philosophy and the Cangelosi & Harnad robotic result is a real experiment,
    but **Taddeo & Floridi (2005, *JETAI* 17(4):419–445) reviewed fifteen years
    of this literature and concluded no approach solves it**, naming four
    (sensorimotor, conceptual-space, evolutionary, social). The design is
    `UNTESTED` and **no trial anywhere grades grounding depth**.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is upstream of Area 24 and Area 33. A confidence number
    attached to a low-grounding claim is a number about the class, not the
    claim, and the arena cannot tell them apart today.
  - **Interaction:** **constrains every area that assigns a grade.** Area 8's
    structural-fidelity claim is about whether a *relation* is faithful; this
    asks whether either endpoint means anything. **Area 35** (sim-to-real) is
    the same question one level up: both ask where the causal link to the world
    is. **Area 20** (image schemas) is the *positive* proposal — grounding
    concepts in sensorimotor primitives rather than in each other — and this
    area is the standing objection to it: Harnad's regress is not dissolved by
    adding a grounding layer if the layer's own primitives are only named, not
    exercised.
- **Rank 2 — Require the grounding trace to be executable, not merely cited:
  a claim about a tool the system can run is grounded by having run it.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** Harnad's **discriminate → identify → describe** ladder —
    semantic weight *"is fixed by the behavioral capacity of the dedicated
    symbol system to discriminate, identify, and describe all the objects and
    states of affairs to which its symbols refer."*
  - **Design:** the citation carries the *executed command*, not the claim
    that one exists. This is the corpus's own operational test and it is
    already the arena's working practice — the SOUL rule that *claimed ≠ done*
    is a grounding rule imported from Harnad without the reference, and **the
    arena is stronger here than its own citations admit.**
  - **Already exists:** the Hermes tool layer is the sensorimotor coupling.
    Adopt-don't-build.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1110 / 1111 §"The Hermes Agent as a Hybrid
    System", §"The Tool-Execution Grounding Problem".
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` as design; the principle is the arena's most
    reliable working rule and has never been tested as one.
  - **Why rank 2:** it is the enforceable half of Rank 1 and **the only slot in
    the arena whose design is already the default behaviour** — which is the
    finding, and the reason it is not rank 1: a rule already obeyed is not a
    design decision, and grading it as one would overstate what changed.
  - **Interaction:** Rank 1's constraint, stated as an action. **Area 76**
    (verification ceiling) is the same demand at a different threshold: a
    system that cannot verify past NP still knows which claims it *executed*.
- **Rank 3 — Treat the regress as unresolvable by design and make the system's
  claims about its own meaning explicitly provisional.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** the same absence, taken as a limit. The file's
    **MacDorman (2007, *Interaction Studies* 8(1):143–158)** critique is the
    mechanism: grounding is a property of **causal interaction with an
    environment**, not of computational architecture, so the
    symbolic/connectionist distinction is a category error and there is no
    internal signal that distinguishes a grounded robot from one that merely
    behaves as if it were — the corpus's own statement of the **other-minds
    problem** applied to a system measuring itself.
  - **Design:** where grounding depth is low, the system says so in the output
    rather than resolving the question in its own favour. This is Area 84
    Rank 1's *report the sweep* applied to meaning instead of availability.
  - **Already exists:** the corpus's own epistemic-protocol practice, under a
    different name.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1110 / 1111 §"The Measurement Problem", §"LLMs
    and the Grounding Crisis"; MacDorman 2007; Taddeo & Floridi 2005.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`, and **deliberately not raised** — Lee & Shee (2023)
    propose an operational test for causal vs. distributional grounding and
    Khanehzar et al. (2023) report multimodal models gaining 30–40pp on visual
    grounding **while still failing compositional reasoning about grounded
    symbols**, so even the partial case is unmeasured here.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the arena's third *do-not-build* boundary (with
    Area 53 Rank 3 and Area 84 Rank 3) and the one with the strongest
    independent backing — a 15-year review concluding the problem is unsolved
    is better evidence than any single trial.
  - **Interaction:** **R-J2** and **Area 24** hold it. It is also the deepest
    constraint on **Area 20**: if the regress cannot be escaped, an
    image-schema grounding layer is a *better-motivated* regress, not a
    solution.

### Area 86 · Coordinate frames as a property of the *readout*, not of the stored item `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Knowledge-Representation/Gain-Fields-Coordinate-Transforms.md`
(ORDER 1098, 347 lines), read whole this pass. Sole earning file.
Corroborated within the tranche by ORDER 1099 (grid cells) and ORDER 1092
(cognitive maps).

**The brain part.** **Gain fields in posterior parietal cortex** — a neuron's
response multiplicatively modulated by a behavioural state variable, so that
**no single neuron is position-invariant but the population pattern supports
any reference frame.** Pouget & Sejnowski (1997, *J. Cognitive Neuroscience*
9:222–237) formalized it as a **basis-function representation**: one
overcomplete population, many linear readouts, and **a change of frame is a
change of readout weights, not a change of representation.** The named
mechanisms: **saccadic remapping** (Duhamel, Colby & Goldberg 1992, *Science*
255:90–92 — LIP receptive fields shift ~130 ms *before* the saccade) and
**compound gain fields** (Chang, Papadimitriou & Snyder 2009, *Neuron*
64:744–755, multiplicative beats additive by ~15% R²).

**Why this is an eighty-sixth structural silence.** Eighty-five areas store
items and retrieve them by similarity. **Every one of them fixes the frame at
write time and never names it.** A claim retrieved for *this* task and the same
claim retrieved for *audit* are stored once and read twice — and the arena has
no vocabulary for the difference, because it has no area about the coordinate
system a retrieval is expressed in. `ARENA.md` holds **zero** occurrences of
*gain field*, *population code*, *reference frame*, or *basis function*.

- **Rank 1 — Separate the stored representation from the readouts, and make
  the frame an explicit argument to every retrieval rather than a property of
  the index.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** the **overcomplete gain-field population and its
    frame-specific linear readout** — the file's load-bearing economic claim is
    that the brain does **not** maintain a separate map per coordinate system
    and that this is why the design scales to many frames.
  - **Design:** retrieval takes a **frame** — task-relative, system-relative,
    audit-relative — and the same stored item is decoded differently per frame.
    Concretely, in this workspace: `LEDGER.md`'s `[x]` is a *system-relative*
    frame, and the arena's "are the 39 citations earned?" question is a
    *task-relative* frame over the **same rows** — and the two disagree,
    because the marks are per-file and the audit is per-citation. **That
    disagreement is invisible in the current design and is exactly what this
    slot makes nameable.**
  - **Already exists:** the graph layer already does part of this — Graphify's
    two graphs are the corpus's version of a multi-frame population, and
    ORDER 1099 supplies the concrete missing half: **phase-difference vector
    arithmetic across modules** gives zero-shot relational inference the arena
    currently gets only by explicit edge traversal. Adopt-don't-build, with a
    named argument that does not exist yet.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1098 §§2.1–2.4, 3.2, 5.2, 5.4; ORDER 1099
    §§"Grid Modules as a Basis for Relational Inference", "Vector-based
    Navigation".
  - **Grade:** `LOW` on the mechanism, `UNTESTED` on the design. Not `HIGH`,
    and the file says why in its own §4.2: **compound gain fields have not been
    independently replicated in a systematic study**, head-position modulation
    *"has been difficult to replicate because it requires recording from freely
    behaving animals"*, and the file names **publication bias toward positive
    findings** explicitly. The design has no trial.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot that makes the arena's existing
    silent frame-switching explicit, and **every other area's retrieval claim
    is a claim in some unnamed frame.**
  - **Interaction:** **Area 72** (grid cells, drift, anchoring) is the same
    organ seen from the metric side — 72 supplies the frame, this supplies the
    readout. **Area 8** (structural fidelity) gains its sharpest form here:
    a *relational* store that can be read in any frame still loses whatever no
    frame can express. **Area 38** (workspace capacity) is a frame question —
    "how much fits" is unanswerable without naming the frame it is measured in.
- **Rank 2 — Use geometric position rather than a similarity score, and let
  interpolation answer questions no edge answers.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** the **multi-modular grid code** — modules at ≈1.4×
    geometric scale ratio, so that **phase differences across modules resolve
    ambiguity a single module cannot**, and **shortcut navigation becomes
    vector arithmetic** (Banino et al. 2018, *Nature* 557:429–433: grid-like
    units emerge in path-integrated agents, and **silencing them selectively
    destroys shortcut-taking** — a causal test, not a correlation).
  - **Design:** a metric layer over the store, so "what lies between A and C"
    is answerable by interpolation rather than traversal. ORDER 1099 supplies a
    runnable sketch (`GridConceptSpace` with phase vectors) — the corpus's own
    code, and **the only implementation sketch in this tranche**.
  - **Already exists:** pgvector cosine is a similarity score, not a coordinate
    with a stated spacing, orientation and phase. **Not built.**
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1099 §§"Grid Modules as a Basis for Relational
    Inference", "Computational Modeling: Grid Cell-Inspired Vector Algebra",
    §4.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`, and **this is the tranche's most tempting and most
    dangerous slot**: the file's own §*Methodological* sections are the reason.
    It states that **fMRI hexadirectional analysis is a population property,
    not a single-cell measurement** (*"the field increasingly uses 'grid-like
    representation' for fMRI findings and reserves 'grid cells' for single-unit
    data"*), that effect sizes are **modest (Z ≈ 3–5)** and fail to replicate in
    samples under n=20, that Constantinescu et al.'s conceptual space is
    **2-D only** against a high-dimensional discrete world, and that **Neta et
    al.'s developmental result is cross-sectional and correlational**. A
    2-D continuous toy space is not a knowledge base.
  - **Why rank 2:** the mechanism is better evidenced than Rank 1's, but it
    **generalises worse** — it is the slot that will look most convincing and
    fail most quietly on a corpus that is mostly discrete and hierarchical.
  - **Interaction:** **complements Area 8 and constrains it.** ORDER 1099 states
    the split plainly: spreading activation *"excels at discrete, categorical,
    hierarchical relations"*; grid codes *"excel at continuous, metric,
    analogical relations."* ORDER 1107 says the same from the other side —
    **vector DBs are purely surface and implement MAC without FAC.** Three
    files, one convergent claim, and it is the strongest triangulated result in
    this tranche.
- **Rank 3 — Do not confuse the geometry with the content: a metric layer
  predicts positions, and the corpus is full of things that are not positions.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** the same organ, as its own limit. ORDER 1099 §3 is the
    finding: grid codes handle **continuous, metric, relational** structure;
    they do **not** handle **discrete, hierarchical, taxonomic** knowledge
    (*"bird" → "sparrow"*), and no hybrid discrete-continuous grid code is
    demonstrated. ORDER 1109 makes the sharper version: a VQ codebook's
    **hard quantization introduces a discontinuity** — *"two vectors that are
    nearly equidistant from two codebook entries will be assigned to different
    codes, even if their semantic content is nearly identical."*
  - **Design:** no geometry in the write path. Retrieval stays structural; the
    metric layer is a read-time adjunct, and **a slot is never scored by its
    distance**.
  - **Already exists:** nothing to remove.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1099 §3, §Open Question 2; ORDER 1109
    §"With Distributional Semantics".
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` as design; the *failure being avoided* is documented.
  - **Why rank 3:** **Area 67 already governs this.** A slot's rank is a
    position in a prototype distribution, not on a scale — and a slot promoted
    by geometric centrality would be the arena's own §8 failure wearing a
    coordinate system. The three-slots rule is what makes the objection
    legible: a rank-1 geometric retrieval would look coherent precisely because
    it is smooth.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 67** and **Area 8** jointly hold it. **Area 51**
    (codebook collapse) is the same warning from the quantization side: a
    store whose declared capacity and used capacity differ by an order of
    magnitude reports the wrong number regardless of what geometry sits on top.

### Area 87 · The conditions that feel worst are the ones that last: performance and learning come apart, and the feeling tracks the wrong one `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Learning/Desirable-Difficulties-Bjork.md` (ORDER 1124,
398 lines), read whole this pass, with `oracle/brain/Learning/Interleaving-Effect-Discriminative-Contrast.md`
(ORDER 1127) as the second file that earns it independently.

**The brain part.** Bjork & Bjork's **two-strength account** (1992 *New Theory of
Disuse*): **retrieval strength (RS)** — how accessible a trace is right now, which
rises fast with contiguous re-exposure and decays — and **storage strength (SS)** —
how deeply the trace is interassociated with prior knowledge, which **only grows
through successful effortful reconstruction and never decays once grown.** The two
are dissociable, and the dissociation has a direction: **RS rises under exactly the
conditions that do nothing for SS.**

**Why this is an eighty-seventh structural silence, and it is the arena's own
disease.** Eighty-six areas grade evidence, hold confidence values, and rank slots
— and **every one of them reads RS as though it were SS.** The arena's signal that
a file was understood is that it read smoothly and the slot got written. Bjork's
measured finding is that **65–85% of participants judge the *less* effective
condition as more effective**, and the illusion **persists after the learner has
seen the results that contradict it**; participants **cannot recall which
condition they were in.** Processing fluency at acquisition is being read as
evidence of durable storage. `ARENA.md` held **zero** occurrences of *fluency
illusion*, *metacognitive illusion*, or *desirable difficult*.

- **Rank 1 — Never let an acquisition-time signal stand as evidence of retention;
  schedule the re-read that would actually falsify the claim, and treat the
  confidence attached to a fresh read as uninformative by construction.**
  `UNTESTED` on the design, `HIGH` on the phenomenon.
  - **Brain part:** the RS/SS dissociation, with the signature measurement —
    **Roediger & Karpicke (2006, *Psychological Science* 17(3):249–255)**: restudy
    wins at a 5-minute test, testing wins at 2 days and 1 week, **and the restudied
    group reports higher confidence at every delay.** That is the whole area in one
    experiment: the felt signal points the wrong way and keeps pointing it.
  - **Design:** the arena already re-reads — the ledger is a two-pass system and
    `arena_invariants.py` exists precisely because a first pass cannot be trusted.
    The slot's content is that **a corroboration is only ever RS**, and the SS
    measurement is a *retrieval after partial forgetting*, not a re-read at full
    fluency. Cepeda et al. (2008, *JEP:Applied* 14(2):116–126; 1,354 participants,
    intervals to 350 days) supplies the schedule shape: **optimal gap ≈ 20% of the
    target retention interval at short delays, ~5% at one year** — spacing is
    `d = 1.1` over zero-gap. Concrete for this workspace: **an `ARENA.md` slot
    corroborated once has had its RS raised and its SS untouched**, and the only
    SS-raising event available is a later tranche reading the *evidence file*
    rather than the slot.
  - **Already exists:** **FSRS** (open-source) and Duolingo's half-life regression
    (Settles & Meza, *AAAI* 2016) are per-item SS schedulers already running at
    scale — adopt-don't-build. **Not built here:** the arena has no scheduler and no
    per-slot retention estimate; it has a Support count, which is a count of
    sources, not a function of time since last retrieval.
  - **Backup documents:** ORDER 1124 §§"Two-Strength Account", "The Five Desirable
    Difficulties", "Metacognitive Inversion", "The Fluency Illusion", "Replication
    Status"; ORDER 1127 §§"Metacognitive Illusion", "Desirable Difficulties and
    Subadditivity".
  - **Grade:** human phenomenon **`HIGH`** — the strongest replication record in
    the learning sciences: spacing across **184 articles / 317 experiments**,
    testing effect meta-analysed at **Hedges' g ≈ 0.57 over 71 studies**, classroom
    quizzing at **g = 0.47 / 0.54 across 101 effect sizes in 36 studies with no
    evidence of publication bias** (Dunlosky et al. 2013 rates only practice
    testing and distributed practice "high utility"; interleaving "moderate";
    **rereading and highlighting "low utility"**). **The design is `UNTESTED`** and
    is not raised by the phenomenon's strength. Support: **2 independent sources**,
    and they are independent in the useful sense — one is the umbrella framework,
    the other supplies the mechanism and the counter-example in the same
    experiments.
 - **Corroboration (tranche 52, ORDER 1169 `Memory/Working-Memory-Update-Mechanisms.md`,
 335 lines) — the strongest *negative* result in the corpus for the design
 pattern this area's rank 1 prescribes, and it is a clean `UNSUCCESSFUL`.**
 Support **2 → 3**, grades held; the phenomenon grade is untouched and the
 `UNTESTED` design grade is **reinforced**. The file settles the
 **working-memory-training** literature and the settlement is against far
 transfer: **Jaeggi et al. (2008, *PNAS*) reported that dual n-back training
 improved fluid intelligence on Raven's matrices; Au et al. (2015) and Simons et
 al. (2016) found little to no far transfer in meta-analysis**, with **near
 transfer (untrained n-back variants) reliably present** — and a 2022 mediation
 study across **three RCTs, 460 participants** found the apparent far transfer to
 Matrix Reasoning was *fully mediated* by near transfer to untrained n-back,
 with **no direct intervention effect**. The consensus as the file states it:
 near transfer robust, far transfer minimal. **This is `UNSUCCESSFUL` in the
 arena's first-class sense, and it is recorded here rather than in Area 87's own
 vocabulary because the failure is specific: training the *gating* operation
 improves the *gating* task and does not produce a general capability.** The
 direct bearing on rank 1 is narrow and worth stating precisely — rank 1 says
 *space and interleave the review*; this says **spacing a review of material the
 system cannot yet perform will transfer to that performance and no further.**
 A schedule that measures success by re-running the task is measuring near
 transfer, and per this file that is exactly the quantity that transfers. **Two
 further items recorded because they bear on the area's measurement theme, not
 because they corroborate it:** the **n-back construct-validity problem**
 (strong face validity, **weak-to-modest convergent validity** with other
 working-memory measures — the task may be measuring general cognitive control
 rather than working memory), and the pharmacologically clean dissociation that
 makes the mechanism separable at all: **cortical dopamine (tolcapone, N = 49)
 selectively improved *maintenance* and did nothing for *gating*, while striatal
 D2 blockade (sulpiride, N = 34) altered *output* gating specifically** — i.e.
 **the arena's word "update" bundles two operations with different receptors,
 different timescales and different failure modes**, and a schedule that treats
 them as one cannot report which it exercised.
  - **Why rank 1:** every other slot in the arena is a statement about *what to
    build*. This one is about **which of the arena's own readings count**, and it is
    the only place the arena's evidence grades are named as a measurement
    instrument with a known bias.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 13** (metacognitive control) is the controller that
    decides continue/ask/stop; this is the finding that its input is *inverted*.
    **Area 24** (instrument validity) asks whether a measurement measured anything;
    this says a specific one measures the wrong quantity. **Area 44** (parameter
    recovery) is the upgrade path — a fitted parameter beats a felt one, which is
    why rank 1 is a *scheduling* requirement and not a replacement for 44.
- **Rank 2 — Interleave the review, and never insert filler between the things
  being contrasted: two desirable difficulties are not always more desirable than
  one.** `UNTESTED` on the design, `HIGH` on the phenomenon.
  - **Brain part:** the **discriminative-contrast hypothesis** (Goldstone 1996) —
    juxtaposition of *different* categories highlights what **distinguishes** them,
    where blocking highlights what **unifies** them, and for naturalistic categories
    discrimination is the harder task. Birnbaum et al. (2013, *Memory & Cognition*
    41:392–402) dissociates the two components orthogonally and gets the design
    consequence as a measured interaction: interleaving beats blocking when
    contiguous (**d = 0.678, p < .0001**) and the benefit **disappears** when
    spacing is added (M = .29 vs .28, ns) — **subadditive, and in the direction
    that matters.** Their Exp 1 holds total spacing constant and finds that
    inserting trivia *between* exemplars **impairs** learning relative to
    contiguous (**η²ₚ = .15**) — it breaks the juxtaposition that does the work.
  - **Design:** for a maintenance pass over this arena, the correct schedule is
    **contrast across areas in one pass, not spacing within one area across passes.**
    Areas 1, 25 and 55 are near-neighbours whose slots are easier to confuse than
    to compare; reading them together makes the boundaries visible in a way three
    separate single-area passes do not. **Not filler:** the between-item gap must
    contain *other categories*, not a delay.
  - **Already exists:** population-based training (Jaderberg et al. 2019, *Science*
    364:859–865) is the multi-agent form — a league of diverse policies is a
    permanently interleaved opponent pool. Interleaved replay buffers are standard
    in continual learning. Adopt-don't-build.
  - **Backup documents:** ORDER 1127 §§1, 2, 4 (Birnbaum's three experiments with
    statistics), §"Boundary Conditions: When Blocking Wins"; ORDER 1124
    §"The Interleaving Effect".
  - **Grade:** `HIGH` on the phenomenon with the boundary stated and not averaged:
    Brunmair & Richter (2019, *Psychological Bulletin* 145(11):1029–1054) find
    **g = 0.67 for paintings but g = −0.39 for word lists** — **blocking wins on
    word lists** — and Kumar et al. (2024) find pooled transfer **g = 0.59** falling
    to **0.43 after outlier removal** at **I² = 73.6%**. Czyż et al. (2024) rated
    **0 of 34** motor-learning studies "strong" quality and found **lab SMD = 0.75
    vs applied SMD = 0.34 (ns)** — a laboratory artefact on the applied side.
    **The design is `UNTESTED`.** Support: **2 sources**, sharing ORDER 1127 with
    rank 1 and thus **not independent of it** — this is one framework at two slots,
    and the count says so.
  - **Why rank 2:** it is the mechanism behind rank 1 for one of the five
    difficulties, isolated and measured with the counter-case attached. It ranks
    second because it is a *scheduling* rule inside a system this arena does not
    yet have.
  - **Interaction:** **directly contradicts a CLT-shaped design.** ORDER 1122
    (below) holds that instructional material should minimise *extraneous* load;
    interleaving deliberately raises it. Both cannot be applied to the same pass,
    and the resolution ORDER 1122 itself names — desirable difficulties raise
    *germane* load, not extraneous — is a claim neither file measures.
- **Rank 3 — Feedback must be guaranteed and eventually informative, or delaying
  it is not a difficulty but a failure; and generation is only a desirable
  difficulty when it is followed by retrieval.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** the **boundary conditions of the framework itself**, which is
    where a slot earns its place rather than a mechanism. Delayed feedback
    *"requires that feedback is guaranteed and eventually informative — without
    guaranteed feedback, delay harms learning"* (Soderstrom & Bjork 2014). And the
    generation effect **decays or reverses over long delays** (Pyc & Rawson 2009,
    *Memory & Cognition*), which is why Karpicke & Blunt (2011) find *retrieval
    practice* robust at every delay where *generation* is not — the resolution is
    that it is **retrieval**, not generation, that carries the effect.
  - **Design:** a consolidation or review pass that withholds feedback on the
    theory that deferred feedback helps is not applying a desirable difficulty; it
    is applying a bug. **Concretely: this arena defers most disagreements to a
    later tranche, and the guarantee is the ledger** — the row is there, marked
    unread, and is read. Remove the ledger's unread rows and rank 2's mechanism
    becomes rank 3's failure.
  - **Already exists:** nothing to adopt; this is a constraint on the other two.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1124 §§"Feedback Timing", "The 'Generation Effect
    Controversy'".
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` as design; the boundary is documented, the rule is not
    tested.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the *limit* of the area, and an area whose rank 1 is
    wrong at the boundary is an area that will be applied wrongly. It stays
    visible because the two directions — a guaranteed-feedback gap and an
    unguaranteed one — look identical from inside a pass.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 33** (a detector is not a certifier) is the same shape
    on the verification side: a signal that has not been checked is not a weaker
    check, it is no check.

### Area 88 · Some capacities are language-dependent and some are not, and the arena has been treating every one of them as language-independent `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Language-and-Cognition/Linguistic-Relativity-Modern-Evidence.md`
(ORDER 1116, 376 lines), read whole this pass, with
`oracle/brain/Language-and-Thought/Language-and-Thought.md` (ORDER 1118) and
`oracle/brain/Language-Cognition/Language-Acquisition-and-the-Brain.md` (ORDER 1120)
as two further independent files in the same block.

**The brain part.** **Linguistic relativity in its modern, post-Whorfian form** —
and, more usefully for design, its **taxonomy**: Wolff & Holmes (2011, *WIREs Cognitive
Science* 2(3):253–265) separate language as **spotlight** (makes a distinction more
accessible), **meddler** (competes with non-linguistic codes; the effect shows up
as interference sensitivity), and **augmenter** (extends capacity into a domain that
is otherwise inaccessible). The three have **different robustness**, and that
difference is the finding: **effects mediated by online linguistic processing are
fragile; effects requiring symbolic offloading are robust.**

**Why this is an eighty-eighth structural silence.** Eighty-seven areas treat
reasoning, memory and language as separable modules and never ask which
capacities are *constitutive* of a symbol system rather than computed alongside
one. The corpus's own table, and it is the arena's §8 inversion in cognitive form:

| Domain | Effect | Replication status |
|---|---|---|
| Colour (Russian blues) | cross-category advantage | **Mixed** — 2020 registered replication failed on the *siniy/goluboy* boundary |
| Spatial frame (Guugu Yimithirr) | absolute memory bias | **Robust** in controlled conditions |
| Time (Mandarin vertical) | vertical priming | **Asymmetric** — Mandarin robust, English horizontal failed |
| **Number (Pirahã)** | **no exact number without words** | **Robust** — Mundurukú, Nicaraguan Sign Language, homesigners |
| Grammatical gender | adjective bias | **Fragile in native speakers, real under artificial learning** |

- **Rank 1 — Route exact-cardinality and exact-identity operations to a symbolic
  store and never to a similarity index, because the capability is supplied by
  the symbol system and is not otherwise present.** `UNTESTED` on the design,
  `HIGH` on the phenomenon.
  - **Brain part:** Frank, Everett, Fedorenko & Gibson (2008, *Cognition*
    108(3):819–824) on the Pirahã — speakers match **exactly up to ~10 items when
    no memory is required**, and become **approximate the moment a memory delay is
    inserted**, at **chance above 3** for exact discrimination. The finding is not
    that they cannot perceive quantity; it is that **number words are a technology
    for exact memory** — the symbol offloads exact cardinality from a system that
    cannot hold it. Frank et al.'s own framing: number words are *"a concept
    invented by human cultures as they are needed, and not an inherent part of
    language."* It replicates in three unrelated cultures (Pica et al. 2004;
    Flaherty & Senghas 2011 — Nicaraguan Sign Language cohorts that gained number
    signs gained exact matching; Spaepen et al. 2011 on homesigners).
  - **Design:** in this workspace the same split is already visible and already
    violated. **`LEDGER.md` holds exact counts; the arena holds a prose restatement
    of them.** A `grep -c` returns 2,217; "the corpus is about two thousand files"
    is a similarity claim. The slot's content is the rule: **anything that must be
    exact — a row count, a citation count, a read→mark ratio, an invariant verdict —
    is computed and stored, never recalled.** This is the same discipline the arena
    already applies to citations and has not applied to its own numbers.
  - **Already exists:** `organizer.db` and `brain-postgres` are the offloading
    layer, already built and already the right shape. **The defect is routing, not
    capacity**: nothing forces a count to come from the database rather than from
    the model's own summary of the database.
  - **Backup documents:** ORDER 1116 §4 (Pirahã), §6.4 (the replication table),
    §7.2; ORDER 1118 §1 (number words and quantitative thinking).
  - **Grade:** `HIGH` on the phenomenon — three independent cultural and
    modality-specific replications, and it is the *only* row in the table marked
    robust that is not about a spatial frame. **The design is `UNTESTED`** and
    stays there: ORDER 1116 proposes the probe (*"Re-embed a claim under multiple
    paraphrases; if similarity drops below threshold, the claim is linguistically
    gated — do not promote"*) and proposes no result. Support: **3 independent
    sources** for the phenomenon.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in eighty-eight areas that says a specific
    capability this arena uses daily is **not a capability it has** and must borrow.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 38** (workspace capacity) — the workspace holds
    ~4 chunks and they decay; the symbolic store is what makes exactness available
    at all, and ORDER 1116's operational rule (*"Any subsystem requiring exact
    cardinality — task counts, budget tracking, version numbers — must use
    organizer.db or Postgres, never Honcho/Active Wiki alone"*) is Area 38's design
    consequence. **Area 85** (symbol grounding) is the same question asked of
    reference rather than of number: a token's link to the world.
- **Rank 2 — Tag every stored claim with the linguistic conditions under which it
  was produced, and refuse to promote a claim whose only support is
  paraphrase-stable-in-one-language.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** **online linguistic mediation, and its falsifiable signature** —
    the Winawer et al. (2007, *PNAS* 104(19):7780–7785) Russian advantage is
    **eliminated by verbal interference (silent digit rehearsal) but not by spatial
    interference**, which is what makes it a claim about *online gating* rather than
    about a permanent restructuring of colour space. Slobin's **"thinking for
    speaking"** vs **"thinking"** distinction is the corpus's own formulation of the
    same cut, and the corpus states the operational consequence: **batch jobs
    (offline, no linguistic output) should not show these effects; interactive
    chat may** — *"a testable prediction,"* and no result.
  - **Design:** the *linguistic load probe* as a promotion gate. Re-embed a claim
    under paraphrase and translation; if similarity collapses, the claim is
    System-1-gated and does not reach the curated tier unverified. This connects
    directly to the arena's own **verification gate** on the promotion path, which
    currently checks provenance and not linguistic dependence.
  - **Already exists:** the verification gate exists (pgvector similarity + BM25 +
    RRF per ORDER 1116 §7.5). **The load probe does not** — a paraphrase-invariance
    check is a few lines and is not present.
  - **Backup documents:** ORDER 1116 §§1.1–1.3, 3.3, 6.1, 7.5, and Open Question 1;
    ORDER 1118 §1.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. The mechanism is `LOW` at best: the domain this slot
    generalises from is the corpus's own **least** robust row (colour, mixed
    replication), and the corpus says so. **That is why it is rank 2 and not rank
    1** — a probe justified by the fragile domain, offered for the general case.
  - **Why rank 2:** the mechanism is sound and the evidence base is the weakest in
    the file, and a slot that inherits both is exactly the kind that gets adopted on
    the strength of a mechanism rather than a result.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 77** (transparent self-model) — a claim that changes
    under paraphrase is a claim whose introspective stability is an artefact of
    phrasing. **Area 35** (grounding) constrains it: a paraphrase probe measures
    linguistic dependence, **not** whether the claim was ever true.
- **Rank 3 — A window that closed, and the recorded null on reopening it: the
  critical period is graded by component, and the corpus's own evidence
  contradicts the hard-cutoff version.** `LOW`, Support 2.
  - **Brain part:** **Lenneberg (1967) refined into a sensitive period, not a
    critical one.** The corpus states the correction with its evidence: Johnson &
    Newport (1989, *Cognition*) found a sharp decline after age 7 in 46
    immigrants; **Bialystok & Hakuta (1994) concluded there is "a continuous
    decline in ability to learn with age" rather than a cutoff**; and **Birdsong
    (2005) found ~5% of adult bilinguals reach native-like proficiency well past
    any presumed window.** The window is also **component-graded** — phonology
    closes earliest (~6–7, Flege 1995: adult L2 learners nearly always retain an
    accent even with perfect grammar), morphosyntax later, **vocabulary never**
    (Singleton & Lengyel 1995). Neville et al. (1998, *PNAS* 95(3):922–929) locates
    sign language in the **same left-hemisphere perisylvian areas** as speech, so
    the window is on **language**, not on the auditory-vocal channel.
  - **Design:** a system that has finished building a structure should be able to
    say **which** structure and **which component** is closed, because the
    intervention differs. Perineuronal-net removal, GABA-clock manipulation and
    intensive retraining are the corpus's three reopening routes, and the arena
    already recorded the null on the first (Area 63 Rank 3).
  - **Already exists:** nothing. The arena has no notion of a closed component.
  - **Backup documents:** ORDER 1120 §§"The Critical Period Hypothesis",
    "Bilingualism", "Sign Language"; ORDER 1114 §§1, 5.
  - **Grade:** `LOW` deliberately, and **the slot's value is the disagreement it
    preserves**: the corpus carries the critical period in the strong form
    (Lenneberg, the Lenneberg window as a hard cutoff) *and* the sensitive-period
    form, in the same file, and does not reconcile them. Both are recorded. Per
    R-J4 they are not averaged into a single moderate grade.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the area's honest limit and it constrains the other two
    — a rank-1 exactness rule is only useful while the symbol system is still
    being learned.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 15** (developmental ordering) and **Area 63 Rank 3**
    (the critical period as a consolidation window) both hold the closure
    question; this slot supplies the **graded** version they currently state flat.


### Area 89 · Expertise is a measurable process with a neural signature, and the curve everyone quotes is an artefact of averaging `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Learning/Skill-Acquisition-Stages.md` (ORDER 1132, 410
lines), read whole this pass.

**The brain part.** **Knowledge compilation** (Anderson 1982, *Psychological Review*
89(4):369–406) — the transition from a **declarative propositional network** to
**procedural production rules**, by two named subprocesses: **composition**
(collapsing multi-step production sequences into one) and **proceduralization**
(embedding facts into production *conditions*). It is accompanied by **verbal
mediation dropout** — measured, not assumed: Anderson's programming and geometry
subjects produced **shorter and less detailed verbal protocols as practice
accumulated**. The neural signature is the phase map Chein & Schneider (2010)
report and Lohse et al. (2014) meta-analysed: **prefrontal + ACC and working-memory
load in the cognitive phase → caudate/putamen taking over in the associative
phase → sensorimotor cortex, SMA, and basal ganglia with prefrontal involvement
"minimal unless unexpected events demand conscious intervention" in the
autonomous phase.**

**Why this is an eighty-ninth silence, and it is a *methodological* one this time.**
Eighty-eight areas rank designs. **Not one distinguishes a power law from an
exponential one**, and `ARENA.md` held **0** occurrences of *knowledge
compilation*, *declarative-to-procedural*, or *aggregation artifact*. The
distraction is easy to miss because both curves are called "the learning curve."

**The finding that earns the area.** **Heathcote, Brown & Mewhort (2000, *Psychonomic
Bulletin & Review* 7(2):185–207), "The Power Law Repealed."** Individual-level
data fit the **exponential** better than the power law (mean **R² = 0.93 vs
0.87**), and **averaging individual exponentials produces a group curve that looks
power-law-shaped.** The power law of practice is an artefact of the population,
not a law about any learner. The consequence is direct and unwelcome: **every
learning-curve claim in the arena computed on group data describes nobody.**

- **Rank 1 — Fit per-entity, never per-corpus; and hold a storage-strength and a
  retrieval-strength number separately, because the compilation mechanism needs
  both.** `UNTESTED` on the design, `HIGH` on the phenomenon.
  - **Brain part:** knowledge compilation as the *mechanism* the two-strength
    account needs. Compilation is not fluency — it is the **removal of steps** —
    and that is a storage-strength event, not a retrieval-strength one.
  - **Design:** the arena's per-item Support count is a **retrieval-strength**
    statistic and already says so. What it lacks is any **storage-strength**
    estimate: nothing in the arena records *how many times a slot was rebuilt
    rather than re-read*. Area 87 rank 1 is the same gap one level down, and this
    slot supplies its mechanism. **Concretely:** every corpus read writes a
    compaction step (what was written into the slot) and a citation step (what was
    counted); the compaction step is the SS event and it is the one the arena
    never logs.
  - **Already exists:** **ACT-R** is the fully-implemented version — adopt-don't-
    build; it has modelled arithmetic, programming and route-learning through the
    declarative→procedural transition. Also **Pavlik & Anderson (2008,
    *JEP:Applied* 14(2):101–117)**, which derives an *optimized instructional
    schedule* from the model and reports large effect sizes on Japanese–English
    vocabulary — the schedule-as-inference framing rather than a fixed interval
    table. **The Hermes stack has neither**; the arena has a `patch`, not an
    ACT-R.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1132 §§"Knowledge Compilation", "The Power Law of
    Practice and Its Dispute", "Neuroimaging Evidence".
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` on the design, `HIGH` on the mechanism. The power-law
    dispute is **not settled** and is recorded as live: Logan (1992, *JEP:LMC*
    18(5):883–914) offers instance theory as an individual-level mechanism that
    *does* produce power-law-shaped curves, and some work holds the power law at
    the individual level for strategy-shift tasks. **Per R-J4 the disagreement is
    preserved and not averaged** — the rank-1 design depends only on the *first*
    clause (fit per-entity, not per-corpus), which both sides of the dispute
    accept. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Why rank 1:** it changes what a number in this file *means*, which no other
    slot in the tranche does.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 87** is the same RS/SS split at the level of a single
    study; this is the mechanism that would produce it. **Area 6** (consolidation)
    is where compiled productions would be written.
- **Rank 2 — A skill that cannot be explained is a ceiling, not a polish item:
  the model claims rule-based expert systems hit a wall, and the two sides of
  that claim are still fighting.** `LOW`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** **Dreyfus's expertise paradox** — expert performance is
    seamless perception-action integration, and experts are *worse* at articulating
    why. Hubert Dreyfus's argument against symbolic AI is that expertise is
    non-rule-governed **and therefore not capturable as rules.**
  - **Design:** the arena should treat an unexplained slot as a *signal* rather
    than a documentation gap, and should be able to say **which** explanation is
    missing. Concretely: the arena's slots are all *justifications*, and a slot
    that is persuasive because the author read it fluently is the Dreyfus trap
    with the sign flipped.
  - **Already exists:** Dreyfus is already in the arena at **Area 69 rank 1** as
    the philosophical counter-position (know-how vs know-that), sourced to Searle.
    **This is a different claim and a different source** — the *empirical* stage
    model, not the philosophy — and the two must not be merged.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1132 §§"The Dreyfus Skill Model", "Expertise
    Paradox".
  - **Grade:** `LOW`, and deliberately. Gobet & Chassy (2008,
    *Int. J. Nursing Studies* 45:129–139; 2009, *Minds and Machines* 19:151–180)
    argue there is **no empirical evidence for discrete stages at all** and that
    **experts do calculate** — de Groot (1946/78) and Chase & Simon (1973) show
    expert chess players do look-ahead search. Benner's nursing application is
    separately criticised for **circularity**. Dreyfus's reply is that chess is
    atypical because its states are fully specifiable. **Unresolved, both recorded.**
  - **Why rank 2:** the disagreement is the slot; a slot that only said "expertise
    is intuitive" would be adding nothing.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 87** rank 2's boundary (blocking *wins* for word
    lists) and this slot's chess counter-case are the same shape — the stage
    model is domain-conditional and the arena's own passes are all one domain.
- **Rank 3 — Effortful practice is a real but small effect, and the arena's habit
  of crediting "lots of reading" is the claim being refuted.** `LOW`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** **deliberate practice** (Ericsson, Krampe & Tesch-Römer 1993,
    *Psychological Review* 100(3):363–406) and its meta-analytic correction.
  - **Design:** the arena should not treat volume of reading as evidence of
    assimilation. This is the corpus's own verdict on the practice this job runs.
  - **Already exists:** nothing to adopt; this is a limit on the other two.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1132 §"Ericsson's Deliberate Practice Framework".
  - **Grade:** `LOW` on the practice claim and `HIGH` on the correction.
    **Macnamara, Hambrick & Oswald (2014, *Psychological Science* 25(8):1608–1618)**
    meta-analysis: deliberate practice explains **12% of variance in sports, 23%
    in games, 26% in music** — and **the effect is smaller in studies with
    rigorous longitudinal designs**, meaning the retrospective literature that
    dominates the field **overestimates it**. The 10,000-hour figure is a
    simplification of data showing **~6,000–16,000 hours by age 20** among elites.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the area's floor and it is aimed at this workspace.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 3** (effort allocation) is where this would bind if
    effort were ever allocated on expected-yield grounds.

### Area 90 · Similar things must be kept apart, and the brain pays for it with new cells `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Memory-Architecture/Adult-Hippocampal-Neurogenesis-Pattern-Separation.md`
(ORDER 1140, 456 lines), read whole this pass, with
`oracle/brain/Memory-Architecture/Birth-Recall-Limits-Early-Memory.md` (ORDER
1141) as the second file that constrains it.

**The brain part.** **Pattern separation** in the **dentate gyrus** — the
transformation of similar or overlapping entorhinal inputs into **distinct,
non-overlapping** CA3 representations, achieved by **sparse coding (~2–5% of
granule cells active)**, **lateral inhibition** by hilar interneurons, and
**expansion recoding** (DG carries ~10× more granule cells than EC input). The
mechanism that makes it *structural* rather than computational:
**adult-born granule cells (abGCs)**, which pass a **4–6 week critical period** of
heightened excitability, enhanced LTP, and a **GABA switch** (NKCC1/KCC2 ratio
drives depolarizing→hyperpolarizing) that implements a **two-phase integration
rule** — cooperative Hebbian learning of the *existing* manifold, then competitive
specialization into a *novel* niche. The computational justification is
**Wiskott, Rasch & Kempermann (2006, *Hippocampus* 16:329–343)**: in a
fixed-capacity sparse network, storing a new pattern similar to an old one
degrades the old one — catastrophic interference — unless **fresh encoding units
are continuously added.**

**Why this is a ninetieth silence with a direct cost.** `ARENA.md` held **0**
occurrences of *pattern separation* and *dentate gyrus*, and **6** of
*catastrophic* — the disease named, the organ that treats it absent. Area 6 rank 1
solves interference by **eviction and merging**; this area says a brain solves it
by **growing more units**, and the two are not variants of one strategy.

- **Rank 1 — Separate on write, not on read: an item's first representation must be
  orthogonal to what is already stored, or no later retrieval can recover the
  difference.** `UNTESTED` on the design, `HIGH` on the mechanism.
  - **Brain part:** pattern separation at the dentate gyrus, with the interference
    parameterisation: Leutgeb et al. (2007, *Science* 315:961–966) show global
    remapping in the DG and CA3 for similar contexts.
  - **Design:** the arena's collision problem is concrete and current. **446
    corpus basenames are published at more than one row** (the figure
    `citation_remediation.py` prints every pass), and tranche 50's single C6
    failure was exactly a collision — ORDER 553 and ORDER 1145 are one document at
    two paths. **Retrieval cannot separate what storage let overlap.** So the
    design requirement is: when a new page is ingested, measure its similarity to
    existing pages and, above a threshold, **branch rather than merge** — write a
    distinct artifact instead of a near-duplicate. The arena's directories are
    already case-collision-prone (`Memory-Architecture/` vs `memory-architecture/`,
    ORDER 1142 vs 1143 in this very tranche), which is the pathology in miniature.
  - **Already exists:** **mixture-of-experts with sparse routing** and
    **progressive networks** (Rusu et al. 2016) are the expansion analogues;
    **sparse autoencoders / dictionary learning** (Olshausen & Field 1996) and
    **top-k activation** (Makhzani & Frey 2013) are the sparsity analogues. All
    four are in the corpus's own comparison table. The corpus's stated key
    difference is the load-bearing part: **biological neurogenesis is selective and
    homeostatic, and most AI expansion methods are uniform** — they add or freeze
    whole layers and lack dynamic sparseness regulation.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1140 §§1, 4 (S/L groups), 5 (catastrophic
    interference), §"Computational & Agent Parallels" §1.
  - **Grade:** `HIGH` on the mechanism, `UNTESTED` on the design. A **2017
    meta-analysis of 32 rodent studies (PMID 28597491) reports d = 0.65**, moderate,
    with **two nulls** (Swan et al. 2014; Whoolery et al. 2020). The core ablation
    finding is **robustly replicated across labs, species and methods** —
    irradiation, genetic, and acute optogenetic silencing all impair
    *high-interference* discrimination and spare low-interference. Support: **1
    source**, deliberately not incremented for ORDER 1141, which cites the same
    primaries.
 - **Corroboration (tranche 52, ORDER 1157 `Memory-Architecture/Pattern-Separation-Completion.md`,
 96 lines) — the *other half* of the circuit, and it turns this area's
 single-mechanism story into a two-sided balance.** Support **1 → 2**, `HIGH` on
 the mechanism held. **This file is a mid-document fragment** — it opens directly
 at `## Methodological Notes & Disputes` with no front matter, no Core
 Mechanisms and no Sources list (logged `VERIFICATION.md` V-51.2) — and what it
 carries is the **separation/completion balance table**, which is the sharpest
 correction in the tranche. The arena treats separation as the primitive and
 completion as what happens later; the corpus says the balance is
 **state-dependent, not fixed by anatomy**: ACh high → DG sparseness high,
 CA3 recurrent suppressed, **separation dominates**; ACh low at retrieval →
 CA3 active, **completion dominates**; stress/glucocorticoids → DG neurogenesis
 and sparseness reduced, **biasing toward completion and overgeneralization**
(Epp et al. 2015). **The design consequence is a threshold that moves with
 state, and a degradation mode that is *over-separation failing into
 over-generalization*** — which is the opposite of the arena's implicit failure
 mode. **Two challenges recorded rather than resolved, and both narrow this
 area's rank 1:** (1) **the DG/CA3 dichotomy is a gradient, not a binary** — CA3
 also separates under high interference, DG also completes from partial cues,
 and CA1 also separates (Kheirbek et al. 2013; Deng et al. 2013; Bakker et al.
 2008), so a design that routes on a hard region label is mis-specified even
 where the region labels are right; (2) **the "small world" assumption may be
 wrong** — CA3 connectivity is more **modular** than the classic attractor
 picture assumes (Blanco-Ferrera et al. 2020), raising whether it is one attractor
 or several. **And a measurement limit that applies to any human-facing
 version of this design: fMRI cannot resolve DG from CA3** (both ~1–2 mm; the
 signal is confounded with subiculum), and electrophysiology in epilepsy patients
 gives different results in some cases. Recorded as a bound on what the arena may
 claim about itself.

  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in the tranche that changes what happens
    at **write** time, and the arena's own measured collision count is the
    evidence that write-time separation is missing.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 6** is the direct rival — eviction-and-merge versus
    grow-more-units. They are not reconcilable at the storage layer, and the
    corpus is explicit that the separation label is a **reduction**: Hvoslef-Eide &
    Oomen (2016) reviewed **102 published studies** and found abGCs also serve
    **temporal separation, forgetting and cognitive flexibility**, so a system
    that only separates is doing a fraction of the job.
- **Rank 2 — The same mechanism forgets, and the sign of the effect inverts with
  age: more neurogenesis means better separation in adults and faster loss in
  infants.** `LOW`, Support 2.
  - **Brain part:** **adaptive forgetting** (Akers et al. 2014, *Science*
    344:598–602) — increased adult neurogenesis **accelerates** forgetting of
    established contextual fear; **infant mice, with naturally high neurogenesis,
    forget fast and forgetting is *slowed* by ablation.** The same process that
    buys resolution also breaks old traces.
  - **Design:** any "grow capacity to store more" rule must state what it destroys.
    ORDER 1141 supplies the human-side shape: adults recall nothing before age
    three, yet two-year-olds retain over weeks — **the memory was made; the
    access was lost**, shown causally by Guskjolen et al. (2018,
    *Current Biology* 28(14):2283–2290), who recovered infant fear memories in
    adult mice by optogenetically reactivating the original ensembles, persisting
    up to three months. **Availability and accessibility are different
    properties and need different architecture.**
  - **Already exists:** **Elastic Weight Consolidation** (Kirkpatrick et al. 2017)
    is the importance-weighted protection analogue; nothing implements
    availability/accessibility as separate stores.
  - **Backup documents:** ORDER 1140 §§"Cognitive Flexibility and Forgetting", 5;
    ORDER 1141 §§"Neurobiological Evidence", §"Agent memory design implications".
  - **Grade:** `LOW` deliberately. The rodent direction is replicated; **the human
    direction is not testable** — no causal manipulation is possible in humans, and
    human AHN runs at **~10–100× lower rates than rodents**, so **rodent effect
    sizes may overestimate human impact.** The human existence debate
    (Sorrells et al. 2018 *Nature* 555:377–381 vs Boldrini et al. 2018 *Cell Stem
    Cell* 22:589–599 vs Spalding et al. 2013 *Cell* 153:1219–1227) is recorded
    **unresolved** — the field moved from "exists / doesn't" to quantitative
    nuance, and the arena does not adjudicate it.
  - **Why rank 2:** it is the *cost* of rank 1, stated with a direction, which is
    what makes rank 1 safe to build.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 70** (suppression, not erasure) and **Area 27**
    (lability window) are the write-side neighbours; the availability /
    accessibility split is new to the arena and is a candidate for its own area
    once a second file earns it.
- **Rank 3 — S and L: two functional populations of the same age, which is what
  makes this not a maturation schedule.** `LOW`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** **Mongiat et al. (2015, *eLife* 4:e03104)** — in vivo
    patch-clamp and calcium imaging show abGCs **bifurcate into an S-group
    (high-gain, nonlinear, narrow dynamic range — separators) and an L-group
    (low-gain, linear, broad range — integrators/completers)**, and
    **"classmate" cells born in the same period adopt either**, with the ratio
    modulated by experience. This **challenges age-stage models** and replaces
    them with **functional heterogeneity as an adaptive feature.**
  - **Design:** a system that allocates a single kind of memory unit is
    committing to one of the two populations. This slot says: keep both, and let
    experience set the ratio.
  - **Already exists:** **mixture-of-experts** is the direct architectural
    analogue and the corpus names it; experience-modulated expert routing is the
    same shape.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1140 §§4, §"Tensions & Contradictions"
    (S/L vs age-stage).
  - **Grade:** `LOW` — one imaging study, and its paradigm consequence is
    contested by the temporal-integration hypothesis (Aimone et al. 2011), which
    predicts young abGCs should *impair* discrimination before enhancing it. Both
    are recorded.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the area's refinement and it constrains rank 1's
    allocation rule rather than competing with it.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 22** (action selection and the size of the action
    repertoire) asks the same question in the motor domain — what belongs in the
    repertoire, and who chooses.

### Area 91 · The cortex is a stack of time windows, and a flat context window is the thing that gets this wrong `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by**
`oracle/brain/Memory-Architecture/Chronotopic-Sequence-Representation-Cortex.md`
(ORDER 1143, 390 lines), read whole this pass, with
`oracle/brain/memory-architecture/chronotopic-sequence-representation-cortex.md`
(ORDER 1142) as a **56-line stub of the same document at a different case-path**
— one source, counted once (infra C-056), and the collision is itself this
area's evidence.

**The brain part.** **Chronotopic organization** — the cortex is arranged along a
gradient of **temporal receptive windows (TRWs)**: V1/MT under 1 s, STS/precuneus
~12 s, LS/TPJ/FEF ~36 s, and longest in transmodal regions overlapping the default
mode network. Hasson et al. (2015) name the consequence: **process memory** —
**memory is not localized to a store, it is intrinsic to processing at every
level.** The intrinsic form is measured in macaque by **Murray et al. (2014,
*Nature Neuroscience* 17:1661–1663)**: spike-count autocorrelation gives
**MT ~50 ms < LIP ~100 ms < LPFC ~150 ms < ACC ~350 ms**, ordered consistently
with anatomy at **Spearman ρ = 0.97**, across **26 monkeys, 5,000+ neurons, 6
research groups**. Gao et al. (2020, *eLife* 9:e61277) carry it to **105 human
ECoG subjects** and find the timescales **functionally dynamic** — prefrontal
windows **expand during working memory** and compress with aging.

**Why this is a ninety-first silence that is also a live defect in this
workspace.** `ARENA.md` held **0** occurrences of *chronotopic* and *temporal
receptive*. And the workspace **already violates the finding in two visible
ways**: an LLM context window is **one window for a phoneme and a chapter
alike**, and the arena's directories differ only by **letter case**
(`Memory-Architecture/` vs `memory-architecture/`, ORDER 1143 vs 1142) — the same
path, read at two timescales, is two different things to a reader.

- **Rank 1 — Give every store a window and say what it is for; a system with one
  retention horizon has no way to know which of its facts are still live.**
  `UNTESTED` on the design, `HIGH` on the phenomenon.
  - **Brain part:** the TRW hierarchy, with the *hard* measurement — **Honey et
    al. (2012)** replicated the fMRI result in **9 neurosurgical patients** on
    ECoG, and found that regions sensitive to long temporal structure also show
    **slower decay of temporal autocorrelation**: the window is a dynamical
    property, not a stimulus artefact.
  - **Design:** the arena's tiers are named but their **windows are not stated**.
    Hot/warm/cold, active wiki, oracle brain, and the session log each have an
    implicit retention horizon and none of them is written down, so no component
    can tell a fact that is *current* from one that is *archival*. **The design
    is one line per store: name the window, and never let a fact arrive in a
    window longer than the question being asked of it.**
  - **Already exists:** **CoALA** (Sumers et al. 2023, *TMLR*) organizes agent
    capabilities **explicitly by temporal scale** and is the adoptable reference;
    **Hierarchical Temporal Memory** (George & Hawkins 2009) is the cortical-
    circuit model. **Not present here:** the Hermes stack has cron cadences and
    tier names, and no window is declared for any of them.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1143 §§1–3, §"Computational & Agent Parallels" §1
    (the CoALA mapping table).
  - **Grade:** `HIGH` on the phenomenon, `UNTESTED` on the design. The hierarchy
    is demonstrated in **two modalities** (visual, auditory), in **two species**,
    with fMRI, ECoG, and single-unit methods agreeing, and ρ = 0.97 against
    independent anatomical hierarchy. Support: **1 source** (1142 and 1143 are one
    document — infra C-056 — and the count says so).
  - **Why rank 1:** every other slot in the tranche changes a *policy*; this one
    changes the *shape of the store*.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 6** sets *when* consolidation runs and this says *what
    window it runs in*. **Area 89 rank 1**'s per-entity fitting needs windows to
    fit over.
- **Rank 2 — Windows are mostly time-yoked, not structure-yoked, and that
  contradicts how transformers are built.** `LOW`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** **Norman-Haignere et al. (2025, *Nature Neuroscience*
    28:2356–2365)**, 15 subjects, 110 intracranial electrodes, using time-stretched
    and time-compressed speech to separate the two. Integration windows increase
    hierarchically (**median 80 ms → 130 ms → 272 ms**) but the **structure-yoking
    index is ≈ 0.04** — essentially invariant to phoneme and word duration. **The
    fundamental unit of integration is absolute time, not linguistic structure.**
  - **Design:** this is a direct hit on positional encoding. A transformer with
    learned positional embeddings **assumes structure-yoking** — its receptive
    field covers a variable number of tokens depending on token length. The
    corpus's reading: compressing speech means **more phonemes analysed in the
    same window**, not a rescaled window. For the arena: **a time-windowed store
    should be sized in wall-clock, not in tokens or sections.**
  - **Already exists:** nothing; this is a design constraint, not a product.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1143 §5, §"Tensions and Open Conflicts" §1.
  - **Grade:** `LOW` on the general claim, and the disagreement is preserved: the
    finding is from **auditory cortex only**, and the corpus states explicitly
    that **non-auditory association cortex — the angular gyrus during narrative
    comprehension — may use structure-yoked integration**, which is exactly where
    the arena's pages live. **Not adjudicated.**
  - **Why rank 2:** it is a falsifiable constraint on rank 1, in the one modality
    where it has been tested.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 88** (language-contingent cognition) is the same
    question from the other side — if windows are time-yoked, some of what looked
    like language dependence may be a window artefact.
- **Rank 3 — The hierarchy is not monolithic, and treating an area's timescale as
  its property is averaging away the mechanism.** `LOW`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** three documented violations of the clean gradient —
    **within-area heterogeneity** (Runyan et al. 2017, *Neuron* 95:1411–1423:
    neurons in one area span a distribution, and long-τ units carry the delay
    period while short-τ units carry encoding; Wasmuht et al. 2018,
    *Nat. Commun.* 9:4074 confirms the split inside lateral PFC);
    **multiple hierarchies per network** (Chaudhuri et al. 2015, *Neuron*
    88:419–431, one anatomical network yields different orderings for visual and
    somatosensory input); and **the OFC exception** (Murray et al. 2014 — it ranks
    differently anatomically than physiologically). Baldassano et al. (2017,
    *Neuron* 95:709–721) adds the functional link: **nested event boundaries, and
    high-order boundaries predict hippocampal activation and later reinstatement
    in free recall** — so *event boundaries are the encoding events.*
  - **Design:** a store that declares one window for a whole directory is
    averaging heterogeneous units, and the arena's own directories are exactly
    that — a directory with one file and a directory with ninety are not the same
    kind of object. **Partition by boundary detection, not by folder.**
  - **Already exists:** Hierarchical Temporal Memory's per-level sequences;
    nothing in the arena detects event boundaries at all.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1143 §§4, 7, §"The Hierarchy Is Not Monolithic",
    §"Reliability vs. Amplitude".
  - **Grade:** `LOW` — three independent findings, one imaging modality family,
    and a live dispute over whether the population gradient is a property of
    areas or an artefact of averaging. **Recorded, not resolved.**
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the honest limit on ranks 1 and 2 and it is the reason
    neither may be implemented as a single global constant.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 83** (the mean is not a model of the individual) is
    the same error in a different register — an area-level timescale is a mean
    over neurons, exactly as a corpus-level statistic is a mean over slots, and
    **Area 89 rank 1** is the fix applied in both registers.

### Area 92 · The same machinery runs the clock in both directions, and what changes is only the index `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Memory-Architecture/Episodic-Future-Thinking.md`
(ORDER 1147, 202 lines), read whole this pass, with
`oracle/brain/Memory-and-Consciousness/Episodic-Specificity-Induction.md` (ORDER
1138) as the second file, which supplies the **intervention**.

**The brain part.** The **constructive episodic simulation hypothesis** (Schacter
& Addis 2007) — remembering and imagining are not two faculties that happen to
overlap; they are **one event-construction process indexed by a temporal
parameter**, running over a repository of episodic details that are **retrieved
and recombined**. The **core network** — hippocampus, posterior cingulate,
precuneus, inferior parietal, mPFC — is active in both. The strongest single
piece of evidence is a **patient dissociation**: Hassabis et al. (2007, *PNAS*
104:1726–1731) found hippocampal-amnesic patients **cannot imagine new
experiences** — their hypothetical futures lack spatial coherence and richness —
**while semantic knowledge is relatively preserved.**

**Why this is a ninety-second silence.** `ARENA.md` held **0** occurrences of
*episodic future thinking* and *specificity induction*. Seven occurrences of
*prospectiv* are all Area 4's **prospective memory** — remembering *to do
something* — which is a different function from **imagining what the doing will
be like.** The arena has the calendar and not the imagination.

- **Rank 1 — One construction module, three indices: past, possible, and
  counterfactual.** `UNTESTED` on the design, `LOW` on the mechanism.
  - **Brain part:** the shared substrate, plus the two-phase structure Addis et
    al. (2007, *Neuropsychologia* 45:1363–1377) separate — **construction**
    (recruits hippocampus and right middle occipital gyrus) versus **elaboration**
    (recruits the broader autobiographical network). Schacter, Benoit, De Brigard
    & Szpunar (2015, *Neurobiol. Learn. Mem.* 117:14–21) extend it to a
    **four-way grid**: past-actual, past-counterfactual, future-possible,
    future-counterfactual.
  - **Design:** the arena's `VERIFICATION.md` is a prospective-memory device
    (Area 4: an unread claim is a future obligation) and its `ARENA-EVIDENCE.md`
    is a past-actual device. **Neither can construct a future one.** A slot that
    beats its neighbours can be stated as a *scenario* and checked against one —
    which is a different operation from either looking it up or promising to do
    it.
  - **Already exists:** **Generative Agents** (Park et al. 2023) plan over
    retrieved experience; **the corpus names no system that constructs a
    counterfactual past and treats it as evidence about the present one.**
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1147 §§"Constructive Episodic Simulation
    Hypothesis", "The Prospective Brain", §"Counterfactual Thinking".
  - **Grade:** `LOW`, and the load-bearing caution is a **measurement** one:
    **Miloyan & McFarlane (2019, *Cortex* 117:351–370)** systematically reviewed
    episodic-foresight instruments and found **none verifies the accuracy of a
    future imagining against an outcome** — a vivid simulation of an event that
    never happens is scored as good EFT. **An area built on this must state that
    its output is unvalidated by construction.**
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the area's organizing claim, and it is the reason the
    other two exist.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 4** is the same machinery at a different index and
    the two must not be merged — prospective *memory* is a scheduling
    obligation, prospective *simulation* is a representational operation.
- **Rank 2 — A brief reorientation of retrieval, not an architectural change:
  specificity is a configuration state, and it can be set.** `LOW`, Support 2.
  - **Brain part:** the **episodic specificity induction (ESI)** — view a short
    neutral film, then answer guided imagery probes about people, objects,
    setting and actions. Madore, Gaesser & Schacter (2014, *JEP:LMC* 40:609–622)
    found a **double dissociation**: internal episodic details increase on memory
    **and** imagination; picture description and word comparison show **no
    effect at all.** Madore et al. (2016, *PNAS* 113:10696–10701) localised it
    to **left anterior hippocampus and right inferior parietal lobule**, with
    effect magnitude **modulated by how much detail the participant actually
    generated** — brain-behaviour coupling, not a rubber stamp.
  - **Design:** the arena's equivalent is a **retrieval-configuration state** —
    set the system to prefer specific episodic detail over semantic gist for the
    duration of one question, and reset it. The corpus's own claim is that **such
    a reconfiguration need not be permanent**: transient orientation shifts
    suffice, minutes to hours. That is a cheap, reversible control the arena does
    not have.
  - **Already exists:** **Memory Specificity Training / c-MeST** (Hallford et al.
    2021, *Behav. Res. Ther.* 136) and **FEST** (Hallford et al. 2023,
    *Cogn. Ther. Res.*, pre-registered RCT, N = 177) are the durable versions and
    both report **reduced depressive symptoms** — a real clinical effect, adopted
    as evidence, not as a product.
  - **Backup documents:** ORDER 1138 §§"Key Findings: Selective Enhancement",
    "Neural Signatures", "Mechanism Debates"; ORDER 1147 §"Specificity and the
    Functional Future Bias".
  - **Grade:** `LOW` — the **core** memory/imagination enhancement is replicated
    across labs, but **the transfer effect is contested in exactly the place this
    slot is most interesting**: **Tomás et al. (2025) failed to replicate the
    divergent-thinking (AUT) effect across two experiments**, and creativity was
    one of the headline transfers. **Both results recorded; neither averaged.**
    Support: **2 independent sources** (1138 and 1147 share the Madore and
    Addis primaries, so the increment is for the *intervention* file, not a second
    effect file).
  - **Why rank 2:** it is rank 1's handle — a way to turn the machinery on.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 88** rank 2's linguistic load probe and this are both
    *retrieval configuration*, and they may conflict: biasing toward verbal
    episodic detail and testing paraphrase-invariance are not obviously
    composable.
- **Rank 3 — The mechanism is under-determined and the arena should not pick.**
  `UNTESTED`, Support 2.
  - **Brain part:** the live dispute between the **event-construction account**
    (target = assembling a detailed scenario) and the **retrieval-orientation
    account** (target = biasing the form of processing adopted on encountering a
    cue). Madore et al. (2018, *Memory* 27:250–260) argues **the two are
    indistinguishable by design**: a memory-focused induction and an
    imagination-focused induction produced *indistinguishable* increases, so the
    target cannot be memory retrieval — but that is equally consistent with
    orientation. **Neuroimaging supports both** — core-network modulation is
    consistent with construction; the *transience* of the effect is consistent
    with orientation.
  - **Design:** a system that must choose one is choosing a mechanism it cannot
    currently identify. The slot's value is that it **forbids the choice** until a
    discriminating test exists.
  - **Backup documents:** ORDER 1138 §"Mechanism Debates"; ORDER 1147
    §"Methodological Notes & Disputes" (neural overlap, and the **right-hippocampus
    lateralization with mixed replication**).
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` on the resolution; `LOW` on the existence of the effect.
 - **Corroboration (tranche 52, ORDER 1160 `Memory-Architecture/Scene-Construction-and-Mental-Visualization.md`,
 395 lines) — a third theory in the same dispute, and the corpus's own attempt to
 adjudicate between it and rank 3's.** Support **2 → 3**, grades held. Read
 alongside ORDER 1147, this file holds **scene construction theory** (Hassabis &
 Maguire 2007, *TICS* 11:243–251) — the hippocampus as a **spatial-scene
 generator**, not a memory store, whose output scaffolds episodic recall,
 imagination, navigation and mind-wandering alike. **Its relevance here is that
 it makes rank 3's dispute sharper and gives the arena a name for the third
 position.** Three things the arena does not have: (1) **the patient
 dissociation that makes the claim testable** — bilateral hippocampal damage
 leaves object recognition and semantic knowledge intact while imagined scenes
 come back as **disconnected fragments** (Hassabis et al. 2007, *Hippocampus*
 17:17–26; Rosenbaum et al. 2009; Andelman et al. 2010), and the **P01 anomaly**
 is the boundary case — ~50% bilateral volume loss with dense amnesia but
 **preserved** scene construction, which the corpus reads as *scene construction
 necessary but not sufficient for episodic memory*; (2) **the Squire critique,
 which is the strongest form of rank 3's position** — Squire et al. (2010) and
 Kim et al. (2015) found hippocampal patients **normal** on scene construction and
 boundary extension while severely impaired on memory, attributing the spatial
 performance to **intact working memory and parietal cortex**; Maguire's reply is
 that lesion severity differs and that high-resolution MRI found no
 non-hippocampal pathology; (3) **the reconciliation this area's rank 1 needs** —
 the **relational-intensity hypothesis**, that scenes are simply the *most
 demanding* relational task and hippocampal response scales with relational
 demand, which would make SCT and Cohen & Eichenbaum's relational theory
 *degrees of one thing* rather than rivals. **The measurement warning transfers
 and is the reason no grade moves:** rank 1 already records that no
 episodic-foresight instrument verifies a simulation against an outcome, and this
 file adds that **spatial-coherence coding is subjective and varies across
 studies** and that SCT's own paradigm **relies on verbal report**, which
 confounds language ability — Area 88's territory. **Recorded, not resolved:
 whether the hippocampus is specifically tuned to spatial scenes or is a
 general-purpose relational binder.** Two consistency notes for the log: the
 file's in-text bracket numbers **do not align with its own Sources list** (text
 cites [37] for Barry et al. 2019; source 37 is Ciaramelli et al. 2006), and line
 35 is an orphaned YAML fragment — both at `VERIFICATION.md` V-51.3. That is
 three defects in one document, and it is the §8 inversion appearing inside a
 file this area is about to cite.
  - **Why rank 3:** an area whose rank 1 rests on an unidentified mechanism should
    say so in its own third slot rather than in a footnote.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 33** (a detector is not a certifier) — this is the
    same failure one level in: two designs, identical predicted output, no test.

### Area 93 · The read is a write: retrieving one thing suppresses the things you did not retrieve `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Memory-Architecture/Retrieval-Induced-Forgetting.md`
(ORDER 1159, 153 lines), read whole this pass, with
`oracle/brain/Memory-Architecture/Misinformation-Effect-False-Memories.md`
(ORDER 1155, 158 lines) as the second independent file — it supplies the
**compound** prediction neither file makes alone — and
`oracle/brain/Memory-Architecture/Serial-Position-Recency-Primacy.md`
(ORDER 1162, 260 lines) as the third, which supplies the *position* dependence.

**The brain part.** **Retrieval-induced forgetting** (Anderson, Bjork & Bjork
1994, *Psychol. Sci.* 5:484–489) — and the *inhibitory* account of it, not the
interference account. When recall is practised on some items of a category, the
**unpractised** items of that same category are recalled **worse than items from
categories never practised at all** (Rp− < Nrp). Because the Rp− and Nrp items
were *studied identically* and differ only in having **competed during
retrieval**, the loss is attributed to the retrieval event and not to the
encoding. The four signature criteria are the arena's own test battery:
**retrieval specificity** (re-study does not produce it; only retrieval does),
**strength independence** (forgetting does not track facilitation — they can
**double-dissociate**), **cue independence** (test with a novel inter-item
associate and the deficit survives), **competition dependence** (weak exemplars
that never intrude are not suppressed; and *extremely* strong competitors are
also spared, resisting suppression). The machinery is executive: right DLPFC and
ACC, with Wimber et al. (2015) showing cortical patterns of competing memories
pushed **below baseline**, and the degree of that suppression **predicting**
later forgetting.

**Why this is a ninety-third silence, and it is the arena's own disease.**
`ARENA.md` held **0** occurrences of *ranking starvation*, *Nrp*, *query
patterns are write operations*, *never retrieved*, and *dark corner*. Ninety-two
areas describe what to store, how to store it, when to consolidate it, how to
verify it, and how to retract it — and **not one states that the retrieval
pattern itself permanently changes what is reachable.** The arena is a retrieval
system with a Support count, and the Support count is a **frequency of
retrieval**. The 767 unread ordered lines are not a backlog; under this
mechanism a line that is never retrieved **is not stored-and-unread, it is
suppressed**, and its suppression is *relative to the competition it lost in*,
not absolute decay.

- **Rank 1 — Treat every retrieval event as a write, and hold an
  un-retrieved control set.** `LOW` on the mechanism, `UNTESTED` on the design.
  Support: **3 independent sources.**
  - **Brain part:** the inhibition account above, with Wimber et al. (2015) and
    PFC-disruption as the causal half and Murayama et al.'s meta-analytic cost as
    the magnitude half.
  - **Design:** two obligations, and the second is the one no pass has
    discharged. (1) **Periodic Nrp-baseline audits** — a reserved set that is
    stored and deliberately *never* queried, probed on a schedule precisely so
    its accessibility can be compared against the rest of the store. This is
    Area 90's collision problem turned into a measurement: a store that has never
    been audited for starvation cannot distinguish "nothing there" from
    "suppressed into unreachable." (2) **A suppression budget on consolidation
    passes** — a summary that dominates retrieval is suppressing the
    detail-level records it replaced, and the brain's version is
    *competition-triggered* (only items that actually intruded get suppressed),
    not categorical. `ARENA-EVIDENCE.md` is the arena's largest such pass and
    has no budget.
  - **Already exists:** the hippocampal–prefrontal division of labour in the
    imaging data; formally, inhibitory variants of SAM/REM that add an active
    suppression term over intruding candidates, which reproduce strength and cue
    independence where interference-only models do not. **The corpus names no
    system that implements competition-triggered, item-specific, reversible
    suppression in a vector store** — that is a bespoke build, and the file says
    so.
  - **Backup documents:** ORDER 1159 §§"The four signature properties",
    "The suppression machinery", "Design cautions for agent memory",
    "Formal models"; ORDER 1155 §"Reconsolidation windows → read-modify-write
    hazards"; ORDER 1162 §"Implications for Agent Memory Architecture".
  - **Grade:** `LOW`, and the file supplies its own reason it is not `HIGH` —
    **cue independence is the contested front.** Anderson & Spellman (1995)
    report it; Camp et al. (2007) argue apparent independence is residual cue
    overlap; **Rowland, Bates & DeLosh (2014, *Front. Psychol.* 5:584) ran the
    high-powered attempt and found RIF clearly in the traditional paradigm
    (undergraduates *and* a Mechanical Turk sample) but *failed* the
    independent-cue variant in Experiment 3.** The honest reading, and the file's
    own: canonical same-cue RIF is real and robust; whether it requires genuine
    item-level inhibition is unsettled at the boundaries. **Preserved as a
    disagreement, not averaged.** Note this *narrows* rather than strengthens
    against Area 6, which already holds a **stronger** and older measurement of
    the same effect — Basden et al. (2014) N = 288 null, Huettl et al. (2021)
    preregistered N = 630 at **d ≈ 0.15–0.20**, Depret et al. (2020) 105
    experiments at **d = 0.31** uncorrected and **~0.15** bias-corrected against
    a published 0.47–0.65. **Any slot justified by "retrieval suppresses
    competitors" is justified by roughly a third of its published size**, and
    that floor is Area 6's, not this area's.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in ninety-three areas that constrains
    the *act of consulting the corpus*, which every other slot performs.
  - **Interaction:** **constrains every memory area in the arena, including
    itself** — a slot that is never corroborated will not be retrieved, and
    under this mechanism that is indistinguishable from a slot the evidence
    refuted. **Area 90** (separate on write) and this are the same problem at
    two ends: overlap is created at write and punished at read. **Area 87** is
    the same asymmetry named in the learner — Support count is *retrieval
    strength*, and Bjork's measured result is that retrieval strength rises under
    exactly the conditions that do nothing for storage strength.
- **Rank 2 — Retrieval and suggestion enter the same door: the most dangerous
  moment for a record is the moment it is being updated.** `LOW`, Support 2.
  - **Brain part:** **reconsolidation lability.** Hupbach, Gomez, Hardt & Nadel
    (2007, *Learn. Mem.* 14:47–53) showed in humans that a **subtle reminder
    before new learning** caused List-2 items to intrude into List-1 recall
    **24 hours later** — time-dependent, context-dependent, and **selective for
    the reactivated memory only.** Reactivation *without* a reminder produced no
    intrusions, which makes the reminder the write path rather than the
    reactivation.
  - **Design:** ORDER 1155's compound prediction is the whole content of this
    slot and neither file makes it alone: **a retrieval event simultaneously
    opens a window for new information and suppresses the un-retrieved
    competitors.** For an agent that means any RAG read followed by a write-back
    — summarization, dedup, consolidation — is a reconsolidation window, and the
    same act that surfaces a record makes its neighbours unreachable. A
    contaminated replay buffer does not merely preserve errors, it manufactures
    new ones by stitching associations across contexts. **The honest resolution,
    recorded rather than solved:** gate the **writes** (provenance, conflict
    checks, immutable raw capture) and do **not** deny lability — the lability is
    also the only path by which a correction ever lands.
  - **Already exists:** Oeberst et al. (2021, *PNAS* 118:e2026447118) reversed
    *rich* false memories in humans, with **personalized error-feedback** more
    effective than source-sensitization — the design lesson being that a
    correction should show the model *where* the false belief entered, not
    merely assert the opposite.
  - **Backup documents:** ORDER 1155 §"Reactivation windows", §"Reconsolidation
    windows → read-modify-write hazards", §"Reversibility"; ORDER 1159
    §"Relation to other forgetting mechanisms".
  - **Grade:** `LOW` — Hupbach is a small human study and the effect is
    **selective**, which is the part that matters and the part that has not been
    shown at scale. **Recorded caveat carried from ORDER 1155 and not resolved
    there either:** whether gist-level, schema-consistent knowledge — the kind
    agents mostly store — has equivalent update windows, or is modifiable
    continuously, **is open**, and it determines whether agent distillations need
    reconsolidation-style write gating at all.
  - **Why rank 2:** it is rank 1's write side, and it is the only slot that
    makes the corruption *mechanism* rather than the accessibility gradient.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 27** (lability and prediction error) is the same
    window described from the consolidation side; the new content here is that
    the window is *opened by retrieval* and therefore unavoidable for any
    read-then-write agent. **Area 45** (efference copy, provenance) supplies the
    field this slot gates on.
- **Rank 3 — The standard, cheap implementation is a decay curve, and it is the
  wrong one.** `UNTESTED`, Support 2.
  - **Brain part:** the **inhibition/interference dispute** that ORDER 1168
    formalises, brought to bear here. The brain's suppression is **targeted,
    reversible, and recruited only under genuine competition**; crude global
    decay approximates it badly and cannot produce the double dissociation.
  - **Design:** the slot exists to forbid the default. A store that implements
    forgetting as a global recency-weighted decay has *no way* to express
    "suppress B because it intruded on A" and will suppress the wrong thing —
    which is the RIF design caution in ORDER 1159 §4 in its sharpest form
    (*negative caching and down-weighting superseded facts are the legitimate
    uses; crude global decay is not*). It is ranked 3 and not 1 because nothing
    in the corpus has trialled the targeted version; it is ranked at all because
    the corpus is unambiguous that the untargeted one is a different mechanism.
  - **Already exists:** negative caching and superseded-fact down-weighting, both
    ordinary; **the competition-triggered version does not exist.**
  - **Backup documents:** ORDER 1159 §"Inhibition as a feature, not just a bug";
    ORDER 1168 §§3.2, 4.2, 5.1 (SOB's superposition and feature-overwriting;
    TBRS\*-I's confusion and superposition interference).
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` on the design. The *mechanism* it forbids is well
    supported; the replacement has no trial in the corpus.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is a constraint on the other two rather than a design,
    and it says so.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 6** is the same choice made at the consolidation
    boundary instead of the retrieval boundary, and Area 6 holds the effect-size
    floor that this slot's premise depends on.

### Area 94 · Memory belongs to the planner, not the executor: the ablation that says feeding retrieval to the wrong module makes things worse `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by**
`oracle/brain/Memory-Architecture/Memory-Intelligence-Agent-MIA.md` (ORDER 1154,
253 lines), read whole this pass, with
`oracle/brain/Memory-Architecture/Ontology-As-Kernel-Dynamic-Graph.md`
(ORDER 1156, 274 lines) and
`oracle/brain/Memory-Architecture/Self-Optimizing-Memory-SelfMem.md`
(ORDER 1161, 256 lines) as two further independent files — three papers, three
labs, one measured finding each.
`…/Related-Work-Agent-Memory-SelfMem-DMem-MIA.md` (ORDER 1158) restates all
three primaries and is counted **zero** times here (infra C-047: one source
published three times is one source).

**The brain part.** McClelland's **index/content division** — the hippocampus
is not a store of answers, it is an **index that makes neocortical content
addressable**; the computation happens elsewhere. Expressed as a design
question it is: **retrieved material is handed to the module that plans, not the
module that executes.** ORDER 1154 names the cognitive analogue explicitly —
Executor (fast, reactive, tool-using) as System 1, Planner (deliberative,
memory-guided) as System 2 — and ORDER 1156 supplies the same structure as
**vmPFC-as-conductor → hippocampus-as-binder → neocortex-as-detail-retriever**
(Ciaramelli et al. 2019, *Neurosci. Biobehav. Rev.* 105:1–10).

**Why this is a ninety-fourth silence.** `ARENA.md` held **0** occurrences of
`2604.04503` (MIA), `2607.03726` (SelfMem), `2603.18631` (D-Mem), and
`2608.22974` (OaK) — four 2026 papers, none read, none cited, in an arena with
ninety-three areas about memory. The gap is not that the arena lacks a memory
architecture; it is that it has **no slot about which module receives the
retrieval**, and that is the one variable three independent ablations moved.

- **Rank 1 — Route retrieval to planning; the executor gets the plan, not the
  archive.** `LOW`, Support: **3 independent sources.**
  - **Brain part:** index/content, above. The supporting datum is structural
    rather than a single study: in MIA the Manager holds episodic trajectories,
    the Planner holds **parametric** planning knowledge internalized into weights
    by GRPO, and the Executor holds neither — it receives the plan and reports
    back. MIA's own §"Overlaps" calls the bidirectional loop *"computationally
    equivalent to hippocampal replay."*
  - **Design:** the corpus's strongest and most falsifiable memory result this
    pass, from MIA's own ablation table: **"Only Memory" — adding non-parametric
    memory to the Executor with no planner — *decreased* multimodal average
    accuracy by 0.4 points.** **"Memory for Planner" — the same memory, routed to
    the Planner instead — gained +3.5 multimodal and +4.15 text-only.** Same
    memory, same retrieval, opposite sign on the ablation. Every other
    component contributed too (+2.37 for the trained Planner, +3.23 for
    test-time learning, +0.43/+3.28 for reflection), so this is not a
    single-axis result; it is the largest single gain in the table and the only
    negative one.
  - **Already exists:** MIA, arXiv **2604.04503** (ECNU-SII, repo
    `ECNU-SII/MIA`) — Manager/Planner/Executor with bidirectional
    parametric↔non-parametric conversion, alternating GRPO, and test-time
    learning. Adopt, do not rebuild. The arena's `hermes-brain` profiles and
    skills are the natural Planner-side recipient; the researcher subagent is
    Executor-side.
  - **Backup documents:** ORDER 1154 §§"Bidirectional Memory Conversion Loop",
    "Ablation Studies", "Tensions and Incompatibilities"; ORDER 1158 §"Key
    Innovation: Memory as Planning Prior" (**reach confirmed, count not
    incremented**).
  - **Grade:** `LOW`, and one grade-relevant caveat is recorded rather than
    smoothed: MIA's own reviewers note the headline advantage *"could partly
    come from extra rollout compute, plan examples, or a strong planner/router
    scaffold rather than the full bidirectional parametric/non-parametric
    memory loop,"* and disentangling those needs targeted ablations the paper
    does not run. **The specific claim this slot rests on — memory-to-planner
    beats memory-to-executor — is the one the published ablation table does
    test**, which is why the slot survives the caveat and the broader
    bidirectional-conversion claim does not get a higher grade.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is a routing decision, it costs nothing to adopt, and it
    is the only slot in the arena with a **measured negative** on the wrong
    routing.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 93** is the reason this is urgent rather than
    optional — if retrieval patterns write, then routing them to the wrong module
    is a write to the wrong place. **Area 7** (dual-process) supplies the
    System 1/2 frame; the new content is the *routing of the retrieval*, which
    Area 7 does not state.
- **Rank 2 — Schema-adapted procedures beat graph access; removing the typed
  function layer collapses performance.** `LOW`, Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** the vmPFC-as-conductor hierarchy (Barry et al. 2019/2020
    MEG: vmPFC **drives** hippocampal theta during scene construction, then
    mutual entrainment in later stages) as a claim about *who addresses whom*.
  - **Design:** OaK freezes a kernel **K = (S, F)** — a schema plus a typed
    function set — and routes **every** agent action through it, so the agent
    *"cannot name a concept or invoke a computation the kernel does not
    declare."* The ablation is the argument: **removing the function module
    entirely** (letting the model reason over the full graph with minimal
    retrieval) drops CRMArenaPro from **78.38% to ~35%** on DeepSeek-v4-flash;
    removing only function *composition* drops TravelPlanner from **55.90% to
    ~30%**. **Graph access is not a substitute for typed procedures** — the
    corpus's sentence, and it is the same shape as MIA's finding one paper
    earlier: a substrate handed to the wrong module is worse than no substrate.
  - **Already exists:** OaK, arXiv **2608.22974** (Nanjing University), with
    a judge-guided repair loop and a **formal-verification** step (schemas
    encoded as OWL and checked by a reasoner for disjointness, restriction,
    property-level and global consistency, and unsatisfiable classes) —
    deterministic verification of a neural-generated artefact, which is rare and
    which the arena's `verification` skill treats as the preferred order.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1156 §§"The Kernel: K = (S,F)", "Function
    Composition from Generic Operators", "Ablation Study", "Tension 5".
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. **Discrepancy preserved, not resolved:** ORDER 1156 gives
    the kernel as **K = (S, F)**; ORDER 1170 gives the same paper as
    **K = (S, F, G)** with the knowledge graph as a third component. Both files
    are read and both are cited; the difference is whether instantiation is part
    of the kernel or downstream of it, and the corpus does not settle it. That
    matters for a design only mildly — either way the graph is schema-grounded —
    but it is exactly the kind of drift this arena grades others for, so it is
    logged at `VERIFICATION.md` rather than smoothed.
  - **Why rank 2:** it is the same routing law as rank 1 with the substrate
    swapped, and it is ranked lower because its evidence is **one paper** and its
    benchmarks are **closed-world with ground-truth answers** — ORDER 1156's own
    open question asks what happens on open-ended tasks with no objective
    quality metric, and does not answer it.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 8** (structural fidelity) is the mirror image and the
    two must not be merged: Area 8 asks whether a structure is *true*, this asks
    whether a structure is *reachable through a typed procedure*. ORDER 1156's
    ablation says the second question has a large answer even when the first is
    perfect.
- **Rank 3 — Do not build a vector-memory layer until the routing question is
  settled: the strongest baseline in the field is chunk-and-embed.** `LOW`,
  Support 1.
  - **Brain part:** none claimed, and that is the point of the slot. It is the
    **null result** that bounds the other two.
  - **Design:** ORDER 1161's uncomfortable finding, reported by the paper that
    beat it: **simple RAG — chunk by conversational turn, embed with
    `text-embedding-3-small`, FAISS — is the strongest baseline at 100K, 500K and
    1M tokens**, scoring 0.339 / 0.346 / 0.320 against SelfMem's 0.504 / 0.487 /
    0.454. It does not build adaptive memory, has no consolidation, no
    provenance, and no write policy. It also **costs $18.830 at 1M tokens with
    25,779 embedding requests to reach 0.292** where SelfMem reaches 0.454 at
    **$2.004 with zero embedding requests** — so the honest reading is not "RAG
    is enough" but **"the bottleneck in this task family is initial retrieval
    quality, not memory architecture sophistication, and the architecture earns
    its keep by making retrieval better, not by being elaborate."** For the
    arena: the `brain_sync` / Brain Search stack is already a RAG-plus-more
    system, and this slot says the *more* has not yet paid for itself against the
    plain version.
  - **Already exists:** the RAG baseline itself; MemGPT, Mem0, A-Mem, LoCoMo,
    RAG — all measured in the same table, all beaten on score and all beaten on
    cost by the same system.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1161 §§"BEAM Benchmark Results", "The RAG
    Competitiveness Surprise", "Cost Efficiency".
  - **Grade:** `LOW` on the null. **Recorded limitation, from ORDER 1161's own
    §"Limitations":** all experiments used one benchmark (BEAM), one answer model
    and one judge (GPT-5.4 family), and the strategy-refinement study ran only at
    100K — so the comparison is clean but narrow, and R-J2 forbids this arena
    from manufacturing its own benchmark to widen it.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is a bound, not a design, and it is the slot that keeps
    ranks 1 and 2 honest — both are *routing and typing* claims, and neither has
    been shown to beat a well-tuned flat retriever on a second task family.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 30** (per-episode cost) is the same arithmetic from
    the other end. **Area 6** is what a RAG-shaped store is missing.

### Area 95 · The self-graded loop: a memory that rewards its own mistakes, and the independence requirement that breaks it `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by:** `oracle/brain/.meta/archive/ontology-rounds/frontier-research-ontology-round60-2026-10-20.md` §1 (**Memory Reward
Inflation in Self-Improving LLM Agents**, Asadolahi, Amini, Talebi, Farhadi,
Zamanifar, arXiv 2608.00017), **ORDER 1211** — read whole this pass, the
twentieth and last file of the tranche.
**Cite this file by its full path, not its basename.** Three ORDER lines share the
basename `frontier-research-ontology-round60-2026-10-20.md` — **1211** (this
area's earning file, `oracle/brain/.meta/archive/`), **82** (a second copy under
`active-wiki/.meta/archive/`, read and `[x]`), and **1809** (a third under
`oracle/brain/research/`, still `[ ]`). A basename citation is ambiguous across
three different files, and C6 resolved this one to the unread 1809. Full paths
only from here.
**Why this is a brain part and not a fourth facet of Area 24 or Area 33.** Area
24 asks whether an *instrument* is faithful. Area 33 asks what class of claim a
detector can support even when it is. This is one level up the loop and one
question earlier in time: **the score a system assigns to its own past output is
an input to its next output, and the assignment is not a measurement but a
generation.** Neuroscience names the organ the corpus is reaching for: the
**mesolimbic dopamine loop's RPE signal is computed against a *teaching signal
that comes from outside the action policy*** — a real prediction error requires a
signal whose error is uncorrelated with the error being corrected. A system that
grades itself supplies no such signal, because the grader's mistakes and the
graded mistakes are drawn from the same distribution. The loop is not merely
noisy; it is **positively coupled to its own bias**.

- **Rank 1 — The independence requirement on any signal that writes to memory:
  it must fail differently from the thing it is scoring.**
  - **Brain part:** the **teacher signal's independence from the action policy** —
    the RPE's requirement that the teaching signal carry information the policy
    does not already have. An in-model judge is not a weaker teacher; it is not a
    teacher.
  - **Design:** no LLM-self-assigned utility score may be the sole gate on a
    memory write. A write requires at least one **answer-free** signal whose
    failure mode is decorrelated from the grader's — execution/engine feedback,
    cross-episode logical consistency, schema or structural conformance. This is
    the arena's own §8 finding operationalised: the *validated code path*
    (does it parse? does it execute? is it consistent with a stored constraint?)
    is the only signal here that is not the model's opinion of itself.
  - **Already exists — adopt the correction, do not build the diagnosis:**
    **LUCID** (Label-free Utility Correction via Independent De-inflation), in
    the same paper. On BIRD text-to-SQL: naive self-graded memory **54.0%**
    execution accuracy → LUCID **56.9%** (+2.9 pts), closing most of the gap to
    the oracle upper bound, using answer-free signals only (execution errors,
    cross-episode consistency, structural validation). The paper's **Error
    Independence Assumption (EIA)** is the formal statement of the requirement:
    a de-inflation signal is valid only if it tracks truth *and* does not echo
    the self-grade bias. **An LLM judge fails EIA** because its errors correlate
    with the original grader's — stated as a theorem, not a caution.
  - **Backup documents:** `oracle/brain/.meta/archive/ontology-rounds/frontier-research-ontology-round60-2026-10-20.md` §1
    (ORDER 1211, arXiv 2608.00017), read
    this pass. **Corroboration in this workspace's own corpus, from the
    independent direction — ORDER 1207
    `oracle/brain/.meta/archive/ontology-rounds/frontier-research-ontology-round56-2026-09-05.md` §1, Semantic
    Training Gap (arXiv 2605.11234), read this pass:** the same failure with a
    different name and a different mechanism. A manufacturing agent meets three
    designators for one physical sensor and treats them as three data points
    because it has statistical fluency without operational semantics — and in a
    multi-agent configuration this becomes **semantic drift**, where each agent's
    interpretation diverges, neither detects the mismatch because **both outputs
    are linguistically coherent**, and the error compounds down the chain. That
    is a bias the system cannot see from inside any single agent. Support **1 → 2
    independent sources**, and the second is a *different* failure (entity
    resolution across agents) reaching the *same* requirement (a check the system
    cannot perform on itself).
  - **Grade:** design `LOW` for the correction (LUCID has one reported trial, on
    one task family, at N=500 queries) · the **diagnosis is `HIGH` within the
    corpus**: ORDER 1211 reports the inflation appears across model families on a
    live factual bank, proves it compounds *multiplicatively* through the
    retrieval-and-trust loop (Theorem 1), and shows the corrupted attractor is
    analytically predictable from independently measured parameters (Theorem 2).
    Per §8, the diagnosis is `unverified` against a primary source — read this
    pass, not checked against the paper — so the honest grade for the *diagnosis*
    is `LOW` and the theorems are recorded as claims, not established results.
    The two grades are deliberately not averaged.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in the arena that constrains **every**
    other area's write path. Areas 6, 12, 23, 30 and 94 all write to memory; all
    five currently allow the grader and the graded to be the same system.
  - **Interaction:** **this is the enforcement half of Area 33 Rank 1.** Area 33
    says a detector is not a certifier; this says a *self-detector is not even a
    detector*, and compounds. Constrained by R-J2: EIA is a property of the
    signal's failure mode, not a new self-measurement, so adopting it does not
    breach the no-self-measurement rule. **Area 82** (the non-neuronal gating
    layer) is the nearest structural analogue — a gate that is not part of the
    thing it gates.
- **Rank 2 — Retrieval-side de-biasing: make the trust amplifier and the
  retrieval policy separately falsifiable.**
  - **Brain part:** the **retrieval-and-trust loop's** two amplification stages,
    which the corpus separates — *trust amplification* (the planner weights
    high-scored memories above 1.0) and *retrieval amplification* (similarity
    retrieval surfaces memories similar to *successes* even when wrong, κ > 0).
    The brain's retrieval is not driven by a score the recalled item assigned to
    itself.
  - **Design:** the memory bank stores the score **and** its provenance — which
    grader, on what signal — and a write cannot raise its own retrieval priority.
    Where two independent scorers disagree, that disagreement is itself a stored
    feature rather than something to average away (R-J4).
  - **Already exists:** the same paper's two amplification mechanisms and its
    corrupted-attractor prediction (p\* = 0.45 predicted, **0.42** observed);
    ORDER 1211 §1.
  - **Backup document:** `oracle/brain/.meta/archive/ontology-rounds/frontier-research-ontology-round60-2026-10-20.md`
    §1 (ORDER 1211).
  - **Grade:** design `UNTESTED` · Support: **1 source**. The mechanisms are
    specified with a predicted-and-observed match; the *design* that fixes them
    has no trial.
  - **Why rank 2 and not rank 1:** rank 1 corrects the *score*; this corrects
    the *selection*. A correct score that the retrieval policy then over-weights
    is still an Echo Gap. It cannot be rank 1 because it presumes a score worth
    trusting has already been produced, which is rank 1's job.
  - **Interaction:** upstream of **Area 93** (the read is a write) — this is the
    write-side half of the same coupling. Downstream of Area 30: a
    self-inflated bank also grows faster than its own correction can prune.
- **Rank 3 — Bound the trust radius: a memory's influence decays with its
  uncorroborated reuse count rather than with its age.**
  - **Brain part:** **reconsolidation's gating of re-entry** — a memory's
    strengthening on retrieval is a function of what corroborates it, and
    un-corroborated re-retrieval is not among the things that strengthen it.
  - **Design:** reuse count is tracked per memory, and an entry that has been
    retrieved and acted on without independent corroboration **loses** priority
    rather than gaining it. This inverts the default in every design this arena
    has read, all of which treat retrieval as evidence of usefulness.
  - **Already exists:** the adjacent finding is held from elsewhere — ORDER 1194
    (`oracle/brain/.meta/archive/ontology-rounds/frontier-research-ontology-round44-2026-09-10.md`
    §2, Experience Compression Spectrum, arXiv
    2604.15877), read this pass: **curated Level 2 skills add +16.2pp on
    SkillsBench while LLM self-generated skills add +0.0pp.** That is a large
    effect in the same direction and a different measurement (curation by
    something outside the model, versus decay of uncorroborated reuse) — so it
    is a *neighbour*, counted here as a second source for the requirement and
    **not** as a trial of this design.
  - **Backup documents:** `oracle/brain/.meta/archive/ontology-rounds/frontier-research-ontology-round60-2026-10-20.md`
    §1 (ORDER 1211); `oracle/brain/.meta/archive/ontology-rounds/frontier-research-ontology-round44-2026-09-10.md`
    §2 (ORDER 1194).
  - **Grade:** design `UNTESTED` · Support: **2 sources, neither a trial of
    this design.** The ECS result is a trial of *curation*, not of *decay*; it
    supports the axis, not the mechanism.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the weakest of the three and the only one that changes
    a default rather than adding a check. It is kept visible because it is the
    only slot that would make the arena's own retrieval behaviour falsifiable.
  - **Interaction:** competes with **Area 6** (consolidation and
    interference-gated forgetting) for the same decay parameter, and with
    **Area 27** (lability) for the window in which the demotion may occur.

**The disagreement this area preserves, and does not resolve (R-J4).** The
corpus is split on whether the Echo Gap is a property of *label-free* settings
generally or of *self-grading* specifically. ORDER 1211 argues the latter and
makes the de-corroboration argument the load-bearing one: an LLM judge fails EIA
because its errors correlate with the original grader's. But ORDER 1193
(`oracle/brain/.meta/archive/ontology-rounds/frontier-research-ontology-round43-2026-09-09.md`
§2, KBevo, arXiv 2608.26386, COLM 2026), read this
pass, reports **outcome-based reward driving joint KB-and-reasoning improvement**
— grounding 48% → 55%, reasoning correctness 45% → 55% — with no de-corroboration
step of any kind, and ablation showing reasoning optimisation alone cannot
compensate for a weak KB but a strong fixed KB can match co-evolution. On that
reading, grounding the score in an **external** outcome rather than in the
model's own judgement is sufficient, and the independence requirement is weaker
than rank 1 states. **Both are recorded. Neither is averaged.** What survives
the disagreement is the narrower claim the area rests on: *a score generated by
the same process being scored cannot detect that process's errors*, which holds
on both readings.

### Area 96 · The substrate is a budget, and exhaustion has an order `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by:** `oracle/brain/Metabolic-Cognition/Brain-Energy-Budget-Ketone-Fasting.md`
(ORDER 1231, 291 lines) §§"Core Mechanisms: The Brain Energy Budget", "The
Astrocyte-Neuron Lactate Shuttle (ANLS)", "Intermittent Fasting, BDNF, and
Cognitive Enhancement", "Hypoglycemia as a Model of Metabolic Cognitive Failure".
Read whole this pass. This is the first non-catalogue file in three tranches and
the first from a corpus directory (`Metabolic-Cognition/`) the arena had no
occurrence of.

**Why this is not a fourth facet of Area 82.** Area 82 asks *whether the substrate
is in a state that permits a write at all* — a **gate**, binary, on the write
path. This area asks *which cognitive function is sacrificed first when the budget
runs short* — an **allocation order**, continuous, over the whole repertoire. A
gate says no; an order says what goes instead. The arena has a gate and no order.

- **Rank 1 — A declared degradation order, and the first refusal is the most
  expensive function, not the least important one.**
  - **Brain part:** the **metabolic-demand hierarchy of the CNS**, and its
    behavioural shadow under substrate exhaustion. McNay & Gold (2002) and
    McNay et al. (2006): moderate hypoglycemia (plasma glucose ~3.0 mM)
    **impairs hippocampal-dependent memory formation while leaving
    hippocampal-independent memory intact**, and the corpus's ordering is
    explicit — **complex executive function (planning, decision-making,
    cognitive flexibility) fails first; attention and working memory next;
    simple motor function is preserved longest.** The file states *why*, and
    that is the load-bearing clause: the order **mirrors per-gram metabolic
    rate** — prefrontal cortex highest, primary motor cortex most resilient.
    Vulnerability is not a property of importance; it is a property of
    **energy intensity per unit of tissue**.
  - **Design:** every slot in this file carries a declared `metabolic_cost`, and
    under a declared pressure the system **sheds in the brain's order** —
    the most energy-intensive reasoning is deferred or refused *first*, and the
    simple reactive path is preserved longest. The clause that makes this a
    design rather than a slogan: **deferral must be reported as deferral.** A
    system that silently drops its most expensive function and returns a cheap
    answer has produced a *confident wrong answer*, which is the failure mode
    this arena has named in nine areas and never in this one.
  - **Already exists:** nothing in the Hermes stack carries a metabolic cost
    field. `arena_invariants.py` and `verify_reads.py` are **first-order by
    construction** (Area 76's own phrase) — they are cheap, always-on checks,
    and the corpus's own rule is that *the brain spends >95% of its energy on
    intrinsic, ongoing processes with <5% on task-specific demands*, so
    optimising the cheap checks while the expensive pass runs unbounded is the
    biologically predicted failure, not an accident.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1231 §§"Hypoglycemia as a Model of Metabolic
    Cognitive Failure", "Computational & Agent Parallels" §6.
  - **Grade:** design `UNTESTED` — bespoke, no trial. Human mechanism **`LOW`**
    and deliberately not `HIGH`: the hierarchy is well replicated in the animal
    literature the file cites, but its transfer to a symbolic system is
    established nowhere in this corpus, and the file's own §"Individual
    Differences" records that vulnerability is **not uniform** — diabetes,
    aging, GLUT1/3 variation and prior hypoglycaemic episodes all move it.
    **A single global order is therefore a first approximation the corpus
    already qualifies, and the qualification is carried in the slot.**
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot in ninety-six areas that says what the
    system gives up when it cannot do everything, and the arena's existing
    gates (33, 25, 27, 82) all answer a different question.
  - **Interaction:** **completes Area 82** — Area 82's gate plus this area's
    order is one mechanism; a gate with no order produces arbitrary refusals.
    **Constrains Area 3** (effort allocation): Area 3 allocates by objective
    drift, this allocates by substrate. **Constrains Area 13** (metacognitive
    control): the controller's own substrate is the first thing it cannot see.
    **Interacts with Area 38** (workspace capacity) — the two ceilings are
    different and the arena currently conflates them.
- **Rank 2 — An internal substrate model the controller can read, and cannot
  introspect honestly.**
  - **Brain part:** the brain monitors its own metabolic state (**AMPK, mTOR,
    sirtuins**) while the corpus states plainly that whether this constitutes a
    self-monitoring *accessible to cognitive control* is **unclear** — the
    file's own Open Question 2, and it is asked as a question. The design
    consequence is the interesting part: the substrate state is **real,
    load-bearing, and not directly introspectable**, exactly like the
    attentional state in Area 84 Rank 1 (a sweep leaves a trace and no report).
  - **Design:** an explicit `substrate` record — available compute, context
    pressure, accumulated error — that **modulates confidence and deferral but
    is never reported as a felt state**, because no channel carries it. The
    corpus's rule is adopted verbatim: *the energy budget is a latent variable*.
  - **Already exists:** `verified: []` plus `stale_after:` on OKF v0.2
    frontmatter is a capacity field with **no enforcement and no reader** —
    Area 82 Rank 1 already identified it; this slot is what would read it.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1231 §§"Metabolic Phenotyping", "Open Questions"
    Q2 and Q5, "Computational & Agent Parallels" §7.
  - **Grade:** design `UNTESTED`, Support **1 source**. The file itself records
    the biological question as **open** — a slot built on an open question is
    held at `UNTESTED` and is not promoted by the arena wanting an answer.
  - **Why rank 2:** it is the reading half of rank 1 and cannot be the writing
    half, because the quantity it needs does not have a reportable channel.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 44** (self-diagnosis by parameter recovery) is the
    same shape with a different parameter; **Area 75** (four uncertainty
    channels) is the schema precedent, and this is a **fifth** quantity the
    arena's single `confidence:` scalar cannot carry.
- **Rank 3 — Scheduled metabolic switching: alternate throughput and
  consolidation rather than running one mode continuously.** **`UNSUCCESSFUL`,
  and kept precisely because it is a recorded dead end.**
  - **Brain part:** **intermittent metabolic switching** — the glucose→ketone
    switch at 12–24 h, BHB as an HDAC inhibitor upregulating hippocampal BDNF
    (Duan et al. 2001; Mattson 2012; Mattson et al. 2018), adult
    hippocampal neurogenesis.
  - **Design:** periodic offline consolidation windows alternating with active
    inference, on the fasting analogy. The corpus states the agent mapping
    directly: scheduled offline periods that *"alternate with active inference
    periods may enhance long-term knowledge maintenance and prevent
    metabolic stagnation (context drift, knowledge decay)."*
  - **Already exists:** the arena already runs the offline window — this job's
    own `RUNLOG.jsonl` consolidation, `brain_sync` cron, the archival
    session-export cron. **The design is deployed; the justification is what
    fails.**
  - **Grade:** **`UNSUCCESSFUL` for the cognitive claim**, per the corpus's own
    record and not the arena's scepticism: **Martin et al. (2006) found no
    significant cognitive differences between continuous caloric restriction
    and control in a large randomised trial.** Positive human results exist and
    are small — Mattson et al. (2006) verbal memory in older adults over three
    months; Witte et al. (2009) **+20% verbal memory** over three months of
    caloric restriction in healthy elderly — and the file's own summary is that
    *the field is limited by small sample sizes, short durations, and
    difficulty controlling for confounding variables (sleep, hydration,
    caffeine).* The positive BDNF mechanism is `LOW`; **the transfer from
    fasting to a scheduled consolidation window is `UNSUCCESSFUL`** and the
    schedule should be kept on operational grounds, not metabolic ones.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the tranche's most attractive analogy and its weakest
    evidence, and the arena records dead ends so they are not rebuilt blind.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 23** (consolidation switch policy) and **Area 6**
    both schedule the offline window; neither may cite this slot as
    physiological support. **Area 30** (per-episode cost) is where a
    metabolically-motivated schedule would have to pay for itself.

**The disagreement this area preserves, and does not resolve (R-J4).** ORDER 1231
§"Evidence and Controversy" records a live dispute about the mechanism rank 1's
neighbour depends on: **Dienel (2012)** argues lactate accumulation during
activation is a minor fraction of glucose metabolised, that stimulated glycolysis
is not exclusive to astrocytes, and that lactate is *"an opportunistic
glucose-sparing substrate when present in high concentrations"* rather than a
primary fuel. **Pellerin & Magistretti (2012)** hold the shuttle as
load-bearing. The file's own resolution — *both* cell types glycolyse, but
astrocytes preferentially so and neurons preferentially oxidatively — is
**recorded as the current consensus and is not a finding of either paper.**
Both are kept; neither is averaged away.

### Area 97 · The architecture of the memory system is itself the plastic variable `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by:** `oracle/brain/.meta/archive/ontology-rounds/frontier-research-ontology-round85-2026-09-12.md`
(ORDER 1230, 373 lines) §1 (**MemEvolve**, arXiv 2512.18746, ICML 2026), read
whole this pass. **The brain-part naming below is the arena's mapping, not the
file's claim** — the file supplies the mechanism and the measurement and names no
anatomy, and the arena says so rather than borrowing a citation it did not earn.

**Why this is not a fifth facet of Area 66.** Area 66 is **metaplasticity** — the
*learning rate* is a function of recent activity, the substrate's willingness to
change is itself plastic. This is one level up: not the gain parameter, **the
architecture of the memory system** — its encode/store/retrieve/manage
decomposition — is the thing that varies. Area 66 changes how hard the substrate
learns; this changes what the substrate *is*.

- **Rank 1 — Bilevel evolution of the memory architecture over a declared
  modular design space, with the outer loop selected on held-out tasks.**
  - **Brain part:** **experience-expectant architecture** — the developmental
    claim that the brain's connectivity is built from statistical regularities
    *across* experience rather than from specific experiences, and the
    metaplastic claim one level up: the learning system's own configuration is
    a function of its performance history. Neuroscience names the substrate-level
    version (BCM's sliding threshold, **Area 66**); this is the architecture-level
    version, and **no corpus file supplies it as anatomy** — the mapping is the
    arena's and is graded accordingly.
  - **Design:** decompose memory into **Encode / Store / Retrieve / Manage**;
    inner loop evolves memory *content* with the architecture fixed; outer loop
    **modifies and recombines the architecture itself**, selecting on a
    tournament over held-out tasks. EvolveLab is the standardised substrate
    (12 memory systems behind one interface).
  - **Already exists:** **MemEvolve**, ICML 2026, code at
    `github.com/bingreeky/MemEvolve`. EvolveLab ships as a comparable harness.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1230 §1.
  - **Grade:** design `LOW` · Support **1 source** for the mechanism.
    **Measured, and the number is the reason this is not `HIGH`:** **+17.06%**
    over SmolAgent and Flash-Searcher, with **2.0–9.09%** transfer to unseen
    benchmarks and backbones. The corpus records the limits in its own
    `UNCERTAINTIES` block: **broader generalisation untested** beyond four
    benchmarks, and the `CONFLICTS` block concedes hand-designed systems have
    **interpretability and control advantages that were not measured** — which is
    the trade this arena cares about most, since a self-evolving schema is
    unauditable by construction while `ARENA.md` is auditable precisely because
    its schema is fixed.
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only design in ninety-seven areas that changes the
    *shape* of the memory system from evidence rather than from argument.
  - **Interaction:** **directly in tension with this workspace's own audit
    surface.** Every checker here — `arena_invariants.py`, `verify_reads.py`,
    `citation_remediation.py` — assumes a **fixed** ledger schema and a fixed
    slot format. An evolved memory architecture is **unverifiable by a checker
    written against the previous schema**, which is Area 33's detector/certifier
    asymmetry arriving as an engineering constraint. **Competes with Area 95**
    (the self-graded loop): both are second-order loops over memory, and
    MemEvolve's outer loop selects on task performance, which is exactly the
    self-graded signal Area 95 shows compounds its own bias.
- **Rank 2 — Evolved architectures as evidence about what good memory design
  looks like, read as description rather than as prescription.**
  - **Brain part:** the same mapping, narrowed — the *emergent design
    principles* an outer loop converges on (agentic involvement, hierarchical
    organisation, multi-level abstraction) are claims about what a well-built
    memory system looks like, arrived at by search rather than by assumption.
  - **Design:** treat the three named evolved architectures (**Riva, Cerebra,
    Lightweight**) as **descriptive findings about the design space**, and adopt
    the principles without adopting the meta-loop. Lightweight's *predictive
    behaviour* — anticipating target information in image content and guiding
    the agent to locate it — is the useful one here.
  - **Already exists:** MemEvolve's released evolved architectures, per the file.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1230 §1, "Evolved architectures" and Key Results.
  - **Grade:** design `UNTESTED` · Support **1 source**, and the same source as
    rank 1 — **this is one paper, not two supports.**
  - **Why rank 2:** it survives the objection rank 1 cannot. If the outer loop
    cannot be audited, its *output* can still be read.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 12** (procedural memory) and **Area 91** (the cortex
    as a stack of time windows) both concern memory structure; hierarchical
    multi-granularity storage is the same claim from the ML side.
- **Rank 3 — The arena's own method is a self-reinforcing loop, and it is now
  measurable.** `UNTESTED`, Support 1 — and this is the slot the tranche
  actually needed.
  - **Brain part:** none, deliberately, and that is the point. **Area 62 Rank 3
    already recorded the arena's `"earlier tranches were right"` constraint as a
    silent self-reinforcing loop.** MemEvolve supplies the *mechanism by which
    such a loop is detected*: outer-loop selection on held-out tasks is the only
