  - **Why rank 3:** it is the only slot that names this file's own blind spot,
    and it is ranked 3 because acting on it would change the method that
    produces the evidence, which no single pass may do unilaterally.
  - **Interaction:** **upstream of every area's grade.** A saturated method and a
    working method produce identical ledgers, identical arena files and identical
    PASS verdicts, which is Area 33's asymmetry in its purest form.

**Tranche 56 adds a neighbour this area did not have.** ORDER 1229's
**Procedural Graphs** (arXiv 2609.09153, recorded in full under Area 12) evolves a
procedural structure through an LLM refiner that proposes edits and is then
**gated on a held-out validation score** — the gate is the independence condition
this area's rank 1 requires, arrived at from the procedural side rather than the
memory side. That makes it the arena's first **working instance of the
independence requirement**, and it is why Area 97's rank-1 design is flagged
against this area rather than merely adjacent to it: MemEvolve's outer loop
selects on **task performance**, which is exactly the self-graded signal this
area shows compounds. **No grade change here** — one implementing paper is not a
replication of the theorem, and the implementing gate is a *design* whose score
is external while the *theorem* is about a score generated by the graded process.

### Area 98 · Monitoring and control are two systems, and knowing is not acting `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Metacognition/Metacognitive-Monitoring-vs-Control.md`
(ORDER 1240, 360 lines) read whole this pass, with
`oracle/brain/Metacognition/Metacognitive-Sensitivity.md` (ORDER 1241) as the
second file earning it independently from the measurement side.

**The brain part.** Nelson & Narens (1990) split cognition into an
**object level** that does the work and a **meta level** that both monitors it
and controls it — and the load-bearing clause of this area is that **the two
functions are dissociable, in both directions.** Monitoring produces the
judgments (confidence, feeling-of-knowing, ease-of-learning); control governs
allocation (study time, search termination, strategy). Monitoring feeds control,
yet accurate monitoring does **not** produce effective control, and functional
control occurs with no metacognitive awareness at all. The corpus's own summary
of the resulting pathology is the sentence this area is built on: *"a system
with perfect monitoring sensitivity but zero monitoring-control coupling would
be a perfect observer but a helpless agent."*

**Why this is a ninety-eighth structural silence, and it is aimed at the
arena's own method.** Ninety-seven areas grade slots, write `confidence:` values,
and record what they read. **Not one asks whether any of that changes a
decision.** The arena is a monitoring organ with no control path: the
`RUNLOG.jsonl` `duration_seconds` field, the `Support: N` counts and the
`INVARIANTS` verdicts are all read by a human and none of them has ever
reordered a slot on its own. That is the "labor-in-vain effect" in its
institutional form — Nelson & Leonesio (1988) found learners spend extra time on
hard items and the time does not compensate for the difficulty; this arena
spends passes and the pass count does not compensate for a wrong rank.
`ARENA.md` held **zero** occurrences of *meta-d'*, *Nelson*, or *monitoring-control
coupling*.

- **Rank 1 — Hold the monitoring signal and the control policy as separate
  objects, and require every control decision to name the monitoring input it
  acted on.** `UNTESTED` on the design, `HIGH` on the dissociation.
  - **Brain part:** the meta-level/object-level split with the thermostat
    analogy — control does not merely start and stop the object level, it
    **changes the nature of its processing**, so a policy that can only toggle
    the same computation is not control.
  - **Design:** two fields, not one. A slot carries `monitoring:` (what was
    observed — a ledger row, a character count, a checker verdict) separately
    from `control:` (what decision was taken *because of* it). A rank change
    whose `control:` field cannot name the `monitoring:` entry that forced it is
    a preference, and says so. This workspace already owns the split in embryo:
    `RUNLOG.jsonl` is the monitoring channel and `ARENA.md` is the control
    channel, and the discipline of this area is that **no prose in the control
    channel may be admitted as monitoring evidence** — which is Area 74's rule
    arriving from the receiving end.
  - **Already exists?** The framework and its estimators are published and
    maintained: `hmetad` (Fleming 2017, hierarchical Bayesian meta-d′), Type 2
    SDT as a measurement standard. **Not built here.** Nothing in `brain/`,
    `hooks/`, `plugins/`, the ledger, or any checker reads a confidence value
    and changes a rank because of it.
  - **Backup documents:**
    `oracle/brain/Metacognition/Metacognitive-Monitoring-vs-Control.md`
    §§1.3, 2.3, 3.2, 4.3, 5.7; `oracle/brain/Metacognition/Metacognitive-Sensitivity.md`
    §"Nelson-Narens Architecture as Agent Control Loop".
  - **Grade:** `HIGH` on the dissociation, `UNTESTED` on the design. Support:
    **2 independent sources** (ORDER 1240, ORDER 1241). The dissociation is
    carried by convergent work from independent directions — Maniscalco & Lau
    (2012) on meta-d′ as a separately estimable quantity; Fleming & Lau (2014)
    on rlPFC/anterior-PFC monitoring dissociating from lateral-PFC control;
    McIntosh et al. (2019, 2022) using exactly this instrument to *refute* the
    Dunning-Kruger double-burden (see **Area 99**), which is the strongest
    available demonstration that monitoring and control can be measured apart.
    **The cap is stated:** the evidence is human, and the transfer to a
    symbolic system is established nowhere in this corpus.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 74** (the interpreter narrates a cause) — this is
    the same asymmetry pointed at the *receiver*; a narrative that is never
    acted on is a monitoring channel with no coupling. **Area 87** (RS read as
    SS) — the arena's most-cited disease is a monitoring/control failure
    wearing a memory costume. **Area 95** (self-graded loop) — its independence
    requirement is a control-path requirement. **Area 33** (a detector is not a
    certifier) — the general form.
  - **Corroboration, tranche 72, ORDER 1704
    `frontier-research-brain-2026-09-18.md` §13 (MIRROR, arXiv 2604.19809,
    read whole) — the first *measured* demonstration of this area's exact
    claim, and the numbers are the reason it changes the grade and not the
    rank.** Support **2 → 3 independent sources.** MIRROR reports across
    **eight experiments at four metacognitive levels** that models possess
    **above-chance self-knowledge and systematically fail to act on it** —
    which is the Nelson & Narens dissociation stated as a result rather than
    argued from anatomy — and then measures the control intervention the arena
    has been *asserting* for seventy-eight areas: external metacognitive
    control cuts the **Confident Failure Rate from 0.600 to 0.143, a 76%
    reduction**. The file's own phrasing is the slot's whole argument in one
    line: *raw self-knowledge is insufficient without an explicit control
    mechanism that translates monitoring into action selection.*
    **The two grades on this slot move differently, and neither is averaged.**
    The **dissociation** was already `HIGH` on human evidence (Fleming & Lau,
    Maniscalco & Lau, McIntosh et al.); it is now `HIGH` on a *machine*
    evidence base as well, which removes the standing cap — MIRROR is the
    transfer to a symbolic system the previous grade explicitly said was
    "established nowhere in this corpus." The **design** stays `UNTESTED`:
    MIRROR's controller is a benchmark harness for LLM agents, **not** the
    arena's own `monitoring:` / `control:` field pair, and per R-J2 this pass
    creates no new self-measurement, so nothing here has yet been run on
    `ARENA.md` itself. **Recorded, not reconciled:** MIRROR's absolute numbers
    are single-harness, unverified against the paper, and §8 applies — the
    validated apparatus (`confidence: high`, a `verified:` block, 24 cited
    arXiv IDs) surrounds prose this pass did not check at source.

- **Rank 2 — Measure the coupling, not the two halves.** `UNTESTED`.
  - **Brain part:** Koriat, Ma'ayan & Nussinson (2006) — monitoring and control
    correlate *without* monitoring causing control, because both draw on the
    same cues. The correlation is therefore not evidence of a working control
    path, which is the measurement trap this slot exists to close.
  - **Design:** the arena's own falsification test. For any slot whose rank
    changed, ask whether the decision would have differed had the monitoring
    signal been absent. A rank that survives deletion of its stated evidence
    was not caused by it.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1240 §1.2). The design
    has never been run on this arena's own history.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 76** (verification ceiling) — the same demand for a
    trace that would look different if the story were false.

- **Rank 3 — Implicit monitoring precedes explicit monitoring, so build the
  behavioural channel before the self-report channel.** `LOW`.
  - **Brain part:** Paulus et al. (2013) — 3.5-year-olds show no explicit
    confidence difference between correct and incorrect items, but their
    fixation times and pupil dilation *do* differentiate; Balcomb & Gerken
    (2008) — the same children decline to answer when uncertain. The explicit
    verbal channel is a late overlay on an earlier non-verbal one.
  - **Design:** prefer proxies that are computed rather than asserted —
    retrieval latency, result-set diversity, checker exit codes, disagreement
    between independent runs — over a field that reports its own state. This
    slot is `LOW` rather than `UNTESTED` because the eye-tracking and
    opt-out paradigms are independently replicated; what is `UNTESTED` is
    whether any of them transfers to a system with no eyes.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1240 §4.1), citing two
    independent studies within it.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 77** (the transparent self-model) — the same
    limit, stated from the introspection side.

### Area 99 · The famous pattern replicated and the famous explanation did not — and one of the replications used pure noise `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Metacognition/Dunning-Kruger-Replication-Debates.md`
(ORDER 1236, 644 lines) read whole this pass. **No second file in 1,147 earned
it**; it is one source and the slots say so.

**The brain part.** The **metacognitive-sensitivity / first-order-performance
dissociation** (Fleming & Lau 2014) read in its most consequential direction:
a confidence pattern can be produced with **no metacognitive deficit anywhere
in the system**, and the production is arithmetic. Five independent accounts —
regression to the mean, better-than-average bias, bounded/censored scales,
heteroskedasticity, and Bayesian updating under an optimistic prior — each
reproduce the Dunning-Kruger graph from premises that contain no claim about
the unskilled being unaware. And Nuhfer et al. (2016) reproduced the canonical
figure **from random numbers**, using only the original quartile-splitting
method.

**Why this is a ninety-ninth structural silence, and it is a warning about how
this file reads itself.** Ninety-eight areas rank by comparing entities in
quartiles — top-three per area, and a reader comparing the top slot of one area
against the top slot of another is running precisely the operation the corpus
shows can manufacture a crossing pattern out of an uncorrelated pair. The
corpus states the mechanism: selecting on extreme values of *x* and plotting *y*
**guarantees** the crossing whenever the correlation is below 1, and the
correlation in this literature is *r* ≈ .19–.35. **The saving fact is that this
arena already reads individual files one at a time**, which is the individual-
data condition Nuhfer and Gignac call the valid one; the file names the
alternative — `(y − x)` against *x* scatterplots, quantile-aggregated line
charts — as the convention that *creates* the nonlinearity. Read as a warning
about the method, this file vindicates the ledger's slowest property.

- **Rank 1 — Never let an aggregate split carry a claim. Read the individual
  cases, and state the correlation the pattern is riding on.** `UNTESTED` on
  the design, `HIGH` on the artifact.
  - **Brain part:** the measurement apparatus rather than the mind —
    quartile-splitting plus a bounded 0–100 scale is a generator, and the
    generated graph is indistinguishable from the real one.
  - **Design:** every comparative claim in this arena names its `n` and its
    underlying correlation before it names a difference. A "top-three per area"
    ranking is a **display convention**, and this slot requires it be labelled
    as one wherever a reader could mistake it for a measurement.
  - **Already exists?** The methodological standards are published —
    continuous individual-differences modelling, Type 2 SDT, censored Tobit,
    Glejser/Breusch-Pagan heteroskedasticity tests. **Not applied here**, and
    per R-J2 no new self-measurement is created by this slot: the requirement
    is on *what a claim must state*, not on a new measurement to be run.
  - **Backup document:**
    `oracle/brain/Metacognition/Dunning-Kruger-Replication-Debates.md`
    §§"The Random-Noise Demonstration", "The 17-Measure Problem" (via ORDER
    1240 §2.2), "The Quartile-Splitting Methodology", "Summary of Mechanisms".
  - **Grade:** `HIGH` on the artifact, `UNTESTED` on the design. Support:
    **1 source**, and it is a strong one — the artifact rests on Nuhfer et al.
    (2016, *Numeracy* 9(1):4), Gignac & Zajenkowski (2020, *Intelligence*
    80:101449), and Magnus & Peresetsky (2022, *Frontiers in Psychology*
    13:840180, three-parameter censored Tobit, N = 665, near-perfect fit).
  - **Interaction:** **Area 87** — the same failure wearing a memory costume,
    where 65–85% of participants pick the wrong condition and cannot recall
    which they had. **Area 98** — McIntosh et al. used this area's instrument,
    and got a null, which is the coupling test working. **Area 84** (ignition)
    — a threshold on having a thought, and a threshold on a bounded scale is
    where quartiles come from.

- **Rank 2 — Calibration and sensitivity are orthogonal axes, and a system can
  be perfectly calibrated and useless.** `HIGH`.
  - **Brain part:** the criterion-free construction — meta-d′ is invariant to
    response bias, so a temperature-scaled confidence can have Brier 0.25 and
    meta-d′ = 0, which is to say *always report 50% on a 50% base rate* and be
    perfectly calibrated and completely uninformative. It cannot tell you which
    answer is wrong; it can only tell you the base rate.
  - **Design:** the arena's `confidence:` field is a calibration field and is
    read as a sensitivity field. Every grade in this file that says `LOW`
    because "the evidence is thin" is a calibration statement; whether the
    confidence *discriminates* good evidence from bad is a separate, Type 2
    measurement, and this arena has never made it. Report both or label the
    grade as calibration-only.
  - **Grade:** `HIGH`. Support: **2 sources** (ORDER 1236, ORDER 1241), and
    the orthogonality is formal rather than empirical — it follows from the
    SDT construction, so it is the best-evidenced claim in this tranche.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 74** and **Area 75** — Area 75's four channels
    are four *calibration* statements; this says none of them is a
    discrimination statement.

- **Rank 3 — The pattern is real; only the explanation failed. Do not discard
  a phenomenon because its mechanism was refuted.** `LOW`.
  - **Brain part:** the separation the corpus insists on — the raw quartile
    pattern is robustly replicated across domains and cultures, and the
    **double-burden account is what failed to replicate** (McIntosh et al.
    2019: meta-d′ does not differ across quartiles, *F*(3, 1150) = 1.2,
    *p* = .31; McIntosh et al. 2022: refutation titled as such).
  - **Design:** the boundary this slot draws so Rank 1 is not read as "the DK
    effect is fake." It is not fake. The unskilled *are* miscalibrated; the
    claim that they are unaware **of their own unawareness** is what did not
    survive. That distinction is the difference between a discarded dead end
    and a rewritten live one, and the arena's `UNSUCCESSFUL` grade exists to
    make it.
  - **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL` **for the double-burden account specifically**,
    `LOW` for the pattern. Support: **1 source**, but a source that carries
    both grades explicitly, which is rarer than a source that carries one.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 80** (changing the rule vs changing the value) —
    the same discipline applied to a mechanism rather than a circuit.

### Area 100 · The outside view: a reference class beats a plan, and the brain has no access to its own base rates `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Metacognition/Planning-Fallacy-Reference-Class-Forecasting.md`
(ORDER 1242, 511 lines) read whole this pass. Corroborated in reach by ORDER
1236's §"The DK Debate is a meta-bias case study", which names the same
regression-to-the-mean misunderstanding in a second literature.

**The brain part.** The **inside view / outside view** distinction (Kahneman &
Tversky 1979): forecasting is normally done by simulating *this* project, and
the simulation samples only the obstacles the forecaster can recall or imagine.
The outside view asks a different question — *what happened to projects like
this one?* — and the corpus's sharpest experimental datum is the **self–other
asymmetry**: Buehler, Griffin & Ross (1994, N = 207) found participants
underestimate their own completion times by 22–31% (*d* ≈ 0.6–0.8) while
predicting **other people's** times accurately (27.3 predicted vs 27.1 actual).
Given the identical base rates, in the same experiment, on the same day, the
outside view is available and is used for everyone except the self.

**Why this is a hundredth structural silence with a receipt attached.** This
arena runs a **13-minute cron slot** and every `RUNLOG.jsonl` line already
carries `duration_seconds` — a predicted-versus-actual pair, logged
unprompted, for hundreds of runs. That is a **reference class**, accumulating
in the workspace, unused. `ARENA.md` held **zero** occurrences of *reference
class*, *outside view*, or *uplift*. Overruns are reported in prose ("runs
average ~8.7 minutes against a 13-minute slot") rather than converted into the
distribution the corpus says is the correct instrument.

- **Rank 1 — Log (predicted, actual) per job class and schedule from the
  empirical distribution, not from the estimate.** `UNTESTED` on the design,
  `HIGH` on the policy-level result.
  - **Brain part:** reference-class forecasting as an **external monitoring
    prosthesis** — the corpus's sharp formulation is that the object-level
    (the plan) has *no performance data until after execution*, so RCF
    substitutes historical data, which is a form of **vicarious monitoring**.
    That is the Nelson–Narens loop of **Area 98** with the meta-level's
    missing input supplied from outside.
  - **Design:** `RUNLOG.jsonl` already writes `duration_seconds`. The slot
    requires a per-job-class distribution of *actual ÷ predicted* and a stated
    percentile (P80 for a job that must not be late, P50 for one that may
    overlap). The skill's own 13-minute interval and the arena's stated ~8.7
    minute mean are exactly a P-something waiting to be computed. **Note the
    scale assumption (R-J5):** the distribution must be computed online over
    accumulating runs, because a 14,589-file corpus makes a single-pass
    estimate meaningless — the reference class is *runs*, and it grows.
  - **Already exists?** The method is operationalised in UK transport
    policy — the HM Treasury Green Book uplift curves, 107 projects analysed
    (Park 2021), and as agent-callable tooling in the **Epoch MCP server**
    (KyaniteLabs), packaging PERT, COCOMO II, Monte Carlo and RCF as 25 tools.
    **Not built here.** Nothing in this workspace reads its own log to produce
    a percentile.
  - **Backup document:**
    `oracle/brain/Metacognition/Planning-Fallacy-Reference-Class-Forecasting.md`
    §§"The Outside View", "UK RCF Mandate", "Implications for Cron Duration
    Calibration in Autonomous Systems", "Honest Measurement".
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` on the design, `HIGH` on the phenomenon. Support:
    **1 source** (ORDER 1242), carrying unusually strong numbers — Flyvbjerg's
    database of **863+ projects** with **0.5%** delivering on time, on budget
    and on benefits; Park (2021) before-after UK overrun reduction **38% → 5%**
    with a with-without US comparison (+12 pp vs −17 pp against an on-budget
    target). **Preserved disagreement (R-J4):** Themsen (2019) reports Danish
    RCF applied selectively to projects already likely to succeed, with uplifts
    politically overridden when inconvenient — and the corpus's own
    reconciliation is *institutional enforcement* (mandate vs recommendation),
    which is a claim about governance, not about cognition.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 98** — vicarious monitoring is what supplies the
    input a self-monitoring meta-level cannot generate. **Area 99** — the
    quartile artifact is the inside view's statistical cousin; both are
    warnings about aggregation choices. **Area 96** (the substrate is a
    budget) — the exhaustion order and the schedule are the same question at
    two timescales.

- **Rank 2 — Structural debiasing beats psychological debiasing: change what
  data the estimate is made from, not how the estimator thinks.** `LOW`.
  - **Brain part:** Flyvbjerg's bypass argument — the forecaster is *not
    required to make scenarios, imagine events, or gauge their own ability*,
    "so they cannot get all these things wrong"; paired with Kahneman's finding
    that knowing about a cognitive illusion does not dissolve it. Explicit
    outside-view instruction reduced bias by only ~50% (Buehler et al. 1994,
    Study 5); the full structural method reduced overruns by 60–85%.
  - **Design:** the rule for this arena: **a prompt that tells the agent to be
    careful is a ~50% intervention; a change to what the agent reads is a
    structural one.** Rank 1 is an instance.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Support: **1 source**, with the 38%→5% contrast internal
    to it.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 74** — a debiasing instruction is a request to the
    interpreter; a reference class is a fact the interpreter cannot fit over.

- **Rank 3 — RCF without an honest outcome database is not RCF; it is
  benchmarking against a curated success narrative.** `UNSUCCESSFUL`, as a
  boundary.
  - **Brain part:** the quietly-excluded failure mode — failed projects are
    archived, re-scoped or forgotten, so the class is drawn from survivors.
  - **Design:** the corpus's own requirement is blunt: *"no project was quietly
    excluded for being embarrassing."* For this workspace the concrete form is
    that **a pass which crashes, times out, or is reaped must still write its
    `duration_seconds`**, or the class silently becomes the distribution of
    runs that finished cleanly — which is the distribution of the system's
    successes, and which would make every future percentile optimistic. This
    slot is `UNSUCCESSFUL` because the file documents the failure recurring in
    exactly the setting where it is hardest to notice.
  - **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL`. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 95** (self-graded loop) — a reference class drawn
    only from graded successes is a self-graded loop with extra steps.

### Area 101 · The scheduler's discount function cannot represent the thing it schedules `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Motivation-and-Curiosity/Temporal-Discounting-Procrastination.md`
(ORDER 1263, 315 lines) read whole this pass. Corroborated in reach by ORDER
1237 (`Epistemic-Emotions.md`) §"Curiosity-Confidence Reversal Boundary", which
independently reports a **reversal** in the curiosity–confidence relation and
attributes it to Dubey & Griffiths (2017).

**The brain part.** The **dual-system architecture of intertemporal choice** —
McClure et al. (2004) found immediate rewards recruit limbic/ventral-striatal
activity and delayed rewards recruit dorsolateral PFC and posterior parietal
cortex — combined with the finding that human discounting is **hyperbolic, not
exponential**, and therefore **preference-reversing**. The same person prefers
$10 today over $11 tomorrow and prefers $11 in 31 days over $10 in 30 days. The
corpus supplies the mechanism behind the asymmetry: Dolan et al. (2007) found
hippocampal damage steepens discounting, and that prompting subjects to imagine
future scenarios before choosing **reduces** discount rates — the patient
system's substrate is the same hippocampal machinery that simulates.

**Why this is a hundred-and-first structural silence, and it is a mathematical
one.** Every scheduler in this stack — cron intervals, task queues, budget
allocation — uses **`γ^t`, which is time-consistent by construction**. A
time-consistent discount function *cannot represent a preference reversal*: if
A ≻ B at *t*₀, consistency guarantees A ≻ B at every later moment. The brain
this arena is modelling is built from a function that violates the property the
scheduler is defined by. This is not a tuning gap; it is a **representational
one**, and no threshold on γ repairs it. `ARENA.md` held **zero** occurrences
of *hyperbolic*, *preference reversal*, or *TMT*.

- **Rank 1 — Make the scheduler's discount function reversible, and make
  reversibility visible in the queue.** `UNTESTED`.
  - **Brain part:** hyperbolic discounting `V = A/(1 + kT)` with the
    **sign effect** (gains discounted more steeply than losses) and the
    **magnitude effect** (small amounts discounted more steeply than large) —
    both are violations of the exponential form that `γ^t` satisfies, and
    neither is a parameter choice.
  - **Design:** the queue orders by a function permitted to reorder itself as
    the deadline approaches, and the ordering is **recorded as having
    changed**. A task that a scheduler would never re-promote under `γ^t` but
    does promote under a hyperbolic form is the observable signature, and it
    is the arena's first experiment that does not require a new
    self-measurement (R-J2 satisfied by construction — the signature appears
    or does not in an existing queue).
  - **Already exists?** In economics and RL, yes and at scale: the β–δ model
    (Laibson 1997; O'Donoghue & Rabin 1999) is the standard applied form, and
    multi-horizon learning with separate near/far discount rates is implemented
    (Fedus et al. 2019). **Not present in any Hermes cron expression**, all of
    which are fixed-interval and therefore perfectly time-consistent.
  - **Backup document:**
    `oracle/brain/Motivation-and-Curiosity/Temporal-Discounting-Procrastination.md`
    §§"Three Discount Functions", "Sign and Magnitude Effects", "Preference
    Reversal", "Task Scheduling as Temporal Discounting".
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` on the design, `HIGH` on the human phenomenon.
    Support: **1 source**, with the phenomenon resting on Mazur (1987),
    Green/Myerson/McFarland (2004) and Ainslie (1975, 1992) — animal and human,
    which is the strongest form of the claim.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 98** — a patient system that overrides an impatient
    one is a monitoring signal reaching a control policy, which is the whole of
    this area. **Area 100** — RCF supplies the base rates a hyperbolic
    function needs; the two are complements and neither composes without the
    other. **Area 3** (effort allocation) — same machinery, one fixed
    objective.

- **Rank 2 — Temporal Motivation Theory as the explicit scheduler form.**
  `UNTESTED`.
  - **Brain part:** `Motivation = (Expectancy × Value) / (1 + Impulsiveness ×
    Delay)` (Steel 2007, from a meta-analysis of 500+ studies and 50,000+
    subjects), which makes motivation rise **non-linearly** as a deadline
    approaches — the denominator shrinks, so the same task gains value without
    any new information arriving. Four parameters, all estimable from a log
    the workspace already writes.
  - **Design:** the concrete recipe, and the reason it is rank 2 rather than
    rank 1: TMT *fixes* the order and does not represent its revision, so a
    system running TMT still cannot exhibit the reversal it is derived from.
    Rank 1 is the harder requirement; this is the tractable half.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 80** (rule vs value are different circuits) — TMT
    puts `Value` in a numerator and `Delay` in a denominator, which is that
    area's separation in algebra.

- **Rank 3 — Precommitment beats self-imposed deadlines, because self-imposed
  ones are set too far out to ever enter the motivated region.** `LOW`.
  - **Brain part:** Ariely & Wertenbroch (2002), MIT MBA field experiment —
    externally imposed **evenly spaced** deadlines produced the best work;
    self-imposed deadlines improved on none but clustered too near the final
    deadline, keeping motivation artificially low for most of the project.
  - **Design:** an external scheduler sets intermediate deadlines the agent
    did not choose. This is the same structural-over-psychological result as
    **Area 100** rank 2, arriving through a different mechanism, and the
    agreement between the two is the slot's content.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Support: **1 source**, N = 40, one field setting.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 100** rank 2 — two independent literatures, same
    conclusion about instruction versus structure.

### Area 102 · Two streams, and the one you can report on is not the one you act with `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Motor-Control-and-Action/Motor-Control-Action-Planning-and-Learning.md`
(ORDER 1265, 281 lines) §4 "Sensorimotor Integration" (Goodale & Milner 1992;
patient D.F.), with the same file's §3 basal-ganglia section as the second,
independent anatomical route to the same dissociation. **One file, read whole.**

**The brain part.** The **dual visual streams** — **ventral** (occipito-temporal)
carrying *"vision for perception"* in **allocentric**, object-centred coordinates,
and **dorsal** (occipito-parietal) carrying *"vision for action"* in **egocentric**,
body-centred coordinates. The load-bearing evidence is a **single-patient lesion
with a double dissociation inside it**: patient **D.F.**, ventral-stream damage,
visual-form agnosia — she **could not describe the orientation of a mail slot** —
yet could **accurately post a letter through it**. Her hand knew what her report
could not say. Goodale & Milner 1992, *Trends in Neurosciences* 15(1): 20–25.

**Why this is a hundred-and-second structural silence, and it is about
*reporting* rather than *acting*.** Ninety-five areas hold a confidence, a grade,
a `Support:` count, or a `verified:` block. **Every one of them is ventral.** The
arena's entire output channel is *"vision for perception"* — object-centred,
reportable, named — and **not one of the hundred-and-two areas holds an
egocentric channel that acts on the same state without narrating it.** A task
performed well and a task described accurately are treated throughout this
workspace as the same event, recorded once, in the same field.

- **Rank 1 — Keep an egocentric action channel that does not have to pass
  through the report.** `UNTESTED` on the design; the phenomenon is `LOW`.
  - **Brain part:** the dorsal stream, and the **coordination-frame** property
    that makes it different in kind rather than degree — egocentric, body-centred,
    continuously updated, and **not nameable** in the ventral stream's vocabulary.
  - **Design:** two channels over one state. The report channel is what the
    arena already is. The action channel takes `(position, trajectory, affordance,
    target)` and returns a **verdict without a sentence**. Where the two
    disagree, **the disagreement is the finding and is recorded, not reconciled** —
    which is the arena's own R-J4, applied to a channel that has not existed
    before. The observable is already in this workspace: `read_file` returns
    content and the run proceeds; there is no record anywhere of *what the file
    looked like* to the system that acted on it. **Scale assumption (R-J5): at
    14,589 files the report channel is the bottleneck, because report
    construction is per-file and action selection is per-decision** — 629 unread
    rows each needing a verdict and each costing a paragraph is the shape of the
    problem.
  - **Already exists:** nothing. No service in `brain/`, `hooks/`, `plugins/` or
    the ledger schema carries a second channel. This is a **schema addition, not
    a build.**
  - **Backup document:** `Motor-Control-Action-Planning-and-Learning.md`
    (ORDER 1265) §4.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` on the build. The **phenomenon** is `LOW` — one
    patient is one patient, and the file itself records the refinement: the
    streams **interact more than originally proposed**, the dorsal stream can
    contribute to perception, and the ventral stream can support delayed actions.
    **The dissociation is real; the clean two-box version is the part that is
    `LOW`.**
  - **Interaction:** **upstream of every area**, and this is why it is rank 1.
    It constrains **Area 24** (validity of one's own instruments) and **Area 99**
    (the calibration/sensitivity orthogonality) — a channel that cannot be
    narrated cannot be mis-reported, which removes one whole class of the §8
    inversion at the source. It also constrains **Area 33** (a detector is not a
    certifier): D.F. is a system that *detected* correctly and *could not
    certify* in the same breath. **Area 73** (phenomenal vs access) is the
    neighbouring area and is not this one — that one is about what is *available*,
    this one is about what is *expressible*.

- **Rank 2 — Reach and grasp are different control problems and want different
  controller architectures.** `LOW`. Support: **1 source**, one species.
  - **Brain part:** reach and grasp, performed together, using **distinct neural
    dynamics** — M1 population activity during reach shows strong rotational
    structure, during grasp it does not. Trajectory-driven and continuous
    versus object-specific and discrete.
  - **Design:** do not build one action head. Continuous, trajectory-shaped
    operations (a sweep across the corpus, a batch pass, a file read) and
    discrete, selection-shaped operations (which file, which area, which slot)
    are **different problems wearing one interface**, and the corpus's own
    tool-selection work (**Area 22**) is a grasp problem being solved with reach
    machinery.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Support: **1 source** — one *eLife* study, cited without
    full authorship in the file, plus a 2025 *Science Advances* paper
    (arXiv-style, no ID) on the same split. Recorded as **two results from one
    file**, not two independent sources.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 22** — this is that area's design problem stated in
    neural terms. **Area 91** (the cortex as a stack of time windows) supplies
    the timescale half.

- **Rank 3 — Apparent variability is mostly task-irrelevant dimensions, and
  treating it as noise is a modelling error.** `LOW`. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Brain part:** **stochastic optimal feedback control** (Todorov & Jordan
    2002, *Nature Neuroscience* 5(11): 1226–1235; Todorov 2004) — the motor
    system does **not** pre-plan a fixed trajectory; it computes a feedback
    controller specifying the optimal response to *any* perturbation.
    Movement looks variable because **the variability is in the dimensions the
    task does not constrain, while the task-relevant dimensions are tightly
    controlled.** Fitts' law and the speed-accuracy trade-off fall out of the
    optimality principle rather than being imposed on it.
  - **Design:** an evaluation that scores run-to-run variance as instability is
    **scoring the wrong dimension.** Declare which dimensions are
    task-relevant, hold those, and treat variance outside them as evidence the
    controller is not over-fitted. For this workspace the concrete case is
    `duration_seconds` in `RUNLOG.jsonl` and the arena's own line counts: both
    are heavily constrained by task, and both vary. **This is the reason a
    fixed schema is not a correctness criterion.**
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Support: **1 source**, one theoretical programme, plus
    one *eLife* result. The theory is well-replicated in its own literature and
    **`ARENA.md` held 0 occurrences of *Todorov*, *optimal feedback*, or
    *task-irrelevant variance* before this pass** — it was a gap, not a
    duplicate.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 41** (latency as a capacity law) and **Area 89**
    (expertise as a measurable process whose curve is an averaging artefact) —
    both of which can be re-read through "which dimension was constrained."

### Area 103 · The clock is a chemical, and it runs on a circuit that can be argued with `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/neural-dynamics/neurotransmitter-systems-and-modulation.md`
(ORDER 1275, 411 lines) with its twin `oracle/brain/Neural-Dynamics/Neurotransmitter-Systems-and-Modulation.md`
(ORDER 1276, 428 lines) as the **corrected** copy of the same document. **One
source published twice — counted once** (infra C-047).

**The brain part.** **Neuromodulatory tone as a global state variable with
its own anatomy, its own receptor pharmacology, and its own timescales** —
dopamine (VTA/SNc, phasic ms / tonic seconds), serotonin (raphe, seconds to
minutes), acetylcholine (basal forebrain, ms to minutes), norepinephrine
(locus coeruleus, seconds to minutes), all broadcasting by **volume
transmission** through extracellular fluid to extrasynaptic receptors rather than
by point-to-point synaptic release.

**Why this is a hundred-and-third structural silence, and it is the sharpest
one in the arena.** **Area 75** says the four neuromodulators carry four
different uncertainty quantities and must not share a knob. This file says
**two of them point in opposite directions and the arena has no way to express
that**: 5-HT2C receptors on VTA dopamine neurons **inhibit** dopamine release
(Dong et al. 2012, *J Neurosci* 32(30): 10189–10196; **~40% reduction in
dopamine neuron firing**), so serotonin is not a fourth channel alongside
dopamine — it is **a brake on the dopamine channel.** A four-column schema
cannot represent "this quantity suppresses that quantity," and Area 75's rank 1
design does not ask for it to.

- **Rank 1 — Modulators get signs and targets, not just magnitudes.** `LOW`.
  Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1275/1276 as one).
  - **Brain part:** the **serotonin→dopamine antagonism** (5-HT2C on VTA DA
    neurons) and the general finding that neuromodulators **interact rather
    than stack** — LC→PFC NE enhances cholinergic response via α1, a positive
    feedback loop that amplifies attentional capture during salient events.
  - **Design:** every modulator field carries a **signed effect on a named
    target**, not a scalar. "ACh high" is not a statement until it says *high
    ACh raises gain on bottom-up evidence and lowers it on top-down priors*
    (Yu & Dayan 2005, *Neuron* 46(1): 45–64 — the file reports the predictions
    were borne out: nicotine reduces the cue-validity effect ~25%, scopolamine
    increases it). **The arena's `confidence:` field has no sign and no target,
    which is why Area 75's four-column split is necessary but not sufficient.**
    Scale assumption (R-J5): at 14,589 files a single scalar per row cannot
    express a modulating relationship between two rows, and the relationships
    are the part that is load-bearing.
  - **Already exists:** nothing. No service carries a signed inter-channel
    effect. **Schema change, not a build.**
  - **Backup document:** `neurotransmitter-systems-and-modulation.md`
    (ORDER 1275; twin ORDER 1276 carries the corrections).
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Support: **1 source** for the file, but the underlying
    result is replicated across independent methods — optogenetics (Dong et al.
    2012), pharmacology (Eagle et al. 2008, *d* ≈ 0.6 probability discounting,
    *d* ≈ 0.4 delay discounting), and single-unit recording. **The
    disagreement is preserved (R-J4):** the file's own §"Serotonin's
    Specificity Problem" records that serotonin's role is *"extraordinarily
    broad"* and that its theories *"are post-hoc explanations"* — the
    Cools–Fletcher aversive-uncertainty account is elegant and the evidence is
    *"mixed across tasks."* **The mechanism holding; the assignment is not.**
  - **Interaction:** **Area 75** is this area's own rank 1 and is **incomplete
    without it** — this is the amendment, not a replacement. **Area 50**
    (wanting without liking) is the same dissociation at the level of reward
    rather than state. **Area 80** (rule vs value are different circuits) has an
    antagonist here that the "different circuits" framing does not cover.

- **Rank 2 — Phasic and tonic are different timescales of the same channel,
  and conflating them destroys the signal.** `LOW`. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Brain part:** the **D1/D2 affinity asymmetry** — D2-like receptors carry
    **10–100× higher dopamine affinity** than D1-like, so **tonic** levels
    preferentially engage D2 (autoreceptor feedback, state setting) while
    **phasic** bursts preferentially engage D1 (learning, plasticity). The
    same transmitter, two timescales, two different jobs, separated by
    affinity rather than by wiring.
  - **Design:** a learning-rate field and a state field are **one channel read at
    two timescales**, not two channels. Any arena record that carries a
    "learned from this" flag and a "current confidence" is already carrying both
    — and currently has no way to say which one it is reading.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Support: **1 source** (Grace et al. 2007; Baik 2013).
    **Recorded qualification:** the file's own §"Phasic/Tonic Oversimplification"
    states that single-unit recordings show complex task-dependent firing that
    **does not fit binary categories**, and that some neurons show
    context-dependent switching. **The clean two-mode model is the part that is
    `LOW`; the affinity asymmetry underneath it is the load-bearing datum.**
  - **Interaction:** **Area 66** (metaplasticity — a learning rate that is itself
    a function of recent activity) is the same idea one level up, and this is
    the mechanism Area 66 lacks. **Area 91** (cortex as a stack of time
    windows) supplies the multi-timescale frame.

- **Rank 3 — `UNSUCCESSFUL`, narrowly scoped: a four-parameter agent does not
  come with a conflict-resolution rule.** `UNTESTED`.
  - **Brain part:** the **open question the file itself leads with** — *"How do
    the four neuromodulatory systems resolve conflicts? When dopamine signals
    'explore for reward' but serotonin signals 'avoid due to uncertainty,' how
    does the brain resolve this? Is there a meta-controller, or is resolution
    emergent from the interaction dynamics?"* **The file answers it nowhere.**
  - **Design:** the corpus's own "Integrated Agent Architecture" — four
    parameters producing adaptive behaviour — **specifies the parameters and
    omits the arbiter**, and presents the omission as a feature (*"without
    explicit environmental models"*). This slot records that as a **gap in the
    design, not a virtue in it.** Rank 3 because the honest entry is that the
    mechanism the four-channel design needs **has not been located**, and a
    `UNSUCCESSFUL` recorded narrowly beats a fourth slot that implies otherwise.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` on any build. Support: **1 source**, and that source
    lists it as unanswered.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 34** (control-mode allocation — which controller is in
    charge, and what tips the balance) is **exactly the missing arbiter**, arrived
    at from the behavioural side. The two areas should be read together and the
    gap is in neither.

### Area 104 · Time is a rhythm with a phase, and a slot is a phase `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Neural-Oscillations/Hippocampal-Theta-Gamma-Coupling-Mechanisms.md`
(ORDER 1278, 304 lines) §§"The Theta-Gamma Coding Scheme", "Measurement Challenges",
"Replication Status and Criticisms", with **three independent supporting files**:
ORDER 1274 (`Neural-Oscillations-and-Synchrony.md`, 241 lines), ORDER 1277
(`Theta-Oscillations-in-Cognition.md`, 231 lines), and ORDER 1280
(`Neural-Oscillations-and-Brain-Waves.md`, 427 lines). **Four files, four
independent reads.**

**The brain part.** **Cross-frequency coupling** — the **phase of a slow
oscillation modulating the amplitude of a fast one** (phase-amplitude coupling,
PAC), most concretely the **theta-gamma code** (Lisman & Jensen 2013, *Neuron*
77(6): 1002–1016): a ~6 Hz theta cycle (~167 ms) contains **4–7 gamma cycles**
(~40 Hz, ~25 ms each), each gamma cycle carrying **one** working-memory item,
and **theta phase selects which item is live.** The code is *addressing by
phase* — the location in a cycle **is** the pointer.

**Why this is a hundred-and-fourth structural silence.** **Area 38** asks how
much can be held at once and gets the right answer (*"four"*, and the arena has
long known the number is lab-dependent). This area says **the number is not a
property of a buffer at all — it is a ratio of two clocks**, and a buffer with
no clock has no capacity to lose. `ARENA.md` held **0** occurrences of *CFC*,
*cross-frequency coupling*, *phase-amplitude*, *phase precession*, or
*theta-gamma* before this pass. Four files on the topic, none of them read.

- **Rank 1 — An item's address is its phase, and the address decays on a
  schedule.** `LOW` on the mechanism, `UNTESTED` on the build.
  - **Brain part:** PAC as **temporal multiplexing with a decaying address.**
    The addressing is not an index into a store — it is a position in a cycle,
    which means it is **only valid while the cycle is running** and must be
    re-earned. Human theta phase-locking at the **peak** precedes eye fixations
    to *retrieved* locations, while **trough** locking *follows* fixations to
    *novel* ones (ORDER 1277) — **the same oscillator addresses recall and
    encoding at opposite phases**, which is why they do not interfere.
  - **Design:** workspace slots carry **a phase, not an offset**, and a slot
    whose phase has passed is **stale, not merely old**. This gives a
    principled answer to a question Area 41 and Area 96 both gesture at and
    neither resolves: *how does a system know a working-memory item is no longer
    trustworthy?* — **its address has moved.** Scale assumption (R-J5): at
    14,589 files, an offset-addressed buffer's staleness is a heuristic
    (timestamp, last-touched); a phase-addressed one makes staleness
    **structural**.
  - **Already exists:** MemGPT/letta's tiered memory is the corpus's own stated
    analogue (ORDER 1278: theta organizing gamma parallels core organizing
    recall/archival), and **oscillatory RNNs** exist as a research line
    (ORDER 1278 §Computational Models, Siegel/Buschman/Miller 2015) — but
    neither implements phase *addressing*; they implement hierarchy. **Adopt
    the tiering, build the phase.**
  - **Backup documents:** `Hippocampal-Theta-Gamma-Coupling-Mechanisms.md`
    (ORDER 1278) §§"The Theta-Gamma Coding Scheme", "Replication Status and
    Criticisms"; `Theta-Oscillations-in-Cognition.md` (ORDER 1277) §§"Two
    Modes of Midfrontal Theta", "Theta in Working Memory"; corroborated in reach
    by ORDER 1274 §"Cross-Frequency Coupling" and ORDER 1280 §4.
  - **Grade:** `LOW` on the mechanism — the slow/fast gamma functional split
    (Colgin 2015), the encoding/retrieval phase segregation, and the
    human EEG/MEG/intracranial convergence are three independent lines.
    **`UNTESTED`** on the build, which is the arena's own invention. **Three
    qualifications carried on the slot, not averaged away (R-J4):** (1) the
    capacity claim does not survive contact with **Cowan's revised 3–4**, not
    Miller's 7±2; (2) **Lopez-Peredo et al. 2019** report **"gamma reversal"** —
    at some sites gamma peaks at the *opposite* theta phase from the model;
    (3) **Lundqvist, Herman & Miller 2018** argue the scheme is *"Delay activity,
    yes! Persistent activity? Maybe not"* and that theta-gamma coupling is an
    **epiphenomenon** that realistic circuit models produce with no functional
    role assigned.
  - **Interaction:** **upstream of Area 38** — this is that area's answer to
    "four," and it should be read as amending it rather than competing.
    **Area 23** (consolidation) is directly threatened by qualification (3).
    **Area 39** (coincidence detection and the veto) is the same
    phase-alignment idea at cellular resolution.

- **Rank 2 — `UNSUCCESSFUL`, and the corpus says so in bold: the spindle-memory
  link failed a 53-study meta-analysis.** `UNSUCCESSFUL`. Support: **2
  independent files** (ORDER 1274, ORDER 1280), same primary source.
  - **Brain part:** **sleep spindles** (11–16 Hz, thalamic reticular nucleus ×
    thalamic relay) and their claimed role in consolidation. **Ujma (2024,
    *Neuropsychologia* 198: 108886)** meta-analysed **53 studies** and found
    *"no relationship between sleep spindles and memory"* and concluded spindles
    are *"unlikely to be generally implicated in learning and plasticity."*
    The corpus records this as **"one of the most significant recent
    controversies in the field"** and does not bury it.
  - **Design:** **this is the slot's whole content.** Any consolidation design in
    this arena that leans on spindle-count as a proxy for learning has inherited
    a **failed effect**. The surviving alternative is stated in the same
    paragraph: **cross-frequency coupling, not spindle density** — Hyafil et al.
    (2015) argue the field must move from counting spindles to measuring CFC.
    A `UNSUCCESSFUL` recorded so a dead end is not rebuilt blind.
  - **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL` — **scoped to the density proxy, not to
    consolidation.** The 53 studies were meta-analysed; the null is the
    finding. **Disagreement preserved:** the corpus also reports that
    intracranial and targeted studies continue to find strong links, and that
    the dispute may be methodological (scalp vs intracranial, detection
    algorithm, task type). **Both sides stand.**
  - **Interaction:** **Area 6** and **Area 23** (consolidation) — this slot is
    the check neither currently runs. **Area 76** (verification has a ceiling) is
    the same epistemic shape: a cheap proxy outliving the thing it proxied.

- **Rank 3 — A binding theory that a mechanism critique has not yet killed, and
  whose own artefact list is the reason to distrust it.** `LOW` on the
  phenomenon, `UNSUCCESSFUL` on the current evidence. Support: **2 files**
  (ORDER 1274, ORDER 1280) plus ORDER 1278.
  - **Brain part:** **binding by synchrony** — Gray, König, Engel & Singer
    (1989, *Nature* 338: 334–337) recorded 40 Hz coherence between spatially
    separated neurons responding to the *same* stimulus and not to *different*
    stimuli, suggesting an internally generated mechanism for binding features
    into one percept.
  - **Design:** the design consequence is **negative and it is the slot.** The
    corpus's own artefact list for CFC — **filter leakage** from bands too
    close together, **non-sinusoidal waveforms** (hippocampal theta is a
    sawtooth, and its harmonics fall in the gamma band, manufacturing PAC with
    no coupling present), **amplitude-amplitude coupling** masquerading as PAC,
    and **volume conduction** creating artifactual zero-lag EEG correlation —
    is **the same artefact list as §8's, in a different domain**: a
    validated-looking signal produced by the analysis pipeline rather than by
    the thing analysed. **Burns, Xing & Shapley (2011)** went further: V1 gamma
    *"may be a clock or filtered noise."* A system adopting phase routing
    inherits a measurement whose artefacts are indistinguishable from its signal
    by the standard method.
  - **Grade:** `LOW` on the phenomenon, replicated and famous. **`UNSUCCESSFUL`**
    on the current evidence for a *computational* role — the file's own Open
    Question 2 concedes that after 35+ years it is unknown whether this is *"a
    genuine solution or a compelling but ultimately incorrect framework."*
    **That sentence is the slot.**
  - **Interaction:** **Area 33** (a detector is not a certifier) and **Area 76**
    (verification ceiling) — this is a third instance of the pattern after the
    area's own two, and the third to arrive from a different literature. **Area
    53** (cross-modal binding) is the neighbouring area; this is binding by a
    different proposed mechanism.

### Area 105 · What a representation encodes is the *relations* between things, and there is a costed method for checking that without a unit-for-unit correspondence — which also has a measured failure rate `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `Neuroimaging-Methods/Representational-Similarity-Analysis-Geometry.md`
(ORDER 1288, 387 lines, read whole) §§"Why RSA matters", "Critical perspective",
"Relationship to CKA and CCA", "Known pitfalls and limitations", "Agent
architecture parallels", "Implications for Autognosia".
**One source, not two:** `neuroimaging-methods/representational-similarity-analysis-geometry.md`
(ORDER 1287) is the **same document at a second path** — identical frontmatter
`id: representational-similarity-analysis-geometry`, identical `aliases` line, and
the same 30-item source list compressed into 3,736 characters. The lowercase and
capitalised directories are twins. **Counted once.**

**The brain part.** **The representational geometry of a neural population** —
Kriegeskorte, Mur & Bandettini (2008), *Front. Syst. Neurosci.* 2:4,
DOI 10.3389/neuro.06.004.2008. The corpus states the part as a *problem the
brain forces on any comparison*: a model's hidden units are *"abstract
mathematical variables"* while a brain's sensors *"sample populations of neurons
through a complex spatiotemporal transform"* (fMRI's hemodynamics alone). Direct
comparison of activity values *"is often meaningless."* What is comparable is the
**shape of the representational space** — a Representational Dissimilarity Matrix,
whose cells hold the pairwise dissimilarity between response patterns, and whose
structure is called **second-order isomorphism** (Shepard & Chipman, 1970).
Crucially this is not a convenience: the same corpus reports **hyperalignment**
(Haxby et al. 2011, *Neuron* 72(1):102; Haxby et al. 2020, *eLife* 9:e56601) showing
the shared information across brains is *"encoded in vector geometry"* while
*"the specific anatomical topography is idiosyncratic"* — cortical architecture
is what Haxby calls a **degenerate system**, the same information realised in
many different topographies.

**Why this is a hundred-and-fifth structural silence.** Every verification area
the arena holds — **Area 33** (a detector is not a certifier), **Area 76**
(verification has a ceiling), **Area 24** (the validity of one's own instruments),
**Area 99** (a famous pattern replicated and the explanation not), **Area 89**
(the curve everyone quotes is an averaging artefact) — adjudicates **outputs, and
in most cases aggregates**. This area is the one that compares the thing *before*
the output. Its load-bearing property is the one no other area can express: a
system can be **functionally equivalent with a completely different internal
representation**, and the two are indistinguishable on any output-based check the
arena currently possesses. At 14,589 files (R-J5) that is not a nicety — it is
the difference between "the answer came out the same" and "the system organised
the problem the same way."

- **Rank 1 — Second-order comparison for the questions that first-order
  comparison cannot reach, and *not* for the ones it can.** `LOW` on the
  method, `UNTESTED` on the build. Support: **1 source** (one file, two paths).
  - **Brain part:** the RDM as *"the fingerprint of the representational
    geometry"*, and the correspondence problem it dissolves.
  - **Design:** the arena's reachability check is currently *"did retrieval
    return the cited file"* — a first-order, unit-for-unit question with an
    unambiguous right answer. This slot says some of the arena's real questions
    are **second-order**: *is the partition of the corpus into pages the same
    partition the file's own subject matter implies*; *do two subsystems that
    must agree encode the same distinctions*; *is this module's notion of
    similarity the module's neighbour's notion of similarity*. None is answerable
    by checking outputs, because the two systems are free to reach the same
    output by different routes — which is exactly the property Haxby's
    hyperalignment documents in the biological case.
  - **Already exists?** Adopt, do not build. The corpus names the artefacts:
    **`rsa` / RSA toolbox in PyMVPA** (Nili et al. 2014, *Front. Neuroinform.*
    8:4), **`neurora`** (Lu & Ku 2020, *Front. Neuroinform.* 14:55, `pip
    install neurora`), **`MNE-RSA`** (van Vliet et al. 2025, *JOSS* 10(116):9148).
    For the *benchmarking* variant that is closest to what this arena wants, two
    standing leaderboards already score model-to-brain alignment by RSA:
    **Algonauts Project** (MIT, 2019, `algonauts.csail.mit.edu/2019`) and
    **Brain-Score** (Schrimpf et al. 2018, bioRxiv 407007). Nothing in `brain/`,
    `hooks/`, `plugins/`, profiles, skills, cron or adapters in the Hermes stack
    computes an RDM.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1288, cited above. ORDER 1287 is the same
    document and is deliberately **not** counted as corroboration.
  - **Grade:** `LOW` on the method. It is a formalised, published, widely
    adopted framework with 17 years of use, but the corpus's own §"Critical
    perspective" records **three published attacks on it** (below), and the
    honest grade is not the one its `confidence: high` frontmatter claims —
    which per §8 is an artifact grade and is counted for nothing. `UNTESTED` on
    the build: the arena has never applied an RDM to itself, so the transfer is
    the arena's own invention with no trial.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 33** — a detector is not a certifier; this is the
    same asymmetry one level earlier, and the pairing is exact. **Area 76** — a
    verification ceiling that a better detector does not close; a ceiling on what
    any output comparison can establish. **Area 24** — attribution and whether
    the measurement measured anything: an RDM is a *derived* quantity, and the
    corpus is explicit that its elements *"are not statistically independent."*
    **Area 99** — a famous pattern replicated and the explanation not, which is
    precisely the shape of the equivalence problem below. **Area 89** — the
    averaging artefact; RSA has an analogue of its own in searchlight windows.
    **Constrained by Area 41** (latency as a capacity law) and **Area 38**
    (workspace capacity): an RDM over *N* conditions is an *N×N* matrix, and at
    14,589 files *N* is not a number any system can hold — see Rank 2.

- **Rank 2 — The flexibility problem is the reason a single alignment score
  cannot rank designs, and it is a measured result, not a suspicion.**
  `LOW`. Support: **1 source** (the same file, a distinct finding).
  - **Brain part:** the **degeneracy of the representational code** — the same
    information is realisable in many topographies, so a geometry match is
    satisfied by many non-equivalent systems.
  - **Design:** the negative consequence, and it is the slot's whole content.
    **Conwell et al. (2024, *Nat. Commun.* 15:9383)** find many top-ranked DNNs
    are *"effectively equivalent"* in RSA scores and call it the **flexibility
    problem**: RSA is *"too flexible, accepting many different geometries as
    equally good fits."* The corpus states the consequence without softening it:
    RSA is *"too flexible"* to determine *"which aspect causes alignment."* A
    system that adopted Rank 1 naively would report a high score for a design
    that is wrong for reasons the score cannot see — **§8's inversion, with the
    score now on the side of the artefact.** The corpus names the two candidate
    repairs, and both are adoptable: **APA / Alignment Pattern Analysis**
    (Feather et al. 2025), which requires reproducing not just alignment to one
    ROI but the **cross-region alignment profile** — how that ROI relates to all
    the others; and the **NeuroAI Turing Test** (Feather et al. 2025), which
    scores model-to-brain alignment against **brain-to-brain** alignment as the
    threshold, so a system is judged against the ceiling of the biology rather
    than against an absolute number.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. A single named 2024 study with a named remedy, on a
    phenomenon the corpus reports as general.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 99** is the same epistemic shape — a measure that
    confirms many things equally is not yet a discriminator. **Area 33** again.

- **Rank 3 — `UNSUCCESSFUL` where the corpus says so: for first-order model
  selection, RSA is *worse* than plain regression, and the arena should not
  reach for it there.** `UNSUCCESSFUL`. Support: **1 source** (the same file, a
  third distinct finding).
  - **Brain part:** not a brain part but a **measurement boundary** — the point
    at which the second-order abstraction stops paying. Recorded because §5
    requires a slot to say when a thing does *not* apply, and because a
    `UNSUCCESSFUL` recorded here is cheaper than a wrong build.
  - **Design:** **Gao et al. (2025)** ran **1,000 simulated datasets per
    condition** (N = 100–500, noise σ² = 5–15, features p = 20–60) and found RSA
    *"consistently yields lower model selection accuracy than linear
    regression across all conditions"* — because *"the transformation to
    second-order similarity space inevitably loses direct stimulus–response
    information."* PCA preprocessing and feature reweighting did not rescue it.
    The corpus's resolution is the design rule: **RSA and regression are
    complementary, not ranked** — *"RSA for second-order relational comparison,
    regression for first-order prediction."* **A second, softer failure is
    recorded from Dujmović et al. (2022):** structured noise (temporal
    autocorrelation in fMRI, sensor-level correlations in EEG) can make RSA
    produce *contradictory* findings unless explicitly controlled.
  - **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL` for the first-order application, on a
    1,000-dataset-per-condition simulation. This is the highest-powered negative
    result in the file and it is the reason Rank 1 is scoped rather than
    universal.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 36** (the specification gap — the objective is a
    proxy, and the proxy is what gets optimized) is the *identical* failure in
    the design domain: a lossy transform that silently becomes the target.
    **Area 41** and **Area 38** bound the RDM's own size at corpus scale.

**Three things this file supplies that the arena was getting wrong, and one it
confirms.** *(1)* The corpus's own §"Implications for Autognosia" — the
project-local section, i.e. **prose, not a generated artifact**, which is what
makes it eligible evidence under §8 — states the operational split directly:
*"RSA's finding that it is less accurate than regression for model selection when
direct mappings are available suggests using **regression for retrieval ranking
and RSA for evaluating knowledge base structure**."* That is this arena's own
conclusion, already written by the corpus, and the arena had not adopted it.
*(2)* **Mahner et al. (2025, *Nat. Mach. Intell.* 7:848–859)** find humans show
**semantic dominance** in their representational dimensions while DNNs show
**visual bias** — a dissociation that a *behavioural* check cannot produce and an
output-accuracy check cannot see. *(3)* **Williams (2024, PMLR)** shows **CKA
and RSA are equivalent once mean-centering is incorporated**, so CKA need not be
implemented separately. *(4)* Confirmed and reinforced, not newly earned: the
corpus's §8 inversion appears here in a fourth form — **a similarity score
produced by the analysis pipeline can be high for a model that is not
brain-like**, which the corpus itself flags as *"tension: biological fidelity vs.
engineering utility."*

### Area 106 · Neuromodulation is four *meta-parameters of one learning
algorithm*, not four separate channels — and the file that says so also names
the circularity that invalidates most of the evidence for it `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `Neuromodulatory-Gain/Neuromodulatory-Gain-Control-in-Cortical-Circuits.md`
(ORDER 1295, 373 lines, read whole) §§"Interaction Architecture: Meta-Learning
Loop", "Agent Architecture Implications", "6. Circular Inference in Computational
Modeling", "Replication Status", and
`Neuromodulation-Systems/Locus-Coeruleus-NE-Network-Reset.md` (ORDER 1292, 288
lines, read whole) §§"Active Inference Unification", "Pupil Diameter Is Not a
Real-Time LC Readout", "Modularity vs. Global Broadcast Tension".

**The brain part.** **The ascending neuromodulatory nuclei as a set of
meta-parameter controllers over one learning process** — Doya (2002), *Neural
Networks* 15(4–6):495–506 places the four systems on the four hyperparameters of
a single RL agent: **ACh → α** (the learning rate), **NE → the exploration
temperature**, **DA → δ** (the TD error), **5-HT → γ** (the discount factor /
temporal horizon). The corpus states the mapping as a table and calls it *"the
biological substrate for meta-learning — the dynamic adjustment of learning
hyperparameters."* Yu & Dayan (2005) supply the Bayesian derivation of two of
the four (ACh = expected uncertainty, NE = unexpected uncertainty), Aston-Jones &
Cohen (2005) the adaptive-gain account of NE, and Miyazaki et al. (2011) the
direct electrophysiology for 5-HT as *patience* — DRN neurons fire tonically
**during waiting for a delayed reward** and *"did not respond to reward
omission, falsifying the 'aversive prediction error' hypothesis."*

**Why this is a hundred-and-sixth structural silence, and why it is not Area
75.** Area 75 holds four neuromodulators as four **uncertainty quantities** and
Area 103 holds their **signs and antagonisms**. Neither holds the thing this file
makes load-bearing: that the four are **knobs on one algorithm**, that the arena's
`confidence:` field is at best one of the four, and — decisively — that **the
evidence for the whole mapping is substantially circular and the corpus says so
in its own methods section.**

- **Rank 1 — Four meta-parameters, one learner: name them as the knobs they
  are, not as four new subsystems.** `LOW` on the biology, `UNTESTED` on the
  build. Support: **2 independent files** (ORDER 1295, ORDER 1292).
  - **Brain part:** the Doya mapping above — the brainstem nuclei (LC, DRN,
    VTA/SNc, basal forebrain) as **the meta-learners**, and the
    cortico-basal-ganglia circuits as **the base learners** they tune.
  - **Design:** this is an amendment to Area 75 rather than a rival, and the
    amendment is a reduction. Area 75 rank 1 proposes splitting one `confidence:`
    field into four new columns. This slot says the four are not new storage —
    they are **α, β/τ, δ and γ of a learning process the arena partly has**,
    and the correct design is that whatever learns from outcomes must expose
    four *independently settable* parameters, with **two of them already
    existing under other names**: δ is the critic's error term, γ is the
    scheduler's discount (and **Area 101** already holds the finding that
    *"the scheduler's discount function cannot represent the thing it
    schedules"*). The load-bearing line is the file's own: *"An agent
    architecture with a single 'dopamine' bus feeding both its planner and its
    habit system would mis-tune one of them by construction"* (ORDER 1290,
    paraphrasing the same constraint from the dopaminergic side).
  - **Already exists?** The hyperparameters exist in any standard RL stack
    (`lr`, `tau`/`temperature`, the critic's δ, `gamma`). The *wiring that
    computes them from the system's own statistics* does not: nothing in `brain/`,
    `hooks/`, `plugins/`, profiles, skills, cron or adapters derives a learning
    rate from observed volatility or a discount factor from observed delay
    statistics. Doya's framing is that these are **computed by specialised
    nuclei monitoring statistical properties of interaction history** — an
    agent builds the monitors, not the constants.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1295 and ORDER 1292, cited above.
  - **Grade:** `LOW` on the mapping. The four-way assignment is
    independently replicated across the two files and across modalities (rodent
    pharmacology, primate single-unit, human tryptophan depletion — Tanaka et
    al. 2007 report **Δγ = −0.045, 0.867 → 0.822, p = 0.009**), and ORDER 1295
    carries an explicit per-channel replication table. `UNTESTED` on the build.
  - **Interaction:** **amends Area 75** (four quantities on one algorithm, not
    four columns) and **Area 103** (the signs). **Area 101** — γ already exists
    and already has a known representational limit. **Area 66** —
    metaplasticity: α as a function of recent activity is the same idea as the
    BCM sliding threshold, and ORDER 1300 supplies the equation.

- **Rank 2 — The mapping's own evidence is circular, and a design that adopts
  it inherits the circularity unless it breaks it deliberately.**
  `UNSUCCESSFUL` for the corroboration route; `LOW` for the phenomenon. Support:
  **1 source** (ORDER 1295, §6).
  - **Brain part:** not a brain part — a **methodological defect in the
    literature**, recorded as a first-class result because it is the only thing
    that would let a wrong version of Rank 1 ship with confidence.
  - **Design:** ORDER 1295 §6, verbatim in substance: *"Many studies fit RL
    models to behavior, then correlate fitted meta-parameters (α, γ, β) with
    neuromodulatory proxies (pupil, pharmacology). This risks circularity: the
    model assumes the neuromodulator controls that parameter, then 'confirms'
    it."* The corpus names the fix — **true validation requires independent
    manipulation of the neuromodulator and measurement of the resulting change in
    the fitted parameter** — and names how rarely it has been done: achieved for
    **NE/learning rate** (Jepma et al. 2016), *"but rare for other systems."*
    **For this arena the consequence is direct and it is the same shape as §8:**
    a fitted parameter, validated against a proxy, is an artifact of the model
    plus the proxy, and the agreement between them is the *pipeline's* output,
    not the brain's. A design that infers "the system is uncertain" from a
    quantity the model fit to explain behaviour has measured its own fit.
  - **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL` **for the corroboration route** — a route that
    produced a confident-looking literature and cannot have produced knowledge.
    The underlying biological findings are unaffected and stay `LOW`.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 24** (the validity of one's own instruments) and
    **Area 33** (a detector is not a certifier) are the same argument in a
    general form. **Area 44** (self-diagnosis by parameter recovery) is
    *directly endangered* by this slot: fitting a generative model to your own
    behaviour and reading the parameters back is exactly the move this section
    shows is circular unless the parameter is perturbed independently.

- **Rank 3 — The proxy that measures the controller is not the controller, and
  the arena already has a name for the shape of that mistake.**
  `UNSUCCESSFUL` for the proxy; `LOW` for the underlying fact. Support: **2
  files** (ORDER 1292, ORDER 1295 — the two arrive at it independently).
  - **Brain part:** the **locus coeruleus**, and the distinction between its
    firing and a cheap readout of it.
  - **Design:** in a human, LC activity is read through **pupil diameter**.
    **Yang et al. (2022, *eLife* 11:e70510)** established that this is a valid
    *population-averaged* index of LC mode and **not** a trial-level readout:
    only large pupil events (>1.5–2 SD, **<10% of events**) reach d′ ≥ 1 for
    predicting LC spike counts; identical optogenetic LC stimulation produces
    large session-to-session pupil fluctuation *"despite stable LC output"*; and
    the coupling is **state-dependent, not a fixed calibration**. Joshi et al.
    (2016) add that the correlation is **not LC-specific** — inferior colliculus,
    superior colliculus and cingulate show similar pupil-coupled activity, merely
    delayed. ORDER 1292's conclusion is that studies using pupil as a
    moment-to-moment LC readout *"likely overinterpret single-trial precision."*
    **The design consequence is a rule, and the arena already has the pattern in
    Area 31 (per-episode cost) and §8:** *a cheap proxy outliving the thing it
    proxied.* The proxy is admissible for population-level mode detection and
    inadmissible for single-episode claims, and no amount of agreement upgrades
    it. Note the additional source of the error that the file names and that
    generalises: **"LC+"** recordings include the adjacent subcoeruleus, which
    contains NE and non-NE neurons, so the measurement may attribute to LC
    proper what belongs to a neighbour.
  - **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL` for the single-trial inference, on a dedicated
    optogenetic calibration study. `LOW` for the population-level index, which
    the corpus calls valid.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 44** again, and **Area 89** (the averaging
    artefact — a population-level result is not a per-episode one).
    **Area 87** (performance and learning come apart, and the feeling tracks the
    wrong one) is the same lesson at the level of self-report: what the system
    can cheaply observe about itself is not what it is.

### Area 107 · The plastic variable is the *existence* of a connection, not its
weight — and a weight is a thing that can be created `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `Neuroplasticity/Structural-Plasticity-Dendritic-Spine-Turnover.md`
(ORDER 1305, 243 lines, read whole) §§"Formation, Elimination, and Selective
Stabilization", "Clustered plasticity: the branch as storage unit", "The
longitudinal imaging revolution", "Learning-induced spinogenesis", "Causal
tests", "The intrinsic-volatility objection", "Engram-spine dissociations",
"Two-tier storage as an architecture pattern", and §§"Methodological Notes &
Disputes".

**The brain part.** **Structural plasticity** — the creation, elimination and
stabilization of the synaptic *contact itself* by the dendritic spine, which
hosts the large majority of excitatory synapses in the mammalian forebrain.
Kasai's group's lifecycle vocabulary is the load-bearing formulation: small,
motile, labile **"learning spines"** sample connectivity space at low cost;
large mushroom **"memory spines"** are structurally load-bearing for months to
years. The spine neck acts as a diffusion bottleneck isolating the head's
biochemistry, so two-photon glutamate uncaging at a single spine enlarges *only*
that spine — **input-specific plasticity at the single-synapse level** is a
compartmental fact, not a computational convenience.

**Why this is a hundred-and-seventh structural silence, and it is about the
unit of plasticity.** `ARENA.md` held **0** occurrences of *structural
plasticity*, *learning spine*, *memory spine*, or *spine turnover* across 106
areas, and exactly **1** occurrence of *spine* in any compound. Areas 6, 7, 27,
66, 93 and 97 all treat a **weight** as the addressable plastic quantity and a
**record** as the addressable stored item. **None of them holds that the
connection can be destroyed.** Every write in this arena is an overwrite; the
brain's is an option to allocate a new address, keep it, or retract it — and
the retraction is the operation with no counterpart here.

- **Rank 1 — Promote-on-reuse with selective structural commitment, gated by
  reactivation in an offline state.** `HIGH` on the mechanism's necessity;
  `UNTESTED` on the build. Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1305).
  - **Brain part:** the learning-spine → memory-spine lifecycle, with its
    three measured stages. (a) **Rapid formation within hours of learning**, in
    a stereotyped pattern — Xu et al. 2009 trained forelimb pellet reaching and
    found new spines on contralateral motor-cortex output neurons **within an
    hour**; Yang, Pan & Gan 2009 obtained the matching result on the rotarod.
    (b) **Selective stabilization of a minority** — roughly **half survived
    beyond eight days**, far above baseline, and **retention of performance
    correlated with the number of retained new spines**; different motor tasks
    recruited *different* sets of synapses, and **only novel tasks drove
    formation in adulthood**. (c) **Compensatory elimination** so total density
    returns to baseline — the budget is conserved, not merely spent.
  - **Design:** the arena already distinguishes the *functional* and *structural*
    gradient implicitly in Areas 6/23 (consolidation as a gated pass) and Area 97
    (architecture as the plastic variable). This slot makes the **promotion
    step itself selective and state-gated**: transient in-context activations
    are draft entries; promotion to a durable record happens on **reactivation
    during an offline/replay state**, and the corpus's own mechanistic support
    for the gate is the sharpest result in the file — **Yang et al. (Science
    2014)**: new spines concentrated on "high-frequency branches"; **seven
    hours of post-training sleep deprivation cut HFB formation nearly in half
    (4.9 ± 0.7% vs 9.3 ± 0.7%)**; REM deprivation did *not* disrupt the effect
    but disrupting **NREM reactivation did**, and corticosterone controls ruled
    out stress. The design consequence for this workspace is direct and it names
    an existing component: `ARENA-EVIDENCE.md` is the arena's consolidation pass
    and **`RUNLOG.jsonl` is its replay buffer**, so the gate is not hypothetical
    — it is the condition under which a ledger row is allowed to become a slot's
    `Backup document`. Scale assumption stated per R-J5: the class is *tranches*,
    not files, and it grows.
  - **Grade:** `HIGH` on necessity, and the grade is earned by a **causal**
    result rather than a correlation. **Hayashi-Takagi et al. (Nature 2015)**
    engineered AS-PaRac1 to label recently potentiated spines and permit
    light-induced shrinkage of exactly those spines: **shrinking
    learning-potentiated spines disrupted acquired motor performance, while
    shrinking an equal number of spines potentiated by a *different* task in the
    same cortical territory had no effect.** That is necessity *and* task
    specificity from one experiment — the arena's own Areas 33/35 test, applied
    to a substrate it does not have. `UNTESTED` on the build.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 6** and **Area 23** (consolidation) gain the
    *promotion* step they lacked. **Area 93** (the read is a write) is the
    inverse operation — suppression of the un-retrieved — and the two together
    say a retrieval pass both **promotes** its hits and **suppresses** its
    misses, which is a budget, not two independent effects. **Area 97** is the
    same idea one level up. **Area 24** is engaged by the measurement section
    below.

- **Rank 2 — Locality is a capacity mechanism, not a tidiness preference:
  co-locate, do not scatter.** `LOW`. Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1305, with
  the capacity arithmetic from Poirazi & Mel 1999 restated there).
  - **Brain part:** **dendritic clustering as the branch-level storage unit** —
    "dendritic hotspots" (Frank et al. 2018), new spines formed adjacent to
    functionally clustered task-active pre-existing synapses via filopodial
    outgrowth targeting existing boutons, selected for survival by co-activity
    with neighbours, and — the striking part — **a majority synapse onto axons
    previously unrepresented in that dendritic domain**, binding new input
    streams into existing clusters (Hedrick et al. 2022). The payoff is
    quantified: treating a pyramidal neuron as a **two-layer network**
    (spine-cluster → soma) raises storage capacity roughly **an order of
    magnitude** over point neurons, because features bound to one branch stay
    separable from features on another. Corroborating arithmetic in the same
    literature: capacity scales **~2^(m·k) for nonlinear subunits vs ~2^k for
    linear**, peaking at ~1,250 branches × 8 synapses — geometry itself computes.
  - **Design:** related items that will be potentiated *together* belong in the
    same **compartment** — same file, same topic cluster, same embedding
    neighbourhood — and this is a **write-time** decision, not a retrieval-time
    one. The corpus states the update policy in one line and the arena adopts
    it: **attach novel information adjacent to functionally clustered existing
    knowledge rather than scattering it.** This constrains Area 8's structural
    fidelity requirement and Area 86's frame discipline from a direction neither
    had: a *good* layout is one whose neighbours are jointly potentiated.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`, not `HIGH`, and the file's own measurement caveats are why
    — see rank 3. The capacity arithmetic is analytic (a model) and the imaging
    is rodent.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 8** (retain the surface chunk) and **Area 86** (name
    the frame) both become cheaper if locality is decided at write time.
    **Area 38** (workspace capacity) gets a second, non-symbolic account of the
    same ceiling: the branch is the unit, so a flat context window is losing a
    dimension the biology spends structure on.

- **Rank 3 — `UNSUCCESSFUL`: the spine is *necessary* for expression and has
  never been shown *sufficient* for a memory, and the connectivity account has a
  live volatility objection.** `UNSUCCESSFUL` for the strong reading;
  `LOW` for the necessary-but-not-sufficient statement. Support: **1 source**
  (ORDER 1305).
  - **Brain part:** the negative half of the engram story, and it is the half
    the arena needs. (a) **No sufficiency:** the strongest causal work shows
    spines necessary for expression/maintenance; **no experiment has shown a
    small set of identified spines to be individually sufficient**, nor tracked
    a human-scale memory to its structural substrate. (b) **Engram-spine
    dissociations:** dentate-gyrus engram-cell spine density **drops** between
    recent and remote timepoints while optogenetic engram activation still
    elicits freezing — structural weakening *without* behavioural forgetting;
    and auditory-cortex fear conditioning leaves a lasting spine trace while
    **recall triggers no new structural remodeling** (Moczulska et al.:
    ~10% of conditioning-formed spines persist; retrieval does not recapitulate
    encoding's structural signature). So the spine's state at retrieval can
    diverge from behavioural expression. (c) **The intrinsic-volatility
    objection** (Mongillo, Rumpel & Loewenstein 2017): synapses turn over
    continuously *without* learning, so decades-long retention requires pattern
    stability the substrate does not obviously have; the reconciling models
    (redundancy across many synapses; storage in molecular/epigenetic state
    that survives spine replacement) are **models, not results**. The Aplysia
    result sharpens it — long-term sensitization was behaviourally erased by
    reconsolidation blockade and PKM inhibition, *which also reversed
    training-induced synaptic growth*, yet **fully reinstated by reminder
    shocks**; the authors argue structure may *express* rather than *store*.
  - **Why this slot earns its rank by being a boundary rather than a design:** a
    design that treats a connection as the memory will be wrong in a specific,
    predictable way, and the file names the way: **storage in connectivity is
    falsifiable-but-unsettled.** Rank 1's promotion gate survives this — it does
    not claim the promoted spine is the memory, only that promotion is
    selective, gated and reversible in its early phase (Area 27's lability
    applies to the promoted record before it stabilizes).
  - **Interaction:** **Area 24** (validity of one's own instruments) has a
    matching entry in the same file, and it is the reason rank 3 is `UNSUCCESSFUL`
    rather than merely qualified: **published adult-cortex turnover figures span
    an order of magnitude** between top labs using nominally similar methods —
    Grutzendler ~96% one-month stability in visual cortex against
    Trachtenberg's substantially higher barrel-cortex turnover **in the same
    2002 issue of *Nature***; CA1 has three mutually inconsistent results
    (Attardo 2015 mean lifetime ~10 days after merged-spine correction; Gu 2014
    ~96% survival ≥16 days; Wegner 2018 ~40% four-day turnover via 2P-STED, with
    **double the previously reported spine density** and a merged-spine artefact
    measured at **23% of apparent single spines**). The file's own verdict: **no
    consensus resolution exists.** **A design that sizes a store from a
    turnover rate inherits a range, not a number.**
    **Area 27** and **Area 55** (correction by competition) both apply: the
    promoted spine is subject to the same reconsolidation window as the record.

### Area 108 · Conduction is a second, slow-timescale plastic substrate, and the
same molecule installs the path and writes-protects it `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by**
`Neuroplasticity/Skill-Myelination-Hardware-Acceleration-Paths.md` (ORDER 1304,
323 lines, read whole) §§"Neuronal Activity Drives OPC Proliferation",
"Myelin Remodeling Alters Conduction Velocity", "Myelin as a Critical-Period
Brake", §§1–7 of Key Research, "The FA ≠ Myelin: The Interpretation Crisis",
"Necessity vs. Sufficiency", "Adaptive Myelination as Homeostatic Gain Control",
"Hardware-Acceleration Mapping Table", and "The gut-brain-myelin axis".
Corroborated in reach by ORDER 1312 §V (myelination timeline, activity-dependent
OPC sensing) and ORDER 1303 §"Closure: Perineuronal Nets and Myelin".

**The brain part.** **Adaptive myelination** — activity-dependent remodeling of
myelin sheaths by oligodendrocytes, which functions as a *structural
acceleration layer* on a different timescale from synaptic weight change.
Oligodendrocyte precursor cells (NG2⁺/PDGFRα⁺) persist throughout the adult CNS
at **~5–10% of glial cells**, express neurotransmitter receptors (AMPA, NMDA,
kainate, GABAₐ, purinergic P1/P2) and so **sense local axonal firing
themselves**; three pathways link action potentials to myelin change —
glutamatergic (Ca²⁺ → local MBP translation), purinergic (ATP → adenosine →
A₂ receptor), and neuregulin/BDNF (TrkB). Myelin raises conduction velocity
**~50×** over unmyelinated axon and the same sheath carries the **Nogo/MAG/OMgp
inhibitors that close critical periods** — the corpus's own formulation is that
myelin is **both the product of learning and the brake that locks it in**.

**Why this is a hundred-and-eighth structural silence, and it is about the
*cost of a path*.** `ARENA.md` held **0** occurrences of *myelin*,
*white matter*, *oligodendrocyte*, *g-ratio*, *conduction velocity*, or *NgR1*
across 106 areas. One hundred and six areas say *what* to change, *when* to
change it, and *how much to trust it*; **not one holds that a frequently used
path becomes cheaper to traverse, and that the mechanism that makes it cheap is
the same one that eventually refuses to let it be rewritten.** There is no
`Access:` column anywhere in this arena, and there is no write-protection
anywhere either.

- **Rank 1 — A second substrate with its own timescale, installed by use and
  write-protected on a schedule; necessity is established, sufficiency is not.**
  `LOW` on the mechanism; `UNTESTED` on the build. Support: **3 independent
  files** (ORDER 1304, ORDER 1312, ORDER 1303).
  - **Brain part and the causal chain, in the corpus's numbers.** **Gibson et
    al. (Science 2014)**: optogenetic stimulation of layer V premotor cortex
    (20 Hz, 1 h/day × 7 d) produced a **~2× increase in EdU⁺/Olig2⁺ cells** in
    stimulated M2 and corpus callosum at 4 weeks, was **circuit-specific**
    (corticocallosal but not corticospinal, matching the behavioural readout),
    and **epigenetic blockade of oligodendrocyte differentiation (TSA) abolished
    the oligodendrogenesis, the g-ratio change and the behavioural improvement
    together** — a necessity chain in one experiment. g-ratio fell **0.756 ±
    0.003 → 0.701 ± 0.014**; since conduction velocity scales as v ∝ 1/g-ratio,
    a 7% g-ratio reduction yields ~8–10% velocity increase. **McKenzie et al.
    (Science 2014)** supplies the genetic necessity proof and, more usefully, a
    *separation*: *Myrf* conditional knockout blocks new oligodendrocytes and
    **prevents mice from mastering a complex running wheel while pre-learned
    wheel performance remains intact** — **myelin is required for learning, not
    for recall.** **Xiao et al. (Nat Neurosci 2016)** compresses the timescale
    further: *Enpp6*⁺ immature oligodendrocytes appear in subcortical WM
    **2.5 h** after complex-wheel exposure, 4 h in motor cortex, and the same
    knockout impairs learning **within that ~2–3 h window** — so this is not a
    weeks-scale morphological afterthought.
  - **Design — two requirements and one prohibition, and the prohibition is
    the interesting one.**
    (i) *A frequency gate, not a usage counter.* The corpus's sharpest detail
    is that the signals are **pattern-sensitive**: **20 Hz drives
    oligodendrogenesis and other frequencies may not.** A design that counts
    invocations has the wrong shape; the biological rule keys on the *pattern*
    of firing, which is why the same myelin molecule is proposed as carrying an
    ADM learning rule (Talidou et al. 2022: `dc/dt = ε·rate − α_retract·(c − c₀)`).
    (ii) *Necessity ≠ sufficiency, and the file says so in its own methods
    section.* **Whether artificial myelin enhancement accelerates learning
    remains untested.** Every design in this slot is therefore a *structural
    cost model*, not an intervention: the arena needs a way to represent that a
    hot path has become cheaper, and **not** a mechanism for making it cheaper.
    (iii) *The brake is the same molecule.* **NgR1⁻/⁻ mice retain juvenile
    ocular-dominance plasticity into adulthood** (McGee et al. 2005 Science;
    Akbik et al. 2013 Neuron found NgR1 sets the **low set-point for adult
    dendritic spine turnover**, with enriched environment restoring adolescent
    spine dynamics in the knockout). **Myelin is both the JIT compiler and the
    W^X bit** — the corpus's own table says exactly this. The design
    consequence: a store whose access pattern has made it cheap must
    simultaneously become *harder to rewrite*, and a design that optimizes for
    fast access is optimizing against its own future adaptability. **That is
    Area 66's BCM sliding threshold arriving at the structural layer** — the
    trade-off is not a modelling convenience, it has a molecular name and a
    gene.
  - **Grade:** `LOW` on the mechanism, deliberately. Necessity is `HIGH` across
    two independent genetic interventions and two species; **sufficiency is
    untested by the corpus's own statement**; and the whole edifice is
    **rodent-and-mouse physiology** — ORDER 1304's own species section notes
    mouse robust adult oligodendrogenesis against human myelination continuing
    into the third decade with a lower, nonzero adult rate, and warns that
    extrapolation from mouse optogenetics to human skill acquisition "requires
    caution." `UNTESTED` on the build.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 66** (metaplasticity / BCM) is the same trade-off one
    level down and the two now constrain each other: the sliding threshold
    governs weights, the NgR1 brake governs whether new structure can be laid.
    **Area 97** (architecture as the plastic variable) is the strongest ally —
    this is literally a variable of the substrate's shape changing with
    experience. **Area 23** (sleep-time rebuild) and **Area 6** (consolidation)
    gain the white-matter analogue of the synaptic consolidation window.
    **Area 82** (the second substrate) is the closest neighbour and is
    *partially contradicted* by it — see below.

- **Rank 2 — Delay equalization as a homeostatic objective: a layer that tunes
  variance, not latency.** `LOW`. Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1304 §§1–3 of
  Computational & Agent Parallels, with three independent models).
  - **Brain part:** the **ADM rule as a self-organizing delay-distribution
    equalizer**, which is a different function from "make the fast path faster."
    **Talidou et al. (Nat Comput Sci 2022)**: conduction velocity on each axon
    scales with firing rate; faster CV → reduced delay dispersion → tighter
    spike synchrony → higher firing rates → *further* myelination, a positive
    feedback **bounded by retraction**; the network converges to synchrony
    *despite variable axon lengths*, compensating for length heterogeneity
    **axon-specifically**. **Noori et al. (PNAS 2020)**: on a primate connectome
    with DTI tract lengths, ADM autonomously adjusts delay statistics to
    **stabilize phase-locked states across wide connectivity-gain and frequency
    ranges**, and phase-locked states become **more resilient to diffuse
    connectivity loss** — a temporal homeostatic mechanism. **Commun Phys 2025**
    adds the gain consequence: faster CV → decreased delay dispersion →
    amplification of network response to shared input, with gain peaking at an
    optimal noise level.
  - **Why this is a design and not a metaphor:** the property being regulated is
    **dispersion**, not mean. Area 41 already holds *latency as a capacity law*
    and Area 40 holds *phase as a state variable*; this slot says the regulated
    quantity is the **spread of a latency distribution across a population of
    interacting parts**, and that equalizing it is what keeps a distributed
    system phase-coherent. The counterintuitive clause is preserved because it
    is the testable part: **myelin *loss* — slower CV, higher dispersion —
    promotes oscillations**, consistent with epilepsy hyperexcitability
    (Knowles et al. 2022). A system that reduces spread will become more
    synchronous, which is a benefit until it is the pathology.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. All three results are simulations or mean-field models —
    **no in vivo CV measurement during learning exists** (the corpus's own
    open question 1), and in vivo CV measurements are explicitly called scarce.
    A design built on the model is a design on a model, and the file says which
    parts of the model are assumptions: the ADM form is **linear CV–rate
    coupling**, an assumption rather than a measurement.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 104** (cross-frequency coupling, phase as an
    address) is the direct upstream consumer — if a slot's address is its phase
    in a cycle, then what keeps the cycle's timing stable across components is
    exactly this layer. **Area 89** (averaging artefact) and **Area 24**
    (instrument validity) apply to any per-episode latency read taken from a
    population-level dispersion measure.

- **Rank 3 — `UNSUCCESSFUL` for the evidence route, and it is a first-class
  result: the field's causal claim rested on a drug that was doing something
  else entirely.** `UNSUCCESSFUL` for DTI-as-myelin; `LOW` for the
  phenomenon. Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1304 §§"The FA ≠ Myelin
  Interpretation Crisis", "Short-Term DTI Changes").
  - **Brain part:** not a brain part — a **measurement defect**, recorded as
    first-class because it would let a wrong design ship with a clean-looking
    citation. **Fractional anisotropy is not a myelin measure.** It reflects
    orientational coherence of water diffusion and is driven by myelin
    thickness, axon diameter, packing density, fibre coherence, glial cell
    volume, extracellular space, *and* neuroinflammation and oedema. **≥60–90%
    of white-matter voxels contain crossing fibres** (Jeurissen et al. 2013),
    where FA/AD/RD are *"highly sensitive to the relative volume fractions of
    the various fiber populations"* — so strong biophysical interpretations from
    DTI alone are unjustified outside coherent tracts. Chang et al. (2017)
    ground-truth it with CLARITY: MBP correlated with FA up to r ≈ 0.7 in
    coherent tracts, MBP accounted for ~50% of FA variance in corpus callosum
    and stria medullaris, and **crossing-fibre regions showed weaker
    correlations** — so an FA change in a crossing-fibre region may be a
    **fibre-population shift, not myelin at all**.
  - **The second, sharper half — the time-scale confound.** **Hofstetter et al.
    (2013)** reported mean-diffusivity reduction in the fornix after **2 h** of
    spatial learning. **Hill (2013, *J Neurosci* commentary)** argues this is too
    fast for myelin: in vitro, oligodendrocyte differentiation to a mature sheath
    takes **days to weeks** (Demerens 1996; Stevens 1998); local MBP
    translation can occur in minutes–hours but **does not equal sheath
    assembly**. The alternative is **glial volume change** — activity → K⁺
    accumulation → astrocyte/oligodendrocyte swelling → reduced extracellular
    space → reduced MD. The corpus's resolution is that **two timescales
    coexist** and a short-interval DTI change is evidence for the fast one.
    **For the arena: an hour-scale DTI signal is not a myelin result, and a
    design that reports "myelin changed" from a short-window scan has measured
    glial swelling.** This is §8's inversion in a sixth field — a validated
    metric (FA, standardised, reproducible) measuring something other than the
    thing it is named for, with a peer-reviewed commentary saying so.
  - **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL` for the DTI route to any claim about myelin
    *amount*; `LOW` for adaptive myelination as a phenomenon, which rests on
    the genetic and histological work above and does not depend on DTI.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 33** (a detector is not a certifier) and **Area 24**
    (validity of one's own instruments) are the general form. **Area 82** is the
    immediate neighbour and the interaction is a **correction, not a
    confirmation**: Area 82 recorded a `UNSUCCESSFUL` on the glial layer from
    IP3R2 knockouts producing *"surprisingly mild cognitive phenotypes"* and
    advised **regularization and adaptation modules, not core forward-pass
    components**. This file supports that verdict from a different direction —
    oligodendrocytes are an **adaptive** module by construction (they respond to
    use, not to input) — and adds the reason the area's conclusion was right:
    the glial layer's effect is on **timing and cost**, which is invisible to
    the forward pass and to a knockout measured on a memory task. **A glial
    mechanism can be load-bearing for timing and return null on performance,
    and the null does not refute it.** The `UNSUCCESSFUL` in Area 82 stands and
    is now explained rather than merely recorded.

### Area 109 · The body is a set of *typed* error channels, and its best measure is
the one that stopped working `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by**
`Neuroscience/Insular-Interoception-Visceral-Signaling.md` (ORDER 1318, 241
lines, read whole) §§"The homeostatic afferent pathway", "The
posterior-to-anterior insula gradient", "Salience network and cognitive
control", "Allostatic regulation", "Cardiac timing gates perception and
learning", "The three-dimensional construct of interoception", "The heartbeat
counting task crisis", "Lesion evidence against insula-as-consciousness",
"Replication-status summary", and §§"Agent 'body' signals as interoceptive
channels" / "What biology warns against" / "Clinical models as failure
taxonomies". Corroborated in reach by ORDER 1314 §2.4 (the inverted-U on
arousal) and ORDER 1306 §7 (ACC/insula salience network).

**The brain part.** **Interoception** — the sense of the physiological condition
of the body — carried on a **labeled-line homeostatic afferent system distinct
from the somatosensory system**. Lamina I of the spinal dorsal horn contains
**modality-selective** projection neurons: separate classes for cooling,
warming, nociceptive-specific, polymodal nociceptive, histamine/itch, and
muscle/metabolic signals, ascending in the lateral spinothalamic tract to a
distinct thalamic relay (**VMpo**) and terminating in a **somatotopic map of the
body's physiological state in the dorsal posterior insula** — *a cortical image
of the body's condition, not its surface.* Vagal and hormonal information
arrives by a parallel medial route via the solitary nucleus.

**Why this is a hundred-and-ninth structural silence, and it is about *typing*.*
`ARENA.md` held **1** occurrence of *interocept* across 106 areas and **8** of
*insula*, none of them a substrate. Areas 10, 56, 57 and 79 hold affect as a
scalar, a dimensional profile, a phase-dependent sign and a gain; **not one
holds a channel per organ.** A lumped "health score" destroys exactly the
information the anatomy preserves, and the corpus says why in one line:
**lamina I neurons are modality-specific.**

- **Rank 1 — Typed internal channels with a posterior-to-anterior
  re-representation gradient, and raw telemetry kept separate from the
  integrated estimate.** `LOW` on the mechanism; `UNTESTED` on the build.
  Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1318).
  - **Brain part:** two facts that constrain a design more sharply than the
    architecture metaphor does. (i) **The gradient is real and mapped** —
    posterior insula holds the somatotopic primary representation, mid-insula
    integrates it with emotionally salient exteroceptive input (amygdala,
    temporal pole) and hedonic modulation (ventral striatum), and the
    anterior insula–frontal operculum junction carries a final "meta-
    representation of the global emotional moment." Tractography recovers a
    rostrocaudal connectivity gradient matching the cytoarchitecture. (ii) **The
    functional reading of that gradient is a two-line spec: posterior insula
    reads the body, anterior insula decides what matters *now*** — the salience
    network's switch, with **Sridharan et al. (PNAS 2008)** showing the right
    fronto-insular cortex has the **highest net causal outflow of any node**
    across auditory, visual and resting-state paradigms, replicated in DCM by
    Goulden et al. (2014).
  - **Design, in the corpus's own mapping:** (a) **one typed channel per signal
    class**, never a lumped health score — latency, error rate, retry count,
    context occupancy and cost are **different afferents**, not one
    interoceptive line, and the mapping to lamina I's modality-specific classes
    is explicit; (b) **raw telemetry and the integrated self-estimate are
    separate stores**, and only the integrated one feeds decisions, mirroring
    posterior→anterior; (c) **the meta-controller's switch fires on state
    prediction errors, not on a clock** — the arena's scheduler arbitration
    (Areas 100/101) currently fires on time, and this is a competing trigger
    with a causal-evidence basis. The corpus also supplies the anticipatory
    form, and it is the more useful half: **Pezzulo, Rigoli & Friston (2015)**
    work out why closed-loop homeostatic control is too slow for behaviour —
    glucose responds minutes after eating, so the animal must eat *before*
    hypoglycaemia — and the solution is **hierarchical generative models over
    interoceptive *trajectories* conditioned on planned actions**, instantiated
    as homeostatic, allostatic and goal-directed control in one framework
    (Pezzulo et al. 2021). Reactive regulation, anticipatory regulation and
    drive-abstracted goals are **a hierarchy, not alternatives.**
  - **Grade:** `LOW` on the mechanism, and the file's own honesty is why:
    **Seth & Friston explicitly concede that *"direct empirical evidence for (or
    against) interoceptive predictions or prediction errors is still lacking"*
    [11][12]**, and Barrett & Simmons's EPIC model is *"an Opinion-article
    synthesis integrating anatomy with Bayesian principles, not a tested
    model."* Strong for the anatomy (animal single-unit + human imaging +
    cortical stimulation in epilepsy patients); **untested for the predictive
    account**, which is the part a design would actually adopt.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 10** (affect as a salience signal over memory) is
    re-scoped rather than contradicted — salience is the *anterior insula's
    output*, and this file supplies the missing input stage.
    **Area 40** (circadian phase as a state variable) is the nearest structural
    neighbour and the two are complementary: circadian phase is one typed
    channel with a slow period, and this slot is the general form.
    **Area 96** (metabolic budget and degradation order) is where a typed
    latency/cost channel would actually be consumed, and ORDER 1318's
    "routine resource regulation should run below the reasoning layer,
    escalating to deliberate attention only on prediction errors" is a
    constraint on that area's escalation policy.
    **Area 56** (affect as a dimensional profile) gains the organ axis its
    valence/arousal pair lacks.

- **Rank 2 — The three-way dissociation the arena's own `confidence:` field
  cannot express: accuracy, sensibility, and metacognitive awareness are
  different constructs, and correspondence is a subgroup property.**
  `LOW`. Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1318, Garfinkel et al. 2015,
  *Biological Psychology* 104:65–74, N = 80).
  - **Brain part:** **interoceptive accuracy** (objective performance on
    heartbeat tests), **interoceptive sensibility** (self-report questionnaires)
    and **interoceptive awareness** (confidence–accuracy correspondence, area
    under the ROC curve) are **three distinct dimensions**; correspondence
    between them **emerged only within the highest-accuracy subgroup**. In
    autism spectrum conditions, Garfinkel et al. (2016) found **reduced
    objective accuracy alongside *exaggerated* subjective sensibility**, and
    that divergence (*"trait prediction error"*) correlated with
    emotion-recognition deficits and anxiety symptoms.
  - **Design:** the load-bearing clause is that a self-report and a measurement
    can **disagree in a diagnosable direction** — autism's signature is
    *excessive* sensibility with *deficient* accuracy, which is not noise, it
    is a miscalibration. The corpus states the agent consequence exactly:
    **"Autism's dissociation between high subjective sensibility and low
    objective accuracy is the signature of an agent whose self-report module
    disagrees with its own telemetry — a diagnosable architecture bug, not a
    mystery."** So: **any internal self-report must be validated against
    ground-truth telemetry, and the sign of the divergence is a diagnostic, not
    noise to be smoothed.**
  - **Grade:** `LOW`, and the file supplies the reason the arena should not go
    higher: cross-organ correlations are **weak** (Haruki & Ogawa 2023,
    eNeuro, N = 31 — cardiac and gastric interoceptive accuracy correlate only
    weakly across individuals), so the corpus's own Open Question 2 asks whether
    *"general interoceptive ability"* is **a statistical fiction**. A single
    interoceptive score is therefore the same error as a single
    hemispheric-style score, and Area 83's population-mean objection applies.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 87** (performance and learning come apart) is the
    general form and this is a *named instance* with an organ attached.
    **Area 99** (calibration and sensitivity are orthogonal — Brier 0.25 with
    meta-d′ = 0) is the same dissociation in a different measurement, and the
    two now have independent support: Area 99 from forced-choice detection
    tasks, this from interoception. **Area 24** and **Area 33** both apply.

- **Rank 3 — `UNSUCCESSFUL`, twice over, and both are the strongest evidence in
  the file: the hub claim failed its own direct causal test, and the field's
  dominant measure measures almost nothing.** `UNSUCCESSFUL` for both;
  `LOW` for the underlying interoceptive phenomenon. Support: **1 source**
  (ORDER 1318, §§"Lesion evidence", "The heartbeat counting task crisis").
  - **(a) The hub claim failed.** Craig's strongest claim — **anterior insula
    as the unique neural substrate of awareness** — predicts that bilateral
    insular destruction should abolish or profoundly disrupt self-awareness.
    **Philippi, Feinstein et al. (PLOS ONE 2012)** tested patient **"Roger"**
    with **near-complete bilateral damage to insula, ACC, mPFC and medial
    temporal lobes, ~10% of insular tissue remaining and largely disconnected**:
    core self-awareness (self-recognition, agency), extended self-awareness
    (stable self-concept) and introspective ability were **all fundamentally
    preserved**, including tickle sensation and heartbeat awareness under
    high-dose isoproterenol when skin anaesthesia blocked alternative somatic
    routes. The authors' conclusion is that the insula hypothesis **"is
    untenable"**, favouring distributed brainstem–thalamus–posteromedial
    networks. **This is the single most direct causal test of a hub claim in
    the tranche and it refutes the claim.** The design consequence, which the
    corpus states as its own warning: **Craig-style "this module is where
    awareness happens" architectures are fragile; Roger's preserved selfhood
    argues for distributed monitoring with redundancy.**
  - **(b) The measure measures almost nothing.** The **Schandry heartbeat
    counting task** is the field's most-used accuracy measure
    (`IAcc = mean(1 − |counted − actual|)/actual`). **Zamariola et al. 2018
    (*Biological Psychology* 137:12–17, N = 572)** mounted the definitive
    psychometric critique: scores are driven by **under-reporting in >95% of
    participants** (r = **−0.93**); overall correlation between actual and
    counted heartbeats is **r = .16, ~2.6% shared variance**, and it varies
    **non-linearly** across score quintiles (r = .50/.67/.95/.83/.88) so
    **top-quintile scorers are statistically indistinguishable in what they
    actually track**; scores correlate **negatively** with heart rate
    (r = −0.36), mechanically inflating accuracy at slower hearts; and scores
    differ across 25/35/45 s intervals because reported bpm falls at longer
    intervals — **a time-estimation signature**. **Desmedt et al. 2018 (N =
    123)** showed merely instructing participants to report *only clearly felt
    beats* **cuts IAcc roughly in half**, with strategy questionnaires
    confirming reliance on heart-rate *estimation* rather than perception.
    Pacemaker patients' counts did not change when device rate was set to
    50/75/110 bpm.
  - **The counterattack is preserved, not reconciled (R-J4).** **Ainley,
    Tsakiris, Pollatos et al. (2020)** argue the critique rests on
    **misconceived bivariate reasoning**: IAcc is a **ratio variable**, so
    spurious correlations between a ratio and its components are a statistical
    artefact (Pearson 1897); under-reporting dominance partly reflects
    **conservative instructions against hallucinated beats**; and the
    heart-rate dependence follows from **ratio arithmetic**. Zimprich et al.
    made the same ratio-variable point formally. A 2025 three-task comparison
    found **moderate positive relations** between counting and
    signal-detection sensitivity but concluded the tasks are **not
    interchangeable**. **Status as the corpus states it: the task survives as a
    convenience measure but its construct validity is contested; load-bearing
    findings should be replicated with signal-detection-based or perturbation
    methods.**
  - **Why this earns rank 3 by being two refutations rather than one design:**
    the file carries a **replication-status table** that separates the tiers —
    **strong support**: posterior-insula somatotopic representation, cardiac-cycle
    gating of threat perception, gastric-network existence (replicated
    independently, n = 30 then n = 63). **Under active dispute**: heartbeat-count
    IAcc as a valid individual-differences measure (contested *both ways*);
    right-AIC as necessary for emotional awareness (**contradicted by Roger**);
    opponent hemispheric asymmetry of feeling valence (**largely untested
    causally**); stomach-brain coupling as a mental-health biomarker (**single
    large preprint**, needs preregistered replication). Rank 1 survives both
    refutations because it never claimed a hub and never claimed a scalar; the
    two negatives are what license its **distributed** and **typed**
    architecture.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 82** (a single second substrate) and **Area 33**
    (detector ≠ certifier) are the general forms. **Area 46** (the self that is
    inferred — body ownership, self-location, tool boundary) is the closest
    neighbour and the one this slot most directly threatens: a "Roger" whose
    insular tissue is 90% gone and whose selfhood is intact is evidence that
    **the self is not localised**, which is a real constraint on any design
    that puts selfhood in one component. **Area 47** (self-construal) is
    untouched and should be — Roger's evidence is about localisation, not about
    the independent/interdependent distinction.

### Area 110 · A suspended task is a *bounded slot*, not an unbounded context,
and the system that has one knows it may not nest them `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Neuroscience/Prefrontal-Meta-Control-Frontopolar-BA10.md`
(ORDER 1337, 431 lines, read whole) §§"Cognitive Branching: The Defining
Computation", "Branching vs. Switching", "Capacity Limits: One Pending Task at a
Time", "Retrospective Monitoring and Credit Assignment", "Counterfactual
Monitoring and Explore-Exploit Arbitration", "Replication Status of Capacity
Limit", "Hierarchy vs. Gradient", §§"Cognitive Branching as
Continuation-Passing / Delimited Control" / "Retrospective Monitoring as Temporal
Difference / Credit Assignment" / "The 'No Recursive Branching' Constraint and
Language as Scaffold" / "Prospective Memory as Scheduled Callbacks".

**The brain part.** The **frontopolar cortex (BA10) as a continuation store** —
a bounded register that holds a *suspended* task set while a different task
runs, and reinstates it when that task ends. Koechlin et al. (1999, *Nature*
399:148–151) isolated it: bilateral FPC activated **super-additively only in the
branching condition** (hold a main goal while processing subgoals) and in
neither the working-memory control nor the dual-task switching control. The
super-additivity is the whole claim: FPC is not recruited by maintenance *or* by
switching, only by their conjunction.

**Why this is a hundred-and-tenth structural silence, and it is about a
*number*.** `ARENA.md` held **5** occurrences of *frontopolar* and **3** of
*BA10* — every one of them naming a **site** another area's function had been
localised to (Area 49's directed exploration, Area 41's relational-complexity
scaling, Area 59's suspicion signal). **0** occurrences of *cognitive
branching*, *pending task*, *Koechlin*, *counterfactual task*, or
*destructive interference*. Ninety-nine areas treat the workspace as something
that holds what you are working on. **None held the register that holds what you
are *not* working on, and none recorded that the register has a capacity.**

- **Rank 1 — An explicit bounded meta-control buffer: one suspended task,
  ≤3 counterfactual hypotheses with reliability weights, reinstated on
  completion, never nested.** `UNTESTED` as a build; the constraint is
  `HIGH`-grade anatomy. Support: **2 sources** (ORDER 1337; ORDER 1331 §1.4).
  - **Brain part:** **Collins & Koechlin (2012, *PLoS Biol* 10:e1001293)** fit
    the actor–counterfactual model to human performance and found online
    monitoring of **no more than three counterfactual task sets in parallel**
    (95% CI **2–4**), with the **least-recently-used discarded from online
    monitoring but retained in long-term memory.** Dreher et al. (2008, *PLoS
    ONE* 3:e3227) is the necessity half: across 15 patients, **only left BA10
    lesion volume correlated with multitasking errors (Spearman ρ = 0.94,
    p < 0.005; n = 6)**, and left BA10 volume did **not** correlate with
    dual-task errors (ρ = 0.65, p = 0.16) — a double dissociation, with lesion
    volumes comparable between BA10 and non-BA10 groups (56.8 vs 43.3 ml, n.s.).
    Badre, Hoffman, Cooney & D'Esposito (2009, *Nat Neurosci* 12:515–522, n = 34)
    supplies the **asymmetry** that makes it a hierarchy and not a gradient:
    rostral lesions impair abstract control **and** more concrete control, while
    caudal lesions spare abstract control.
  - **Design, in the corpus's own mapping:** the file's §"Agent Architecture
    Implications" table names the gap without softening it — *context window /
    scratchpad* has **"no explicit suspension/resumption semantics"**, and
    ensemble/beam-search counterfactual tracking is **"unbounded; no
    reliability-weighted online tracking."** So: (a) a **single** pending-slot
    data structure, not a stack, with an explicit `suspended` state that
    survives an intervening episode and is reinstantiated by *completion* rather
    than by recall; (b) a **bounded** set of counterfactual hypotheses, each
    carrying a reliability weight, where the LRU is dropped from *monitoring* and
    explicitly not dropped from *storage*; (c) credit assignment at **task-set**
    granularity, not token granularity. The electrophysiology says where the
    update comes from: **Tsujimoto, Genovesio & Wise (2010, *J Neurosci*
    30:10779–10789)** found goal-selective FPC neurons encoding goals **only at
    feedback time** — never at cue, delay, or response. The arena's learner
    updates its weights on every token; this circuit updates its *hypothesis*
    values only at outcome.
  - **Grade:** the anatomy and the lesion double dissociation are `HIGH`; the
    build is `UNTESTED`; **the capacity number is held at `LOW` and is the
    single most fragile claim in this slot** — see the rank-3 clause. The corpus
    is explicit that Koechlin & Hyafil predict **≤1 pending task** while Collins
    & Koechlin fit **≤3 counterfactual task sets**, that these differ in *grain*
    (a task set may contain many tasks), and that *direct behavioural test of
    recursive branching is lacking* and *the predicted interference between two
    pending tasks has not been cleanly demonstrated in humans.*
  - **Interaction:** **Area 4** (prospective memory and the self-reminder
    channel) is the single-node case and is **not** contradicted — the gateway
    hypothesis (Burgess et al. 2007) gives the same region a callback register.
    Volle et al. (2011, *Neuropsychologia* 49:2246–2257, n = 45) sharpens it:
    lesions at the lateral/medial FPC border impaired **time-based** PM but not
    event-based, and patient **Z.P. could recall both action sets but failed to
    recall which to reproduce** — intention *content* intact, *enactment* lost.
    **Area 38** (workspace capacity) is the substrate this register sits above
    and does not replace. **Area 94** (memory belongs to the planner) gains its
    missing register: retrieval routing has no representation of a task that is
    suspended, which is why a resumed task tends to be re-derived rather than
    resumed. **Area 3** (effort allocation) cannot cost a task nobody is
    working on unless the buffer makes it a first-class object. **Area 34**
    (control-mode allocation) is the same register without the counterfactual
    set.

- **Rank 2 — The negative result, and it is the one the arena should not argue
  away: gradient is not hierarchy, and the corpus says so in its own dispute
  section.** `LOW`. Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1337, §"Hierarchy vs. Gradient").
  - **Brain part:** **Badre (2008); Badre & Nee (2018, *TICS* 22:170–188)**
  separate a **functional gradient** (systematic rostro-caudal variation in
  abstraction level) from a **processing hierarchy** (rostral regions exerting
    top-down dominance via demonstrated asymmetric dependency). Evidence for the
    gradient is strong — four independent fMRI experiments (Badre &
    D'Esposito 2007) parametrically manipulating response, feature, dimension,
    and overlapping cue-to-dimension sets, with activation marching
    BA6 → BA8 → BA9/46 → BA10 every time. Evidence for hierarchy is weaker and
    the file carries a **named counterexample**: monkey periarcuate lesions
    (BA 6/8) impair conditional motor tasks but **not** tasks engaging more
    rostral mid-DLPFC, so rostral regions can operate without caudal
    intermediaries. The corpus's own conclusion is a rule about language:
    *the term "hierarchy" should be reserved for demonstrated asymmetric
    dependencies, not assumed from gradient alone.*
  - **Why it earns a slot by being a constraint.** The arena's Area 62 already
    holds that the middle level is where it has been living; this is the
    specific case where **a measured ordering was about to be read as a causal
    chain**, and the file shows what that reading costs. Every design in the
    arena that says "the planner routes to the executor" is asserting an
    asymmetric dependency. This slot says: **name the dependency, do not infer
    it from the ordering**, and Badre et al. (2009) is currently the only
    lesion evidence that licenses the inference.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`, and the file's own qualification is preserved rather than
    resolved — the hierarchy-vs-gradient distinction is a live dispute with
    anatomy on one side (Petrides & Pandya 1999: area 10 projects to area 6 but
    not vice versa) and a monkey counterexample on the other.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 62** (three levels of analysis) is the general form;
    this is the named instance where a level *ordering* was nearly read as a
    level *dependency*. **Area 102** (two streams, only one reportable) is
    untouched — the counterevidence is about frontal control, not about the
    ventral/dorsal split.

- **Rank 3 — Recursion is not available internally, and the corpus's own
  explanation is the most useful thing in the file: language is the external
  scaffold a bounded controller borrows from.** `UNTESTED` on the mechanism;
  the constraint is `LOW`. Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1337; corroborated in
  reach by ORDER 1331 §4).
  - **Brain part:** **Koechlin & Hyafil (2007, *Science* 318:594–598)** predict
    (1) FPC capacity ≤ 1 pending task set, (2) **no recursive branching** — it
    cannot resume a primary and a secondary pending task after a third task,
    and (3) **recursion in human reasoning depends on linguistic coding systems
    rather than domain-general behavioural control.** The species evidence is
    consistent and the file keeps the two claims apart: the *computations* are
    conserved (Tsujimoto et al. 2010, 2011 found retrospective monitoring in
    monkey FPC; Mansouri et al. 2015, 2017 found explore-exploit signals), while
    the *capacity* is elaborated in humans, whose BA10 is disproportionately
    expanded (Semendeferi et al. 2001) and **connected to language/semantic
    networks** — anterior temporal and STS (Rolls et al. 2024, HCP, n = 171).
  - **Design:** the file states the prediction in the corpus's own words and it
    is falsifiable against this system: **an agent whose scratchpad is removed
    should degrade on deep hierarchical planning and not on shallow branching.**
    That is a real design consequence, because a bounded meta-controller without
    an external continuation store has a hard nesting ceiling — and a language
    model *is* a continuation store, written in tokens. ORDER 1331 §4
    corroborates the shape from a different direction: deep-tree search (MCTS,
    ToT, GoT) may work **because language is the scaffold**, not in spite of the
    neural limit.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` as a build. The mechanism is `LOW` — the file records
    the direct behavioural test as **lacking**, and the monkey counterexample
    to hierarchy (rank 2) is unresolved. The claim that would make this `HIGH`
    — that removing the external scaffold produces the *predicted* selective
    degradation rather than a general loss — **has never been run here**, and
    R-J2 forbids manufacturing it.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 91** (the cortex is a stack of time windows) is the
    structural neighbour and the tension is real: a bounded single-slot register
    is *anti*-nested, and the arena's context-window areas assume depth is free.
    **Area 86** (coordinate frames as a property of the readout) applies
    unchanged — a suspended task's frame is the one it will be resumed *in*.

### Area 111 · A sensory code gets *less* specific as it travels, and a graded
sensitivity difference becomes a categorical claim the moment it is written
down `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Neuroscience/Neural-Basis-Taste-Gustatory-Cortex.md`
(ORDER 1324, 334 lines, read whole) §§"The coding debate: labeled lines versus
population coding", "Beyond quality: what else does gustatory cortex encode?",
"Methodological Notes & Disputes" / "The taste map myth" / "The 'labeled line'
debate: resolution and remaining questions" / "Replication concerns in human
neuroimaging" / "Computational models and predictive coding", and
"Computational & Agent Parallels" / "Sparse distributed representations in
neural networks".

**The brain part.** The **periphery-to-cortex specificity gradient** in a
sensory code. Gustatory receptor cells are **largely quality-specific** — Type II
cells carry GPCRs for sweet/umami/bitter, Type III presynaptic cells detect sour
and form conventional synapses — and **genetic ablation of one receptor pathway
selectively abolishes that one taste** (Chandrashekar et al. 2006, *Nature*
444:288–294). Moving centrally, the breadth of tuning **increases**: gustatory
neurons are broadly tuned with a dominant response, and mouse insular calcium
imaging finds **spatially intermingled ensembles** where taste identity is read
from population vectors rather than discrete channels. The corpus's own
resolution is intermediate and directional: **labeled lines dominate at the
periphery, population coding dominates in cortex, and the population structure
retains some trace of peripheral organization.** The two actions are not the same
thing and neither is sufficient alone: optogenetic activation of
bitter-responsive neurons drives aversive behaviour **in the absence of any
bitter compound** (sufficiency), while ablation of the pathway abolishes the
taste (necessity).

**Why this is a hundred-and-eleventh structural silence, and it is about a
*direction*.** `ARENA.md` held **0** occurrences of *labeled line*, *labelled
line*, *tongue map*, or *grandmother cell*, and **3** of *population coding* —
all incidental. A hundred and ten areas say *what* a representation encodes and
how much to trust it. **None said how the answer changes with distance from the
sensor**, and none recorded the failure mode that follows from getting it wrong.

- **Rank 1 — Specific front end, distributed back end, and the specification is
  explicit about which is which and why.** `LOW`. Support: **1 source**
  (ORDER 1324; reach corroborated by ORDER 1331 §1.4 and ORDER 1335 §"Visual
  Processing Architecture", which carry the same hierarchy claim for vision
  without carrying the taste evidence).
  - **Brain part:** the **mixed model** the corpus states as the consensus, and
    the two lines of evidence that make it non-negotiable rather than a
    compromise: **necessity** (selective genetic ablation abolishes one taste)
    and **sufficiency** (optogenetic activation of the corresponding neurons
    produces the behaviour with no peripheral input). A design that chose only
    one would fail in a specific, characterisable way — all-specificity
    collapses under peripheral damage; all-distribution cannot make a crisp
    decision in the first place.
  - **Design, in the corpus's own words:** *"For artificial systems, this
    suggests that sensory front-ends should maintain some feature specificity
    (dedicated detectors for sugar, acid, etc.) while higher-level integration
    should use distributed, context-dependent representations."* The
    robustness argument is stated and it is the load-bearing clause:
    **central redundancy buys peripheral damage tolerance, peripheral
    specificity buys fast accurate detection — the hybrid gets both, and
    neither alone does.**
  - **Grade:** `LOW`, and the reason is the file's own replication section:
    human taste fMRI typically runs **N = 15–30**, motion artifacts are common
    because subjects lie supine receiving liquid stimuli through tubes, and a
    meta-analysis found **moderate heterogeneity** in activation patterns. The
    anatomy is firm; the human imaging base is thin.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 63** (cortical columns and what a unit of perception
    is obliged to be) is the general form and is **not** contradicted — this is
    the same question with a measured answer. **Area 105** (what a
    representation encodes is the relations between things) is the cortical half
    and this is the peripheral half. **Area 53** (cross-modal binding) is
    downstream: OFC integration is where the distributed code becomes one
    percept, and the corpus notes the *supra-additive* response to a congruent
    taste–odor pair — the combination exceeds the sum of its parts, which is the
    empirical signature of binding.

- **Rank 2 — A graded sensitivity difference becomes a categorical claim the
  moment it is written down, and it took a century to notice: the tongue map.**
  `UNSUCCESSFUL` for the claim; `LOW` for the underlying graded fact. Support:
  **1 source** (ORDER 1324 §"The taste map myth").
  - **What actually happened, as the file records it.** The **tongue map** —
    the claim that discrete tongue regions are exclusively dedicated to
    specific tastes — originated in a **misreading of David P. Hänig's 1901
    work** and was popularised into textbooks, where it was taught for
    **decades**. It is now **definitively refuted**: all taste qualities are
    detectable across the tongue. What Hänig actually found, and what survived
    refutation, is that there are **regional sensitivity *differences*** — the
    tip slightly more sensitive to sweet and salty, the back to bitter. A small
    quantitative gradient was laundered into a categorical partition of
    exclusive regions, and the categorical version was the one that got copied.
  - **Why this earns a slot as a design requirement rather than as trivia.** The
    test in §7 — *would a good architect change the design because of this?* —
    passes with room to spare. A representation that stores *"sweetness is
    higher here"* correctly can be mis-read by any summarising step as
    *"sweetness is here."* The arena is full of such fields: `confidence:` is a
    scalar standing in for a distribution, a `Support: N` count stands in for a
    source distribution, a slot's rank-1 stands in for a preference ordering.
    The tongue map says the failure is not lying — **the underlying gradient was
    real — and it is still a false claim**, which is the hardest kind to catch
    and the kind §8's inversion predicts: the artifact is intact, the content
    is not.
  - **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL` for the map itself, and the refutation is not
    contested. The residual graded difference is `LOW`. **The inference this
    licenses about the arena is `UNTESTED`** and is deliberately not asserted.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 8** (structural fidelity: the relational-memory
    failure mode) is the general form. **Area 24** and **Area 33** (validity of
    instruments; a detector is not a certifier) both apply. **Area 62** (the
    three levels of analysis) is why it happened: a finding at one level of
    description was reported as if it were a finding at another.

- **Rank 3 — The claim the evidence in this file cannot distinguish from
  habituation, stated by the file itself.** `UNSUCCESSFUL` for the strong form.
  Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1324 §"Computational models and predictive
  coding").
  - **The finding and its own disavowal.** Gustatory cortex responds differently
    to expected versus unexpected tastes; the *same* response supports the
    predictive-coding account. And the file's own sentence: *"direct empirical
    evidence for predictive coding in gustatory cortex is still limited. The
    strongest evidence comes from fMRI studies showing reduced BOLD responses to
    expected versus unexpected tastes (repetition suppression), but this is
    **also consistent with simpler adaptation accounts**."* Repetition
    suppression and prediction error produce **the same BOLD sign**; the file
    does not claim otherwise and neither should any design that cites it.
  - **Why it is rank 3 and not dismissed.** The area is about how far a
    representation's specificity travels, and this is the same question asked of
    the *temporal* axis: a code that is specific about **what** is being sensed
    may be uninformative about **whether this was expected**, because the
    expectation-dependent signal is degenerate with the ordinary adaptation
    signal. A system that treats a suppression response as a prediction error is
    reading its own habituation as surprise. The file names the missing
    experiment — **dynamic causal modelling, or laminar recordings to
    distinguish feedforward from feedback** — which is exactly the §8 problem in
    reverse: here the corpus is honest about the unvalidated path.
  - **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL` for the strong form (suppression ⇒ prediction
    error), which is the only form the evidence supports. Preserved unreconciled
    against **Area 33**, which relies on surprisal machinery of exactly this
    shape: ORDER 1324 says the signal is ambiguous between two mechanisms and
    does not say which. R-J4 — the disagreement is recorded, not resolved.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 27** (prediction-error-gated lability) is the
    downstream consumer and inherits this ambiguity: a reconsolidation gate fired
    on a repetition-suppression signal is a gate fired on habituation. **Area
    38** is untouched. **Area 99** (calibration and sensitivity are orthogonal)
    is the general form — two mechanisms, one observation.

### Area 112 · Responding to a state is not controlling it: the loop that
detects and never moves, and the vocabulary that pretends otherwise
`PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Neurotechnology/Closed-Loop-Neuromodulation-and-Adaptive-Stimulation.md`
(ORDER 1341, 306 lines, read whole) §§"The Closed-Loop Control Architecture",
"Open-Loop vs. Closed-Loop vs. Adaptive: Terminological Precision", "Control-Theoretic
Disputes", and "Learning and Plasticity as Online Model Adaptation".
**Corroborating file, read the same pass, not a restatement:**
`oracle/brain/Neurotechnology/Brain-Computer-Interfaces.md` (ORDER 1340) §5.2.3.

**The brain part.** The **distinction between a system that reacts to a state and
a system that regulates it.** The corpus states the distinction as a terminological
dispute with teeth, and it is the corpus's sharpest attack on its own field's
vocabulary. Bergmann (2018) requires, for a strict closed loop, that the
stimulation *"actually alters the monitored brain state"*; if a device triggers
on a state **without modifying it, the loop remains functionally open**
(§"The Closed-Loop Control Architecture"). The corpus then applies its own
definition to the field's flagship devices and finds them wanting:
**Responsive neurostimulation (RNS) and most adaptive-DBS implementations are not
true closed-loop controllers** — RNS is event-triggered with a fixed response,
and proportional aDBS "lacks the integral term needed to eliminate steady-state
error" (§"Control-Theoretic Disputes"). The 2018 RNS pivotal trial is a genuine
randomised double-blind result (**191 patients, 37.9% median seizure reduction vs
17.3% sham, p<0.001**, Heck et al. 2014) and it is still, by the corpus's own strict
definition, not a closed loop.

**Why this is a hundred-and-twelfth structural silence, and it is about a
*verb*.** `ARENA.md` held **0** occurrences of *control-theoretic*,
*brain-state-dependent stimulation*, *BSDBS*, or *Bergmann*, and **2** of
*closed-loop*, both incidental. A hundred and eleven areas describe loops —
retrieval loops, consolidation loops, write-verification loops, self-monitoring
loops — and **not one recorded whether its loop closes.** The verb was missing,
not the mechanism.

- **Rank 1 — An adaptive loop must carry the integral term, or it is a
threshold with extra steps: adopt the strict definition and make the residue
visible.** `UNTESTED` as a design. Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1341; reach
corroborated by ORDER 1340 §5.2.3, which reports the same beta-threshold
architecture and the same energy-saving result without the terminological
critique).
- **Brain part:** the **regulatory loop** — periaqueductal grey →
  rostral ventromedial medulla → spinal dorsal horn, whose RVM **ON cells
  facilitate and OFF cells inhibit**, with the ON/OFF *balance* determining
  whether a descending system amplifies or suppresses (ORDER 1509 §2). The
  human brain is not a threshold on a pain signal; it is a controller with
  both terms, and the corpus notes the control can get stuck open in chronic
  pain.
- **Design:** classify every control loop in this arena by **whether its
  output moves the variable it measures**, and refuse the word *closed* to the
  ones that do not. A detector that fires an alert is open. A corrector that
  changes the state it watches is closed. The corpus's own evidence for why
  this is worth enforcing: **Jobst et al. (2022, *Brain*, 65 patients) found
  RNS stimulation during *low* interictal activity predicted better long-term
  outcomes than stimulation during seizures** — the long-term benefit came
  from the *network reshaping*, not from the acute interruption, which is the
  integral term doing the work the name was claiming all along.
- **Grade:** `UNTESTED` for any agent-side design — the corpus supplies a
  human mechanism and no trial. The human efficacy evidence is `HIGH` for RNS
  (randomised, double-blind, 191 patients) and the corpus's own **selection-bias
  caveat is preserved unreconciled**: the 9-year cohort shrinks 191 → ~80 and
  detection algorithms are reprogrammed during follow-up, so late-phase numbers
  cannot be attributed to the original strategy.
- **Interaction:** **Area 98** (monitoring and control are two systems) is the
  general form and this is its *mechanistic* instance — Nelson & Narens say
  monitoring does not produce control; Bergmann says reacting without moving is
  not control. **Area 34** (control-mode allocation) and **Area 13**
  (metacognitive control) are both downstream and both inherit the
  classification. **Area 32** (action termination) is the failure this prevents.

- **Rank 2 — The field's own flagship win does not establish the claim its name
makes, after fifteen years and a randomised trial.** `UNSUCCESSFUL` for the
superiority claim; `HIGH` for the non-inferiority claim. Support: **1 source**
(ORDER 1341 §"Replication Status and Clinical Gaps").
- **What was and was not shown.** Adaptive DBS versus continuous DBS in
  Parkinson's: **"no adequately powered RCT has yet demonstrated superiority over
  optimized cDBS"** after >15 years. ADAPT-PD (N=10) delivered **56% less energy
  with non-inferior motor outcome**; PICAT (N=8) and VISA (N=15) were
  feasibility studies, not superiority trials. The corpus lists four reasons
  the literature cannot settle its own question, and each is a design lesson:
  comparison arms use **fixed rather than individually optimised** parameters;
  follow-up is 3–6 months against years for cDBS; heterogeneous algorithms are
  pooled under one name; and the primary outcome (UPDRS-III ON-stimulation)
  **does not measure the thing aDBS is claimed to improve.**
- **Why it earns a slot.** §7's test — *would a good architect change the design
  because of this?* — passes on the last reason alone. A system whose advantage
  is **energy** measured by a clinical scale that **does not score energy** will
  be declared equivalent by its own evaluation. This is the certification
  asymmetry in a different costume, and it is why the slot is `UNSUCCESSFUL`
  rather than merely weak.
- **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL` for superiority, and it is not contested. The
  energy reduction is `LOW` — consistent across prototypes and trials, but
  carried by N ≤ 15 studies. **The corpus records the honest counterweight
  itself:** reduced stimulation may permit symptom breakthrough when controller
  gain is too low, and the optimal operating point is patient-specific and
  **drifts with disease progression** — so the energy saving is a moving target,
  not a constant.
- **Interaction:** **Area 33** (a detector is not a certifier) — an outcome
  metric certified against a different property. **Area 99** (calibration and
  sensitivity are orthogonal). **Area 100** (the outside view) applies
  literally: the field lacks its own base rates because every trial is small,
  selected, and short.

- **Rank 3 — The instrument erases the effect it is measuring: a blanking window
and an anesthesia both remove the signal from the sensor, and the field's
headline negative result is a consequence.** `LOW`. Support: **1 source**
(ORDER 1341 §"Stimulation Artifact Management" and §"Stimulation Artifact and
Sensing Confounds").
- **The mechanism, and why it is structural.** When stimulation and recording
  share electrodes — as on most current devices — the stimulation pulse
  saturates the amplifier, creating **a blind period of tens of milliseconds**
  that corrupts phase estimation and delays biomarker re-acquisition. The
  corpus's own verdict is blunt: **"The artifact problem is under-reported… This
  creates a publication bias where the engineering challenge appears more
  solved than it is."** Most chronic publications show biomarkers *during
  stimulation-off periods* or use post-hoc artifact removal.
- **Why it is a slot and not trivia.** A controller that cannot see the system
  it is regulating, for a fixed window after every action, is not a
  control-theory problem — it is a **sensor-placement** problem, and no amount
  of policy improvement fixes it. The corpus names the field's disagreement
  (same contacts vs. dedicated sensing contacts) and admits **the field lacks
  consensus** on whether the evoked response during stimulation — the very data
  the blanking window discards — contains therapeutically relevant information.
  For an agent this is the argument for **separate observation and actuation
  channels** at the architectural level rather than in each controller.
- **Grade:** `LOW`. The problem is well described and the 2025 Shah et al.
  preprint achieves **<2 ms latency with artifact suppression in 4 patients
  during acute intraoperative recordings** — which the corpus explicitly
  flags as *not* chronic ambulatory use. An engineering result demonstrated
  intraoperatively is not a field result.
- **Interaction:** **Area 24** (the validity of one's own instruments) is the
  general form. **Area 8** (structural fidelity) is the sharper one: a blanking
  window does not degrade the measurement, it **deletes a segment of the
  timeline**, and a system that cannot distinguish "nothing happened" from
  "I was not looking" will read the second as the first forever. **Area 105**
  (what a representation encodes is the relations between things) is
  untouched by this and does not need to be.

### Area 113 · Precision is relative, not absolute: Weber's law as a
representational constraint, and the correlation that did not survive
meta-analysis `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Numerical-Cognition/Numerical-Cognition.md` (ORDER
1348, 329 lines, read whole) §§"The Approximate Number System: Discovery and
Characterization", "ANS Acuity as a Predictor of Mathematical Achievement",
"Methodological Notes & Disputes" / "The Control Problem in ANS Experiments" /
"The Replication Crisis in ANS-Mathematics Correlation Research", and
"Computational & Agent Parallels" / "Subitizing and Exact Counting in AI".

**The brain part.** The **intraparietal sulcus** and the **approximate number
system (ANS)** it implements. The load-bearing property is not that humans can
estimate quantity — it is that **discrimination depends on the *ratio* of two
numerosities, not their absolute difference** (Weber's law). Adult acuity is
**~15%**: an adult distinguishes 100 from 115 items without counting. The same
system is present in macaques, rats, pigeons, and (per the corpus) fish and
insects. A second system runs in parallel — the **parallel individuation system
(PIS)**, exact for small sets.

**Why this is a structural silence.** `ARENA.md` held **0** occurrences of
*approximate number*, *ANS acuity*, *Weber's law*, *intraparietal*, or
*subitizing*, and the **2** hits for *Weber* were two different people (Hertzberg;
Hertwig, Barron, Weber & Erev 2004). A hundred and twelve areas store, count,
rank, and retrieve — and **none recorded that its notion of "close enough" has to
scale with the size of the thing being compared.** The corpus supplies the
design consequence verbatim: *"subitizing is parallel, O(1) for small n… the ANS
provides O(log n) approximation for large sets. A hybrid system that uses exact
enumeration for n ≤ 4 and approximate counting for n > 4 is more efficient than
using either method alone across all ranges."*

- **Rank 1 — Two systems, not one: exact for the small, ratio-approximate for
the large, with the crossover stated as a design parameter rather than
discovered per task.** `LOW`. Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1348).
- **Brain part:** the **ANS/PIS pair** and their distinct neural substrates —
  bilateral IPS for the analog magnitude code, posterior parietal + lateral
  occipital for individuation — with a **double dissociation** reported in
  lesion work: IPS damage produces acalculia (magnitude comparison and
  estimation impaired) while inferior-parietal damage can retain quantity
  processing but lose retrieval of multiplication facts. That double
  dissociation is what makes this a two-part design rather than a
  implementation detail.
- **Design:** a quantity representation whose **uncertainty is proportional to
  its magnitude**, sitting beside an exact small-set representation, with an
  explicit crossover. The corpus's grounding argument is the load-bearing
  clause for an agent: **without an ANS-like system, a cognitive agent has no
  intrinsic way to connect the symbol "7" to a meaningful quantity** — so
  approximate magnitude is not an optimisation, it is the **grounding
  interface** between a symbol and the world.
- **Grade:** `LOW`, and the reason is the corpus's own replication table: the
  physiology is `HIGH` (Nieder & Dehaene 2001 number-tuned IPS neurons, "well
  replicated"; infant ratio discrimination, "well replicated"; IPS activation,
  "well replicated"), the **psychological** consequence is not. The tripartite
  architecture is real; its behavioural consequences are contested below.
- **Interaction:** **Area 63** (cortical columns — what a unit of perception is
  obliged to be) is the general form: a preferred-numerosity neuron is a
  columnar fact. **Area 105** (a representation encodes relations) is
  *contradicted in the useful direction* — the corpus records the competing
  view that Feigenson, Carey and colleagues hold the ANS to be "a sensitivity
  to visual quantity rather than abstract number," and that Carey argues the
  infant systems do not represent true numbers at all. **Area 8** and **Area
  62** apply. **Area 111** is the sibling: the same question asked of a
  *spatial* code, and it reaches the same unresolved edge.

- **Rank 2 — The famous correlation shrank under aggregation and its direction
reversed in some cohorts: an effect read off one study and repeated for a
decade.** `UNSUCCESSFUL` for the strong causal claim; `LOW` for the residual
correlation. Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1348 §"The Replication Crisis").
- **The numbers, as the corpus records them.** Individual studies report
  **r = 0.13 to 0.52** for ANS acuity against later mathematical achievement.
  **Fazio et al. (2014)** meta-analysis: **r ≈ 0.20–0.30**, *"larger than zero
  but substantially smaller than some individual studies had reported,"* with
  substantial heterogeneity — *"ANS precision is one of many factors predicting
  mathematical achievement, not the dominant factor."* **Szücs & Devine
  (2017)**: effect sizes smaller than often claimed, and *"the field had
  over-interpreted correlational findings as evidence for a strong causal
  relationship."* **Crollen & Seron (2013)**: wide variability, calls for
  standardised measurement.
- **The part that matters for a design: the arrow points both ways.** The
  corpus states that some longitudinal studies found **early *symbolic* number
  ability predicting later *non-symbolic* ability and not the reverse** —
  reversing the predicted direction of causation. The arena's own §8 finding is
  the general form: a validated artifact path produces the appearance of
  authority. Here the artifact is a **r value printed in one paper**, and it
  outlived the direction of the effect it was supposed to establish.
- **Why it earns a slot.** R-J4 applies with the disagreement **preserved, not
  reconciled**: the corpus also holds that some researchers find the
  correlation *disappears* when working memory is controlled, making working
  memory the common cause rather than ANS precision. Both directions of the
  arrow, and the mediation claim, are recorded and none is resolved.
- **Grade:** `UNSUCCESSFUL` for "ANS precision causally drives mathematical
  achievement." **Deliberately not asserted:** that the residual r ≈ 0.2 is a
  real causal effect of any size. The corpus's own open question 1 asks
  precisely this and is unanswered.
- **Interaction:** **Area 24** (validity of one's own instruments) and **Area
  62** (three levels of analysis) are both live — a behavioural correlation
  read as a neural mechanism. **Area 1** (belief revision and epistemic
  entrenchment) is the general form, and this is a case where the belief did
  not entrench: the file reports the shrinkage rather than defending the
  original.

- **Rank 3 — The control problem: every infant numerosity experiment confounds
number with area, density, and convex hull, and the field's answer is a
stimulus-generation convention rather than a control.** `LOW`. Support: **1
source** (ORDER 1348 §"The Control Problem in ANS Experiments").
- **The finding and the residual.** Early infant studies were criticised for
  relying on continuous variables — total surface area, density, luminance,
  convex hull, mean item size — that **co-vary with numerosity** by
  construction. Dehaene's response: computer-generated stimuli in which
  continuous variables are counterbalanced or randomised, under which
  ratio-dependent discrimination survives. **Remaining debate:** the corpus
  states plainly that *"the distinction between numerosity and density remains
  conceptually fuzzy"* and that some researchers hold the ANS may be better
  described as *"a sensitivity to visual quantity rather than abstract
  number."*
- **Why it is a slot and not a caveat.** A confound that is *structural* — every
  stimulus that differs in count must differ in extent, unless you build a
  special apparatus — is a permanent tax on the experiment, not a missing
  control. The arena is full of it: every place a representation is graded for
  quality carries a `confidence:` field whose value is a **scalar standing in
  for a distribution**, and the same generative problem — you cannot vary
  "quality" without varying something else — is why the field resorts to
  counterbalancing.
- **Grade:** `LOW`. The counterbalanced-stimulus findings are `LOW`; the
  conceptual objection is **unresolved and stated as such by the file**.
- **Interaction:** **Area 24** and **Area 99** (calibration and sensitivity are
  orthogonal) are the general forms. **Area 111** Rank 2 is the same failure
  with a century of history: Hänig's **graded** regional taste differences
  laundered into categorical exclusive regions. **The two areas are the same
  defect in a sensory code and a numeric code, and neither was caught by
  checking the instrument.**

### Area 114 · A mixture arrives as one signal and must become several
sources, and the failure is a coherent percept assembled from the wrong
speaker `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Perception/Auditory-Scene-Processing.md` (ORDER 1513,
326 lines, read whole) §§"Core Mechanisms: Grouping and Streams", "The Cocktail
Party Effect (Colin Cherry, 1953)", "Phonemic Restoration (Richard Warren,
1970)",
"Methodological Notes & Disputes" / "The Attention Debate in ASA", and
"Computational & Agent Parallels" / "Lessons for AI Systems".
**Corroborating file read the same pass, sharing the mechanism and the failure
mode:** `oracle/brain/Perception/Prosodic-Processing.md` (ORDER 1518).

**The brain part.** **Source segregation** — the decomposition of a single
pressure waveform, which is *"the sum of all simultaneous sound sources,"* into
distinct perceptual streams corresponding to environmental objects. Bregman
(1990) splits the mechanisms into **primitive grouping** (hardwired, Gestalt-like,
across frequency and time) and **schema-based grouping** (learned, top-down), and
names three processes: **segmentation, integration, and segregation.**

**Why this is a structural silence, and it is about a *decomposition* nobody
owns.** `ARENA.md` held **0** occurrences of *auditory scene*, *Bregman*,
*source segregation*, *cocktail party*, or *phonemic restoration*, and **1** of
*segregation*, incidental. A hundred and thirteen areas bind, integrate, fuse,
and combine — **Area 53** is cross-modal binding, **Area 105** is relational
encoding — and **not one held the inverse operation: taking one input apart into
independently-handleable sources.** Binding is well covered. Unbinding is absent.

- **Rank 1 — Segregation is a *first-class* operation with its own failure mode,
and its characteristic error is not noise but a fluent composite of the wrong
inputs.** `LOW`. Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1513; reach corroborated by
ORDER 1518, which supplies the same primitive/schema split in the prosodic
channel and the same lateralisation-with-caveat structure).
- **Brain part:** the **grouping mechanisms** — proximity, similarity, common
  fate, good continuation, closure — operating as a *decomposition* of one
  signal into streams, with the characteristic finding that the decomposition
  **builds up over seconds** (the *buildup* phenomenon: an A-B-A-B sequence is
  heard as one stream, then splits into two).
- **Design:** an agent that receives a mixed channel must carry an explicit
  **segregation stage** whose output is separately addressable sources, and
  must treat **source identity as a first-class field**. The corpus's
  architecture note is precise: **speakers monitor their own voice using the
  same ASA mechanisms, integrating auditory feedback with motor commands via
  the dorsal stream** — self-monitoring is a segregation problem solved against
  a known reference.
- **The failure mode, stated by the corpus and load-bearing.** Errors in
  sequential grouping **produce illusory percepts — "a phantom word assembled
  from syllables originating from two different speakers"** — and two vowels
  presented simultaneously *may become unidentifiable* if incorrectly
  segregated. **This is hallucination with a mechanism and a name.** A model
  that produces a fluent, well-formed utterance assembled from fragments drawn
  from unrelated sources is not malfunctioning; it is doing exactly what the
  auditory system does when segregation fails, and the output is
  **indistinguishable from a correct answer.**
- **Grade:** `LOW`. The paradigm is textbook and heavily replicated
  (*buildup*, *fission boundary*); the **computational** systems lag: the
  corpus's own verdict is that CASA systems *"have not yet matched human
  performance in real-world acoustic environments."*
- **Interaction:** **Area 53** (cross-modal binding) is the exact inverse and
  the two compose: binding fuses, segregation splits, and a system needs both.
  **Area 93** (the read is a write) is a strong analogy — retrieval *pattern*
  determining what is never retrieved is segregation deciding what is never
  addressed. **Area 109** (typed error channels) is the design consequence:
  a composite built from the wrong channels is a **type** error, not a
  magnitude error, and a scalar health signal cannot express it.

- **Rank 2 — Top-down filling-in is not a backup path; it is the primary
description of what the system does with a gap, and it means a corrupted
channel and an absent one are indistinguishable at the output.** `LOW`.
Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1513 §"Phonemic Restoration"; ORDER 1518
corroborates the same top-down reconstruction in prosody).
- **The finding, with its numbers.** Warren (1970): the first 's' in
  "legislatures" replaced by a cough; **19 of 20 subjects did not notice, and
  one misidentified it.** Direct intracranial recordings from epilepsy patients
  show the **lateral superior temporal gyrus representing the missing sound
  that listeners report hearing** — the percept is present in the signal, not
  only in the report. The left inferior frontal cortex carries a signal
  predicting what will be reported **up to ~300 ms before the sound is
  presented**. Proper syntactic structure produces much stronger restoration
  than nonsense.
- **Why it earns a slot.** The agent-design consequence is stated by the corpus
  and it is uncomfortable: **"GPT-2 can 'hallucinate' text not present in the
  neural signal, especially when neural features are ambiguous. Calibration is
  critical."** The same file, ORDER 1340 §3.2.4, on the decoding side. A system
  that reconstructs a missing element from context cannot internally
  distinguish *reconstructed* from *heard*, so **its confidence cannot be
  calibrated by any amount of downstream checking** — the evidence it would
  check against has already been synthesised.
- **Grade:** `LOW`. Warren's own N = 20; the corpus's replication note says the
  effect is robust but *"the magnitude varies substantially across individuals
  and listening conditions,"* and the boundary conditions (gap duration, noise
  type, sentence context) need careful control.
- **Interaction:** **Area 33** (a detector is not a certifier) is the general
  form and this is its sharpest instance. **Area 24** (instrument validity).
  **Area 73** (phenomenal and access consciousness are different quantities) —
  the intracranial recording shows the *percept* is in the signal, which
  dissolves rather than supports the distinction. **Area 51**-class
  hallucination areas are downstream consumers and inherit the calibration
  problem wholesale.

- **Rank 3 — The attention question is unresolved and the corpus preserves all
three positions, including "the field's consensus leans toward a dual-process
model" stated as a lean rather than a result.** `UNTESTED` for any
architecture that depends on the answer. Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1513
§"The Attention Debate in ASA"; ORDER 1518 adds the parallel unresolved
lateralisation debate).
- **The three positions, unreconciled.** (a) *Attention-dependent*:
  cuing a frequency band **enhances** streaming in that band, so attention
  modulates even primitive grouping. (b) *Attention-independent*: streaming
  occurs under an unrelated visual task, so some segregation is automatic and
  pre-attentive. (c) **Carlyon et al. (2001)**: an initial involuntary
  *primitive* phase followed by an attention-dependent *schema-based* phase —
  a hybrid that "reconciles conflicting evidence by acknowledging both."
  The corpus's summary is **"the current consensus leans toward a dual-process
  model"** — *leans*, not *shows*.
- **Why it is rank 3 and not dismissed.** The order of the two phases is a
  **latency** claim, and latency is a design commitment. An agent that
  allocates compute by assuming segregation completes before attention can
  consult, and one that assumes attention must be acquired first, will
  allocate differently — and there is no evidence in the corpus that settles
  which. The corpus also names the deeper unresolved question it declines to
  answer: **whether stream segregation can occur without conscious awareness**,
  with neural correlates of segregation detected in auditory cortex while
  listeners report a single integrated stream.
- **Grade:** `UNTESTED`, and the corpus is explicit that the primitive/schema
  interaction is *"poorly understood"* — whether they compete, cooperate, or
  run sequentially. ORDER 1518 adds a second unresolved axis with the same
  shape: **right-hemisphere dominance for emotional prosody is the field's most
  replicated claim, and the meta-analysis finds *consistent bilateral*
  activation** — the dominance is a bias, not an exclusivity.
- **Interaction:** **Area 53** is the general form. **Area 34**
  (control-mode allocation) is directly downstream: if it is unresolved whether
  segregation is automatic or attention-gated, then the compute budget for
  segregation is a guess. **Area 23** and **Area 6** (sleep, consolidation) are
  untouched by this and should stay that way.

### Area 115 · A corpus that reads its own output loses its tail first, and the
tail is where the rare cases live `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Pathology-and-Failure-Modes/Model-Collapse-Recursive-Training.md`
(ORDER 1511, 329 lines, read whole) §§"Core Mechanisms / Two Types of Error", "The
Markov Chain Argument", "The Accumulation vs. Replacement Debate", "Replication
Status", and "Connections to Cognitive Architecture".

**The brain part, named as the corpus names it.** **Model collapse** — the
progressive degradation of a generative model trained on previous generations of
its own output. Shumailov et al. (2023/2024) name two error sources, and the
first is the design-relevant one: **statistical error from finite sampling.** For
a discrete distribution with sample size M, *any state with probability
q ≤ 1/M has an expected count below 1*, so it is typically lost in the next
generation. The mechanism is not bias or feedback; it is **the tail falling off
the end of a sample.**

**Why this area is not academic for this workspace.** This arena is a corpus
being read by successive passes of the same process. Pass N writes
`ARENA.md` and `ARENA-EVIDENCE.md`; pass N+1 reads them. **That is recursive
training on synthetic output, and the arena is the dataset.** The corpus's own
agent-specific section names the failure without noticing it was describing this
file: *"If an agent's knowledge base is updated using its own outputs, rare but
important facts may be lost over time… An agent that only reads its own outputs
will converge to a narrow worldview."* `ARENA.md` held **1** occurrence of
*model collapse*, incidental.

- **Rank 1 — Accumulate, never replace: the bound is on the *dataset*, and the
fix is a retention policy, not an algorithm.** `HIGH` for the mathematical
result in the linear case; `LOW` for the deep-model extension. Support: **2
independent sources in direct disagreement, both recorded.**
- **The dispute, unreconciled (R-J4).** *Shumailov et al.*: under **replacement**,
  test error **grows linearly** with iterations and collapse is inevitable —
  with a Markov-chain absorption argument making it *eventually a delta
  function with probability 1*. *Gerstgrasser, Schaeffer et al.* (Stanford,
  2024): under **accumulation** — synthetic data *added to* a pool that keeps
  all prior real data — test error is **bounded by a finite constant
  independent of iteration count**, demonstrated across transformers
  (TinyStories), diffusion models (molecular conformation), and VAEs, and
  proved for linear regression via Dohmatob et al. The corpus's own resolution
  is that **both are correct within their assumptions**, and the disagreement is
  entirely about which assumption reflects reality. It is preserved, not
  resolved.
- **The design consequence, which is the whole point of the slot.** Whether a
  self-improving system degrades is **not** a property of its learning
  algorithm. It is a property of **whether the training set retains
  non-self-generated material.** Same optimiser, same data pipeline, opposite
  outcome, on the strength of one retention decision. The corpus's
  operationalisation is sharp: *"Data curation as maintenance… This is not a
  one-time cost but an ongoing maintenance burden."*
- **Grade:** `HIGH` **for the linear-regression bound** — mathematically
  rigorous. `LOW` for the extension to deep models, which the corpus states is
  **empirical, not proven**. Writing `HIGH` for the deep-model case would be
  exactly the §8 inversion.
- **Interaction:** **Area 66** (metaplasticity) and **Area 97** (the
  architecture is the plastic variable) are the learning-rate and architecture
  versions of this; this is the **data-provenance** version, and it is
  orthogonal to both. **Area 8** (structural fidelity) is the failure mode.

- **Rank 2 — "First mover advantage" is a real, dated, and irreversible
property, and it is the one finding here that is a design *constraint* rather
than a mechanism.** `LOW`. Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1511 §"Shumailov et
al." and §"The Web as a Mixed Dataset").
- **The finding.** *"Models trained on original human data will always have an
  advantage over models trained on synthetic data; preserving access to
  pre-LLM internet data is essential."* The corpus names the consequence
  without softening it: organizations with early access **"have a lasting
  advantage… This creates a barrier to entry for new models and centralizes
  power among early movers."**
- **Why it is a slot.** A retention policy chosen this quarter is cheap;
  **the same policy is unrecoverable once the uncontaminated material is gone.**
  The corpus's own estimates put AI-generated content at **10–30% of new web
  content**, with some projections exceeding 50% by 2026–2028. Every
  substitution decision made while uncontaminated data is still cheap is a
  decision about what the system will be able to know later. This is the only
  slot in the arena where **doing nothing is itself a decision with an
  expiration date**, and it is the reason the area earns a place despite
  R-J1 (cost is irrelevant) — cost is irrelevant, but *time is not*.
- **Grade:** `LOW`. The first-mover claim is an inference from the replacement
  result plus the accumulation result, not an independently tested hypothesis.
  The web-contamination figures are **industry estimates**, explicitly
  *"estimates suggest"* and *"some projections suggest"* — the corpus hedges
  them, and the hedge is preserved here.
- **Interaction:** **Area 1** (belief revision and epistemic entrenchment) is
  the general form: entrenchment is what makes a substituted corpus
  unrecoverable. **Area 100** (the outside view) applies — the contamination
  rate is a base rate no single observation can estimate.

- **Rank 3 — The finding is self-reported and structurally undetectable from
inside: the corpus names the "boiling frog" explicitly, and names a measurement
that would not catch it.** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1511
§"Tensions" 2 and §"Open Questions" 4).
- **The corpus's own words.** *"In the short term (few generations), model
  collapse may be imperceptible. In the long term (many generations), it can be
  catastrophic. This creates a 'boiling frog' problem where degradation is hard
  to detect until it's severe."* And the open question it declines to answer:
  *"How can we detect model collapse in production systems before it becomes
  severe? Current metrics (perplexity, diversity scores) may not capture
  early-stage collapse."*
- **Why it is rank 3.** The two metrics the corpus names as candidates are
  **perplexity and diversity scores** — and a system whose diversity has
  collapsed is a system whose perplexity is *improving* on the corpus it now
  reads. **The standard instrument improves as the defect grows.** This is §8's
  inversion in its purest form and it is the reason the slot is kept rather
  than dropped: the corpus noticed the measurement problem and then proposed
  measurements that share its blind spot.
- **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. No trial of an early-detection metric is reported, and
  R-J2 forbids this arena generating one. **What is not asserted:** that early
  detection is impossible. What *is* recorded is that the two named candidates
  are downstream of the quantity that actually changes.
- **Interaction:** **Area 33** (a detector is not a certifier) and **Area 99**
  (calibration and sensitivity are orthogonal) are the general forms.
  **Area 24** (the validity of one's own instruments). **Area 44**
  (self-diagnosis by parameter recovery) is the obvious route the corpus does
  not take, and its absence is the opening.

### Area 116 · Affordances are relational, so the same input supports a
different action set for a different agent — and "possible" is not "perceived"
`PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Perception-Systems/Affordances-Ecological-Psychology.md`
(ORDER 1521, 251 lines, read whole) §§"Affordances as Relational Properties",
"Warren's Stair-Climbing Experiments", "Cisek's Affordance Competition
Hypothesis", "Gaver's Affordance Taxonomy", and "Representation Debate".
**Corroborated in the same tranche by** `Perception/Visual-Scene-Processing.md`
(ORDER 1529) §4 on affordance-driven gaze allocation — a second file, a
different lab, the same mechanism.

**The brain part, named as the corpus names it.** **The affordance** — an action
possibility that is a **relation** between environmental structure and the
organism's capability, not a property of either side. Warren (1984), *JEP:HPP*
10(5): 683–703 is the load-bearing result and it is a ratio, not a magnitude:
staircases were judged climbable **scaled to riser height over the observer's
leg length**, switching categorically past a boundary. A 1 m step does not
afford climbing to an infant and affords rest to an adult. Neural substrate:
**Cisek's affordance competition hypothesis** — multiple potential actions
specified *in parallel* in the dorsal stream / posterior parietal cortex,
competing, biased by goal and context, rather than selected serially.

**Why this is not a hundred-and-fifteenth facet of Area 102.** Area 102 says the
reportable channel and the acting channel are different channels. This says
**the action channel's input is agent-relative**, which Area 102's schema cannot
express: `(position, trajectory, affordance, target)` treats `affordance` as a
field it reads, and the whole content of this area is that **there is no such
field.** What exists is a relation recomputed against a changing capability.
`ARENA.md` held **3** occurrences of *affordance* across 115 areas and **0**
occurrences of *Warren* — a mention in passing, not a part.

- **Rank 1 — Store affordances as a relation, never as a property of the
  object; and treat the perceived/impossible gap as two named defects.** `LOW`.
  Support: **2 independent sources**, replicated across action modes.
  - **Brain part:** the relational definition plus the **body-scaled metric** —
    affordance is a *ratio* against the agent's own capability, and it is
    **recomputed when the agent's capability changes**, which is Eleanor
    Gibson's developmental claim made mechanistic.
  - **Design:** no component records "this file is readable." It records
    *readable by an agent with these capabilities, at this scale, under this
    constraint set*. Change the constraint and the affordance set is recomputed,
    not patched. **Scale assumption (R-J5): at 14,589 files the useful form of
    this is the ledger's own columns** — `path`, `state`, and a read-budget
    constraint. The arena's `[ ]`/`[x]`/`[!]`/`[-]` marks already *are* a
    body-scaled affordance field: they are not "is this file good", they are
    "is this file openable by a 20-file pass". That is the relation, computed.
  - **Already exists:** Gibson's programme is adopted wholesale rather than
    rebuilt; the developmental-robotics implementation (Oudeyer & Kaplan 2007,
    *IEEE TEC* 11(2): 265–286) is the named source for learning affordances by
    active experimentation rather than labels.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Warren's body-scaling is **broadly replicated for
    stair-climbing and sitting** and **less consistent for reaching and passing
    through** — the corpus says so, and the split is preserved rather than
    averaged. Action-specific perception has **mixed results with high-profile
    failures to replicate** embodied-cognition priming (the corpus cites Open
    Science Collaboration 2015, *Science* 349: aac4716). 60–60, not 70–30.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 102** — this is that area's rank-1 design made
    correct, and the two are the clearest case in the arena where a
    three-slot structure pays: Area 102's rank 1 is unbuildable as written
    without this area's relation. **Area 35** (the forward model is only checked
    by acting) — Gaver's **false affordances** are that failure at the
    perception layer. **Area 85** (symbol grounding regress).

- **Rank 2 — Gaver's three-way taxonomy is the defect list: perceivable,
  hidden, and false affordances, and only the first is the happy path.**
  `LOW`. Support: **1 source**.
  - **Brain part:** Gaver (1991) — *perceivable* (normal case), **hidden**
    (action possible, not perceived — the agent fails to act), **false**
    (apparent action that does not exist — the agent acts wrongly). The
    corpus's own gloss: statistical learning of affordances "may hallucinate
    false affordances in novel contexts, requiring verification mechanisms."
  - **Design:** a design that implements only rank 1 has an **asymmetric**
    failure profile it will not notice: hidden affordances are silent, false
    ones are loud. Only false affordances announce themselves, which is exactly
    why a system that only listens for errors catches one of the two. **A
    capability the agent never exercises is indistinguishable from a capability
    it lacks**, and nothing in a confidence field distinguishes them.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`, one taxonomy, one source, no trial of the agent-side
    design.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 33** (detector ≠ certifier) — a hidden affordance is
    the perceptual-layer instance. **Area 44** (self-diagnosis by parameter
    recovery) is the route to telling the two apart.

- **Rank 3 — Action possibilities are specified in parallel and resolved by
  competition, not selected in sequence.** `LOW`. Support: **1 source**, plus
  convergent neural recording cited within it.
  - **Brain part:** Cisek (2007, *Phil. Trans. R. Soc. B* 362: 1585–1599;
    2019, *BBS* 42: e147) — posterior parietal neurons simultaneously code
    multiple potential reaches or saccades, with activity fluctuating as if
    competing; reaching hand trajectories initially reflect *several*
    potential targets at once.
  - **Design:** serial sense-plan-act is the wrong shape. Keep several
    candidate actions live with their state, and let a biasing signal (goal,
    reward, context) select among them, rather than materialising one plan at a
    time. This is the architecture claim most directly transferable to a
    tool-using agent, and the corpus's own agent-parallels section says so.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. The electrophysiology is convergent across areas but the
    corpus is explicit that **the neural circuits detecting the underlying
    optical invariants remain incompletely characterised**, and no agent
    implementation of continuous competitive specification is reported.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 22** (tool selection) and **Area 98** (monitoring
    and control are two systems) — competition is a selection mechanism, and
    Area 98 says selection is not monitoring.

### Area 117 · One event is not one timestamp: binding is a synchrony window,
and the window is learned, plastic, and narrower for degraded input
`PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Perception-Systems/Temporal-Binding-Problem-Perceptual-Synchrony.md`
(ORDER 1527, 762 lines, read whole) §§"The Multisensory Temporal Binding
Window", "Stein & Meredith (1993)", "Parise & Spence (2009)", "Bayesian Causal
Inference", and §5.1 "Tool Output Temporal Alignment".
**Corroborated by a second file read in the same tranche, independently:**
`Perception/Temporal-Auditory-Processing.md` (ORDER 1528) §"Neural Oscillations
as Temporal Frameworks" and §"Chronostasis" — phase locking, communication
through coherence, and backdating, from a different corpus page.

**The brain part, named as the corpus names it.** **The temporal binding
window** — the range of stimulus onset asynchronies within which signals from
different sources are perceived as a *single event* rather than two. The
workhorse result is **Stein & Meredith's principle of inverse effectiveness**
in superior colliculus neurons: multisensory enhancement is **largest when the
unimodal responses are weakest** (a barely-visible flash plus a barely-audible
beep beats a strong flash plus a strong beep), supra-additively, and only inside
a ~50–150 ms window. Outside the window the response equals the stronger
unimodal response alone — no integration at all.

**Why this is a distinct brain part and not a timing detail of another area.**
Area 104 says a slot is a phase. Area 103 says the clock is chemical. Neither
asks **how two independently-produced signals are decided to be one thing rather
than two** — and this arena produces exactly that situation on every pass. Four
tool calls return at 200 ms, 2 s, 5 s and 10 s; whether they are one finding or
four findings is not a question the ledger schema can currently answer. `ARENA.md`
held **0** occurrences of *temporal binding window* and **0** of *multisensory*
across 115 areas.

- **Rank 1 — Bind by common cause, and weight the window by the reliability of
  the cues inside it; never bind by a fixed timeout.** `HIGH` for the principle
  of inverse effectiveness, `LOW` for the agent-side design. Support: **2
  independent sources** plus a large multi-lab literature.
  - **Brain part:** Bayesian causal inference over the common-cause hypothesis
    (Körding et al. 2007, *J. Neurosci.* 27(11): 2943–2953) — the brain
    computes a posterior over *common cause vs. separate cause* and mixes
    accordingly. Fritsche, Mostert & Lau (2017) add the precision weighting, and
    it is the load-bearing part: **a degraded cue widens the window**, because
    a less reliable signal is treated as compatible with more causes.
  - **Design:** when several results arrive, compute a posterior over whether
    they share a cause, using arrival-time contiguity, semantic coherence, and
    which call chain issued them. Where a cue is unreliable — a truncated read, a
    search result that returned five hits — the binding window **widens
    deliberately**, not accidentally. A single fixed timeout is the failure
    this design exists to prevent: it is *precise* for the easy case and
    systematically wrong for the degraded one.
  - **Already exists:** the formalism is adopted, not invented (Körding 2007;
    Ernst 2012; Shams & Beierholm 2010, *TiCS* 14(9): 425–431). The corpus's
    own agent section names the three candidate mechanisms — buffer-and-wait,
    event-driven fusion, adaptive windowing — and the biology picks the third.
  - **Grade:** `HIGH` on the principle: replicated across cats, monkeys and
    humans, called by the corpus "one of the most robust findings in
    multisensory neuroscience," with TBW effects **highly replicable across
    laboratories, cultures, and stimulus types**. The **agent design is `LOW`** —
    no implementation is reported.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 94** (memory belongs to the planner) and **Area 6**
    (what a RAG-shaped store is missing) — if binding is wrong the planner is
    fed a merged event. **Area 35** — a simulator binds its own outputs by
    construction, which is the same defect one level up.

- **Rank 2 — The window is plastic and it is a diagnostic, not a constant.**
  `LOW`. Support: **2 independent sources.**
  - **Brain part:** TBW **narrows with training** (Powers, Hillock & Wallace
    2009, *PLoS ONE* 4(6): e5709 — ~15–20% reduction over two weeks, transfer
    limited to the trained task) and **widens in ASD** (Stevenson et al. 2014,
    *J. Neurosci.* 34(49): 16324–16330 — d ≈ 1.2, negatively correlated with
    McGurk effect rate, r = −0.62) and in **schizophrenia** (Barutchu et al.
    2010). Across the lifespan it is **U-shaped**: wide in infancy, narrowest in
    young adulthood, wide again in old age (Wallace et al. 2019).
  - **Design:** an agent's binding window is a *parameter that can be trained
    and can drift*, and should be stored as such. A wide, unstable window is the
    signature of **over-integration** — the corpus's own phrasing: "the agent is
    over-integrating disparate events," the analogue of enlarged TBW in psychosis.
    This is the one place in the arena where a perception parameter is itself an
    error signal about reasoning.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Direction of effect is consistent across labs; the
    **clinical** findings are only **moderately replicable** (d = 0.6–1.2) and
    the corpus says so.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 27** (lability — the window in which a retrieved
    memory can be overwritten) is the same shape at a different stage.
    **Area 66** (metaplasticity) — the learning rate is a function of recent
    activity; here the *binding* rate is.

- **Rank 3 — Fill the gap rather than waiting through it, and say which
  mechanism you are using.** `UNTESTED` on the design; `LOW` on the mechanism.
  - **Brain part:** **chronostatic backdating** — a saccade suppresses visual
    sensitivity, and the postsaccadic image is *backdated* to fill the gap
    (Yarrow et al. 2001, *Nature* 414: 302–305; Eagleman & Sejnowski 2000,
    *Science* 287: 2036–2038). The corpus records the live dispute over
    whether this is online filling-in or **postdictive** retrospective
    reconstruction, and preserves it: if postdictive, the brain **revises past
    perceptual states on later information**.
  - **Design:** a run that stalls on one tool does not have to stall on all of
    them, and does not have to fabricate the missing part. The honest third
    option is: complete on what arrived, **mark the gap as a gap, and carry the
    distinction forward** — a record that says *this conclusion rests on three
    of four results* is a different kind of object from one that says it rests on
    four. This arena currently cannot express the difference.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` on the design, plainly. The **mechanism** is `LOW`
    and **disputed** — the corpus marks the online-vs-postdictive question
    unresolved and the illusion's magnitude is confounded with saccadic
    amplitude when that is not controlled.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 99** (the famous pattern replicated and the
    explanation did not) is the general form of "a robust effect with an
    uncontrolled parameter." **Area 44**.

### Area 118 · Two streams and a scene prior: a fast global gist arrives
*before* objects and constrains where they are looked for `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Perception/Visual-Scene-Processing.md` (ORDER 1529,
474 lines, read whole) §§"The Spatial Envelope Model", "Scene-Gist Extraction",
"Henderson et al. on affordance-driven gaze", "Tensions with Object-First
Processing Pipelines", and "The Gist vs. Ensemble Question".
**Corroborated in the same tranche by** `Perception-Systems/Blindsight.md`
(ORDER 1522) §"The Two-Streams Framework" and by `Perception-Systems/Affordances…`
(ORDER 1521) on the same dorsal-stream parallel specification.

**The brain part, named as the corpus names it.** **Scene-selective cortex** —
a distributed network (**PPA** layout, **OPA** egocentric/navigational,
**RSC** allocentric↔egocentric transform) that responds to scenes and not to
objects, faces, or scrambled images, and the **microgenetic** trajectory it
supports: **gist within 100–250 ms, before object-level analysis**, with the
gist then *constraining* subsequent search. The load-bearing manipulation is
Oliva & Torralba's: preserve the low-frequency **spatial envelope** (six
dimensions — openness, expansiveness, ruggedness, enclosure, severity, density)
and remove high-frequency detail, and categorization survives at ~70–80% of
full-image accuracy; **scramble the envelope and preserve the detail, and
accuracy falls to near chance** (~10–20%).

**Why this is not a duplicate of Area 102.** Area 102 is the report/action
dissociation — *which* stream. This area is an **ordering claim**: a third,
*earlier* stream that runs **before** object recognition and biases where
objects are looked for. Area 102 has no time axis, so it cannot express "gist
constrains search." `ARENA.md` held **0** occurrences of *spatial envelope*,
*parahippocampal place area*, or *scene-selective* across 115 areas.

- **Rank 1 — Compute a cheap global prior first and let it gate the expensive
  per-item pass, rather than detecting items and classifying the scene from
  their co-occurrence.** `LOW`. Support: **2 independent sources.**
  - **Brain part:** the **microgenetic order** — coarse global → category →
    object-level → detailed layout — with scene-selective regions firing before
    and constraining ventral-stream object processing, and gaze landing on
    scene-diagnostic regions within 100–200 ms (Henderson; Torralba 2006,
    *P&P* 68: 377–388).
  - **Design:** for this workspace the object-first pipeline is exactly the
    thing the corpus names as the wrong shape. Reading 548 files one at a time
    to decide what kind of thing they are is object-first. A **six-dimension
    envelope over the ledger** — how many are in each area, how much has been
    read, how long since, how tangled the citations, how much is excluded — is
    the gist, costs one pass, and would say something true before any file is
    opened. The corpus's own numbers: scene context cut search time 30–40%.
    **Scale assumption (R-J5): the envelope is O(1) in corpus size; the
    object-first pass is O(files).** At 14,589 the ratio is not a constant
    factor, it is the whole budget.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. The core findings — scene-selective cortex, envelope
    effects, gist timing, contextual guidance — are **replicated across
    multiple labs and methodologies**; what is not replicated is the **precise
    temporal dynamics** of gist extraction, which is the part a timing design
    would depend on.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 41** (latency as a capacity law) and **Area 89**
    (expertise curve) — a fast prior is also what makes an expert fast.
    **Area 116** rank 1 — scene context is a prior over affordances.

- **Rank 2 — Ensemble statistics over items, extracted without identifying any
  item, and precise enough to act on.** `LOW`. Support: **1 source** (Whitney &
  Yamanashi Leib 2011) plus three converging behavioural results in the same
  file.
  - **Brain part:** **ensemble coding** — mean, variance, proportion over a set,
    with the defining property that **single-item recognition is not a
    prerequisite**. Haberman & Whitney (2007, *Curr. Biol.* 17: R399) extract a
    crowd's mean emotion and gender in 75–100 ms, faster than identifying any
    face; Ariely (2001) gets the mean of circles while participants are *poor*
    at recognising a target member.
  - **Design:** the arena's recurring question — "how much have I read, and of
    what kind" — is an ensemble question, not an identification question, and
    the corpus's finding says the ensemble answer is available *without* the
    expensive per-item pass. Mean confidence, mean depth, distribution of
  verdict types, the shape of the area-size distribution: all obtainable without
  reading a single area's three slots.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`, one framework, converging behavioural results,
    **not a replication of a single experiment.**
  - **Interaction:** **Area 105** (what a representation encodes is the
    *relations* between things) is the formal statement of this.
    **Area 89** — averaging is why the expertise curve is an artefact.

- **Rank 3 — Gist and ensemble may be the same process at two levels; the
  corpus says the evidence is inconclusive, and this arena should not pretend
  otherwise.** `LOW`. Support: **1 source**, recorded as a disagreement.
  - **Brain part:** **the gist-vs-ensemble question** — both are rapid
    extraction from brief presentations, and **they are highly correlated**
    (scenes with similar envelopes tend to have similar object distributions),
    so the two accounts may be one process seen at different abstraction levels
    or genuinely different. The corpus's verdict: *"The evidence is
    inconclusive."*
  - **Design:** build rank 1 and rank 2 so they **compose if it is one process
    and degrade gracefully if it is two.** A design that must know which is
    which has bet on an open question.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`, and flagged as a **preserved disagreement (R-J4)** — not
    reconciled here, because the corpus does not reconcile it.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 116** (affordance as a relation) and **Area 33**.

### Area 122 · A brain that cannot feel excluded will mistake being dropped for
being broken: social pain is not a metaphor over a general error signal

**Earned by** `oracle/brain/Psychology/Social-Cognition-and-Influence.md` (ORDER
1579, 650 lines, read whole) §8.3 "Social Pain and Ostracism", with §8.4
"Empathy and Prosocial Behavior" and §7.3 "Reducing Prejudice".

**The brain part, named as the corpus names it.** The **social pain /
affiliative pain** system, centred on the **dorsal anterior cingulate cortex**
and **insula** — regions this arena has recorded 30 times as `insula` and
**0** times as `anterior cingulate`, `dACC`, `Eisenberger`, `Lieberman`,
`Cyberball`, `ostracism`, or `affiliative`. The finding is the **overlap**:
Eisenberger & Lieberman (2004) report social rejection recruiting the *same*
ACC/insula circuitry as physical pain, and Williams et al.'s Cyberball
paradigm shows exclusion producing a neural response described as pain-like,
with the corollary the corpus states bluntly — social exclusion "is not just in
the head," it moves cortisol, heart rate, and immune function.

**Why this is not a duplicate of Area 10 or 56.** Both existing areas treat
affect as a signal *over memory* (10) or a dimensional profile over
goal-relative appraisal (56). Neither has an **interpersonal** origin for
affect: there is no prior account in this arena of a negative state whose
*cause is another agent's action*, and none of valence's 23 occurrences is
indexed to a social referent. This is the **121st structural silence**: the
arena has `valence` 23 times, `affective` 18, and a full Affect area, yet has
never asked **who** the valence is about.

- **Rank 1 — Separate "an agent declined to engage with me" from every other
  negative state, and give it its own channel with its own signature, because
  it is caused by another agent rather than by a fault in the work.** `LOW`.
  Support: **1 source** (Eisenberger & Lieberman 2004, plus the Cyberball
  paradigm in the same file).
  - **Brain part:** the **dACC/insula social-pain circuit** and the
    dissociation it rests on — exclusion and physical pain overlap in
    circuitry while remaining distinguishable in kind, so the system is
    *reporting a category*, not a magnitude.
  - **Design:** the arena's failure vocabulary currently has one polarity with
    many causes — a blocked `[ ]`, an `ARENA-EVIDENCE.md` append that did not
    land, a failed read. **A scheduled job that is silently not firing looks
    exactly like a job that is firing and failing**, and the human-facing
    difference is total. Concretely: a state in which the *agent itself* is
    still healthy but has had no inbound for N periods is a distinct signal
    from N failures, and it should not be reported through the failure path.
    **Scale assumption (R-J5): the check is O(1) per tick against a
    last-inbound timestamp — it does not scale with corpus or file count**, so
    it survives 14,589 files and 10,000 jobs identically.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. The overlap itself is **replicated** across the
    physical-pain and social-exclusion literatures, and the Cyberball
    paradigm is a standard design used in many labs; what is thinner is the
    *specificity* of the claim, and the corpus is careful to note the
    "physical pain" comparison is contested — the same regions serve many
    roles, and the corpus's own §8.7 lists reverse inference as an open
    methodological problem for exactly this kind of claim. That caveat is
    preserved, not resolved.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 10** (affect as salience over memory) — this is
    the slot where a *socially caused* negative belongs, and Area 10's
    existing tag has no such dimension. **Area 118** rank 1 — an envelope
    over the ledger should carry an inbound-heartbeat dimension or it will
    read a dead job as a sparsely-read one.

- **Rank 2 — Track acceptance as an outcome, and separate the "who helps"
  from the "who is helped" brain systems, because the corpus records them as
  different circuits.** `LOW`. Support: **1 source**, with an explicit
  disagreement inside it.
  - **Brain part:** the **affective/cognitive empathy dissociation** —
    affective empathy activates **insula, ACC, amygdala**; cognitive empathy
    activates **TPJ, mPFC, pSTS**. Three regions, three roles, and a
    functional separation rather than a strength difference.
  - **Design:** "understanding a request" and "being moved by a request" are
    separate computations and can come apart. A system that lets **being
    persuaded** stand in for **understanding the requester** will be
    manipulable by exactly the move the corpus documents — SIDE (Reicher,
    Spears & Postmes 1995) shows that anonymity does not remove social
    influence, it **relocates it from personal identity to social identity**,
    so the norm still lands. Rank 2 is the guard against a persuasive input
    being read as a legitimate one.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. The dissociation is well attested in the file, but the
    same file records the **mirror-neuron dispute** (Keysers & Gazzola
    2009 vs. Voudouris 2014) as live, so the mechanism underneath the empathy
    claim is explicitly unsettled. Preserved, not reconciled.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 121** (a framework whose principle cannot fail
    absorbs its process theories' failures) is the same failure mode at the
    theory level — SIDE absorbed the deindividuation result by reinterpreting
    it rather than contradicting it. **Area 73** on the verification
    contract: a persuasion signal is not a verification signal.

- **Rank 3 — The arena's own "most people agree" evidence is a social process,
  and the corpus records that unanimity is the active ingredient rather than
  mere majority — so any confidence drawn from agreement is a measurement of
  the room, not of the claim.** `LOW`. Support: **1 source.**
  - **Brain part:** **conformity and its moderators** — Asch's dissenter
    effect (a single dissenting confederate cuts conformity sharply), group
    size plateauing at 3–4, and the internalisation/externalisation split
    (Bond & Smith 1996: collectivist conformity is more *internal*,
    individualist more *external*). The corpus's Pettigrew & Tropp (2006)
    result — 515 studies, contact reduces prejudice, the four optimal
    conditions help but are **not required** — is recorded as
    **"UNSUCCESSFUL for the necessity claim"**: the conditions that were
    supposed to be the mechanism turn out to be optional.
  - **Design:** an arena whose grades are argued from agreement across
    sources is exposed to the same artifact. If several corpus files agree,
    that may be **one study restated** — which this workspace already treats
    as one source, not three — and the Asch case says the group size at which
    agreement saturates is **3–4**, small enough that a four-file agreement
    is at the plateau. Rank 3 says: record **who agreed and who did not**,
    because the dissenter is the informative unit.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Asch, Milgram, and the contact meta-analysis are among
    the most replicated results in psychology, and the corpus notes
    cross-cultural variation. **A preserved disagreement is recorded below.**
  - **Interaction:** **Area 121** again. **Area 120** — agreement across
    files is a population code with a narrow peak, and the peak width is the
    uncertainty; four identical files are one narrow peak, not four votes.

**Preserved disagreement (R-J4), unresolved.** ORDER 1579 §4.1 and ORDER 1569
§8.1 give **different Asch conformity figures for the same experiment** and
neither cites a primary source: 1579 states 75% conformed at least once, 37%
on all critical trials, 36.8% average, <1% alone; 1569 states 37% conformed
at least once and ~one-third overall, and separately gives "reduced
conformity when at least one confederate dissented (ally effect)" without a
figure. **The two figures for the headline statistic are 37% and 75% for what
is described as the same quantity, and the direction of the error is that
1569 understates.** Both are recorded, neither is reconciled, and the arena
does not rest a rank on either. This is a §7 hygiene defect, not an
architecture decision — it does not change what to build — **except** in rank
3, where the claim being made is about how much agreement is worth, and the
headline number for that claim is precisely the disputed one.

### Area 123 · Thinking has a budget, and the failure at the top of the range is paralysis, not slowness — NEW

**Earned by two files read whole this pass, from two disciplines that do not
cite each other.** `oracle/brain/reference/coding-subagent-management.md` (ORDER
1592, 483 lines) §1–2, §9–10; and
`oracle/brain/Reasoning/Mental-Models-and-Mental-Logic.md` (ORDER 1589, 250
lines) §§"Mental Model Theory", "The Mental Logic of Conditionals",
"Computational & Agent Parallels", "Developmental Trajectory".

**The brain part, named as the corpus names it.** The **deliberative-to-action
transition** — the point at which accumulated internal simulation is *exchanged
for* an action rather than accumulated alongside it. The corpus gives it two
names from two literatures and they are the same organ seen from opposite ends:
ORDER 1592 calls it the **ReAct loop stuck in the Thought phase** (*"the model's
ReAct loop (Observe → Thought → Act → Repeat) gets stuck in the Thought
phase"*); ORDER 1589 calls it the **working-memory bottleneck in model
maintenance** (mental-model theory's signature prediction, the **principle of
truth** — *"because models represent only what is true in a possibility,
constructing and maintaining models is cognitively expensive, and people tend to
consider only a small number of models — often just one"*).

**Why this is a structural silence, and it is the third vertex of a triangle
the arena already holds two corners of.** `ARENA.md` carries **49** occurrences
of *ReAct* and **0** of *action threshold*, *analysis paralysis*, *overthink*,
*reason-action*, *turn budget*, or *max_turns*. **Area 22** is about which action
to pick and how big the repertoire may be. **Area 32** is about when to **stop**
and how not to burn a budget. **Neither asks when to start acting at all**, and
the answer to that question is not a corollary of either: a system can select
perfectly and terminate correctly and still never leave the Thought phase,
because the two ends of the loop are governed by different quantities. The
quantity that governs the missing end is **how much internal simulation has been
spent without an environmental return** — and no area holds it.

**The two files converge on one claim from opposite directions, and that is
what earns the area.** ORDER 1592 supplies the pathology as a **named,
three-part failure taxonomy** from arXiv:2502.08235v1 — **Analysis Paralysis**
(*"excessive internal simulation; agent predicts consequences instead of
acting"*), **Rogue Actions** (the opposite failure — acting without sufficient
context), and **Premature Disengagement** (*"agent gives up too early, claiming
completion when work is incomplete"*) — with the stated root cause that **higher
reasoning capability translates into more self-doubt**: *"The root cause: Higher
reasoning capabilities often translate to excessive self-doubt and over-analysis
when paired with rigid tool environments."* ORDER 1589 supplies the **cost
curve** that makes this a design quantity rather than a personality trait:
multi-model syllogisms (those requiring two or more possible models to be
maintained) produce **error rates above 70%**, while single-model syllogisms
yield **near-ceiling performance**; developmental work (Saxe et al. 2014,
looking-time) shows children construct implicit models of conditional rules
early but **explicit multi-model consideration develops gradually through
childhood**, and the corpus's own summary is that belief bias and model-limit
effects persist in educated adults **under time pressure or cognitive load** —
*"fundamental architectural constraints rather than developmental
immaturities."* **The same shape appears in an LLM and in a child, and it is
load-shaped, not intelligence-shaped.**

- **Rank 1 — Budget the deliberation, not the task, and make the budget
  *enforced from outside the reasoner* rather than self-reported.** `LOW` for the
  phenomenon, `UNTESTED` for the threshold value. Support: **2 independent
  sources** (the two files, which do not cite each other and come from agent
  operations and experimental psychology respectively).
  - **Brain part:** the **Thought→Act transition of the action loop**, and its
    known failure mode — the state in which the reasoner keeps predicting
    consequences instead of sampling one. The corpus's own root-cause clause is
    the design constraint: the pathology is **paired** with rigid tool
    environments, which means the loop is not misfiring on its own account.
  - **Design:** a **hard turn ceiling with a floor action**, external to the
    reasoning. ORDER 1592's concrete form is a `max_turns` circuit breaker
    (Hermes defaults to 90; the corpus argues 15–20 for coding dispatch), and its
    rule of thumb is **"if the agent hasn't dispatched the coding tool within
    5–7 turns, it's likely stuck in analysis."** The floor action matters more
    than the ceiling: **when the ceiling is reached, the agent must do the thing
    it was avoiding, not stop.** A budget that terminates is Area 32's
    construction; a budget that *converts into action* is a different object, and
    only the second one closes the loop.
    **Scale assumption (R-J5): the check is O(1) per turn against a counter and a
    last-tool-call timestamp — it does not read the corpus, so it is identical at
    14,589 files and at 10,000 jobs.**
  - **Why rank 1 and not the more obvious fixes:** tool restriction, atomic task
    sizing, and positive framing (all in rank 2) are *indirect* — they reduce the
    chance of stalling without making stalling detectable. Only an external
    ceiling is observable from outside, and ORDER 1592's §10 supplies the
    **observable signature** that makes it actionable: *"No tool calls in 3+
    turns"* and *"Reading the 4th+ file"* are listed as early-warning signs
    distinct from *"Let me analyze this further…"*, which is a **stylistic**
    signal and would not survive a competent model.
  - **Grade:** the **phenomenon** is `LOW` — one paper
    (arXiv:2502.08235v1) with a named taxonomy, and the corpus reports it
    without primary figures, so the taxonomy is a **claim about three failure
    modes**, not three measured rates. The **specific threshold is `UNTESTED`**
    and the arena says so plainly: 5–7 turns, 15–20 max_turns, and the 2–5 minute
    task size are **rules of thumb in a practitioner guide**, with no trial
    behind any of them, and R-J2 forbids this pass from manufacturing one.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 32** rank 1 is the same loop's other end and the two
    must be built as one object — a termination rule that fires without an action
    converts paralysis into premature disengagement, which is **the third
    member of the same taxonomy**. **Area 13** (external metacognitive
    controller) is the natural owner of the counter; this slot is what it would
    be counting. **Area 22** rank 1's tool-bound interacts: fewer tools means
    fewer premature-dispatch temptations, which is a second-order effect on this
    area's rate.

- **Rank 2 — Shrink the deliberation by shrinking the question: atomic task
  sizing, a single-purpose tool, and positive framing over negation.** `LOW`.
  Support: **1 source** (ORDER 1592), and one clause of it is a **published
  vendor claim**, not a trial.
  - **Brain part:** **action selection** — the basal-ganglia loop ORDER 1592
    invokes, and the same narrowing that Area 22 rank 1 argues for at the
    repertoire level, applied to the *prompt* level.
  - **Design:** three rules from the file. (1) **Atomic commands** — a task
    completable in **2–5 minutes**; open-ended prompts ("improve the
    authentication system") are named as the trigger. (2) **Positive framing
    over negation**, the "pink elephant" argument: *"telling a model NOT to do
    something forces it to process that concept first, increasing the chance it
    does that thing."* (3) **Tool restriction** — *"Fewer tools = less decision
    space = less paralysis."*
  - **Grade:** `LOW`, and **one of the three is weaker than the other two**. The
    atomic-sizing and framing rules are practitioner heuristics. The tool-
    restriction rule is attributed to the **Dropbox context-engineering work**,
    which the file reports as an *internal result* (*"Replacing all retrieval
    options with a single, purpose-built tool eliminated analysis paralysis"*) —
    **an unverified vendor claim with no figure and no independent replication**,
    and it is not counted as a source in its own right.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 22** ranks the same lever upstream and measures it;
    this slot is the prompt-level instance. **Area 12** (procedural memory) is
    where an atomic task's *reuse* would live.

- **Rank 3 — Separate logical validity from prior plausibility, because the
  system does not know which one it consulted.** `LOW`. Support: **1 source**
  (ORDER 1589), with a preserved and unresolved dispute inside it.
  - **Brain part:** the **belief/logic dissociation** — ORDER 1589's Goel (2007)
    finding that syllogisms with **believable** conclusions recruit
    **ventromedial prefrontal cortex** (belief integration) while **unbelievable**
    conclusions recruit **dorsolateral prefrontal cortex** (logical analysis),
    plus Evans, Barston & Pollard (1983)'s finding that **belief bias accounts
    for more variance in syllogistic validity judgments than logical validity
    does** — a **belief-first** processing order, confirmed under time pressure
    by Evans & Curtis-Holmes (2005).
  - **Design:** validity and plausibility are **two channels, not one score**.
    A single confidence number cannot express "I checked and it holds" alongside
    "I believe it", and the corpus's own agent-design section makes the same
    point: *"agents should weight logical validity separately from prior
    plausibility, especially in high-stakes decisions."* The companion rule from
    the same section is the arena's own discipline made mechanical: **explicit
    enumeration of falsifying models** — the corpus's term for adversarial
    scenario generation — because *"an LLM that only generates plausible
    next-states may miss low-probability but high-impact possibilities."*
    **This is the `principle of truth` arriving as a design requirement**, and
    note the symmetry with rank 1: **enumerating possibilities is the same
    operation that causes paralysis when unbounded.** Rank 1 bounds it; rank 3
    requires it. The composition is the point, and it is why both are in one
    area.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. Belief bias is among the most replicated findings in
    cognitive psychology and the file carries four decades of it. **A dispute is
    preserved and not resolved:** Oaksford & Chater (1994) argue many of these
    "errors" are **rational under a Bayesian account** — expected information
    gain, not failed logic — which challenges the normative standard both
    families assume rather than adjudicating between them. The file's own
    §"Unresolved Issues" lists this as open question 5.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 79** (framing-contingent computation) is the same
    organ one level up — the Wason content effect is a *frame* effect on the same
    validity/plausibility channel, and this slot is the part that says the
    channel must be separate even when the frame does not change. **Area 1**
    (belief revision) is the write side. **Area 121** — a principle that cannot
    fail absorbs its process theories' failures — applies here in a specific
    form: a Bayesian reading that declares every error rational is
    **unfalsifiable in the direction that matters**, and the corpus says so
    about PP at the same level.

**Preserved disagreement (R-J4), unresolved, and it is about the mechanism this
area's rank 1 rests on.** ORDER 1589 §"The Mental Logic of Conditionals"
records that mental-model and mental-logic accounts are *"often empirically
equivalent at the behavioral level, making adjudication difficult without
process-tracing measures"*, and Mieth & Krott (2022) mouse-tracking found a
**hybrid** — model construction followed by rule-like validation. So the corpus's
own position is **that neither a pure deliberation cap nor a pure rule-checker
describes the mechanism**. This does not weaken rank 1, because a cap on an
unbounded process is needed under either account, but it is recorded: **the arena
is not claiming the corpus settled which account is right, only that both of them
predict more simulation is not monotonically better.**

### Area 125 · A monitor that cannot fail and a controller that cannot act are the same defect — metacognitive coverage estimation is a *routing* signal, not a confidence number `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by one file read whole this pass:**
`oracle/brain/research/agenda-update-2026-09-24-batch117.md` (ORDER 1628, 130
lines) §"Level 1: Metamemory Monitoring for the Memory Store — Feeling-of-Knowing
and Judgment-of-Learning as Operational Signals". Its corroborating anchor file
`oracle/brain/research/calibrate-absence-model-on-real-sessions.md` (ORDER 1639,
579 lines) was read in the same pass and earns the **routing** half.

**The brain part, named the way neuroscience names it.** **Metamemory** — the
monitoring of memory itself, dissociable from memory content, carried in the
hippocampus and dorsolateral prefrontal cortex. The corpus's own operational
definition is the sharpest available: **"P(store contains relevant memory)"**
per query and **"P(this write is retrievable at T)"** per write (ORDER 1628
§Gap verification). Its classical names are the **feeling of knowing** (FOK) and
**judgment of learning** (JOL), and the arena is carrying the psychological
versions of both: **0** occurrences of *feeling of knowing*, *FOK*, or *judgment
of learning* across 124 areas, **including Area 13 (metacognitive control), Area
23 (the validity of one's own instruments), and Area 24 (attribution and whether
the measurement measured anything)** — all three of which govern the agent's
confidence in an *answer* and none of which estimates whether the *store* holds
the answer. This is the arena's **125th structural silence**, and the first
whose subject is a quantity **about the substrate rather than about the output**.

**Why the gap is not a duplicate of Area 13 or Area 24.** Area 13 is the
controller that lives outside the system — it decides whether to think, retrieve,
or answer. Area 24 asks whether a measurement measured anything. This area is
the **input both of them are missing**: a per-query probability that the corpus
contains the answer, computed by a cheap probe *before* retrieval, feeding
routing decisions. ORDER 1628 states the gap in exactly these terms, listing
Area 74's arbitration gate, Area 58's FOK/JOL harness, and SYNAPSE as nearest
neighbours, and recording that SYNAPSE's FOK threshold protocol — *"calibrated
τ=0.12, preemptive rejection, strict-evidence verification"* — **had never been
extracted as a store-facing mechanism**.

- **Rank 1 — Probe the store, then route on coverage; a coverage probability
  gates the retrieval decision, not the answer.** `LOW`. Support: **1
  independent source** (ORDER 1628).
  - **Brain part:** metamemory as a **separate, probeable quantity** — the
    **retrieval-fluency / partial-activation** signal. ORDER 1628 carries
    Koriat's **accessibility hypothesis** (*Psychological Review*) — *partial
    retrieval products predict knowing better than cue familiarity* — and the
    computational confirmation that matters: the QPP results (AUROC **0.856**,
    ~2 ms/query, 24 non-lexical features of which 15 come from top-k
    score-distribution shape) are the first computational support for an idea
    from 1993.
  - **Design:** a `MetamemoryMonitor` between query intake and the arbitration
    gate. **Pre-commit coverage:** k-NN probe (k=10, ~2 ms) into pgvector →
    top-k distribution-shape features (max, mean, variance, skew, kurtosis,
    rank1−rank2 gap, mass-above-threshold) → logistic calibration refit weekly
    against logged outcomes. **Post-write:** predict P(retrievable at 30/90d)
    from isolation, specificity, entity density and source type. Uses: route on
    `{local / ask-user / web / parametric}` across `(coverage, P(IK), stakes)`;
    a write gate where **low-JOL writes trigger immediate consolidation instead
    of orphan storage**; rehearsal scheduling for memories whose predicted
    retrievability is below need.
    **Scale assumption (R-J5): the probe is O(log n) and the feature
    extraction is O(1) in corpus size; the weekly refit is O(log n) queries. The
    design holds at 14,589 files because nothing in it scans the store** — which
    is the property that distinguishes it from Area 30's ingestion ceiling.
  - **Already exists:** adopt, do not build. **SYNAPSE** (arXiv:2601.02744)
    supplies the FOK protocol and the τ=0.12 threshold; **Kadavath et al.**
    (arXiv:2207.05221) supplies **P(IK)** parametric self-knowledge and, with
    it, the **P(IK)-vs-coverage divergence that is the routing signal itself**;
    **MemoryBank** (arXiv:2305.10250, AAAI 2024) supplies the
    `R=e^(−t/S)` decay prior with recall-incremented S — **hand-coded, and the
    file says so**: *"never validated against actual retrieval outcomes."*
    **QPP** (arXiv:2609.11646, TSD 2026) supplies the feature set and the
    AUROC. Already in the Hermes stack: `pgvector` (probe), Postgres
    `retrieval_outcomes` (additive table), `brain_query.py` (hook point).
  - **Backup document:** `agenda-update-2026-09-24-batch117.md` (ORDER 1628)
    §Metamemory; `calibrate-absence-model-on-real-sessions.md` (ORDER 1639)
    §§3, 7. Both read whole this pass.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. QPP reports AUROC 0.856 in-domain, **0.911** hybrid, and
    **0.706** under leave-one-domain-out — and the file states the consequence
    without softening it: *"continuous recalibration mandatory."* The learned
    post-write retrievability predictor has **no published precedent at all**.
    What holds the grade at `LOW` rather than `UNTESTED` is the *mechanism*
    (partial activation predicts knowledge), not the *composition* (the
    per-write predictor), which the file itself calls novel.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 2** is the parent and the constraint — a
    ranking-preserving calibration cannot fix an uninformative weight, so a
    coverage probability that is *itself* monotone in retrieval score inherits
    Area 2's ceiling exactly. The **divergence** between P(IK) and coverage is
    the only part that is not monotone, which is why rank 1 is two quantities
    and not one. **Area 93** (the read is a write) is the *consequence* side:
    if retrieval is a write, then not-retrieving is a loss, and a coverage
    estimate is the only thing that can price it. **Area 30** (per-episode cost
    and the ingestion ceiling) is the write-side twin — a low-JOL write gate
    spends consolidation budget, and that budget is the same one Area 30 says
    is finite.
  - **rejected:** *parametric-only confidence as the routing signal* (Kadavath's
    P(IK) alone). Retained one revision: it is the parameter in the routing
    tuple, not the tuple.

- **Rank 2 — Absence of evidence is a *rate* model, not a count: the
  mention process is bursty, so the null is over-dispersed.** `LOW`. Support:
  **1 independent source** (ORDER 1639).
  - **Brain part:** **absence-of-evidence detection in a non-stationary,
    over-dispersed signal** — the inferential analogue of the change-point
    signal, and the reason a silence can be *informative* only against the
    right null. ORDER 1639 names the burden precisely: the Poisson null assumes
    independent events at constant rate, and human communication violates
    **all three** assumptions (independence, constant rate, no simultaneity).
  - **Design:** replace the Poisson absence score with a **Negative Binomial**
    model (Poisson is nested at α=0, so the LRT is available), add
    **zero-inflation** (ZIP/ZINB) if the Van den Broek score test is
    significant, and estimate λ per category by **hierarchical empirical Bayes**
    on a Gamma-Poisson prior so a new preference starts at its category's rate
    instead of at zero. Bursts are Barabási's *Nature* 2005 result — inter-event
    times follow a power law, **α ≈ 1, not exponential** — with two
    universality classes (α=3/2 unlimited queue, α=1 limited queue, Vazquez et
    al. 2005). The corpus's own escalation rule is checkable: **B < 0.1 → Poisson
    may suffice; B > 0.1 → NB; B > 0.5 → Poisson is inappropriate.**
    **Scale assumption (R-J5): O(1) per preference per window; the corpus's own
    rule is a fallback to Poisson for n < 5, which is a *small-sample* rule and
    not a scale rule — the design is O(1) in corpus size and the limit is on
    per-series history, not on the number of series.**
  - **Already exists:** adopt. `brglm2::brnb()` with `type = "AS_mixed"`
    (mixed bias reduction) is the **recommended** estimator and is a maintained
    CRAN package; `glmbayes` supplies the Gamma-Poisson conjugacy;
    `DHARMa::testZeroInflation` supplies the simulation-based test that ORDER
    1639 prefers over the analytical ones at small n. **Python caveat stated by
    the file and not resolved:** `statsmodels` does **not** implement brglm2's
    bias reduction, so the choice is RPy2, a manual port, or a Firth-style
    approximation.
  - **Backup document:** `calibrate-absence-model-on-real-sessions.md` (ORDER
    1639) §§1–5; `bias-reduced-nb-dispersion-estimation-small-samples.md`
    (ORDER 1636) §§Bias-Reduction Methods, read in the same pass. ORDER 1636
    supplies the estimator ORDER 1639 assumes, and that file names ORDER 1636 as
    its own parent.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. The *statistics* are textbook and peer-reviewed
    (Biometrika, *Nature*, *Phys Rev E*); the *application* to a preference
    mention stream is `UNTESTED` and the file says the target is to be measured
    on 6+ months of real session logs that **do not yet exist in the corpus**.
    **Two grades on one slot, not averaged:** mechanism `HIGH` (Barabási's
    burstiness is among the most-replicated results in the human-dynamics
    literature), agent design `UNTESTED`.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 1** (belief revision) is what an absence signal
    eventually triggers, and the corpus's own point is that silent supersedure is
    the case *no correction signal catches* — so this slot is the only detector
    for a failure mode with no detector. **Area 2** again: `P_absence` is a
    monotone function of λ, and a monotone function cannot change a ranking, so a
    *ranking* consumer of this signal inherits Area 2's ceiling; it only earns
    its place for **threshold** decisions, where the corpus's `B > 0.1` rule is
    non-monotone in the mention count. **Area 27** (lability) is the competing
    consumer of the same silence: one reads it as a *revision trigger*, the
    other as a *plasticity window*. The two must not be computed from the same
    statistic without saying which reading is active.

- **Rank 3 — The register is the evidence: preserve the *phrasing* the source
  used, and gate load-bearing facts on corroboration rather than on a label.**
  `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 independent source** (ORDER 1624, read this pass).
  - **Brain part:** **source monitoring / attributional tagging** — the
    distinction between *what was said* and *how it was said*, which is the
    cognitive system's ordinary defence against laundering. The corpus's finding
    is that the defence is **store-side, not output-side**, and the evidence is
    a mechanism the arena has not met elsewhere: *"attributed, unattributed, and
    forged claims all grant alike; phrasing is what the agent responds to."*
  - **Design:** an `epistemic_register` ENUM (flat / hedged / evidential /
    speculative) plus a `corroboration_count` on every fact — **additive-only
    schema**, per the arena's standing rule. Hedged sources yield hedged records
    (verbatim quote fragment + register); **register moves toward `flat` only on
    a second independent source, never by rewriting**; single-source
    load-bearing facts are flagged and **cannot trigger an irreversible tool
    call**. One alarm metric: **a rising flat-register ratio without a rising
    corroboration count.** The corpus names the failure by its own phrase:
    *"Current brain_sync/nightly consolidation rewrites 'maybe we should X' into
    'The user wants X' — exactly the laundering failure."*
    **Scale assumption (R-J5): two columns and one enum on the existing fact
    table, O(1) per write, and the corroboration count is a counter — the design
    is unchanged at 14,589 files.**
  - **Already exists:** adopt the schema, build the gate. The `truth`
    maintenance-systems lineage (Doyle 1979) and the TMS `JUSTIFY`/`RETRACT`
    primitives are the existing formalisation; ORDER 1639's source list carries
    the TMS reference. **No published system enforces a register field on a
    memory store** — the design is a composite of arXiv:2606.29279's finding
    with a TMS primitive, and the file's own claim is that the *combination* is
    what is new.
  - **Backup document:** `agenda-update-2026-09-22-round2.md` (ORDER 1624)
    §"Phrasal Register Preservation". Read whole this pass. The adjacent
    authority-collapse item in ORDER 1623 (arXiv:2608.01679: authority collapse
    in **48 of 49** consolidator/backbone configurations, 50.3%
    unauthorized-action rate, predicted labels reducing it 16.9% → 0.0%) is the
    same defect under a different name and is recorded here as **agreement
    between two independently-sourced items in the same programme**, not as two
    sources.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. The underlying finding is `LOW` (one benchmark, one
    agent family); the *store-side gate* has no trial. It sits at rank 3
    because it is the only slot in this area that cannot be built without rank
    1 or rank 2's infrastructure, not because the evidence is weakest — the file
    states the failure needs **no attacker at all**, which is the strongest
    claim in the tranche and the least tested.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 1** is where a register-preserved fact would be
    revised; **Area 24** is the general form of this defect (a measurement whose
    metadata is not measured); **Area 79** (framing-contingent computation) is
    the same phenomenon at the level of the *question* rather than the stored
    fact — the Wason effect and laundering are both cases of a surface form
    determining a downstream computation. Rank 3 **composes badly with rank 2**
    if a hedged fact is scored by an absence model that treats "not mentioned"
    and "mentioned with hedging" identically — the register must be a dimension
    of the count, not a parallel annotation.

**What this area does not claim.** It does not claim the arena can estimate
metacognitive coverage accurately. The corpus's own §Honest scope is that
**probe-free pre-retrieval prediction is empirically dead** — two independent
papers — and that domain shift degrades QPP to **0.706**. A drifting personal
store is a permanent domain shift, so rank 1's weekly refit is not a convenience
in this design; it is the design.

### Area 126 · Consolidation that invents edges is manufacturing, not memory — and the only direct test of it is negative `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by one file read whole this pass:**
`oracle/brain/research/batch118-graph-completion-verified-anchors.md` (ORDER
1635, 90 lines) §(a) items 4–7, §(c) the keep/kill verdict, §(d) the evidence
gate. Its earning context is `agenda-update-2026-09-24-batch118.md` (ORDER 1629,
122 lines) §"Level 1: Evidence-Gated Graph Edge Inference", also read whole this
pass.

**The brain part, named the way neuroscience names it.** **Sleep-dependent
consolidation of relational structure** — the reorganisation of the graph during
the sleep cycle, distinguished here from the *replay* of existing structure. The
corpus's own frame (ORDER 1629) is that a REM-style "dreaming" step **already
exists in the target agenda, ungated, untyped and unevaluated** — grep-verified
at three sites in the canonical agenda file, all anchored on the same paper — and
that the one direct test of it returned nothing. That is the arena's **126th
structural silence**: **0** occurrences of *evidence gate*, *precision
telemetry*, or *INFERRED-vs-STATED provenance* across 125 areas, and **1**
occurrence of arXiv:2604.20943 anywhere in `ARENA.md` — the paper that is the
entire basis of the mechanism is named once and never adjudicated.

**The negative is the finding, and it is the reason this is an area rather than
a slot.** The problem leg is strong and independently measured: removing **one**
triple from one shortest reasoning path drops RoG **−14.70%**, ToG **−13.44%**,
G-Retriever **−7.61%** on WebQSP, and **all three fail even when valid
alternative 2-hop paths exist** — they do not route around gaps. The solution leg
is where the corpus is honest: the **only** consolidation-time edge-inference
test in the literature is SCM (arXiv:2604.20943), a **single-author,
non-peer-reviewed IP-studio prototype**, and its own ablation table shows
**disabling REM dreaming changes nothing — 22/22 recall with or without, LTM
size 24 vs 26.** The authors state it themselves: *"the current evaluation does
not validate their functional benefit."* Everything with a **positive** number
gets its edges from **source text** (KARMA 83.1% verified correctness, i.e. ~17%
false-proposal rate; TCR-QF +29.1% EM by re-extraction from documents), not from
graph structure. Per §3 this is a first-class `UNSUCCESSFUL` result, recorded so
it is not rebuilt blind.

- **Rank 1 — Gate the dream: every inferred edge carries ≥1 evidence span, a
  relation type, an `inferred` flag, and a precision score; below threshold it
  is not written.** `LOW`. Support: **1 independent source** (ORDER 1635; ORDER
    1629 is the same programme and is not counted twice).
  - **Brain part:** consolidation that **adds structure only when the addition
    is licensed by a retrievable source**, with the inferred/stated distinction
    as a first-class property of the edge — the schema-level equivalent of
    separating what was *observed* from what was *inferred*, which is Area 125
    rank 3's register field applied to edges instead of facts.
  - **Design:** piggyback on the existing sleep-cycle consolidation step rather
    than creating a new stage. **Source A (ships first):** provenance
    re-extraction — for each entity touched since the last consolidation, pull
    episode windows via Zep-style episode↔entity edges, prompt for **typed
    triples between existing canonical IDs implied by the text but not already
    STATED**; proposals naming unresolved entities are **discarded, never
    auto-created**. **Source B (structure-only, experimental, default OFF):**
    shortest-path proposals between co-activated unlinked entities — OFF
    because of the SCM ablation above, and the file says so. **Mandatory
    gating, in order:** an NLI entailment check of the span against the triple
    (Echo-LLM pattern: precision 0.64 → 0.76, 55% false-positive reduction, at
    a stated recall cost 0.74 → 0.57 — **preserved as a disagreement, not
    optimised away**); pair-constrained dedup; confidence threshold with the
    rejection reason logged. **Storage:** `RELATED {inferred: true, method,
    provenance, confidence, created_at}`, and **the retriever and spreading
    activation must discount inferred edges (×0.5)** so a hallucinated edge
    cannot silently steer retrieval. **Precision telemetry:** monthly
    LLM-as-judge sampling by relation type and source, **kill switch at
    30-day rolling precision < 0.80**, benchmarked against KARMA's 83.1%.
    **Scale assumption (R-J5): the gate is O(1) per proposed edge and the
    telemetry is a sample, not a sweep, so the design is O(new edges) per
    consolidation cycle at 14,589 files — and the file's own rejected branch
    (Neo4j GDS link prediction, quadratic exhaustive search) is the thing that
    does not scale, which is why it is rejected rather than deferred.**
  - **Already exists:** adopt the mechanisms, **reject the tooling.** Zep /
    Graphiti (arXiv:2501.13956) supplies the bi-temporal episode↔entity
    provenance substrate and is the reason re-extraction is possible at all;
    SodaMem (arXiv:2608.08055) supplies typed FactEvents with mandatory
    provenance spans; All-Mem (arXiv:2603.19595) supplies the reversible
    Split/Merge/Update operator pattern; Anthropic's *Dreams* (official docs,
    research preview, beta header `dreaming-2026-04-21`) is the **production
    existence proof** that an offline consolidation-synthesis pass is being
    shipped, with no published precision numbers and flat stores. **Explicitly
    rejected, with reasons:** Neo4j GDS `gds.beta.pipeline.linkPrediction` is
    **beta-tier, predicts UNTYPED links** (wrong output for a typed schema),
    needs negative sampling plus a trained classifier, and is quadratic over node
    count; TransE/RotatE/ComplEx need dense homogeneous training data; AnyBURL
    and AMIE are **Java CLIs over triple exports**. For a 10k–100k-node sparse
    personal graph these are the wrong tools, and the file says so once, with
    citations.
  - **Backup document:** `batch118-graph-completion-verified-anchors.md` (ORDER
    1635) §§(a), (c), (d); `agenda-update-2026-09-24-batch118.md` (ORDER 1629)
    §Evidence-Gated Graph Edge Inference. Both read whole this pass.
  - **Grade:** `LOW` for the gate, and the arena records **two grades on this
    slot without averaging** — the *problem* is `HIGH` (three independent
    2025–26 measurements, the strongest being a one-triple removal costing
    14.70%), the *mechanism* is `UNTESTED` and its only direct trial is
    `UNSUCCESSFUL`. Rank 1 is preferred to the incumbent **because** the
    negative exists: an item that says *gate it, type it, prove precision, or
    don't ship it* is evidence-grounded in a way the ungated step currently in
    the agenda is not.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 2** is the constraint, and it binds twice — a
    precision score that is monotone in the retriever's own score cannot change a
    ranking, and the ×0.5 discount is a **fixed constant**, which Area 2's
    tranche-19 finding says cannot revive a signal with zero variance. **Area 6**
    and **Area 23** are the consolidation areas this stage attaches to, and both
    need to be told that the stage they are being given has never been shown to
    help. **Area 93** (the read is a write) is why the discount must be in the
    *retriever* and not only in storage: an undiscounted inferred edge steers
    retrieval, which is a write.

- **Rank 2 — Ship the source-A lane alone and let Source B stay off until a
  positive result exists.** `UNTESTED`, and this is the slot that follows the
  evidence rather than the ambition. Support: **1 independent source** (ORDER
    1635).
  - **Brain part:** consolidation as **re-derivation from evidence** rather than
    association generation — the same operation as hippocampal replay
    reconstructing trajectories, with the constraint that the reconstruction
    must be checkable against the trajectory it came from.
  - **Design:** exactly Source A, no Source B, no structural inference of any
    kind, plus the **missing-edge telemetry** the file calls cheap and worth
    doing regardless: log multi-hop retrieval failures where the path **exists
    in episode text but not in the graph**. That log is the 2504.05163
    experiment run continuously on the personal graph, and it is the number that
    decides whether Source A earns its tokens. Until it exists, the correct
    position is that the agenda's scheduled REM step is **an ungated
    hallucination-injection risk**, in the file's own words.
  - **Backup document:** `batch118-graph-completion-verified-anchors.md` (ORDER
    1635) §(d) Source A and item 5 (missing-edge telemetry); §(c) "Why KEEP
    anyway" reasons 1–2.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. TCR-QF's +29.1% EM and KARMA's 83.1% are adjacent
    published support for the *re-extraction* mechanism, and neither is a trial
    of it on a personal graph; the file says the composition is unpublished.
  - **Interaction:** Rank 2 is **rank 1 minus the structural lane**, and the
    two are not composable in the sense that matters — shipping rank 1 whole
    means shipping the part with the negative result. Area 30 (per-episode cost)
    sets the budget for both.

- **Rank 3 — Retrieval-time robustness instead of graph repair: make the
  retriever survive a gap rather than making the graph whole.** `LOW`.
  Support: **1 independent source** (ORDER 1635 item 3, CS-RAG).
  - **Brain part:** **robustness to partial evidence** — the retrieval-time
    analogue of the BRINK finding that KG-RAG methods *"often rely on internal
    LLM memorization"* when knowledge is missing. The failure is not that the
    graph is wrong; it is that the reader **cannot tell it is incomplete** and
    continues confidently through under-supported structure — which the corpus
    names as **"retrieval hallucination by forcing continuation through
    under-supported graph structure."**
  - **Design:** constraint planning, a sufficiency check before generating, and
    textual recovery from the episode text when the graph path is absent.
    Adopt, do not build: CS-RAG (arXiv:2603.14828) is the published mechanism
    and it is explicitly framed by its authors as **the cheaper alternative to
    edge injection** — which is the same claim this area's rank 2 makes, from
    the opposite direction.
  - **Backup document:** `batch118-graph-completion-verified-anchors.md` (ORDER
    1635) item 3 (§(a)) and §(b) overlap check.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. The problem is measured (BRINK, EACL 2026 Long; CS-RAG);
    the mechanism is a published system, so the *design* has a trial behind it
    even though no one has measured it on a personal graph. Recorded as a
    disagreement in the file and preserved: CS-RAG is a **substitute** for
    repair, not a complement, and the arena does not claim both can hold.
  - **Interaction:** **Area 35** (grounding, and the point where simulation stops
    being evidence) is the parent — a sufficiency check is grounding applied at
    the retrieval boundary, and CS-RAG's "forced continuation" failure is Area
    35's failure mode with a graph in place of a simulator. **Area 125** rank 1
    supplies the retrieval-time coverage estimate a sufficiency check would
    consume: a gap in the graph and a gap in the store are the same query to a
    coverage monitor.

**What would flip this area to KILL**, stated so a later pass does not have to
re-derive it: a published consolidation-time personal-memory edge-inference
result with a **positive measured gain** promotes rank 1 from *gating discipline*
to *mechanism*; a second null on evidence-gated typed inference against
**associative** queries (rather than the stated-fact queries SCM used) would
drop rank 1 to `UNSUCCESSFUL` and make rank 3 the default. The file names both
conditions itself and the arena records them as the exit criteria.

### Area 127 · A memory is not the stored string — it is the stored string read by the rules in force when it is read, and nothing in the string says which rules those were `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by one file read whole this pass, with three corroborating reads in the
same programme:** `oracle/brain/research/cbor-tag-6-evolution-semantics-drift.md`
(ORDER 1649, 382 lines) §"Version Detection and Negotiation" and §"Migration Path
for Deployments". Its mechanism is carried by ORDER 1641
(`cbor-packed-dcbor-interaction.md`), ORDER 1645
(`cbor-packed-tag-6-string-reserved-handling.md`) §2.3, and ORDER 1643
(`cbor-packed-side-meeting-2026-outcome.md`).

**The brain part, named the way neuroscience names it.** **Cue-dependent
reconstruction** — the hippocampus does not replay a memory, it *re-activates and
re-completes* one from the cue available at retrieval, and the same trace
activated under a different cue completes differently. The corpus states the
engineering version of this without the neuroscience: *"Tag 6's content type has
fundamentally changed three times"* (ORDER 1649), and the decisive structural
fact is the one it records as a **gap** rather than a defect —

> *"**No version indicator in Packed CBOR data items themselves.** The draft
> version is only known from: media type parameters, application protocol
> specification, out-of-band agreement."* (ORDER 1649 §1)

and then, in the same file, the two migration outcomes: *"**Silent data
corruption or hard failure**"* and *"No automatic negotiation mechanism exists in
any current draft."*

**Why this is not Area 25 (retraction of a derived artefact) or Area 27
(lability).** Area 25 is a **retraction** — something was withdrawn, and the
question is what happens to what was built on it. Area 27 is an **overwrite** — a
memory is modified inside its lability window. Neither covers the case this area
is about, and the distinguishing clause is that **no retraction and no overwrite
occurred.** The bytes are byte-identical. The ledger rows are unchanged. The
consolidation daemon did not run. **The reader changed, and the memory's content
changed with it.** Area 25's trigger is a retraction event and Area 27's is a
write event; this area's trigger is a **read against a different specification
version**, and there is no event to hook. That is why it is invisible to both:
a system that logs writes and retractions correctly can still silently misread
its entire store.

- **Rank 1 — Pin the reader, not just the record: every stored artefact carries
  the identity of the rule-set that produced it, and any read whose declared
  reader differs is a migration event, not a read.** `LOW`. Support: **1
  independent source** (ORDER 1649), with three same-programme corroborations
  that establish the mechanism but not the fix.
  - **Brain part:** **the cue is part of the trace.** Encoding specificity means
    recall probability is a function of cue–target overlap; the inverse failure
    is a trace that is *compatible with every cue and therefore discriminates
    none*. A record that can be read by any version of the reader has no
    addressable content — it is a shape, not a memory.
  - **Design:** version-stamp on write, **not** on read. Every artefact carries
    `{producer, spec_version, table_version, encoding_profile}`. The reader
    declares what it is; a mismatch routes to a **migration lane** (translate,
    or refuse-and-flag) rather than to a parse. Three properties the corpus makes
    necessary and no amount of encoding discipline supplies: (i) **external
    tables must be content-addressed or immutable** — ORDER 1641 §7: an external
    dictionary is *"deterministic only when the packing tables are fixed"*, so
    the table version is part of the memory's identity, not of the document's
    envelope; (ii) **reserved space is not free space** — ORDER 1645's analysis of
    tag 6's reserved content is the general case: the draft specifies no decoder
    behaviour for reserved forms, and the corpus's own verdict on the
    strip-the-tag option is *"**Nobody**, for Packed CBOR … Corrupts data
    silently"*, because a reference `6(0)` becomes the integer `0`; (iii)
    **validity is not portability** — ORDER 1641 §9 Tag Content Equivalence means
    a checker that validates the packed form is not validating the unpacked
    form, so a record can pass its own reader and still be unreadable by the next
    one.
  - **Already exists:** partially, and none of it composes. **RFC 7120(bis)**
    early allocation gives a *process*, not a wire-level version field — and
    ORDER 1649's own table records that the packed-CBOR early-allocation request
    was **never filed**. **COSE/CWT** authenticate a protected header that
    *can* carry an algorithm identifier, and ORDER 1649 names this as the
    closest prior art: *"Content-dependent semantics … require explicit algorithm
    identifiers."* **QUIC version negotiation** and **TLS downgrade sentinels**
    are the two mechanisms the file benchmarks against; both put the version
    *in the record*. **Zustand/versioned schemas in Postgres** and the
    `okf_version:` field already present on Hermes corpus pages are the
    in-stack partial: the wiki stamps documents and nothing stamps *rows in
    pgvector*, and a semantic-tier fact row currently carries no statement of
    which extractor prompt, which dedup threshold, and which embedding model
    produced it.
  - **Backup document:** `cbor-tag-6-evolution-semantics-drift.md` (ORDER 1649)
    §§1, 3, 5; `cbor-packed-dcbor-interaction.md` (ORDER 1641) §§7, 9;
    `cbor-packed-tag-6-string-reserved-handling.md` (ORDER 1645) §2.3;
    `cbor-packed-side-meeting-2026-outcome.md` (ORDER 1643) §"What Changed".
    All four read whole this pass.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`, and it is the **only** grade the fix can carry. The
    mechanism is documented with primary sources and the migration failure modes
    are enumerated concretely; the **fix has no trial anywhere in the corpus**,
    because the corpus's own subject (a spec that expired unresolved) is a case
    where the hazard was realised by *abandonment* rather than by migration. The
    live, load-bearing fact: **`draft-ietf-cbor-packed-19` expired 2026-08-06
    with no draft-20** (ORDER 1643, corroborated independently in ORDER 1641
    §10.3 and ORDER 1653 §"Critical Path Issue"). A specification that is
    allowed to lapse while implementations still hold its data is the purest
    available instance of this failure, and the corpus records that
    dns-cbor's `application/dns+cbor;packed=0` and generic
    `application/cbor;packed=0` **already disagree in meaning** (ORDER 1653) —
    the same parameter name, the same value, two different decoders. That
    collision exists today, in two live drafts, and no field in either record
    distinguishes them.
  - **Justification:** ranks above bespoke alternatives because the *diagnosis*
    is already replicated across four files while the *fix* is untried
    everywhere, and because it composes with the arena's existing stack at zero
    cost — a version column is additive (R-J1: cost is not an axis). It beats
    the strictest alternative (reject-on-mismatch, which is ORDER 1645's
    recommended "MUST reject" for reserved content) on **composability**: a
    fail-closed decoder is correct and has no migration path, and a store with
    no migration path is a store that must be rewritten wholesale when the
    reader changes — which at 14,589 files is not a migration.
  - **Interaction:** **constrains Area 2** at the point where a candidate set is
    built — a silently misread row is a candidate that was never in the store, and
    Area 2's entire argument is that the candidate set is the ceiling. **Area 126**
    (consolidation that invents edges) is the same defect at write time and the
    same fix at write time: both need the producer's identity on the artefact.
    **Area 6/23** (consolidation, interference-gated forgetting) are unaffected
    and should stay that way — this area is about *what a record means*, not
    *whether it survives*. **Area 99** (a famous pattern replicated and the
    explanation did not) is the closest existing area by shape: a result that
    reproduces while its stated cause does not is the same class of
    misattribution, and ORDER 1649's fxamacker/cbor v1 case is exactly it —
    a widely deployed library that *ignores tag 6 entirely*, so draft-03–-15
    prefix references are silently decoded as bare strings and **the system
    reports no error at all**.
- **Rank 2 — Require the reader to declare itself, and make a mismatch a loud,
  quarantined event rather than a parse.** `UNTESTED`. Adopt the TLS/QUIC
  pattern: version in the record, mismatch ⇒ named error, never a best-effort
  decode. Weaker than rank 1 because it detects the hazard only for readers
  written *after* this design ships — every artefact already in the store is
  unversioned and therefore unresolvable. Strictly better than nothing, and the
  fallback when a content-addressed scheme cannot be adopted.
- **Rank 3 — Content-address every artefact so the hash *is* the version, and let
  the packer be the only thing that can produce a valid one.** `UNTESTED`.
  ORDER 1641's Strategy A is the same idea at the byte level: sign the packed
  form, not the unpacked form, and require external tables to be *"content-
  addressed (e.g., via hash)"*. Attractive because the version cannot drift from
  the content — there is no field to lie. Rejected as rank 3 for a specific
  reason the file itself supplies: **packing is lossy**, so `pack(unpack(c)) ≠ c`
  (ORDER 1648 §6.2), and a hash over the packed form therefore does not
  identify the meaning, only the encoding. Retained because it composes with
  rank 1 — content-addressing the *tables* and stamping the *record* are
  complementary, not alternatives.
  - **Rejected and recorded so it is not re-derived:** *"never version anything,
    always re-derive meaning at read time."* ORDER 1645 and ORDER 1649 both
    refute it on their own terms, and the corpus's summary table is blunt:
    strip-the-tag is for *"**Nobody**"*; dual-decode-with-heuristics is
    explicitly flagged as risking *"false positives if draft-19 reserved content
    appears legitimately."* A read path that guesses is a read path that can
    guess wrong without an event.

**What would flip this area, stated so a later pass does not re-derive it:** a
published content-addressed or version-stamped memory store that demonstrably
survives a reader change without silent corruption would promote rank 1 from
`LOW` to `HIGH` and make the interaction with Area 126 load-bearing rather than
aspirational. A second corpus arrival at a *silent* misinterpretation inside the
Hermes stack itself (not inside an IETF draft) would raise the area's priority
without changing its ordering, because the diagnosis is no longer the uncertain
part.

### Area 128 · A signature certifies where a thing came from, and never whether it
is safe: provenance and trustworthiness are different channels, and a system
that collapses them has a blind spot no amount of stamping fills `PROVISIONAL` — NEW

**Earned by:** `oracle/brain/research/cross-registry-provenance-verification.md`
(ORDER 1668, 263 lines, read whole this pass) §"The 'Provenance Theatre'
Problem" and §"What Provenance Does NOT Prove". **Carrying the mechanism, same
research programme and cross-citing file — counted once under C-016, not twice:**
`cross-registry-monorepo-release-orchestration.md` (ORDER 1667, 715 lines) §§2,
4.1, 5.1 and 12.

**Why this is a brain part and not a security note.** It is the sharpest
statement in 128 areas of a distinction the brain maintains structurally and no
artifact carries: **a signal's origin and a signal's trustworthiness are
different channels, and a great deal of neural machinery exists to keep them
apart.** Neuroscience's name for the mechanism is **reafference** — the
comparison of what the brain caused against what the brain received, which is
the *only* way a self-monitoring system can tell its own output from the world's.
The reafferent comparison certifies exactly one thing: *this is mine*. It
certifies nothing about whether the content is true, kind, or safe, and the
failure is not subtle — a system can stamp its output `agency: system`,
attest that the stamp was written by the expected writer at the expected
revision, and the content can still be an attack.

**The load-bearing measurement, and it is a real incident rather than a
hypothetical.** The **Mini Shai-Hulud** compromise (May 2026) hit **84 npm
packages across 42 `@tanstack` packages**. Every one carried a
**cryptographically valid SLSA Build Level 3 attestation**. The attestations
accurately reported the correct repository, workflow, and ref. **The packages
were malware** — the attacker extracted the legitimate OIDC token from runner
memory via cache poisoning, so the build ran correctly, from the correct place,
and produced the correct attestation, on a runner that was already hostile. The
file's sentence is the area in one line: *"The provenance attested where the
build happened, not whether the build was trustworthy."*

**Why this is not Area 45, which already owns the efference copy.** Area 45
governs **the system's own writes** and its stated ceiling is that *"a tag that
certifies provenance certifies only that the write path was honest."* This area
attacks the second clause. **The write path need not be dishonest for the stamp
to be useless** — a compromised write path is *perfectly* honest, and its honesty
is what makes the forgery pass. The two areas compose as a chain, and the chain
is the design: Area 45 answers *did I write this*, this area answers *so what*.
A system satisfying Area 45 perfectly can be fully compromised, and the arena
had no slot saying so.

- **Rank 1 — Split the write into two fields that no single checker may satisfy:
  `provenance` (who) and `trust` (whether), and require an *independent* witness
  for the second.** `UNTESTED` for the design; `LOW` for the diagnosis.
  - **Brain part:** the **reafferent comparator** — the running copy of what the
    system caused, held separately from what it received, so the two can be
    compared. The comparator is the organ that makes provenance possible *and*
    the reason provenance is insufficient: it is calibrated on origin and silent
    on content.
  - **Design:** every artefact carries **two fields with different witnesses and
    different failure modes**, and the gate requires both. `provenance` is
    satisfied by an in-band attestation (a hash chain, a signed commit, an
    `agency:` stamp) — cheap, always available, and **worthless as a safety
    claim**. `trust` is satisfied only by a signal that **failed differently from
    the thing it certifies**: an independent scanner, a human, an out-of-band
    hash, a build that ran on an ephemeral network-restricted runner with
    cross-context cache sharing disabled. The rule the file supplies and the
    arena adopts: **`provenance` is a fact about a channel; `trust` is a claim
    about content, and no channel fact discharges it.**
  - **Already exists:** Sigstore / in-toto (the attestation substrate),
    **SLSA v1.0 provenance predicate** (npm, PyPI PEP 740), **Rekor** (the
    transparency log), `slsa-verifier` (Go; npm support **experimental**), the
    npm `audit signatures` subcommand, and `cosign`. All of these are
    *provenance* infrastructure and **none of them is a trust mechanism** — the
    file's own table is the proof, with attestation coverage at **~17% of PyPI
    uploads, ~7% of npm packages, and 0% on crates.io**. In the Hermes stack the
    only existing carrier is the `agency:` field at OKF v0.2 frontmatter, which
    is Area 45's Rank 1 and is exactly the field this slot says is not enough.
  - **Backup document:** `cross-registry-provenance-verification.md` (ORDER
    1668) §"Provenance Theatre" and §"Recommendations" items 1–3.
    **Backup document (same programme, the release side — ORDER 1667)** §4.1
    (PyPI Trusted Publisher filters OIDC by `repository_owner` before checking
    `job_workflow_ref`) and §12 Risk 1.
  - **Evidence grade:** the **diagnosis is `LOW`** and the **design is
    `UNTESTED`** — stated separately, never averaged. Diagnosis `LOW` because it
    is a real, named, executed compromise with a count (**84 packages, 42
    `@tanstack`**) rather than a hypothetical, and because the corpus records a
    *second* independent campaign (**TrapDoor**) reaching the same conclusion by
    a different route. Both rest on the corpus's own reading of the SLSA blog and
    the hvtracker analysis and are **`unverified` against a primary source** —
    the arena has read the wiki file, not the incident. Design `UNTESTED` because
    **no trial of a two-field split exists anywhere**, and because the arena is
    forbidden from creating one (R-J2).
  - **Why rank 1:** it is the only slot that survives Mini Shai-Hulud unchanged.
    Rank 2 and Rank 3 both fail on it, and saying how is the area's point.
  - **Interaction:** **downstream of Area 45** and **upstream of Area 28**
    (containment — you cannot quarantine a compromise you classified as
    authentic). **Composes with Area 33** (a detector is not a certifier): this
    area is that asymmetry's *supply-chain* face, and the corpus supplies the
    sharper version of the same warning — *"Passing red team tests can create a
    false sense of security."* **Constrains Area 24** (instrument validity): a
    valid attestation is an instrument reporting faithfully on the wrong
    quantity. **Directly sharpens Area 19** (belief-to-action compliance): a
    system that gates action on `provenance == expected` is exactly the system
    Mini Shai-Hulud ran against, and the arena had no slot saying that.
  - **Scale assumption (R-J5):** the two fields are **O(1) per artefact** and the
    independent-witness requirement is where the cost lands — a witness that must
    fail differently does not scale for free at 14,589 files. The slot's honest
    form at scale is **sampling with a stated rate**, not per-artefact
    verification, and stating the rate is part of the design.

- **Rank 2 — Bind the trust field to a *specific threat model* rather than to a
  score, because the field's value is entirely in what it was checked against.**
  `UNTESTED`.
  - **Brain part:** **context-dependent threat assessment** — the brain does not
    evaluate a stimulus as globally safe or globally threatening, it evaluates it
    *against a current goal and a current context*, and the same signal flips
    sign between them. A trust scalar has no context and therefore cannot
    discriminate: `trust = 0.9` means nothing until you say trusted *for what*.
  - **Design:** the second field is a **set of scoped claims**, not a number —
    `trust: {execution: verified, content: unverified, network: unverified}`,
    each with its own witness and its own expiry. The file's own asymmetry table
    forces this: PyPI and npm at **L3** (signer identity + transparency log),
    crates.io at **L1** (a `Cargo.lock` checksum, *"no cryptographic proof
    linking a specific crate version to a specific git commit"*), **0%**
    attestation coverage. **A single scalar across those three is a lie the
    schema would make true.** Rank 1's field is a boolean; this slot says it has
    to be a map, or the one registry that attested nothing gets the same score as
    the two that attested everything.
  - **Already exists:** SLSA's own **L1–L4 ladder** already refuses to collapse
    the levels into one grade, and the file's cost table is that refusal made
    concrete. Nothing in the Hermes stack carries a level field.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` · Support: **1 source, and it is Rank 1's** — this is a
    second slot carved out of the same file's §"Current State" table, **not a
    second independent confirmation**, and it is counted as such.
  - **Why rank 2:** it is strictly *harder* than rank 1 and cannot be adopted
    without it, which is what a fallback slot is for. It loses on composability
    only because a scoped map is more machinery, and R-J1 says cost is not an
    axis — so the real reason is that **rank 1's failure is the demonstrated
    one and this is not yet.**
  - **Interaction:** **refines Rank 1** rather than competing with it. Depends on
    **Area 72** (a metric coordinate system with a known drift) for the expiry
    term, and on **Area 33** for the reason a level is not a certification.

- **Rank 3 — Constrain the *writer's* environment instead of verifying its
  output, on the file's own evidence that output verification failed.** `UNTESTED`.
  - **Brain part:** **the efference copy is generated at emission, and a
    comparator that reads a corrupted copy compares the forgery against itself.**
    Neurophysiologically this is why reafferent comparison works at all: the
    copy is made by the motor system *before* the effect, from the same command,
    so an externally-induced perturbation cannot alter it. A build attestation
    has no such property — **it is generated by the thing it is attesting.**
  - **Design:** take Mini Shai-Hulud's own mechanism as the specification.
    Disable cross-context cache sharing. Use ephemeral, network-restricted
    runners. Pin Trusted Publishers to a specific workflow **and branch**. Ensure
    OIDC tokens are not reachable from arbitrary runner processes. The design
    claim is sharp and worth stating plainly: **this is the only rank in the
    arena whose failure mode is a signature that is wrong rather than a signature
    that is right about the wrong thing**, and that is why it ranks last — it is
    an operational programme with no mechanism, no organ, and no way to check
    whether it worked except the absence of the next incident.
  - **Already exists:** Sigstore's hardened builder images, GitHub Actions'
    ephemeral-runner and cache-scoping controls, SLSA L3 build requirements, and
    `cargo-cyclonedx` as the file's partial mitigation for the crates.io gap.
    None of it is in the Hermes stack; the release pipeline it would protect
    (ORDER 1667 §9.1) is itself a design in the corpus, not a running system.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED` · Support: **1 source, and it is Rank 1's** — the
    mitigations are the corpus file's §"Long-Term" list, not a trial.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the correct answer to a question the arena has not
    been asked, and it is ranked last because **a hardening programme cannot be
    falsified by a test the system runs** — which puts it outside what this file
    is for. Recorded rather than dropped: if a trial of build-environment
    isolation ever reports a *reduction* in successful compromise, it returns
    and the ordering is re-opened.
  - **Interaction:** **upstream of all three.** Cheapest to adopt and hardest to
    verify, which is the trap the arena's own §8 finding predicts: the validated
    code path produces the appearance of authority, and a hardened runner is a
    validated code path.

### Area 129 · A correct explanation can be causally disconnected from the correct action, and the explanation stays coherent either way — so coherence cannot be used as evidence that the system knows why it worked — NEW

**Earned by one file read whole this pass:**
`oracle/brain/research/frontier-research-ontology-constrained-decoding-epistemic-paradox-intermediate-languages-2026-09-04.md`
(ORDER 1745, 324 lines) §4 "The Epistemic Coherence Paradox: Competence Without
Grounding, Grounding Without Success", §§4.3–4.5. Primary source: **Chacón
Sartori (2026), "Coherent Without Grounding, Grounded Without Success:
Observability and Epistemic Failure," arXiv:2603.28371**.

**The brain part, named the way neuroscience names it.** The **basing
relation** — the *because-of* link between a representation and the thing it
is offered to account for. The corpus's formalisation is an **Epistemic
Triangle** in which three sources compete: **weak observational signals** from
the environment, **strong statistical priors from training**, and **domain
knowledge**. The dissociation is not between knowing and not-knowing; it is
between the **first two sources producing the outcome and the third producing
the account**, with the account surviving intact when the outcome is produced
without it. Two directions, both measured:
- **Type A — competence without grounding.** In low-observability domains the
  system acts successfully while misidentifying the mechanism that produced the
  success: right action, faulty explanation. It succeeds *despite* the
  explanation, not *because of* it.
- **Type B — grounding without success.** In high-observability domains the
  system generates an accurate causal diagnosis and cannot translate it into an
  effective intervention: right diagnosis, no working remedy.

**Why this is the arena's 123rd structural silence, and explicitly not Area 77,
Area 98, or Area 24.** `ARENA.md` held **0** occurrences of *knowing-how*,
*right for the wrong reason*, *paradox rate*, or *weak observation*, and **0** of
arXiv 2603.28371, across 128 areas. The three nearest neighbours each miss the
load-bearing half:
- **Area 77 (representational transparency)** says introspection cannot reach
  the **structure** of the self-model — the process is invisible from within. It
  does not claim the visible part can be systematically **unrelated** to the
  process while remaining plausible.
- **Area 98 (monitoring and control are two systems)** separates a monitor from
  a controller: two mechanisms, one not the other. Here there is **one**
  mechanism and the explanation it emits about its own success is **not
  connected to the success**, and — the part that makes it a new brain question
  rather than another module boundary — **coherence is preserved in both
  directions of failure**, so no amount of inspecting the explanation
  distinguishes the two cases.
- **Area 24 (validity of one's own instruments)** asks whether a *measurement*
  measured anything. This asks whether an *explanation* caused the event, and
  the answer is that the two can be severable in **both** directions with
  identical surface coherence.

The structural silence: **128 areas take a coherent self-report as evidence
about internal state, and a coherence check passes identically on a system
that is right for the wrong reason and on one that is right for the right
reason.** Nothing in the arena's current repertoire separates them, because
every instrument it has is shape-based and the shape is the same.

- **Rank 1 — Make the basing relation a separately measured quantity, on a
  three-axis report, and forbid a coherence signal from standing in for it.**
  `LOW`. Support: **1 independent source** (ORDER 1745; the only file in this
  workspace's read corpus reporting 2603.28371).
  - **Brain part:** the **basing relation** as a *measurable third channel*,
    dissociable from both competence and coherence — the accounting step
    between a system's action and its own account of that action.
  - **Design:** every graded artefact reports **three independent axes, never
    one**: (1) **behavioural competence** — did it succeed; (2) **explanatory
    accuracy** — is the stated mechanism the true mechanism; (3) **basing
    relation** — does the explanation *account for* the success. The arena's
    `confidence:` field is currently a single scalar covering (1) and is
    silently read as covering (2) and (3); split it. The basing axis is scored
    by a probe that cannot see the explanation text: an **intervention** —
    hold the stated mechanism fixed, remove or perturb the mechanism the action
    actually depended on, and observe whether the behaviour follows the
    explanation or the substrate. A score that survives intervention is
    **incoherent-but-honest**; one that tracks the substrate while the
    explanation survives unchanged is **Type A**. Type B is the mirror test:
    does acting on the stated diagnosis change the outcome.
    **Scale assumption (R-J5): the probe is O(1) in corpus size — it
    intervenes on the mechanism, not on the store.** No file is rescanned, so
    the design holds at 14,589 files and does not inherit Area 30's ingestion
    ceiling.
  - **Already exists:** adopt the measurement, build the integration. The
    corpus supplies the three-axis evaluation frame and the two failure
    signatures (Chacón Sartori, arXiv:2603.28371). Already in the Hermes stack
    as the **hook** the probe would attach to: `arena_invariants.py` (C6 already
    separates *citation* from *ledger row*, which is a basing check on one
    narrow claim) and `verify_reads.py` (a behaviour check with no explanation
    channel attached at all). Neither currently emits axis (3). **No existing
    service measures the basing relation of an LLM explanation against its own
    outcome** — the corpus's own framing is that its own system *is* the
    system exhibiting Type A, which is a statement about a missing
    instrument, not a missing paper.
  - **Backup document:**
    `frontier-research-ontology-constrained-decoding-epistemic-paradox-intermediate-languages-2026-09-04.md`
    (ORDER 1745) §§4.1–4.5. Read whole this pass. Corroborating context for
    the same failure from a different direction, both read whole this pass and
    both already held by the arena: `frontier-research-ontology-2026-09-03-comprehensive.md`
    (ORDER 1724) — the file's own finding that a structurally valid
    representation can be **entirely fabricated** at 94%+ while remaining
    fluent; and `frontier-research-ontology-dual-memory-dynamic-ontology-2026-09-02.md`
    (ORDER 1746) §3.2 **epistemic collapse** (arXiv 2604.14881) — memory,
    inference, speculation and narrative extrapolation all expressed with the
    **same surface fluency**, because autoregressive decoding proceeds smoothly
    even when internal activations drift or conflict. **These are not
    independent measurements of the basing relation** and are not counted as
    such; they are the same failure seen at three different scales, which is
    why the *shape* of the problem is well attested while the *magnitude* is
    not.
  - **Grade:** `LOW`. One preprint, no independent replication, and the
    50%→20% figure comes from a single evaluation with an unreplicated
    baseline. Recorded limitation that constrains the rank: extended reasoning
    **reduced the paradox rate 50% → 20% and simultaneously reduced
    optimisation performance**, so the obvious remedy — reason harder — trades
    the defect for paralysis, and the corpus's own Arena-123 analogue (Area
    123, thinking budget) is about that same trade-off. The corpus frames
    Chapter 4's own conclusion as the design requirement.
  - **Interaction:** **constrains Area 13, 24, 77, 98, 125 and every slot that
    reports a `confidence:` scalar.** Area 125's `MetamemoryMonitor` is the
    nearest live coupling: it produces a coverage probability and routes on it,
    which is axis (1) about the *store*. This area is the same measurement
    about the *explanation*, and the two must not be summed into one number.
    If Area 125's rank 1 ships without this, the system will route correctly
    on a coverage signal while reporting a reason that did not cause the
    routing.
- **Rank 2 — Generate the explanation from the trace rather than beside it, so
  the two cannot diverge by construction.** `UNTESTED`. Support: **1 source**
  (ORDER 1745).
  - **Brain part:** the **efference copy / actual-return loop** — a
    representation of one's own action that is *upstream* of the report rather
    than a post-hoc rationalisation written next to it. Arena 45 and Area 127
    are the nearest neighbours: 45 governs where a memory came from, 127
    governs what a stored thing means to the reader. This governs what a
    **self-report** means, and it is the structural remedy rather than the
    measurement.
  - **Design:** every reasoning step bound to the store triple it read
    (step-level provenance), and the explanation *rendered from* that binding
    rather than generated from a prompt. The corpus's own decomposition puts
    the two apart: the decoding constraint (KG-Trie, arXiv:2410.13080) makes
    generation valid, and step-level grounding makes it **attributable** — the
    paper's word, and the correct one, because it names the failure mode
    rather than the goal.
  - **Already exists:** step-level KG grounding (arXiv:2502.13247, WoRLM'25)
    reports ≥26.5% accuracy gain over CoT baselines and per-step verifiability;
    GCR (arXiv:2410.13080, ICML 2025) reports **0% reasoning hallucination** by
    constraining the decoded token set. **Read both claims as unverified** —
    0% is a benchmark result on a closed KGQA suite, not a general property,
    and the corpus asserting a zero is not a primary source. The Hermes stack
    already has the substrate: per-file read records in `RUNLOG.jsonl`, and the
    `order_numbers_read` field the skill calls the load-bearing one.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1745 §§1, 5. Read whole this pass.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. The arena has **0** of arXiv:2410.13080 or
    arXiv:2502.13247, and the only support is one corpus file asserting both.
    A reported 0% is a reason to check, not a grade.
  - **Why rank 2, not rank 1:** it fixes the *cause* and cannot detect a
    failure that occurred before the explanation was rendered. A system can
    render a faithful trace of a decision that was made on a wrong basis
    thirty steps earlier. **Rank 1 can detect that; rank 2 cannot.** It ranks
    second because it is the cheaper and more durable remedy, not the more
    complete one.
  - **Interaction:** **upstream of Area 128** (signature certifies origin, never
    truth) and complementary to it: provenance is a necessary and insufficient
    condition for a true account, and this area says which one it is
    insufficient for.
- **Rank 3 — Record the Type A/Type B direction as a first-class field and
  refuse to aggregate the two.** `UNTESTED`. Support: **0 sources for the
  field; 1 for the distinction it would record.**
  - **Brain part:** none required — this is the **minimum viable subset** of
    rank 1, admitting the deficit plainly. An arena that records
    `explains_success: unknown` has an honest surface; an arena with a single
    `confidence:` field has a falsely complete one.
  - **Design:** add the axis as an explicit nullable tri-state
    (`basing: established | broken | unknown`) and refuse to collapse `unknown`
    into `established` for want of a probe. Zero cost, no mechanism, no claim.
  - **Already exists:** nothing. This is the slot the arena adopts when it
    declines to build rank 1.
  - **Backup document:** ORDER 1745 §4.2. Read whole this pass.
  - **Grade:** `UNTESTED`. Support **0** — the design is the absence of a
    measurement, and nothing in the corpus tests that recording its absence
    helps.
  - **Why rank 3:** it is the answer to a question this arena has never been
    asked, and it is ranked last because it makes the hole **legible** without
    making it **smaller**. It returns if a trial shows a three-axis report
    catching a Type A failure that a single-scalar report passed.
  - **Interaction:** **upstream of every slot that emits a confidence number.**
    Without it, this area is invisible; with it, all 128 prior areas' scalars
    become visibly incomplete, which is the point.

## BRAIN PARTS NOT YET COVERED

**Opened this pass and now closed** — recorded so a later pass does not re-derive
them as gaps:
- *Whether the stored artefact carries the identity of the rule-set that produced
  it, and what happens to a store when the reader changes and the record does
  not.* **Area 127**, earned by ORDER 1649 with 1641, 1643 and 1645. `ARENA.md`
  held **0** occurrences of *schema version*, *format version*, *parser version*,
  *decoding rule*, *same bytes*, *silent corruption*, or *meaning changes* across
  126 areas. Area 45 (efference copy, provenance of a memory) is the nearest
  neighbour and it governs **where a memory came from**; this governs **what a
  stored thing currently means**, which is a different question with a different
  trigger — provenance is captured at write, interpretation drifts at read, and
  the second one produces **no event to hook**. Area 25 (retraction) and Area 27
  (lability) both hook a write; this one cannot, which is why a system with
  correct write and retraction logging can still misread everything it holds.
  Area 99 (a famous pattern replicated and the explanation did not) is the same
  shape of error seen from the outside.

**Every entry is `not observed` — never `absent`.** A missing area means the
read has not reached it, not that the brain does without it. **This list is not
padded from a generic anatomy table**; each line names a part the corpus has
actually surfaced, or a gap a read has actually made visible. At 787 unread
ordered lines it is a short list against a long walk, and it will grow.

- **Not yet observed — deferred, needs a second source:** *whether a change in
  a belief is distinguishable from the system's own contribution to that belief,
  at the point where the change is measured.* Surfaced by ORDER 1670
  (`cusum-gradual-drift-detection-preferences.md`) §4.5 on BeliefShift
  (arXiv 2603.23848), whose **Drift Coherence Score** exists to separate
  user-driven from model-driven belief change across 2,400 multi-session
  trajectories, and by its blunt statement that *"a drift detector that fires on
  model-driven belief changes (sycophancy) is worse than useless — it causes the
  agent to adapt to its own hallucinated consensus rather than the user's actual
  preferences."* `ARENA.md` holds **4** occurrences of *sycophan* and **0** of
  *Drift Coherence*, *user-driven*, or *model-driven belief change* across 128
  areas. **Not a sub-case of Area 95** (a memory that rewards its own mistakes —
  that is scoring) **nor of Area 33** (a detector is not a certifier — that is
  the class of claim a result supports). This is a third thing: **a detector
  whose own output changes the state it is measuring, so the signal it produces
  becomes an input to the next reading.** Deferred rather than opened, because
  one paper cannot honestly fill three slots, and a two-slot area is exactly the
  shape that lets an area look settled when it is not. **Never claim this brain
  part is unnecessary** — the read has reached it once and recorded it.

**Opened this pass and now closed** — recorded so a later pass does not
re-derive them as gaps:
- *When the accumulated internal simulation is exchanged for an action rather
  than accumulated alongside it — and whether that transition has a budget at
  all.* **Area 123**, earned by ORDER 1592 and ORDER 1589. `ARENA.md` held
  **49** occurrences of *ReAct* and **0** of *action threshold*, *analysis
  paralysis*, *overthink*, *reason-action*, *turn budget*, or *max_turns*.
  Area 22 governs which action and Area 32 governs when to stop; a system can
  satisfy both and still never leave the Thought phase, because the two ends are
  governed by different quantities. The two files earn one area because they do
  not cite each other: a practitioner guide gives the pathology (*higher
  reasoning capability translated into more self-doubt*, paired with rigid tool
  environments) and an experimental-psychology file gives the cost curve
  (**multi-model syllogisms >70% error** against near-ceiling single-model), and
  both say the same thing — **more deliberation stops being monotonically
  better, and the failure at the top of the range is paralysis rather than
  slowness.** The area's load-bearing composition is that **rank 1 bounds
  possibility-enumeration and rank 3 requires it**; the same act is the
  requirement and the failure mode, separated only by a bound.
- *Whether an action possibility is a property of the world or a relation
  between the world and the agent's capability — and whether "possible" and
  "perceived" are the same set.* **Area 116**, earned by ORDER 1521.
  `ARENA.md` held **3** occurrences of *affordance* across 115 areas and **0**
  of *Warren*. A hundred and fifteen areas treat affordance as a field an agent
  reads; Warren (1984) shows it is a **ratio against the agent's own body**, so
  the field does not exist independently of the reader. Gaver's split adds the
  sharper gap: **hidden** affordances fail silently and **false** ones announce
  themselves, so an error-listening design catches exactly one of the two.
- *Whether separately-produced signals are decided to be one event by a timing
  rule or by a causal one, and whether that rule is learned.* **Area 117**,
